## Chapter 4 Equilibria of Collisionless Systems

In SS1.2 we introduced the idea that stellar systems may be considered to be collisionless: we obtain a good approximation to the orbit of any star by calculating the orbit that it would have if the system's mass were smoothly distributed in space rather than concentrated into nearly point-like stars. Eventually, the true orbit deviates significantly from this model orbit, but in systems with more than a few thousand stars, the deviation is small for a time that is much larger than the crossing time \(t_{\rm cross}\). In fact, for a galaxy \(t_{\rm relax}\) is usually much larger even than the age of the universe, so the approximation that the potential is smooth provides a complete description of the dynamics.

In this chapter we consider model stellar systems that would be perfect equilibria if \(t_{\rm relax}\) were arbitrarily large. Such models are the primary tool for comparisons of observations and theory of galaxy dynamics. In Chapter 7 we shall see that they are also applicable to globular clusters, even though \(t_{\rm relax}\) is significantly smaller than the cluster's age, so long as it is recognized that the equilibrium evolves slowly, on a timescale of order \(t_{\rm relax}\).

We assume throughout that the stellar systems we examine consist of \(N\) identical point masses, which might be stars or dark-matter particles. Although unrealistic, this assumption greatly facilitates our work and has no impact on the validity of our results.

In SS4.1 we derive the equation that allows us to find equilibria, and discuss its connection to observational data. In SS4.2 we show that solutions ofthe equation can be readily found if integrals of motion in the galactic potential are known, and in SSSS4.3 to 4.5 we use such solutions to study models with a variety of symmetries. In SS4.6 we show that it is advantageous to express solutions in terms of action integrals. Unfortunately, in many practical cases insufficient integrals are known to obtain relevant solutions, so in SSSS4.7 and 4.8 we discuss alternative strategies, starting with heavily numerical approaches and moving on to approximate techniques that are based on moments of the fundamental equation. In SS4.9 we draw on techniques developed throughout the chapter to hunt for massive black holes and dark halos in galaxies using observations of the kinematics of their stars. In SS4.10 we address the question "what determines the distribution of stars in a galaxy?" This is a difficult question to which we shall have to return in Chapter 9.

### 4.1 The collisionless Boltzmann equation

When modeling a collisionless system such as an elliptical galaxy, it is neither practical nor worthwhile to follow the orbits of each of the galaxy's billions of stars. Most testable predictions depend on the probability of finding a star in the six-dimensional phase-space volume \({\rm d}^{3}{\bf x}{\rm d}^{3}{\bf v}\) around the position \({\bf x}\) and velocity \({\bf v}\). Therefore we define the **distribution function** (or df for short) \(f\) such that \(f({\bf x},{\bf v},t)\,{\rm d}^{3}{\bf x}{\rm d}^{3}{\bf v}\) is the probability that at time \(t\) a randomly chosen star, say star 1, has phase-space coordinates in the given range. Since by assumption all stars are identical, this probability is the same for stars \(2,3,\ldots,N\). By virtue of its definition \(f\) is normalized such that

\[\int{\rm d}^{3}{\bf x}{\rm d}^{3}{\bf v}\,f({\bf x},{\bf v},t)=1, \tag{4.1}\]

where the integral is over all phase space.

Let \({\bf w}=({\bf x},{\bf v})\) be the usual Cartesian coordinates, and consider an arbitrary region \({\cal V}\) of phase space. The probability of finding star 1 in \({\cal V}\) is \(P=\int_{{\cal V}}{\rm d}^{6}{\bf w}\,f({\bf w})\). Let \({\bf W}\) represent some arbitrary set of phase-space coordinates, and let \(F({\bf W})\) be the corresponding df; that is the probability of finding star 1 in \({\cal V}\) is \(P=\int_{{\cal V}}{\rm d}^{6}{\bf W}\,F({\bf W})\). If \({\cal V}\) is small enough, \(f\) and \(F\) will be approximately constant throughout it, and we can take them outside the integrals for \(P\). Thus

\[P=f({\bf w})\int_{{\cal V}}{\rm d}^{6}{\bf w}=F({\bf W})\int_{{\cal V}}{\rm d} ^{6}{\bf W}. \tag{4.2}\]

If the coordinates \({\bf W}\) are canonical, equation (D.81) implies that \(\int_{{\cal V}}{\rm d}^{6}{\bf w}=\int_{{\cal V}}{\rm d}^{6}{\bf W}\). Substituting this relation into (4.2), we conclude that \(F({\bf W})=f({\bf w})\). Therefore, the df has the same numerical value at a given phase-space point in _any_ canonical coordinate system. This invariance enables us henceforth to treat \({\bf w}=({\bf q},{\bf p})\) as an arbitrary system of canonical coordinates.

Any given star moves through phase space, so the probability of finding it at any given phase-space location evolves with time. We now derive the differential equation that is satisfied by \(f\) as a consequence of this evolution. As \(f\) evolves, probability must be conserved, in the same way that mass is conserved in a fluid flow. The conservation of fluid mass is described by the continuity equation (F.3)

\[{\partial\rho\over\partial t}+{\partial\over\partial{\bf x}}\cdot(\rho\dot{{ \bf x}})=0,\]

where \(\rho\) and \(\dot{{\bf x}}={\bf v}\) are the density and velocity of the fluid. The analogous equation for the conservation of probability in phase space is

\[{\partial f\over\partial t}+{\partial\over\partial{\bf w}}\cdot(f\dot{{\bf w}} )=0.\]

We now use Hamilton's equations (D.54) to eliminate \(\dot{{\bf w}}=(\dot{{\bf q}},\dot{{\bf p}})\). The second term in equation (4.4) becomes

\[\eqalign{{\partial\over\partial{\bf q}}\cdot(f\dot{{\bf q}})+{\partial\over \partial{\bf p}}\cdot(f\dot{{\bf p}})&={\partial\over\partial{\bf q}}\cdot \Bigl{(}f{\partial H\over\partial{\bf p}}\Bigr{)}-{\partial\over\partial{\bf p }}\cdot\Bigl{(}f{\partial H\over\partial{\bf q}}\Bigr{)}\cr&={\partial f \over\partial{\bf q}}\cdot{\partial H\over\partial{\bf p}}-{\partial f\over \partial{\bf p}}\cdot{\partial H\over\partial{\bf q}}\cr&=\dot{{\bf q}}\cdot{ \partial f\over\partial{\bf q}}+\dot{{\bf p}}\cdot{\partial f\over\partial{ \bf p}},\cr}\]

where we have used the fact that \(\partial^{2}H/\partial{\bf q}\partial{\bf p}=\partial^{2}H/\partial{\bf p} \partial{\bf q}\). Substituting this result into equation (4.4) we obtain the **collisionless Boltzmann equation1**

Footnote 1: Often also called the Vlasov equation, although it is a simplified version of an equation derived by L. Boltzmann in 1872. See Hénon (1982).

\[{\partial f\over\partial t}+\dot{{\bf q}}\cdot{\partial f\over\partial{\bf q} }+\dot{{\bf p}}\cdot{\partial f\over\partial{\bf p}}=0,\]

which is a partial differential equation for \(f\) as a function of six phase-space coordinates and time.

Equation (4.6) can be rewritten in a number of forms, each of which is useful in different contexts. Equation (4.5) enables us to write

\[\eqalign{0&={\partial f\over\partial t}+{\partial f\over\partial{\bf q}}\cdot {\partial H\over\partial{\bf p}}-{\partial f\over\partial{\bf p}}\cdot{ \partial H\over\partial{\bf q}}\cr&={\partial f\over\partial t}+[f,H],\cr}\]where the square bracket is a Poisson bracket (eq. D.65).

An alternative form of the collisionless Boltzmann equation can be derived by extending to six dimensions the concept of the convective or Lagrangian derivative (see eq. F.8). We define

\[\frac{\mathrm{d}f}{\mathrm{d}t}\equiv\frac{\partial f}{\partial t}+\dot{\mathbf{ w}}\cdot\frac{\partial f}{\partial\mathbf{w}}; \tag{4.8}\]

\(\mathrm{d}f/\mathrm{d}t\) represents the rate of change of the local probability density as seen by an observer who moves through phase space with a star. Comparison of equations (4.6) and (4.7) shows that \(\dot{\mathbf{w}}\cdot(\partial f/\partial\mathbf{w})=[f,H]\), so the convective derivative can also be written

\[\frac{\mathrm{d}f}{\mathrm{d}t}=\frac{\partial f}{\partial t}+[f,H], \tag{4.9}\]

and the collisionless Boltzmann equation (4.6) is simply

\[\frac{\mathrm{d}f}{\mathrm{d}t}=0. \tag{4.10}\]

In words, the flow through phase space of the probability fluid is incompressible; the phase-space density \(f\) of the fluid around a given star always remains the same.2 In contrast to flows of incompressible fluids such as water, the density will generally vary greatly from point to point in phase space; the density is constant as one follows the flow around a particular star but the density around different stars can be quite different.

Footnote 2: A simple example of an incompressible flow in phase space is provided by an idealized marathon race in which all runners travel at constant speeds: at the start of the course, the spatial density of runners is large but they travel at a wide variety of speeds; at the finish, the density is low, but at any given time all runners passing the finish line have nearly the same speed.

In terms of inertial Cartesian coordinates, in which \(H=\frac{1}{2}v^{2}+\Phi(\mathbf{x},t)\) with \(\Phi\) the gravitational potential, the collisionless Boltzmann equation reads

\[\frac{\partial f}{\partial t}+\mathbf{v}\cdot\frac{\partial f}{\partial \mathbf{x}}-\frac{\partial\Phi}{\partial\mathbf{x}}\cdot\frac{\partial f}{ \partial\mathbf{v}}=0. \tag{4.11}\]

In cylindrical coordinates we have (eq. 3.66) \(H=\frac{1}{2}(p_{R}^{2}+p_{\phi}^{2}/R^{2}+p_{z}^{2})+\Phi\) so with (4.7) the collisionless Boltzmann equation becomes3

Footnote 3: A reader unconvinced of the usefulness of the Hamiltonian formalism should try deriving either (4.12) or (4.14) directly from (4.11).

\[\begin{split}\frac{\partial f}{\partial t}+p_{R}\frac{\partial f }{\partial R}&+\frac{p_{\phi}}{R^{2}}\frac{\partial f}{\partial \phi}+p_{z}\frac{\partial f}{\partial z}-\left(\frac{\partial\Phi}{\partial R }-\frac{p_{\phi}^{2}}{R^{3}}\right)\frac{\partial f}{\partial p_{R}}\\ &-\frac{\partial\Phi}{\partial\phi}\frac{\partial f}{\partial p _{\phi}}-\frac{\partial\Phi}{\partial z}\frac{\partial f}{\partial p_{z}}=0. \end{split} \tag{4.12}\]To obtain the Hamiltonian for motion in spherical polar coordinates we replace in (3.218) \(\partial S/\partial r\) by \(p_{r}\), \(\partial S/\partial\theta\) by \(p_{\theta}\) and \(\partial S/\partial\phi\) by \(p_{\phi}\) and find

\[H={{1\over 2}}\Bigl{(}p_{r}^{2}+{p_{\theta}^{2}\over r^{2}}+{p_{\phi}^{2} \over r^{2}\sin^{2}\theta}\Bigr{)}+\Phi.\]

Using this expression in (4.7) we find

\[\eqalign{{{\partial f}\over{\partial t}}&+p_{r}{{\partial f}\over{\partial r} }+{p_{\theta}\over r^{2}}{{\partial f}\over{\partial\theta}}+{p_{\phi}\over r ^{2}\sin^{2}\theta}{{\partial f}\over{\partial\phi}}-\Bigl{(}{{\partial\Phi} \over{\partial r}}-{p_{\theta}^{2}\over r^{3}}-{p_{\phi}^{2}\over r^{3}\sin^{ 2}\theta}\Bigr{)}{{\partial f}\over{\partial p_{r}}}\cr&-\Bigl{(}{{\partial \Phi}\over{\partial\theta}}-{p_{\phi}^{2}\cos\theta\over r^{2}\sin^{3}\theta }\Bigr{)}{{\partial f}\over{\partial p_{\theta}}}-{{\partial\Phi}\over{ \partial\phi}}{{\partial f}\over{\partial p_{\phi}}}=0.}\]

Conversion to rotating coordinates is discussed in Problem 4.1.

**4.1.1 Limitations of the collisionless Boltzmann equation**

**(a) Finite stellar lifetimes** The physical basis of the collisionless Boltzmann equation is conservation of the objects that are described by the df. Stars are not really conserved because they are born and die, so their flow through phase space would be more accurately described by an equation of the type

\[{{{\rm d}f}\over{\rm d}t}={{\partial f}\over{\partial t}}+{\bf v}\cdot{{ \partial f}\over{\partial{\bf x}}}-{{\partial\Phi}\over{\partial{\bf x}}} \cdot{{\partial f}\over{\partial{\bf v}}}=B-D,\]

where \(B({\bf x},{\bf v},t)\) and \(D({\bf x},{\bf v},t)\) are the rates per unit phase-space volume at which stars are born and die. In the collisionless Boltzmann equation, \(B-D\) is set to zero. This is a useful approximation to the truth if and only if \(B-D\) is smaller in magnitude than terms on the left of equation (4.15). The term \({\bf v}\cdot{\partial f}/{\partial{\bf x}}\) is of order \(vf/R\), where \(v\) and \(R\) are the characteristic speed and radius in the galaxy. The ratio \(R/v\) is simply the crossing time \(t_{\rm cross}\) (SS1.2). Similarly, \(\partial\Phi/\partial{\bf x}\) is of order the characteristic acceleration \(a\), so the term \((\partial\Phi/\partial{\bf x})\cdot(\partial f/\partial{\bf v})\) is of order \(af/v\). Since \(a\approx v/t_{\rm cross}\), the two last terms in the middle section of equation (4.15) are of order \(f/t_{\rm cross}\). Thus consider the ratio

\[\gamma=\left|{B-D\over f/t_{\rm cross}}\right|.\]

The collisionless Boltzmann equation is valid if \(\gamma\ll 1\), which requires that the fractional change in the number of stars per crossing time is small.

The significance of this criterion can be clarified by some concrete examples. We consider two contrasting stellar types: M dwarfs, which have masses and \(\mathrel{\hbox to 0.0pt{\lower 4.0pt\hbox{ $\sim$}}\raise 1.0pt\hbox{$<$}}0.5\,{ \cal M}_{\odot}\) and live longer than the age of the universe; and O stars, which have masses \(\mathrel{\hbox to 0.0pt{\lower 4.0pt\hbox{ $\sim$}}\raise 1.0pt\hbox{$>$}}20\,{ \cal M}_{\odot}\) and lifetimes \(\mathrel{\hbox to 0.0pt{\lower 4.0pt\hbox{ $\sim$}}\raise 1.0pt\hbox{$<$}}10\,{\rm Myr}\) (BM Tables 3.13 and 5.3). In an elliptical galaxy the rate of formation of M dwarfs is negligible, and even the oldest M dwarfs have not had time to evolve significantly. Hence, the collisionless Boltzmann equation will apply accurately to the df of M dwarfs (\(\gamma\leq 0.01\)). Now consider the contrasting case of O stars in the Milky Way. These stars have lifetimes significantly shorter than a crossing time \(\sim 100\,\)Myr. In fact, an O star will scarcely move from its birthplace before it dies, and the phase-space distribution of such stars will depend entirely on the processes that govern star formation, and not at all on the collisionless Boltzmann equation (\(\gamma\simeq 10\)). In between these extremes, the collisionless Boltzmann equation will apply quite accurately to main-sequence populations in the Milky Way less massive than \(\sim 1.5\,{\cal M}_{\odot}\), since these stars live for \(\gtrsim 1\,\)Gyr, which will generally be some tens of crossing times. In certain circumstances the collisionless Boltzmann equation may even be applied to a population of short-lived objects, for example planetary nebulae in an elliptical galaxy, because the phase-space distributions of the objects' births and deaths are to a good approximation identical, so \(B-D\simeq 0\).

**(b) Correlations between stars** The average number density of stars in an infinitesimal volume of phase space is \(Nf\). However, in practice all we can hope to measure is the number density in some volume of phase space large enough to contain many stars. The natural assumption to make is that the density in such a volume is simply \(N\overline{f}\), where \(\overline{f}\) is the average of \(f\) within this volume.4 However, this assumption will only be correct if the positions of stars in phase space are uncorrelated: that is, knowing that star 1 is at \({\bf w}\) makes it neither more nor less likely that another star, say star 2, is at an adjacent phase-space location \({\bf w}^{\prime}\). Mathematically, we assume that the probability of finding star 1 in the volume \({\rm d}^{6}{\bf w}\) at \({\bf w}\) and star 2 in \({\rm d}^{6}{\bf w}^{\prime}\) at \({\bf w}^{\prime}\) is simply the product \(f({\bf w}){\rm d}^{6}{\bf w}\,f({\bf w}^{\prime}){\rm d}^{6}{\bf w}^{\prime}\) of the probabilities of finding star 1 at \({\bf w}\) and star 2 at \({\bf w}^{\prime}\)--in SS7.2.4 we shall call such distributions "separable". When the assumption of separability holds, the probability \(P_{\cal V}(k)\) that we will find \(k\) stars in a given volume \({\cal V}\) of phase space is given by the Poisson distribution (Appendix B.8)

Footnote 4: This function is sometimes called the **coarse-grained** df. The standard df is then called the **fine-grained** df to eliminate any danger of confusion with \(\overline{f}\).

\[P_{\cal V}(k)=\frac{\mu^{k}}{k!}{\rm e}^{-\mu}\quad{\rm where}\quad\mu\equiv N \overline{f}{\cal V}. \tag{4.17}\]

It is easy to show that the mean number of stars predicted by this probability distribution is \(\langle k\rangle=N\overline{f}{\cal V}\). Thus \(N\overline{f}\) is indeed the expectation value of the stellar number density, if the df is separable. Two obvious corollaries are that the mean mass within \({\cal V}\) is

\[\langle m\rangle=M\overline{f}({\bf w}){\cal V}, \tag{4.18}\]

where \(M\) is the total mass of the stellar system, and the mean luminosity emitted within \({\cal V}\) is

\[\langle l\rangle=L\overline{f}({\bf w}){\cal V}, \tag{4.19}\]where \(L\) is the system's luminosity.

In reality, the presence of star 1 at \({\bf x}\) always increases the probability that star 2 will be found at some nearby position \({\bf x}^{\prime}\) because stars attract one another. Hence, the assumption that the probability distribution of individual stars is separable is never strictly valid. In Chapter 7 we shall explore the effect of such correlations on the evolution of stellar systems. However, in this chapter we assume that separability holds, as it very nearly does for many stellar systems, because the force on a star from its neighbors is very much smaller than the force from the rest of the system.

#### Relation between the df and observables

At any fixed position \({\bf x}\), the integral

\[\nu({\bf x})\equiv\int{\rm d}^{3}{\bf v}\,f({\bf x},{\bf v}) \tag{4.20}\]

gives the probability per unit volume of finding a particular star at \({\bf x}\), regardless of its velocity. Multiplying by the total number \(N\) of stars in the population, we obtain the real-space number density of stars

\[n({\bf x})\equiv N\nu({\bf x}). \tag{4.21}\]

In the Galaxy \(n({\bf x})\) can in principle be determined from star counts, and thus \(\nu({\bf x})\) can be derived from \(n({\bf x})\). In other galaxies it is not usually possible to count stars, but we can derive \(\nu({\bf x})\) from the luminosity density \(j({\bf x})=L\nu({\bf x})\), where \(L\) is the luminosity of the stellar population (BM SS4.2.3).

It is often convenient to modify the definition of the df so that \(f{\rm d}^{6}{\bf w}\) represents not the probability of finding a given star in the phase-space volume \({\rm d}^{6}{\bf w}\), but rather the expected number, total mass, or total luminosity of the stars in \({\rm d}^{6}{\bf w}\). These modifications correspond to multiplying \(f\) by \(N\), \(M\), or \(L\), respectively. Ideally these different definitions would be reflected in different notations for the df. In practice the definition is usually clear from the context, and \(f\) is conventionally used to denote all of these quantities.

Dividing \(f\) by \(\nu\) we obtain the probability distribution of stellar velocities at \({\bf x}\)

\[P_{\bf x}({\bf v})=\frac{f({\bf x},{\bf v})}{\nu({\bf x})}, \tag{4.22}\]

which can be directly measured near the Sun (BM SS10.3). In external galaxies \(P_{\bf x}\) can be probed through the **line-of-sight velocity distribution** (losvd; BM SS11.1), which gives for a particular line of sight through the galaxy the fraction \(F(v_{\parallel}){\rm d}v_{\parallel}\) of the stars that have line-of-sight velocity within \({\rm d}v_{\parallel}\) of \(v_{\parallel}\). Almost all galaxies are sufficiently far away that all vectors from the observer to a point \({\bf x}\) in the galaxy are very nearly parallel to the fixed unit vector \(\hat{\bf s}\) from the observer to the center of the galaxy. Then \(x_{\parallel}\equiv\hat{\bf s}\cdot{\bf x}\) and \(v_{\parallel}\equiv\hat{\bf s}\cdot{\bf v}\) are the components of \({\bf x}\) and \({\bf v}\) parallel to the line of sight. We also define \({\bf x}_{\perp}\equiv{\bf x}-x_{\parallel}\hat{\bf s}\) and \({\bf v}_{\perp}\equiv{\bf v}-v_{\parallel}\hat{\bf s}\) to be the components of \({\bf x}\) and \({\bf v}\) in the plane of the sky. The relation between \(P_{\bf x}({\bf v})\) and \(F({\bf x}_{\perp},v_{\parallel})\) is

\[\eqalign{F({\bf x}_{\perp},v_{\parallel})&={\int{\rm d}x_{\parallel}\,\nu({\bf x })\int{\rm d}^{2}{\bf v}_{\perp}\,P_{\bf x}(v_{\parallel}\hat{\bf s}+{\bf v}_{ \perp})\over\int{\rm d}x_{\parallel}\,\nu({\bf x})}\cr&={\int{\rm d}x_{ \parallel}{\rm d}^{2}{\bf v}_{\perp}\,f({\bf x},{\bf v})\over\int{\rm d}x_{ \parallel}{\rm d}^{3}{\bf v}\,f({\bf x},{\bf v})}.}\]

The losvd is frequently quantified by two numbers, the mean line-of-sight velocity \(\overline{v}_{\parallel}\) and the dispersion \(\sigma_{\parallel}\) about this mean. We have

\[\eqalign{\overline{v}_{\parallel}({\bf x}_{\perp})&\equiv\int{\rm d}v_{ \parallel}\,v_{\parallel}F({\bf x}_{\perp},v_{\parallel})={\int{\rm d}x_{ \parallel}{\rm d}^{3}{\bf v}\,v_{\parallel}f({\bf x},{\bf v})\over\int{\rm d}x _{\parallel}{\rm d}^{3}{\bf v}\,f({\bf x},{\bf v})}\cr&={\int{\rm d}x_{ \parallel}\,\nu({\bf x})\,\hat{\bf s}\cdot\overline{\bf v}\over\int{\rm d}x_{ \parallel}\,\nu({\bf x})},}\]

where we have defined the **mean velocity** at location \({\bf x}\)

\[\overline{\bf v}({\bf x})\equiv\int{\rm d}^{3}{\bf v}\,{\bf v}P_{\bf x}({\bf v })={1\over\nu({\bf x})}\int{\rm d}^{3}{\bf v}\,{\bf v}f({\bf x},{\bf v}).\]

The **line-of-sight velocity dispersion** is defined to be

\[\eqalign{\sigma_{\parallel}^{2}({\bf x}_{\perp})&\equiv\int{\rm d}v_{ \parallel}\,(v_{\parallel}-\overline{v}_{\parallel})^{2}F({\bf x}_{\perp},v_{ \parallel})\cr&={\int{\rm d}x_{\parallel}{\rm d}^{3}{\bf v}\,(\hat{\bf s}\cdot {\bf v}-\overline{v}_{\parallel})^{2}f({\bf x},{\bf v})\over\int{\rm d}x_{ \parallel}{\rm d}^{3}{\bf v}\,f({\bf x},{\bf v})}.}\]

The line-of-sight velocity dispersion is determined both by the variation in the mean velocity \(\overline{v}_{\parallel}({\bf x})\) along the line of sight, and the spread in stellar velocities at each point in the galaxy around \(\overline{\bf v}({\bf x})\). This spread is characterized by the **velocity-dispersion tensor**

\[\eqalign{\sigma_{ij}^{2}({\bf x})&\equiv{1\over\nu({\bf x})}\int{\rm d}^{3}{ \bf v}\,(v_{i}-\overline{v}_{i})(v_{j}-\overline{v}_{j})f({\bf x},{\bf v})\cr &=\overline{v_{i}v_{j}}-\overline{v}_{i}\overline{v}_{j}.}\]

The velocity-dispersion tensor is manifestly symmetric, so we know from matrix algebra that at any point \({\bf x}\) we may choose a set of orthogonal axes \(\hat{\bf e}_{i}({\bf x})\) in which \(\mathbf{\sigma}^{2}\) is diagonal, that is, \(\sigma_{ij}^{2}=\sigma_{ii}^{2}\delta_{ij}\) (no summation over \(i\), and \(\delta_{ij}=1\) for \(i=j\) and zero otherwise). The ellipsoid that has the diagonalizing coordinate axes \(\hat{\mathbf{e}}_{i}(\mathbf{x})\) for its principal axes and \(\sigma_{11}\), \(\sigma_{22}\) and \(\sigma_{33}\) for its semi-axis lengths is called the **velocity ellipsoid** at \(\mathbf{x}\).

To determine the relation between the velocity-dispersion tensor and the line-of-sight velocity dispersion, we let \(u(\mathbf{x})\equiv\hat{\mathbf{s}}\cdot\overline{\mathbf{v}}(\mathbf{x})- \overline{v}_{\parallel}\) be the difference between the mean velocity parallel to the line of sight at \(\mathbf{x}\) and the mean velocity for the entire line of sight. Then we can rewrite (4.25) as follows:

\[\begin{split}\sigma_{\parallel}^{2}(\mathbf{x}_{\perp})& =\frac{\int\mathrm{d}x_{\parallel}\mathrm{d}^{3}\mathbf{v}\,[ \hat{\mathbf{s}}\cdot(\mathbf{v}-\overline{\mathbf{v}})+u]^{2}f(\mathbf{x}, \mathbf{v})}{\int\mathrm{d}x_{\parallel}\mathrm{d}^{3}\mathbf{v}\,f(\mathbf{x },\mathbf{v})}\\ &=\frac{\int\mathrm{d}x_{\parallel}\,\nu(\mathbf{x})\big{(}\hat{ \mathbf{s}}\cdot\boldsymbol{\sigma}^{2}\cdot\hat{\mathbf{s}}+u^{2}\big{)}}{ \int\mathrm{d}x_{\parallel}\,\nu(\mathbf{x})},\end{split} \tag{4.27}\]

where we introduce the notation \(\hat{\mathbf{s}}\cdot\boldsymbol{\sigma}^{2}\cdot\hat{\mathbf{s}}\equiv\sum_ {ij}\hat{s}_{i}\sigma_{ij}^{2}\hat{s}_{j}\).

These results show that once \(\nu\), \(\overline{\mathbf{v}}\) and \(\boldsymbol{\sigma}^{2}\) are known at each point in a model, the observable quantities \(v_{\parallel}\) and \(\sigma_{\parallel}^{2}\) can be determined for that model. This fact makes \(\nu\), \(\overline{\mathbf{v}}\) and \(\sigma_{ij}^{2}\), all functions of \(\mathbf{x}\), vital links between observations and theoretical models. Moreover, we shall see in SS4.8 that in equilibrium stellar systems there are simple relations between these quantities and the gravitational field (the Jeans equations).

Notice that while \(v_{\parallel}\) depends only on the mean velocity field \(\overline{\mathbf{v}}(\mathbf{x})\), there are contributions to \(\sigma_{\parallel}^{2}\) from both \(\boldsymbol{\sigma}^{2}\) and \(\overline{\mathbf{v}}\). Moreover, both contributions are inherently positive, so \(\sigma_{\parallel}^{2}\) is in general larger than the average of the intrinsic squared velocity dispersion \(\hat{\mathbf{s}}\cdot\boldsymbol{\sigma}^{2}\cdot\hat{\mathbf{s}}\) along the line of sight.

One shortcoming of \(\overline{v}_{\parallel}\) and \(\sigma_{\parallel}^{2}\) as probes of the dynamics of a galaxy is that they are hard to measure accurately because they are sensitive to the contributions of the small number of high-velocity stars.

**An example** In SS4.3.3a we shall encounter an exceptionally simple model system called the Plummer model. This is a non-rotating spherical system in which the velocity distribution \(P_{\bf x}\) depends only on \(v\equiv|{\bf v}|\), and the gravitational potential is given by equation (2.44a). The full curves in Figure 4.1 show \(P_{\bf x}(v)\) at the center of the system and at \(r=2b\), where \(b\) is the Plummer scale length. Notice that \(P_{\bf x}\) vanishes for speeds larger than the escape speed \(\sqrt{2|\Phi({\bf x})|}\) (eq. 2.31). At small radii, where \(|\Phi|\) is relatively large, a graph of \(P_{\bf x}\) versus \(v\) is wide and gently peaked, while at large radii, where \(|\Phi|\) is much smaller, a plot of \(P_{\bf x}(v)\) shows a high, narrow peak.

The losvd\(F(v_{\parallel})\) along a line of sight through a Plummer model depends on the projected distance \(R=|{\bf x}_{\perp}|\) between the line of sight and the center of the model because it is a weighted mean of the velocity distributions \(P_{\bf x}(v)\) for different points along the line of sight (eq. 4.23). The dashed curves in Figure 4.1 show the losvd for the line of sight through the center, and one further out. Notice that the losvd at each radius is more centrally peaked than the velocity distribution at that radius. There are two reasons for this phenomenon. First, \(F(v_{\parallel})\) is depressed at large values of \(v_{\parallel}\) by the integral over \({\bf v}_{\perp}\) in (4.23) because the range of allowed values of \({\bf v}_{\perp}\) diminishes rapidly as \(v_{\parallel}\) approaches the escape speed. A subsidiary effect is that the line of sight through the center samples points that are physically far from the cluster center, where the velocity distribution \(P_{\bf x}\) is narrowly peaked around \(v=0\).

### 4.2 Jeans theorems

In SS3.1.1 we introduced the concept of an integral of motion in a given stationary potential \(\Phi({\bf x})\). According to equation (3.56), a function of the phase-space coordinates \(I({\bf x},{\bf v})\) is an integral if and only if

\[\frac{{\rm d}}{{\rm d}t}I[{\bf x}(t),{\bf v}(t)]=0 \tag{4.28}\]

along any orbit. With the equations of motion this becomes

\[\frac{{\rm d}I}{{\rm d}t}=\frac{\partial I}{\partial{\bf x}}\cdot\frac{{\rm d }{\bf x}}{{\rm d}t}+\frac{\partial I}{\partial{\bf v}}\cdot\frac{{\rm d}{\bf v }}{{\rm d}t}=0,\quad{\rm or}\quad{\bf v}\cdot\frac{\partial I}{\partial{\bf x }}-\frac{\partial\Phi}{\partial{\bf x}}\cdot\frac{\partial I}{\partial{\bf v }}=0. \tag{4.29}\]

Comparing this with equation (4.11), we see that the condition for \(I\) to be an integral is identical with the condition for \(I\) to be a steady-state solution of the collisionless Boltzmann equation. This leads to the following theorem, first stated by Jeans (1915).

**Jeans theorem**_Any steady-state solution of the collisionless Boltzmann equation depends on the phase-space coordinates only through integrals of motion in the given potential, and any function of the integrals yields a steady-state solution of the collisionless Boltzmann equation.__Proof:_ Suppose \(f\) is a steady-state solution of the collisionless Boltzmann equation. Then, as we have just seen, \(f\)_is_ an integral, and the first part of the theorem is proved. Conversely, if \(I_{1}\) to \(I_{n}\) are \(n\) integrals, and if \(f\) is any function of \(n\) variables, then

\[{{\rm d}\over{\rm d}t}f\left[I_{1}({\bf x},{\bf v}),\ldots,I_{n}({\bf x},{\bf v })\right]=\sum_{m=1}^{n}{\partial f\over\partial I_{m}}{{\rm d}I_{m}\over{\rm d }t}=0\]

and \(f\) is seen to satisfy the collisionless Boltzmann equation.\(\triangleleft\)

Many of the results of this chapter will be based on the second proposition stated by the Jeans theorem, namely, that any function of integrals solves the collisionless Boltzmann equation. However, the first of Jeans's propositions, the assurance that the df of any steady-state galaxy must be a function of integrals, is only of limited use since the examples discussed in SSSS3.2 and 3.3 show that orbits often respect integrals for which we lack analytic expressions. In such cases the first part of the Jeans theorem simply tells us, unhelpfully, that the df is a function of integrals whose form is unknown.

Fortunately the time averages theorem (page 215) enables us to show that if all orbits in a galaxy are regular, then we can forget about any non-isolating integrals:

**Strong Jeans theorem**_The df of a steady-state stellar system in which almost all orbits are regular with non-resonant frequencies may be presumed to be a function only of three independent isolating integrals, which may be taken to be the actions._

_Proof:_ Any observable property involves averaging the df over some non-zero region of phase space; we may formalize this by stating that all observations are based on moments of the form \(\langle Q\rangle=\int{\rm d}^{3}{\bf x}{\rm d}^{3}{\bf v}\,Qf\), where \(Q({\bf x},{\bf v})\) is some smooth function on phase space and \(f\) is the df. Since almost all orbits are regular, we can assume that phase space is covered by angle-action coordinates \(({\hbox{\boldmath$\theta$}},{\bf J})\) and we can also write

\[\langle Q\rangle=\int{\rm d}^{3}{\hbox{\boldmath$\theta$}}\,{\rm d}^{3}{\bf J }\,Q({\bf x},{\bf v})f({\bf x},{\bf v},t),\]

where \(({\bf x},{\bf v})\) are to be interpreted as functions of \(({\hbox{\boldmath$\theta$}},{\bf J})\). Since the stellar system is in a steady state, \(\langle Q\rangle\) is time-independent, so

\[\langle Q\rangle=\overline{\langle Q\rangle}=\int{\rm d}^{3}{\hbox{\boldmath $\theta$}}\,{\rm d}^{3}{\bf J}\,Q({\hbox{\boldmath$\theta$}},{\bf J}) \overline{f}({\hbox{\boldmath$\theta$}},{\bf J}),\]

where

\[\overline{f}({\hbox{\boldmath$\theta$}},{\bf J})\equiv\lim_{T\to\infty}{1 \over T}\int_{0}^{T}{\rm d}t\,f({\bf x},{\bf v},t)\]is the time average of the df at the given point in phase space. If \(\delta^{3}{\bf J}\) and \(\delta^{3}{\boldsymbol{\theta}}\) are small coordinate ranges, \(\overline{f}\,\delta^{3}{\boldsymbol{\theta}}\delta^{3}{\bf J}\) is the probability that at a given instant a randomly chosen star has actions in \(\delta^{3}{\bf J}\) and angles in \(\delta^{3}{\boldsymbol{\theta}}\). By the time averages theorem this is \(\delta^{3}{\boldsymbol{\theta}}/(2\pi)^{3}\) times the probability that the star has actions in \(\delta^{3}{\bf J}\), which we denote \((2\pi)^{3}f_{\rm J}({\bf J})\delta^{3}{\bf J}\). Eliminating \(\overline{f}\) from equation (4.32a) we may therefore write

\[\langle Q\rangle=\int{\rm d}^{3}{\boldsymbol{\theta}}\,{\rm d}^{3}{\bf J}\,Q({ \boldsymbol{\theta}},{\bf J})f_{\rm J}({\bf J}), \tag{4.33}\]

which shows that any observable can be evaluated using only the time-independent df\(f_{\rm J}({\bf J})\cdot\)d

In summary, the Jeans theorem tells us that if \(I_{1},\ldots,I_{n}\) are \(n\) independent integrals in a given potential, then any df of the form \(f(I_{1})\), \(f(I_{1},I_{2})\), \(\ldots,f(I_{1},\ldots,I_{n})\) is a solution of the collisionless Boltzmann equation. The strong Jeans theorem tells us that if the potential of a steady-state galaxy is such that almost all orbits are regular, then for all practical purposes the galaxy may be represented by a df of the form \(f(I_{1},I_{2},I_{3})\), where \(I_{1},I_{2},I_{3}\) are three independent isolating integrals.

#### 4.2.1 Choice of \(f\) and relations between moments

**(a) DF depending only on \(H\)** In any steady-state potential \(\Phi({\bf x})\), the Hamiltonian \(H\) is an integral of motion. Consequently, an equilibrium stellar system is obtained by taking \(f\) to be any non-negative function of \(H\)--dfs of this type are called **ergodic**.5 If the potential is constant in an inertial frame, \(H\) will be of the form \(H=\frac{1}{2}v^{2}+\Phi({\bf x})\) and it follows that the mean velocity vanishes everywhere:

Footnote 5: In statistical mechanics and chaos theory the term “ergodic” denotes a system that uniformly explores its energy surface in phase space, which implies that the df is uniform on the energy surface. In our usage the df is ergodic but the motion of individual stars generally is not.

\[\overline{\bf v}({\bf x})=\frac{1}{\nu({\bf x})}\int{\rm d}^{3}{\bf v}\,{\bf v }f({\frac{1}{2}}v^{2}+\Phi)=0, \tag{4.34}\]

where the second equality follows because the integrand is an odd function of \({\bf v}\) and the integral is over all velocity space. A similar line of reasoning shows that the velocity-dispersion tensor is isotropic:

\[\sigma^{2}_{ij}=\overline{v_{i}v_{j}}=\sigma^{2}\delta_{ij},\] (4.35a) where \[\begin{split}\sigma^{2}({\bf x})&=\frac{1}{\nu({ \bf x})}\int{\rm d}v_{z}\,v_{z}^{2}\int{\rm d}v_{x}{\rm d}v_{y}\,f[{\frac{1}{2} }(v_{x}^{2}+v_{y}^{2}+v_{z}^{2})+\Phi({\bf x})]\\ &=\frac{4\pi}{3\nu({\bf x})}\int_{0}^{\infty}{\rm d}v\,v^{4}f({ \frac{1}{2}}v^{2}+\Phi).\end{split} \tag{4.35b}\]An equivalent statement is that the velocity ellipsoid is a sphere of radius \(\sigma\). An **isotropic system** has a velocity-dispersion tensor that is everywhere isotropic. Thus every system with an ergodic df is isotropic.

**(b) DF depending on \(H\) and \(L\)** If the potential \(\Phi({\bf x})\) is spherically symmetric, the three components of the angular-momentum vector \({\bf L}\) are isolating integrals that can be included in the arguments of \(f\) in addition to the Hamiltonian. Since the potential is spherical, we shall confine ourselves to dfs that produce systems that have complete spherical symmetry; that is, in which the three components of \({\bf L}\) can occur only through their contributions to \(L=|{\bf L}|\).6 Thus we consider the case in which the df is some non-negative function \(f(H,L)\). Let \(v_{r}\) and \({\bf v}_{\rm t}\) (for tangential) be the components of \({\bf v}\) parallel and perpendicular to the radial direction, so \(v_{\rm t}^{2}=v_{\theta}^{2}+v_{\phi}^{2}\) in spherical coordinates \((r,\theta,\phi)\). Then \(L=rv_{\rm t}\) and \(H={1\over 2}(v_{r}^{2}+v_{\rm t}^{2})+\Phi(r)\), and the mean velocity is

Footnote 6: Lynden–Bell (1960) discusses spherical systems that rotate.

\[\eqalign{\overline{v}_{r}&={1\over\nu}\int{\rm d}v_{r}\,v_{r}\int{\rm d}^{2}{ \bf v}_{t}\,f[{1\over 2}(v_{r}^{2}+v_{\rm t}^{2})+\Phi(r),rv_{\rm t}]=0,\cr \overline{\bf v}_{\rm t}&={1\over\nu}\int{\rm d}^{2}{\bf v}_{\rm t}\,{\bf v}_ {\rm t}\int{\rm d}v_{r}\,f[{1\over 2}(v_{r}^{2}+v_{\rm t}^{2})+\Phi(r),rv_{\rm t}]=0.}\]

In both cases the integrals vanish because the integrand is an odd function of either \(v_{r}\) or \({\bf v}_{\rm t}\). Similar considerations show that the velocity-dispersion tensor is diagonal in the \((v_{r},v_{\theta},v_{\phi})\) frame, with diagonal components

\[\eqalign{\sigma_{r}^{2}\equiv\overline{v_{r}^{2}}&={1\over\nu}\int{\rm d}v_{r} \,v_{r}^{2}\int{\rm d}^{2}{\bf v}_{\rm t}\,f[{1\over 2}(v_{r}^{2}+v_{\theta}^{2} +v_{\phi}^{2})+\Phi,rv_{\rm t}],\cr&={2\pi\over\nu}\int_{-\infty}^{\infty}{\rm d }v_{r}\,v_{r}^{2}\int_{0}^{\infty}{\rm d}v_{\rm t}\,v_{\rm t}f[{1\over 2}(v_{r}^{2 }+v_{\rm t}^{2})+\Phi,rv_{\rm t}],\cr\sigma_{\theta}^{2}\equiv\overline{v_{ \theta}^{2}}&={1\over\nu}\int{\rm d}v_{\theta}\,v_{\theta}^{2}\int{\rm d}v_{ \phi}\int{\rm d}v_{r}\,f[{1\over 2}(v_{r}^{2}+v_{\theta}^{2}+v_{\phi}^{2})+\Phi,rv_{ \rm t}],\cr&={\pi\over\nu}\int_{0}^{\infty}{\rm d}v_{\rm t}\,v_{\rm t}^{3} \int_{-\infty}^{\infty}{\rm d}v_{r}\,f[{1\over 2}(v_{r}^{2}+v_{\rm t}^{2})+\Phi,rv_{ \rm t}],\cr\sigma_{\phi}^{2}&=\sigma_{\theta}^{2}.}\]

In general \(\sigma_{\theta}^{2}(r)\neq\sigma_{r}^{2}(r)\) because the dependence of \(f\) on \(v_{\rm t}\) differs from its dependence on \(v_{r}\).

We shall see in SS4.8 that in a stellar system \(\nu\mathbf{\sigma}^{2}\) plays a role analogous to that of pressure in a fluid. The inequality of \(\sigma_{r}^{2}\) and \(\sigma_{\theta}^{2}\) implies that in general different pressures act radially and tangentially in a spherical stellar system; in other words pressure is a tensor rather than a scalar.

**(c) DF depending on \(H\) and \(L_{z}\)** If the potential \(\Phi({\bf x})\) is axisymmetric, \(L_{z}=Rv_{\phi}\) is an isolating integral that can be included in \(f\) alongside \(H\)Then, in cylindrical coordinates the mean velocity has components

\[\overline{v}_{R} =\frac{1}{\nu}\int\mathrm{d}v_{R}\,v_{R}\int\mathrm{d}v_{z}\int \mathrm{d}v_{\phi}\,f[\tfrac{1}{2}(v_{R}^{2}+v_{z}^{2}+v_{\phi}^{2})+\Phi,Rv_{ \phi}]=0,\] \[\overline{v}_{z} =\frac{1}{\nu}\int\mathrm{d}v_{z}\,v_{z}\int\mathrm{d}v_{R}\int \mathrm{d}v_{\phi}\,f[\tfrac{1}{2}(v_{R}^{2}+v_{z}^{2}+v_{\phi}^{2})+\Phi,Rv_{ \phi}]=0, \tag{4.38}\] \[\overline{v}_{\phi} =\frac{1}{\nu}\int\mathrm{d}v_{\phi}\,v_{\phi}\int\mathrm{d}v_{R} \int\mathrm{d}v_{z}\,f[\tfrac{1}{2}(v_{R}^{2}+v_{z}^{2}+v_{\phi}^{2})+\Phi,Rv_{ \phi}].\]

The integrals for \(\overline{v}_{R}\) and \(\overline{v}_{z}\) vanish because the integrands are odd functions of \(v_{R}\) and \(v_{z}\), respectively. The integral for \(\overline{v}_{\phi}\) will also vanish if \(f\) is an even function of \(L_{z}\). In general \(f(H,L_{z})\) can be decomposed into a part that is even in \(L_{z}\) and a part that is odd:

\[f(H,L_{z})=f_{+}(H,L_{z})+f_{-}(H,L_{z})\] (4.39a) where \[f_{\pm}(H,L_{z})\equiv\tfrac{1}{2}[f(H,L_{z})\pm f(H,-L_{z})]. \tag{4.39b}\]

The even part of \(f\) will not contribute to \(\nu\overline{v}_{\phi}\), while \(f_{-}\) will not contribute to the corresponding integral (4.20) for \(\nu\). The velocity-dispersion tensor is diagonal in the \((v_{R},v_{z},v_{\phi})\) frame, with non-zero components

\[\overline{v_{R}^{2}}=\sigma_{R}^{2} =\frac{1}{\nu}\int\mathrm{d}v_{R}\,v_{R}^{2}\int\mathrm{d}v_{z} \int\mathrm{d}v_{\phi}\,f[\tfrac{1}{2}(v_{R}^{2}+v_{z}^{2}+v_{\phi}^{2})+\Phi,Rv_{\phi}],\] \[\sigma_{z}^{2} =\sigma_{R}^{2},\] \[\sigma_{\phi}^{2} =\frac{1}{\nu}\int\mathrm{d}v_{\phi}\,(v_{\phi}-\overline{v}_{ \phi})^{2}\int\mathrm{d}v_{R}\int\mathrm{d}v_{z}\,f[\tfrac{1}{2}(v_{R}^{2}+v_ {z}^{2}+v_{\phi}^{2})+\Phi,Rv_{\phi}]. \tag{4.40}\]

The pressure that acts in the azimuthal direction is in general different from that which acts in any direction within the meridional plane.

### 4.3 DFs for spherical systems

The simplest stellar systems are spherical. A study of spherical models not only provides a good introduction to the structure of more general systems, but is also of considerable practical interest because some elliptical galaxies and clusters of galaxies, and most globular clusters, are very nearly spherical. For simplicity we shall consider the case in which the system has only one stellar population, so all stars are identical and there is a single df \(f\). We shall also generally assume that the mass density that generates the system's gravitational potential is proportional to \(\int\mathrm{d}^{3}\mathbf{v}\,f\)--such systems are called **self-consistent** because the density distribution determines the potential through Poisson's equation, and the potential must also determine the density consistently through the collisionless Boltzmann equation. Many of the models we study are readily generalized to the more realistic case of multiple stellar populations, each of which will have its own df and its own contribution to the total mass density.

We shall find it convenient to define a new gravitational potential and a new energy. If \(\Phi_{0}\) is some constant, then let the **relative potential**\(\Psi\) and the **relative energy**\({\cal E}\) of a star be defined by

\[\Psi\equiv-\Phi+\Phi_{0}\quad\mbox{and}\quad{\cal E}\equiv-H+\Phi_{0}=\Psi-{ {1\over 2}}v^{2}. \tag{4.41}\]

In practice, we generally choose \(\Phi_{0}\) to be such that \(f>0\) for \({\cal E}>0\) and \(f=0\) for \({\cal E}\leq 0\). If an isolated system extends to infinity, \(\Phi_{0}=0\) and the relative energy is equal to the binding energy. The relative potential of an isolated system satisfies Poisson's equation in the form

\[\nabla^{2}\Psi=-4\pi G\rho, \tag{4.42}\]

subject to the boundary condition \(\Psi\to\Phi_{0}\) as \(|{\bf x}|\to\infty\).

##### 4.3.1 Ergodic DFs for systems

Suppose we observe a spherical stellar system that is confined by a known spherical potential \(\Phi(r)\). Then it is possible to derive for the system a unique ergodic df that depends on the phase-space coordinates only through the Hamiltonian \(H({\bf x},{\bf v})\). In this section we shall express this df as a function of the relative energy \(f({\cal E})\). To derive this df we note that the probability density \(\nu(r)\) can be written as the integral of \(f\) over all velocities. Since \(f\) depends on the magnitude \(v\) of \({\bf v}\) and not its direction, we can immediately integrate over angular coordinates in velocity space to produce \(4\pi\). We then have

\[\nu(r)=4\pi\int{\rm d}v\,v^{2}f(\Psi-{{1\over 2}}v^{2})=4\pi\int_{0}^{ \Psi}{\rm d}{\cal E}\,f({\cal E})\sqrt{2(\Psi-{\cal E})}, \tag{4.43}\]

where we have used equation (4.41) and assumed that the constant \(\Phi_{0}\) in the definition of \({\cal E}\) has been chosen such that \(f=0\) for \({\cal E}\leq 0\). Since \(\Psi\) is a monotonic function of \(r\) in any spherical system (Problem 2.16), we can regard \(\nu\) as a function of \(\Psi\) instead of \(r\). Thus

\[{1\over\sqrt{8}\pi}\nu(\Psi)=2\int_{0}^{\Psi}{\rm d}{\cal E}\,f({\cal E})\sqrt {\Psi-{\cal E}}. \tag{4.44}\]

**Box 4.1: An isolated system**

**with an ergodic DF is spherical**

An obvious question is whether there is any _self-consistent_ non-spherical stellar system with an ergodic df--in mathematical terms, are there finite, non-spherical solutions of the equations \(\rho({\bf x})=M\int{\rm d}^{3}{\bf v}\,f[{1\over 2}v^{2}+\Phi({\bf x})]\) and \(\nabla^{2}\Phi=4\pi G\rho\), in which \(\Phi\to 0\) as \(|{\bf x}|\to\infty\)?

The first of these equations shows that the density depends on position only through the potential \(\Phi\); thus the surfaces of constant density and potential coincide, so within the system the potential is a function \(\Phi(\rho)\) of the density. Now define

\[p(\rho)=-\int_{0}^{\rho}{\rm d}\rho^{\prime}\,\rho^{\prime}{{\rm d}\Phi\over{ \rm d}\rho}(\rho^{\prime}).\]

Using Problem 4.4, it is easy to see that \({\rm d}\Phi/{\rm d}\rho<0\), so \(p(\rho)>0\). Taking the gradient of this equation yields \(\mathbf{\nabla}p=-\rho\mathbf{\nabla}\Phi\), which is the equation of hydrostatic equilibrium (F.12) for a barotropic fluid with equation of state \(p(\rho)\) (cf. eq. F.27).

We may now employ **Lichtenstein's theorem** on the symmetries of self-gravitating fluids, which states (Lindblom 1992): _Consider an isolated, self-gravitating, barotropic fluid of finite extent that is in a steady state, so the velocity \({\bf v}\) and density \(\rho\) at every point are independent of time. If there is an axis \(\hat{\bf e}_{z}\) such that \({\bf v}\cdot\hat{\bf e}_{z}=0\), then the density distribution has a symmetry plane perpendicular to \(\hat{\bf e}_{z}\)._

For a static fluid (\({\bf v}=0\)), there must be a symmetry plane perpendicular to every axis, so the fluid must be spherically symmetric. Thus _all isolated, finite, static, self-gravitating, barotropic fluids must be spherical_. Since we have shown that a stellar system with an ergodic df satisfies the same equations--Poisson's equation, the equation of hydrostatic equilibrium, and the equation of state \(p(\rho)\)--we conclude that _any isolated, finite, stellar system with an ergodic df must be spherical_.

Differentiating both sides with respect to \(\Psi\), we obtain

\[{1\over\sqrt{8}\pi}{{\rm d}\nu\over{\rm d}\Psi}=\int_{0}^{\Psi}{\rm d}{\cal E }\,{f({\cal E})\over\sqrt{\Psi-{\cal E}}}.\]

Equation (4.45) is an Abel integral equation having solution (B.74)

\[f({\cal E})={1\over\sqrt{8}\pi^{2}}{{\rm d}\over{\rm d}{\cal E}}\int_{0}^{{ \cal E}}{{\rm d}\Psi\over\sqrt{{\cal E}-\Psi}}{{\rm d}\nu\over{\rm d}\Psi}.\]

An equivalent formula is

\[f({\cal E})={1\over\sqrt{8}\pi^{2}}\biggl{[}\int_{0}^{{\cal E}}{{\rm d}\Psi \over\sqrt{{\cal E}-\Psi}}{{\rm d}^{2}\nu\over{\rm d}\Psi^{2}}+{1\over\sqrt{{ \cal E}}}\biggl{(}{{\rm d}\nu\over{\rm d}\Psi}\biggr{)}_{\Psi=0}\biggr{]}.\]This result is due to Eddington (1916b), and we shall call it **Eddington's formula**. It implies that, given a spherical density distribution, we can recover an ergodic df that generates a model with the given density. However, we have no guarantee that the solution \(f({\cal E})\) to equations (4.46) will satisfy the physical requirement that it be nowhere negative. Indeed, we may conclude from equation (4.46a) that _a spherical density distribution \(\nu(r)\) in the potential \(\Phi(r)\) can arise from an ergodic df if and only if_

\[\int_{0}^{{\cal E}}{{\rm d}\Psi\over\sqrt{{\cal E}-\Psi}}{{\rm d}\nu\over{\rm d }\Psi}\]

_is an increasing function of \({\cal E}\)._ Note that this result holds regardless of whether the potential is self-consistently generated by the df.

**(a) Ergodic Hernquist, Jaffe and isochrone models** In SS2.2.2g we introduced the Jaffe and Hernquist models, which are members of the family of two-power density models. We now use Eddington's formula to recover the ergodic dfs of these models, as well as the df of the isochrone model that was introduced in SS2.2.2d.

From the second of equations (2.66) we have that the mass of a Hernquist model is related to the scale density \(\rho_{0}\) and radius \(a\) by \(M=2\pi\rho_{0}a^{3}\). The density of the Hernquist model is non-zero at all finite radii, so we choose \(\Phi_{0}=\Phi(r\to\infty)=0\). Then from equation (2.67) radius is related to potential by

\[{r\over a}={1\over\widetilde{\Psi}}-1\quad{\rm where}\quad\widetilde{\Psi} \equiv{\Psi a\over GM}=-{\Phi a\over GM}.\]

Using this result to eliminate \(r/a\) from equation (2.64) with \(\alpha=1\) and \(\beta=4\) we obtain

\[\nu(\Psi)={\rho\over M}={1\over 2\pi a^{3}}{\widetilde{\Psi}^{4}\over 1- \widetilde{\Psi}}.\]

Differentiating with respect to \(\Psi\) we have

\[{{\rm d}\nu\over{\rm d}\Psi}={1\over 2\pi a^{2}GM}{\widetilde{\Psi}^{3}(4-3 \widetilde{\Psi})\over(1-\widetilde{\Psi})^{2}}.\]

Equation (4.46b) gives the df to be

\[f_{\rm H}({\cal E})={\surd 2\over(2\pi)^{3}(GM)^{2}a}\int_{0}^{{\cal E}}{{ \rm d}\Psi\over\sqrt{{\cal E}-\Psi}}{2\widetilde{\Psi}^{2}(6-8\widetilde{ \Psi}+3\widetilde{\Psi}^{2})\over(1-\widetilde{\Psi})^{3}}.\]

The evaluation of this integral is straightforward but tedious. The final result is (Hernquist 1990)

\[\eqalign{f_{\rm H}({\cal E})&={1\over\sqrt{2}(2\pi)^{3}(GMa)^{3/2}}{\sqrt{ \mathaccent 869{\cal E}}\over(1-\mathaccent 869{\cal E})^{2}}\cr&\qquad\times \left[(1-2\mathaccent 869{\cal E})(8\mathaccent 869{\cal E}^{2}-8\mathaccent 869{ \cal E}-3)+{3\sin^{-1}\sqrt{\mathaccent 869{\cal E}}\over\sqrt{\mathaccent 869{ \cal E}(1-\mathaccent 869{\cal E})}}\right],\cr}\]where \(\widetilde{\cal E}\equiv-Ea/GM\). The full curve in Figure 4.2 shows this df.

An analogous calculation yields the df of the Jaffe model. We now have

\[{r\over a}={1\over{\rm e}^{\widetilde{\Psi}}-1}\quad{\rm so}\quad\nu={1\over 4 \pi a^{3}}{\rm e}^{-2\widetilde{\Psi}}({\rm e}^{\widetilde{\Psi}}-1)^{4}.\]

Differentiating this expression and then evaluating the integral of equation (4.46b) we find that the ergodic df of the Jaffe model is (Jaffe 1983)

\[\eqalign{f_{\rm J}({\cal E})={1\over 2\pi^{3}(GMa)^{3/2}}&\biggl{[}F_{-} \left(\sqrt{2\widetilde{\cal E}}\right)-\sqrt{2}F_{-}\left(\sqrt{\widetilde{ \cal E}}\right)\cr&-\sqrt{2}F_{+}\left(\sqrt{\widetilde{\cal E}}\right)+F_{+} \left(\sqrt{2\widetilde{\cal E}}\,\right)\biggr{]},\cr}\]

where \(F_{\pm}(z)\) is Dawson's integral (Appendix C.3).

The Jaffe df is shown by the dashed curve in Figure 4.2. At the smallest values of \({\cal E}\), \(f_{\rm J}\to{1\over 2}f_{\rm H}\) because at large radii each model potential tends to \(-GM/r\) and for given model mass \(M\), the density in the Jaffe model is half that in the Hernquist model. The dfs differ profoundly at large values

Figure 4.2: The ergodic dfs that generate stellar systems with the Hernquist (full curve) and Jaffe (dashed curve) density profiles. \(M\) is the model’s mass and \(a\) is its scale length. The dotted curve shows the ergodic df for the isochrone model, with \(a\) now denoting the scale length labeled \(b\) in equation (2.47). The long-dashed curve shows the df of the Plummer model (eq. 4.83) with \(a\) the structure radius labeled \(b\) in equation (4.91.)

of \({\cal E}\) because the Jaffe model has a divergent central potential, while the central potential of the Hernquist model is \(-GM/a\). On account of the deeper potential well of the Jaffe model, stars are distributed through a larger volume of phase space than they are in the Hernquist model, so the df is smaller at all energies.

From Eddington's formula one can determine the ergodic df of the isochrone model (SS2.2.2d and Henon 1960b)

\[\eqalign{f_{\rm I}({\cal E})&={1\over\sqrt{2}(2\pi)^{3}(GMb)^{3/2}}{\sqrt{{ \cal E}}\over[2(1-\widetilde{{\cal E}})]^{4}}\Biggl{[}27-66\widetilde{{\cal E} }+320\widetilde{{\cal E}}^{2}-240\widetilde{{\cal E}}^{3}\cr&\qquad\qquad+64 \widetilde{{\cal E}}^{4}+3(16\widetilde{{\cal E}}^{2}+28\widetilde{{\cal E}}-9 ){\sin^{-1}\sqrt{\widetilde{{\cal E}}}\over\sqrt{\widetilde{{\cal E}}(1- \widetilde{{\cal E}})}}\Biggr{]}.}\]

The dotted curve in Figure 4.2 shows this df. At small binding energies \(f_{\rm I}\to f_{\rm H}\) because both models have \(\nu\propto r^{-4}\) at large \(r\) (cf. eqs. 2.51 and 2.64). At the largest binding energy attainable in the isochrone model, \({1\over 2}GM/b\), the phase-space density is finite, while to generate the model's central cusp the Hernquist df has to diverge as \({\cal E}\) tends to the central potential.

**(b) Differential energy distribution** In statistical physics an important role is played by the **density of states**\(g\). In the limit of classical physics \(g(E)\) is the volume of phase space per unit energy. We now evaluate \(g\) under the assumption that the system is spherical, so the potential is a function \(\Phi(r)\) of \(r\) only. We have (eq. C.7)

\[\eqalign{g(E)&=\int{\rm d}^{3}{\bf x}\,{\rm d}^{3}{\bf v}\,\delta(H-E)\cr&=(4 \pi)^{2}\int_{0}^{r_{\rm m}(E)}{\rm d}r\,r^{2}\int{\rm d}v\,v^{2}\delta({ {1\over 2}}v^{2}+\Phi-E),}\]

where \(r_{\rm m}(E)\) is the radius at which \(\Phi=E\). To evaluate the inner integral, we change the integration variable to \(\xi\equiv{1\over 2}v^{2}\). Then

\[\eqalign{g(E)&=(4\pi)^{2}\int_{0}^{r_{\rm m}(E)}{\rm d}r\,r^{2}\int{\rm d}\xi \sqrt{2\xi}\,\delta(\xi+\Phi-E)\cr&=(4\pi)^{2}\int_{0}^{r_{\rm m}(E)}{\rm d}r \,r^{2}\sqrt{2(E-\Phi)}.}\]

Applying this formula to the Hernquist model, we find that

\[\eqalign{g_{\rm H}(E)&=(4\pi)^{2}a^{3}\sqrt{2|E|}\int_{1}^{A}{\rm d}X\,(X-1)^ {2}\left({A\over X}-1\right)^{1/2}\cr&=(4\pi)^{2}a^{3}\sqrt{2|E|}\Bigl{[}\sqrt {A-1}\left({{1\over 8}}A^{2}-{{5\over 12}}A-{{1\over 3}}\right)\cr&\qquad+{ {1\over 8}}A(A^{2}-4A+8)\cos^{-1}(A^{-1/2})\Bigr{]},}\]where

\[A\equiv{GM\over a|E|}. \tag{4.57b}\]

The left panel in Figure 4.3 shows that \(g_{\rm H}(E)\) increases rapidly with \(E\).

The **differential energy distribution**

\[N(E)\equiv g(E)f(E) \tag{4.58}\]

is such that the fraction of the system's stars that have energies in the range \((E+{\rm d}E,E)\) is \(N(E)\,{\rm d}E\). For most realistic stellar systems the increase with \(E\) in \(g\) overwhelms the decrease of \(f\), with the result that \(N(E)\) is an increasing function of \(E\). The right panel of Figure 4.3 illustrates this by showing the differential energy distributions of the Hernquist and \(R^{1/4}\) models (eq. 1.17 with \(m=4\)). In the case of the \(R^{1/4}\) model7\(N(E)\propto{\rm e}^{\beta E}\) is approximately valid for \(E/|\Phi(0)|\mathrel{\hbox to 0.0pt{\lower 4.0pt\hbox{ $\sim$}}\raise 1.0pt \hbox{$>$}}-0.8\). The rise in \(N(E)\) with \(E\) reflects the fact that there are a large number of stars in the low-density envelope of a galaxy, and these stars are crowded into a relatively small range in binding energy.

Footnote 7: In fact \(N(E)\propto{\rm e}^{\beta E}\) for all Sérsic models (Ciotti 1991).

#### DFs for anisotropic spherical systems

From Eddington's formula we can find an ergodic df\(f(H)\) that generates any given spherical density distribution \(\nu(r)\) in a given potential \(\Phi(r)\). However, there is no guarantee that this df will satisfy the physical requirement \(f\geq 0\). We now show that it _is_ always possible find a non-negative df if we consider dfs of the form \(f(H,L)\), by building the system using only circular

Figure 4.3: Left panel: the density of states \(g_{\rm H}(E)\) of the Hernquist model. Right panel: the differential energy distributions of the isotropic Hernquist model (full curve) and the \(R^{1/4}\) model (dashed curve).

orbits. By combining circular orbits of a given relative energy \({\cal E}^{\prime}\) with their angular-momentum vectors uniformly distributed over a sphere, we generate a spherical shell with the radius of circular orbits of energy \({\cal E}^{\prime}\). Any density profile \(\nu(r)\) can then be formed by adding such shells with a suitable radial weighting. We can express this idea mathematically by noting that the df of the spherical shell is proportional to the product of two delta functions, \(f_{\rm s}({\cal E},L)=\delta({\cal E}-{\cal E}^{\prime})\delta[L-L_{\rm c}({ \cal E}^{\prime})]\), where \(L_{\rm c}({\cal E}^{\prime})\) is the angular momentum of a circular orbit of relative energy \({\cal E}^{\prime}\). For a suitably chosen non-negative function \(F({\cal E}^{\prime})\) the integral

\[f_{\rm c}({\cal E},L)\equiv\int_{0}^{{\cal E}_{\rm max}}{\rm d}{\cal E}^{ \prime}f_{\rm s}({\cal E}^{\prime},L)F({\cal E}^{\prime})=F({\cal E})\delta[L- L_{\rm c}({\cal E})]\]

is a df that generates the required density distribution \(\nu(r)\).

The circular-orbit df\(f_{\rm c}\) is associated with vanishing radial dispersion \(\sigma_{r}\). If a non-negative ergodic df\(f_{\rm i}({\cal E})\) also exists, then these two dfs will be joined by a continuum of dfs of the form

\[f_{\alpha}\equiv\alpha f_{\rm i}+(1-\alpha)f_{\rm c}\quad(0\leq\alpha\leq 1).\]

As \(\alpha\) increases the orbits become steadily more eccentric and \(\sigma_{r}\) increases to equality with \(\sigma_{\theta}\). This sequence may even continue to dfs with \(\alpha>1\) for which \(\sigma_{r}>\sigma_{\theta}\), but such a continuation is not guaranteed: the more heavily we weight highly eccentric orbits, the more strongly \(\nu(r)\) is constrained by the requirement that \(f_{\alpha}\) be non-negative. Conversely, the circular-orbit df will still be non-negative even when \(\nu(r)\) is such that \(f_{\rm i}({\cal E})\) is somewhere negative, and it is likely that we can construct some non-negative dfs that have \(\sigma_{r}\neq 0\), even though an ergodic df is not allowed.

We define the **anisotropy parameter** to be

\[\beta\equiv 1-{\sigma_{\theta}^{2}+\sigma_{\phi}^{2}\over 2\sigma_{r}^{2}}=1-{ \overline{v_{\theta}^{2}+\overline{v_{\phi}^{2}}}\over 2v_{r}^{2}}.\]

This parameter quantifies the system's degree of radial anisotropy: if all orbits are circular, \(\sigma_{r}=0\) and \(\beta=-\infty\); if the df is ergodic, \(\beta=0\); if all orbits are perfectly radial, \(\sigma_{\theta}=\sigma_{\phi}=0\) and \(\beta=1\). dfs with \(\beta>0\) are said to be **radially biased**, while those with \(\beta<0\) are **tangentially biased**. The value of \(\beta\) is determined by the way in which \(f\) depends on total angular momentum \(L\).

To explore the effect of anisotropy on the structure of a stellar system, we shall find it useful to have available explicit expressions for dfs that all generate the same radial profile, but have different degrees of radial anisotropy.

**(a) Models with constant anisotropy** Models in which the anisotropy parameter takes some fixed non-zero value \(\beta\) at all radii can be generated by taking the df to be of the form (Problem 4.6)

\[f({\cal E},L)=L^{-2\beta}f_{1}({\cal E}),\]where \(f_{1}\) is an arbitrary non-negative function. In terms of the polar coordinates for velocity space,

\[v_{r}=v\cos\eta\quad;\quad v_{\theta}=v\sin\eta\cos\psi\quad;\quad v_{\phi}=v\sin \eta\sin\psi, \tag{4.63}\]

we may write

\[\nu(r)=\int\mathrm{d}^{3}{\bf v}\,f({\cal E},L)=2\pi\int_{0}^{\pi}\mathrm{d} \eta\,\sin\eta\int_{0}^{\sqrt{2\Psi}}\mathrm{d}v\,v^{2}f\left(\Psi-{\frac{1}{2}}v^ {2},rv\sin\eta\right). \tag{4.64}\]

For a df of the form (4.62) this expression may be rewritten

\[\nu(r)=\frac{2\pi I_{\beta}}{r^{2\beta}}\int_{0}^{\infty}\mathrm{d}v\,v^{2-2 \beta}f_{1}[\Psi(r)-{\frac{1}{2}}v^{2}],\] (4.65a) where \[I_{\beta}\equiv\int_{0}^{\pi}\mathrm{d}\eta\,\sin^{1-2\beta}\eta=\sqrt{\pi} \frac{(-\beta)!}{({\frac{1}{2}}-\beta)!}\quad(\beta<1). \tag{4.65b}\]

The integral in (4.65a) is similar to that occurring in equation (4.43), so after multiplying through by \(r^{2\beta}\) we obtain an equation that is closely analogous to equation (4.44):

\[\frac{2^{\beta-1/2}}{2\pi I_{\beta}}\,r^{2\beta}\nu=\int_{0}^{\Psi}\mathrm{d} {\cal E}\,\frac{f_{1}({\cal E})}{(\Psi-{\cal E})^{\beta-1/2}}\quad(\beta<1). \tag{4.66}\]

When the left side is considered to be a function of \(\Psi\) rather than \(r\), this becomes an Abel integral equation (B.74a) so long as \({\frac{1}{2}}<\beta<{\frac{3}{2}}\). Moreover, when \(\beta\leq{\frac{1}{2}}\), the equation can be reduced to an Abel equation by differentiating both sides one or more times with respect to \(\Psi\). For example,

\[\frac{2^{\beta-1/2}}{2\pi I_{\beta}}\frac{\mathrm{d}}{\mathrm{d}\Psi}(r^{2 \beta}\nu)=({\frac{1}{2}}-\beta)\int_{0}^{\Psi}\mathrm{d}{\cal E}\, \frac{f_{1}({\cal E})}{(\Psi-{\cal E})^{\beta+1/2}}, \tag{4.67}\]

which is an Abel equation for \(-{\frac{1}{2}}<\beta<{\frac{1}{2}}\). Hence we can analytically solve for \(f_{1}({\cal E})\) whenever we can express \(r^{2\beta}\nu\) as a function of \(\Psi\).

Equation (4.66) is exceptionally simple when \(\beta={\frac{1}{2}}\), corresponding to \(\sigma_{\theta}^{2}=\sigma_{\phi}^{2}={\frac{1}{2}}\sigma_{r}^{2}\). In this case the denominator of the integrand becomes a constant. Differentiating both sides with respect to \(\Psi\) we find

\[f_{1}(\Psi)=\frac{1}{2\pi^{2}}\frac{\mathrm{d}}{\mathrm{d}\Psi}(r\nu)\qquad( \beta={\frac{1}{2}}). \tag{4.68}\]

In the case of the Hernquist model, from equations (4.47) and (4.48) we find that

\[f_{1}({\cal E})=\frac{3\widetilde{{\cal E}}^{2}}{4\pi^{3}GMa},\quad\widetilde{ {\cal E}}=\frac{{\cal E}a}{GM}. \tag{4.69}\]A contrasting case of almost equal simplicity is \(\beta=-\frac{1}{2}\), corresponding to \(\sigma_{\theta}^{2}=\sigma_{\phi}^{2}=\frac{3}{2}\sigma_{r}^{2}\). Then equation (4.66) becomes

\[\frac{1}{2\pi^{2}}\,\frac{\nu}{r}=\int_{0}^{\Psi}\mathrm{d}\mathcal{E}\,f_{1}( \mathcal{E})(\Psi-\mathcal{E}). \tag{4.70}\]

Differentiating through twice with respect to \(\Psi\) we have

\[f_{1}(\Psi)=\frac{1}{2\pi^{2}}\,\frac{\mathrm{d}^{2}(\nu/r)}{\mathrm{d}\Psi^{2 }}\qquad(\beta=-\tfrac{1}{2}). \tag{4.71}\]

In the case of the Hernquist model, this yields

\[f_{1}(\mathcal{E})=\frac{1}{4\pi^{3}(GMa)^{2}}\frac{\mathrm{d}^{2}}{\mathrm{d }\widetilde{\mathcal{E}}^{2}}\left(\frac{\widetilde{\mathcal{E}}^{5}}{(1- \widetilde{\mathcal{E}})^{2}}\right), \tag{4.72}\]

which one may easily show is non-negative for \(\widetilde{\mathcal{E}}\leq 1\).

Figure 4.4 shows the line-of-sight velocity dispersion \(\sigma_{\parallel}\) of a Hernquist model as a function of projected radius when the df is (i) ergodic (eq. 4.50) labeled "0"; (ii) radially biased (eqs. 4.62 and 4.69) labeled \(\frac{1}{2}\), and (iii) tangentially biased (eqs. 4.62 and 4.72) labeled \(-\frac{1}{2}\). In the radially biased system, the central value of \(\sigma_{\parallel}\) is nearly twice that in the isotropic system, and more than twice that in the tangentially biased system. Conversely, at

Figure 4.4: Line-of-sight velocity dispersion as a function of projected radius, from spatially identical systems that have different df. In each system the density and potential are those of the Hernquist model and the anisotropy parameter \(\beta\) of equation (4.61) is independent of radius. The curves are labeled by the relevant value of \(\beta\). In the isotropic system, the velocity dispersion falls as one approaches the center (cf. Problem 4.14).

large radii the tangentially biased system has the largest value of \(\sigma_{\parallel}\), and the radially biased system the smallest.

It is easy to understand why radial bias increases line-of-sight velocity dispersion at small radii while tangential bias increases it at large: along the line of sight through the center, only \(v_{r}\) contributes to \(\sigma_{\parallel}\); conversely, in the outer envelope, where the galaxy's density profile is steeply falling, the dominant contribution to the dispersion comes from where the line of sight makes its closest approach to the center of the galaxy. At this point of closest approach, only the tangential velocity contributes to \(\sigma_{\parallel}\), so a bias towards tangential velocities increases \(\sigma_{\parallel}\). The tendency of radial bias to make \(\sigma_{\parallel}\) a steeper function of radius is general, and constitutes one of the most vexing sources of uncertainty in attempts to measure the mass distributions of stellar systems (SS4.9).

**(b) Osipkov-Merritt models** Models of galaxy formation generally imply that \(\beta\) increases with radius, corresponding to a nearly ergodic df near the center and a radially biased df in the outer envelope (SS4.10.3). Simple models in which \(\beta\) increases with radius can be constructed as follows (Osipkov 1979; Merritt 1985). We assume that \(f\) depends on \({\cal E}\) and \(L\) only through the variable

\[Q\equiv{\cal E}-{L^{2}\over 2r_{\rm a}^{2}},\]

where \(r_{\rm a}\) is a constant, called the **anisotropy radius**. In terms of the polar coordinates defined by equations (4.63), the definition of \(Q\) becomes

\[Q=\Psi-{{1\over 2}}v^{2}\left(1+{r^{2}\over r_{\rm a}^{2}}\sin^{2} \eta\right).\]

We substitute the df\(f({\cal E},L)=f(Q)\) into equation (4.64) and replace the integration variable \(v\) with \(Q\). At constant \(r\) and \(\eta\), \({\rm d}Q=-\left[1+(r/r_{\rm a})^{2}\sin^{2}\eta\right]v\,{\rm d}v\), and thus

\[\nu(r)=2\pi\int_{0}^{\pi}{\rm d}\eta\,\sin\eta\int_{0}^{\Psi}{\rm d}Q\,f(Q){ \sqrt{2(\Psi-Q)}\over\left[1+(r/r_{\rm a})^{2}\sin^{2}\eta\right]^{3/2}},\]

where we have imposed the condition \(f(Q)=0\) for \(Q\leq 0\). We interchange the order of integrations in equation (4.75), and the inner integral becomes

\[\int_{0}^{\pi}{\rm d}\eta\,{\sin\eta\over\left[1+(r/r_{\rm a})^{2}\sin^{2}\eta \right]^{3/2}}={2\over 1+(r/r_{\rm a})^{2}}.\]

Hence

\[\left(1+{r^{2}\over r_{\rm a}^{2}}\right)\nu(r)=4\pi\int_{0}^{\Psi}{\rm d}Q\, f(Q)\sqrt{2(\Psi-Q)}.\]But the right side of this equation is identical with the right side of equation (4.43), with \({\cal E}\) replaced by \(Q\). Hence by equation (4.46b) we have

\[f(Q)={1\over\sqrt{8}\pi^{2}}\bigg{[}\int_{0}^{Q}{{\rm d}\Psi\over\sqrt{Q-\Psi}}{{ \rm d}^{2}\nu_{Q}\over{\rm d}\Psi^{2}}+{1\over\sqrt{Q}}\left({{\rm d}\nu_{Q} \over{\rm d}\Psi}\right)_{\Psi=0}\bigg{]},\]

where

\[\nu_{Q}(r)\equiv\left(1+{r^{2}\over r_{\rm a}^{2}}\right)\nu(r).\]

A model for which the df has the form \(f(Q)\), where \(Q\) is given by equation (4.73), is called an **Osipkov-Merritt model**.

The anisotropy parameter of any model with a df of the form \(f(Q)\) is (Problem 4.13)

\[\beta(r)={1\over 1+r_{\rm a}^{2}/r^{2}}.\]

This function rises from zero at \(r\ll r_{\rm a}\) (ergodic df) to unity at \(r\gg r_{\rm a}\) (radial df), and already exceeds 0.9 for \(r>3r_{\rm a}\).

**(c) Other anisotropic models** The degree of anisotropy in the models described above is controlled by a single parameter, either \(\beta\) or \(r_{\rm a}\). We can obtain more flexible models by considering a df of the form

\[f({\cal E},L)=G({\cal E})h(x),\quad{\rm where}\quad x\equiv{L\over L_{0}+L_{ \rm c}({\cal E})}\]

with \(L_{0}\) a free parameter and \(L_{\rm c}({\cal E})\) is the angular momentum of the circular orbit with energy \({\cal E}\). The variable \(x\) increases from zero for radial orbits to \(L_{\rm c}/(L_{0}+L_{\rm c})\) for circular orbits, and the model will be tangentially biased if \(h\) is an increasing function of \(x\), and radially biased if \(h\) is a decreasing function of \(x\). For any chosen function \(h(x)\), one can numerically determine \(G({\cal E})\) such that the model has a given radial density profile (Gerhard 1991). Hence this approach makes it possible to construct models that have the same density profile but a wide variety of functional forms of \(\beta(r)\).

**(d) Differential-energy distribution for anisotropic systems** It proves useful to calculate the differential energy distribution of a model with df\(f(H,L)\). With \(v_{\rm t}=L/r\) the tangential speed, we have (cf. eq. 4.55)

\[\eqalign{N(E)&=\int{\rm d}^{3}{\bf x}{\rm d}^{3}{\bf v}\,\delta(E-H)f(H,L)\cr& =8\pi^{2}\int{\rm d}r\,r^{2}\int{\rm d}v_{r}{\rm d}v_{\rm t}\,v_{\rm t}\,\delta( E-H)f(H,L)\cr&=8\pi^{2}\int{\rm d}r\int{\rm d}v_{r}{\rm d}L\,L\,\delta(E-H)f(H,L)\cr&=(4 \pi)^{2}\int{\rm d}r\int{\rm d}L\,{Lf(E,L)\over\sqrt{2(E-\Phi)-L^{2}/r^{2}}},}\]

where the last equality uses \(v_{r}^{2}=2(E-\Phi)-L^{2}/r^{2}\) to eliminate \(v_{r}\) in favor of \(E\); a factor of two arises because \(v_{r}\) can be positive or negative. The integral is taken over the region in \((r,L)\) space in which the argument of the square root is non-negative. Figure 4.5 shows \(N(E)\) for the Hernquist models with constant anisotropy parameters \(\beta=-{1\over 2}\) (dashed curve), \(\beta=0\) (full curve) and \(\beta={1\over 2}\) (dotted curve). Even though the dfs of these models are very different, they yield extremely similar differential energy distributions. The only significant difference is at large binding energy, and in the sense that the radially biased model (\(\beta={1\over 2}\)) has fewer very tightly bound stars than does the tangentially biased model. This result arises because stars that are on eccentric orbits contribute to the density at radii that are much smaller than the radius of the circular orbit that has the same energy. This difference may significantly affect the vulnerability of the central regions to disruption by external perturbers. Thus we expect that galaxies with radially biased dfs are more fragile than galaxies with ergodic dfs.

**4.3.3 Spherical systems defined by the DF** In the previous subsection we found dfs that generated a given density profile. Not surprisingly, the resulting system had a simple functional form for the density in real space, and usually a much more complex expression for the density in phase space (cf. eq. 4.54). In this section we proceed in the reverse order: we choose the functional form of the density in phase space and then investigate what the system looks like in real space.

The Jeans theorem and the system's spherical symmetry allow us to assume that \(f\) is a function of the relative energy \({\cal E}=\Psi-{1\over 2}v^{2}\) and the magnitude of the angular momentum, and to write Poisson's equation in the form

\[\frac{1}{r^{2}}\frac{\mathrm{d}}{\mathrm{d}r}\bigg{(}r^{2}\frac{\mathrm{d}\Psi}{ \mathrm{d}r}\bigg{)}=-4\pi G\rho=-4\pi GM\int\mathrm{d}^{3}\mathbf{v}\,f\left( \Psi-\tfrac{1}{2}v^{2},|\mathbf{r}\times\mathbf{v}|\right), \tag{4.82}\]

where \(M\) is the system's total mass. For any given function \(f(\mathcal{E},L)\), this is an integro-differential equation for \(\Psi(r)\). Since we have to solve for the potential after we have chosen \(f\), we cannot normalize \(f\) to have unit integral over all phase space as we have done hitherto, until after we have solved equation (4.82) for \(\Psi(r)\). For this reason it is convenient to redefine our normalization so that the integral of \(f\) over phase space is the total mass; thus, in this section we shall assume that the mass density \(\rho\) is given by \(\rho=\int\mathrm{d}^{3}\mathbf{v}\,f\).

**(a) Polytropes and the Plummer model** A simple form for the df is

\[f(\mathcal{E})=\left\{\begin{array}{ll}F\mathcal{E}^{n-3/2}&(\mathcal{E}>0 )\\ 0&(\mathcal{E}\leq 0).\end{array}\right. \tag{4.83}\]

With this form of \(f\) we have for the density \(\rho\) at radii where \(\Psi>0\)

\[\rho=4\pi\int_{0}^{\infty}\mathrm{d}v\,v^{2}f(\Psi-\tfrac{1}{2}v^{2})=4\pi F \int_{0}^{\sqrt{2\Psi}}\mathrm{d}v\,v^{2}\left(\Psi-\tfrac{1}{2}v^{2}\right)^ {n-3/2}. \tag{4.84}\]

If we make the substitution \(v^{2}=2\Psi\cos^{2}\theta\), this becomes

\[\rho=c_{n}\Psi^{n}\qquad(\Psi>0),\] (4.85a) where \[c_{n}\equiv 2^{7/2}\pi F\bigg{[}\int_{0}^{\pi/2}\mathrm{d}\theta\,\sin^{2n-2} \theta-\int_{0}^{\pi/2}\mathrm{d}\theta\,\sin^{2n}\theta\bigg{]}=\frac{(2\pi )^{3/2}(n-\tfrac{3}{2})!F}{n!}. \tag{4.85b}\]

If \(c_{n}\) is to be finite, we must have \(n>\tfrac{1}{2}\).

In these models the density rises as the \(n\)th power of the relative potential when \(\Psi>0\) and is, of course, zero when \(\Psi\leq 0\). No finite ergodic stellar system is homogeneous, for this would correspond to \(\rho\propto\Psi^{0}\) or \(n=0\), which would violate the constraint \(n>\tfrac{1}{2}\)--in other words there is no stellar-dynamical analog of a self-gravitating sphere of incompressible liquid.

When we use equation (4.85a) to eliminate \(\rho\) from Poisson's equation, we find

\[\frac{1}{r^{2}}\frac{\mathrm{d}}{\mathrm{d}r}\bigg{(}r^{2}\frac{\mathrm{d} \Psi}{\mathrm{d}r}\bigg{)}+4\pi Gc_{n}\Psi^{n}=0. \tag{4.86}\]

Polytropic gases have an equation of state \(p=K\rho^{\gamma}\), where \(K\) is a constant and \(\gamma\) is the ratio of principal specific heats (cf. eq. F.46). Thus the equation of hydrostatic equilibrium for a self-gravitating sphere of polytropic gas (eq. F.12),

\[\frac{\mathrm{d}p}{\mathrm{d}r}=-\rho\frac{\mathrm{d}\Phi}{\mathrm{d}r}, \tag{4.87a}\]becomes

\[K\gamma\rho^{\gamma-2}\,\frac{{\rm d}\rho}{{\rm d}r}=\frac{{\rm d}\Psi}{{\rm d}r}. \tag{4.87b}\]

If we set the constant involved in the definition of \(\Psi\) such that \(\Psi=0\) on the edge of the system, equation (4.87b) yields on integration

\[\rho^{\gamma-1}=\frac{\gamma-1}{K\gamma}\Psi. \tag{4.88}\]

Equation (4.88) is the same as equation (4.85a) with

\[c_{n}=\left(\frac{\gamma-1}{K\gamma}\right)^{1/(\gamma-1)}\quad\mbox{and} \quad\gamma=1+\frac{1}{n}. \tag{4.89}\]

Hence _the density distribution of an ergodic stellar system with df_ (4.83) _is the same as that of a polytropic gas sphere with \(\gamma=1+1/n\)_. For this reason stellar systems with dfs given by (4.83) are known as polytropes. Note that polytropic gas spheres with \(\gamma>3\) require \(n<\frac{1}{2}\), so these spheres have no stellar-dynamical analogs. A full account of gaseous polytropes can be found in Chandrasekhar (1939) and Horedt (2004).

The simplest solutions of equation (4.86) are obtained by assuming that the density varies as a power of radius, \(\rho\propto r^{-\alpha}\). Since \(\rho\propto\Psi^{n}\) in a polytrope, we have \(\Psi\propto r^{-\alpha/n}\). Inserting this ansatz into both sides of equation (4.86), and requiring the same power of \(r\) to occur on each side, we find that \(\alpha=2n/(n-1)\). Since the potential cannot decrease with radius faster than in the Keplerian case \(\Psi\propto r^{-1}\), we have \(\alpha/n\leq 1\), so solutions of this type are feasible only for \(n\geq 3\). The mass contained within radius \(r\) is

\[M(r)=-\frac{r^{2}}{G}\frac{{\rm d}\Psi}{{\rm d}r}\propto r^{1-\alpha/n}=r^{(n- 3)/(n-1)}, \tag{4.90}\]

which is independent of radius in the case \(n=3\) but otherwise tends to zero as \(r\to 0\). Hence in the case \(n=3\) our power-law solution represents a massless halo orbiting in the potential of a point mass, but for \(n>3\) our solution is a self-gravitating system. In the limit \(n\to\infty\) the potential becomes proportional to \(\ln r\); we shall encounter this model below as the "singular isothermal sphere." Problem 4.14 gives the velocity dispersion in power-law models.

To obtain models in which the central potential and density are finite, we eliminate \(r\) and \(\Psi\) from equation (4.86) in favor of the rescaled radial variables,

\[s\equiv\frac{r}{b}\quad\mbox{and}\quad\psi\equiv\frac{\Psi}{\Psi_{0}},\quad \mbox{where}\quad b\equiv(\frac{4}{3}\pi G\Psi_{0}^{n-1}c_{n})^{-1/2} \tag{4.91a}\]is the scale radius and \(\Psi_{0}=\Psi(0)\). Now equation (4.86) takes the simple form

\[\frac{1}{s^{2}}\frac{\mathrm{d}}{\mathrm{d}s}\left(s^{2}\frac{\mathrm{d}\psi}{ \mathrm{d}s}\right)=\left\{\begin{array}{ll}-3\psi^{n}&\psi>0;\\ 0&\psi\leq 0.\end{array}\right. \tag{4.91b}\]

This equation is known as the **Lane-Emden equation** after H. Lane and R. Emden who studied it in connection with polytropic gas spheres. The natural boundary conditions to impose on it are: (i) \(\psi(0)=1\) by definition; (ii) \(\mathrm{d}\psi/\mathrm{d}s\big{|}_{0}=0\), since in the absence of a central singularity in the density, the gravitational force must vanish at the center.

For general \(n\), (4.91b) cannot be solved in terms of elementary functions. However, there are two special cases for which simple analytical solutions are available. (i) When \(n=1\), equation (4.91b) becomes the linear Helmholtz equation familiar from the theory of spherical waves (see Problems 4.15 and 4.16); and (ii) when \(n=5\), we obtain a model discovered by Schuster (1883) that is worth describing in some detail because it provides the simplest plausible model of a self-consistent stellar system.

Consider the function

\[\psi=\frac{1}{\sqrt{1+s^{2}}}. \tag{4.92}\]

Differentiating with respect to \(s\) we find that

\[\frac{1}{s^{2}}\frac{\mathrm{d}}{\mathrm{d}s}\left(s^{2}\frac{\mathrm{d}\psi}{ \mathrm{d}s}\right)=-\frac{1}{s^{2}}\frac{\mathrm{d}}{\mathrm{d}s}\left(\frac {s^{3}}{(1+s^{2})^{3/2}}\right)=-\frac{3}{(1+s^{2})^{5/2}}=-3\psi^{5}. \tag{4.93}\]

Therefore \(\psi\) is a solution of equation (4.91b) with \(n=5\). Since \(\psi\) also satisfies the central boundary conditions, it represents a physically acceptable potential. In fact, it is a dimensionless form of equation (2.44a), the potential of the Plummer model introduced in SS2.2.2c. That is, _the \(n=5\) polytrope is a Plummer model_. The density of this model, given by equation (2.44b), is everywhere non-zero, declining as \(r^{-5}\) for \(r\gg b\). The total mass is finite, however, with value

\[M=\frac{1}{G}\left(r^{2}\frac{\mathrm{d}\Phi}{\mathrm{d}r}\right)_{r\to \infty}=-\frac{b}{G}\left(s^{2}\frac{\mathrm{d}\Psi}{\mathrm{d}s}\right)_{s \to\infty}=\frac{b\Psi_{0}}{G}. \tag{4.94}\]

In general, the extent of the outer parts of a polytropic model increases with \(n\); for \(n<5\) the density goes to zero at a finite radius, for \(n=5\) the density is non-zero everywhere but the total mass is finite, and for \(n>5\) the density falls off so slowly at large \(r\) that the mass is infinite.

**(b) The isothermal sphere** We have just seen that to every polytropic gas sphere with \(\gamma<3\), there corresponds a stellar-dynamical polytrope with index \(n=1/(\gamma-1)>\frac{1}{2}\). Thus stellar polytropes with large \(n\) correspond to gaseous polytropes for which \(\gamma\simeq 1\). Hence in the limit \(n\to\infty\), the corresponding gaseous system has \(\gamma=1\), which implies that \(p=K\rho\). Thisis the equation of state of an isothermal gas. The equation governing the structure of a self-gravitating isothermal sphere of ideal gas can be derived by taking a suitable limit of the Lane-Emden equation as \(n\to\infty\) (Hunter 2001), but a more illuminating derivation starts with the equation of hydrostatic equilibrium, which reads

\[\frac{\mathrm{d}p}{\mathrm{d}r}=\frac{k_{\mathrm{B}}T}{m}\frac{\mathrm{d}\rho} {\mathrm{d}r}=-\rho\frac{\mathrm{d}\Phi}{\mathrm{d}r}=-\rho\frac{GM(r)}{r^{2}},\] (4.95a) where \[k_{\mathrm{B}}\] is Boltzmann's constant, \[p\] and \[T\] are the pressure and temperature of the gas, \[m\] is the mass per particle, and \[M(r)\] is the total mass interior to radius \[r\] (eqs. 12 & 13 ). Multiplying equation ( 4.95a ) through by \[(r^{2}m/\rho k_{\mathrm{B}}T)\] and then differentiating with respect to \[r\], we obtain \[\frac{\mathrm{d}}{\mathrm{d}r}\biggl{(}r^{2}\frac{\mathrm{d}\ln\rho}{\mathrm{d }r}\biggr{)}=-\frac{4\pi Gm}{k_{\mathrm{B}}T}r^{2}\rho,\] (4.95b) where we have used the relationship \[\mathrm{d}M/\mathrm{d}r=4\pi r^{2}\rho.\] Now suppose we have a stellar-dynamical system whose df is \[f(\mathcal{E})=\frac{\rho_{1}}{(2\pi\sigma^{2})^{3/2}}\,\mathrm{e}^{\mathcal{E }/\sigma^{2}}=\frac{\rho_{1}}{(2\pi\sigma^{2})^{3/2}}\exp\bigg{(}\frac{\Psi- \frac{1}{2}v^{2}}{\sigma^{2}}\bigg{)}.\] (4.96) Then, integrating over all velocities, we find \[\rho=\rho_{1}\mathrm{e}^{\Psi/\sigma^{2}}.\] (4.97) Poisson's equation for this system reads \[\frac{1}{r^{2}}\frac{\mathrm{d}}{\mathrm{d}r}\biggl{(}r^{2}\frac{\mathrm{d}\Psi }{\mathrm{d}r}\biggr{)}=-4\pi G\rho,\] (4.98) or, with equation ( 4.97 ) \[\frac{\mathrm{d}}{\mathrm{d}r}\biggl{(}r^{2}\frac{\mathrm{d}\ln\rho}{\mathrm{d }r}\biggr{)}=-\frac{4\pi G}{\sigma^{2}}r^{2}\rho.\] (4.99a) For future reference, note that if we eliminate \[\rho\] rather than \[\Psi\] between equations ( 4.97 ) and ( 4.98 ), we obtain \[\frac{\mathrm{d}}{\mathrm{d}r}\biggl{(}r^{2}\frac{\mathrm{d}\Psi}{\mathrm{d}r} \biggr{)}+4\pi G\rho_{1}r^{2}\mathrm{e}^{\Psi/\sigma^{2}}=0,\] (4.99b) which is the analog of the governing equation of polytropes, ( 4.86 ).

Equations (4.95b) and (4.99a) are identical if we set

\[\sigma^{2}=\frac{k_{\rm B}T}{m}. \tag{4.100}\]

Therefore _the structure of an isothermal self-gravitating sphere of gas is identical with the structure of a collisionless system of stars whose df is given by equation (4.96)._

A little thought shows why there is this correspondence between the gaseous and stellar-dynamical isothermal spheres. The distribution of velocities at each point in the stellar isothermal sphere is the **Maxwellian** or **Maxwell-Boltzmann distribution**

\[{\rm d}n\propto\exp\Big{(}-\frac{|{\bf v}|^{2}}{2\sigma^{2}}\Big{)}\,{\rm d}^{ 3}{\bf v}. \tag{4.101}\]

However, kinetic theory (e.g., Pathria 1972) tells us that this is also the equilibrium Maxwell-Boltzmann distribution which would obtain if the stars were allowed to bounce elastically off each other like the molecules of a gas. Therefore, if the df of a system is given by equation (4.96), it is a matter of indifference whether the particles of the system collide with one another or not.

Notice that the correspondence between a gaseous polytrope with \(\gamma>1\) and the corresponding stellar-dynamical model is not as close as that between the two isothermal systems; the gas molecules always have a Maxwellian distribution (with temperature depending on radius), while the stellar velocity distribution is given by (4.83). Thus a stellar polytrope would be drastically altered if elastic collisions were allowed to occur between its stars.

The mean-square speed of the stars at a point in the isothermal sphere is

\[\overline{v^{2}}=\frac{\int_{0}^{\infty}{\rm d}v\,v^{4}\exp\left(\frac{\Psi- \frac{1}{2}v^{2}}{\sigma^{2}}\right)}{\int_{0}^{\infty}{\rm d}v\,v^{2}\exp \left(\frac{\Psi-\frac{1}{2}v^{2}}{\sigma^{2}}\right)}=2\sigma^{2}\frac{\int_{ 0}^{\infty}{\rm d}x\,x^{4}{\rm e}^{-x^{2}}}{\int_{0}^{\infty}{\rm d}x\,x^{2}{ \rm e}^{-x^{2}}}=3\sigma^{2}. \tag{4.102}\]

Thus \(\overline{v^{2}}\) is independent of position. The dispersion in any one component of velocity, for example \((\overline{v_{r}^{2}})^{1/2}\), is equal to \(\sigma\).

It is easy to find one solution of equation (4.99a). If we set \(\rho=Cr^{-b}\), the left side of the equation is found to equal \(-b\), while the right side equals \(-(4\pi G/\sigma^{2})Cr^{2-b}\). Therefore, we must set \(b=2\) and \(C=\sigma^{2}/(2\pi G)\), which yields

\[\rho(r)=\frac{\sigma^{2}}{2\pi Gr^{2}}. \tag{4.103}\]This solution describes the **singular isothermal sphere**. The mass interior to radius \(r\), the circular speed and the gravitational potential are (eqs. 2.60, 2.61, and 2.62)

\[M(r)=\frac{2\sigma^{2}r}{G}\quad;\quad v_{\rm c}(r)=\sqrt{2}\sigma\quad;\quad \Phi(r)=2\sigma^{2}\ln(r)+{\rm constant}, \tag{4.104}\]

and the surface density is (eq. 2.59)

\[\Sigma(R)=\frac{\sigma^{2}}{2GR}. \tag{4.105}\]

The singular isothermal sphere has infinite density at \(r=0\). To obtain a solution of equations (4.99) that is well behaved at the origin, it is convenient to define new dimensionless variables \(\widetilde{\rho}\) and \(\widetilde{r}\) to replace \(\rho\) and \(r\); we define these in terms of the central density \(\rho_{0}\) and the **King radius**\(r_{0}\) by

\[\widetilde{\rho}\equiv\frac{\rho}{\rho_{0}}\quad{\rm and}\quad\widetilde{r} \equiv\frac{r}{r_{0}},\quad{\rm where}\quad r_{0}\equiv\sqrt{\frac{9\sigma^{2 }}{4\pi G\rho_{0}}}. \tag{4.106}\]

We shall find that \(r_{0}\) is the radius at which the projected density of the isothermal sphere falls to roughly half (in fact, 0.5013) of its central value, and because of this some authors call \(r_{0}\) the core radius in analogy with the usual observational definition (page 30 and BM p. 366). In terms of our new variables, equations (4.99) become

\[\frac{{\rm d}}{{\rm d}\widetilde{r}}\left(\widetilde{r}^{2}\frac{{\rm d}\ln \widetilde{\rho}}{{\rm d}\widetilde{r}}\right)=-9\widetilde{r}^{2}\widetilde{\rho}\] (4.107a) or \[\frac{{\rm d}}{{\rm d}\widetilde{r}}\left[\widetilde{r}^{2}\frac{{\rm d}(\Psi/ \sigma^{2})}{{\rm d}\widetilde{r}}\right]+9\widetilde{r}^{2}\exp\left[\frac{ \Psi(r)-\Psi(0)}{\sigma^{2}}\right]=0. \tag{4.107b}\]

In Figure 4.6 we show the function \(\widetilde{\rho}(\widetilde{r})\) obtained by numerically integrating equation (4.107a) from \(\widetilde{r}=0\) outward, starting from the boundary conditions \(\widetilde{\rho}(0)=1\) and \({\rm d}\widetilde{\rho}/{\rm d}\widetilde{r}=0\). Notice that by about \(\widetilde{r}=15\), \(\widetilde{\rho}(\widetilde{r})\) is declining as a straight line in the log-log plot of Figure 4.6; in fact, the solution is approaching8 the singular isothermal sphere of equation (4.104), which in these variables has the form \(\widetilde{\rho}=\frac{2}{9}\widetilde{r}^{-2}\). This is shown as a dotted line in the figure.

Footnote 8: The asymptotic behavior of the isothermal sphere as \(r\to\infty\) is described more accurately in Problem 7.6.

In Figure 4.6 we also plot the surface density \(\Sigma(R)\) of the isothermal sphere in units of \(\rho_{0}r_{0}\). For \(R\gg r_{0}\) the surface density asymptotes to that of the singular isothermal sphere (eq. 4.105).

If \(M(r)\) is the mass interior to \(r\), the circular speed at \(r\) is given by

\[v_{\rm c}^{2}(r)=\frac{GM(r)}{r}.\] (4.108a) On integrating equation ( 4.99a ), we find that \[v_{\rm c}^{2}=-\sigma^{2}\frac{{\rm d}\ln\rho}{{\rm d}\ln r}.\] (4.108b) In Figure 4.7 we plot \[{\rm d}\ln\rho/{\rm d}\ln r\] for the isothermal sphere, which for \[r\gg r_{0}\] tends to \[-2\]. Thus the circular speed at large \[r\] is constant at \[v_{\rm c}=\sqrt{2}\sigma\], the value for the singular isothermal sphere (eq. 4.104 ). At \[\widetilde{r}\lesssim 2\] ( \[r\lesssim 2r_{0}\] ) a useful approximation to \[\widetilde{\rho}(\widetilde{r})\] is the modified Hubble model introduced in SS2.2.2e, \[\widetilde{\rho}(\widetilde{r})\approx\widetilde{\rho}_{\rm h}(\widetilde{r}) \equiv\frac{1}{(1+\widetilde{r}^{2})^{3/2}}.\] (4.109a) The error in using this equation as an approximation to the isothermal sphere is less than \[7\%\] for \[\widetilde{r}<4\]. The surface density to which \[\widetilde{\rho}_{\rm h}\] gives rise is (eq. 2.55 ) \[\Sigma_{\rm h}(\widetilde{R})=\frac{2}{1+\widetilde{R}^{2}}, \tag{4.109b}\]

Figure 4.6: Volume (\(\rho/\rho_{0}\)) and projected (\(\Sigma/\rho_{0}r_{0}\)) mass densities of the isothermal sphere. The dotted lines show the volume- and surface-density profiles of the singular isothermal sphere. The dashed curve shows the surface density of the modified Hubble model (4.109a).

where \(\widetilde{R}\equiv R/r_{0}\).

The density distribution \(\widetilde{\rho}_{\rm h}\) does not fit the isothermal profile well at \(\widetilde{r}\gtrsim 3\) because it settles asymptotically to a logarithmic slope of \(-3\) rather than \(-2\) as is required by the isothermal profile. On the other hand, at large radii, \(\widetilde{\rho}_{\rm h}\) has another use: when \(\widetilde{r}\gg 1\) the projected density (4.109b) to which it gives rise is very similar to the Hubble-Reynolds law (eq. 2.52), which fits the surface-brightness profiles of many elliptical galaxies rather well. Thus \(\widetilde{\rho}_{\rm h}\) provides a simple analytical approximation to the inner parts of an isothermal sphere, or to the outer parts of a galaxy that obeys the Hubble-Reynolds law. It does _not_ fit the outer parts of an isothermal sphere or the inner parts of the Hubble-Reynolds law. This dual application of \(\widetilde{\rho}_{\rm h}\) has produced a certain amount of confusion in the literature.

From the astrophysical point of view, the isothermal sphere has a serious defect: its mass is infinite. Thus from equation (4.104), we have that \(M\propto r\) at large \(r\). No real astrophysical system can be modeled over more than a limited range of radii with a divergent mass distribution. On the other hand, the circular-speed curves of spiral galaxies (BM SS8.2.4) _are_ often remarkably flat out to great radii, and the divergent mass of the isothermal sphere is a useful reminder that we have little direct knowledge of the mass distribution in the outer parts of galaxies.

**(c) Lowered isothermal models** We seek a model that resembles the isothermal sphere at small radii, where the majority of stars have large values of the relative energy \({\cal E}\), but is less dense than the isothermal sphere at large radii, so its total mass is finite. We may obtain the df\(f_{\rm K}\) of such a model by simply diminishing the df of the isothermal sphere at small values of \({\cal E}\). Thus we modify the df (4.96) of the isothermal sphere in such a way that \(f_{\rm K}=0\) for \({\cal E}\leq{\cal E}_{0}\). We may exploit the arbitrary constant \(\Phi_{0}\) in the definition (4.41) of \({\cal E}\) to set the critical relative energy \({\cal E}_{0}=0\). Therefore \(f_{\rm K}({\cal E})\) should be of the same form as equation (4.96) for \({\cal E}\gg 0\) and zero for

Figure 4.7: The logarithmic density gradient of the isothermal sphere. Note that the density gradient oscillates at large radii, a phenomenon that is explored in Problem 7.6.

\({\cal E}<0\). A suitable function is

\[f_{\rm K}({\cal E})=\cases{\rho_{1}(2\pi\sigma^{2})^{-3/2}\bigl{(}{\rm e}^{{\cal E }/\sigma^{2}}-1\bigr{)}&${\cal E}>0$;\cr 0&${\cal E}\leq 0$.\cr} \tag{4.110}\]

This df defines the family of **King models**.9 We now derive the density profiles and other properties of these models.

Footnote 9: dfs of the form (4.110) were actually introduced by Michie (1963) and studied in detail by Michie & Bodenheimer (1963), but King (1966) made them well known—see King (1981) for a discussion of their history.

We proceed much as in the case of the isothermal sphere. Substituting into equation (4.110) for \({\cal E}\) from equation (4.41) and integrating over all velocities, we obtain the density at any radius as

\[\eqalign{\rho_{\rm K}(\Psi)&={4\pi\rho_{1}\over(2\pi\sigma^{2})^{3/2}}\int_{0}^ {\sqrt{2\Psi}}{\rm d}v\,v^{2}\biggl{[}\exp\left({\Psi-{1\over 2}v^{2}\over \sigma^{2}}\right)-1\biggr{]}\cr&=\rho_{1}\biggl{[}{\rm e}^{\Psi/\sigma^{2}} \,{\rm erf}\left({\sqrt{\Psi}\over\sigma}\right)-\sqrt{{4\Psi\over\pi\sigma^{ 2}}}\left(1+{2\Psi\over 3\sigma^{2}}\right)\biggr{]},\cr} \tag{4.111}\]

Figure 4.8: (a) Density profiles of four King models: from top to bottom the central potentials of these models satisfy \(\Psi(0)/\sigma^{2}=12,\,9,\,6,\,3\). (b) The projected mass densities of these models (full curves), and the projected modified Hubble model of equation (4.109b) (dashed curve). The squares show the surface brightness of the elliptical galaxy NGC 283 (Lauer et al. 1995).

where \({\rm erf}(x)\) is the error function (Appendix C.3). Poisson's equation for \(\Psi\) may therefore be written

\[\frac{{\rm d}}{{\rm d}r}\left(r^{2}\frac{{\rm d}\Psi}{{\rm d}r}\right)=-4\pi G\rho _{1}r^{2}\biggl{[}{\rm e}^{\Psi/\sigma^{2}}\,{\rm erf}\left(\frac{\sqrt{\Psi}}{ \sigma}\right)-\sqrt{\frac{4\Psi}{\pi\sigma^{2}}}\left(1+\frac{2\Psi}{3\sigma ^{2}}\right)\,\biggr{]}. \tag{4.112}\]

We integrate this ordinary differential equation for \(\Psi(r)\) outwards from \(r=0\), where we set \({\rm d}\Psi/{\rm d}r=0\) and choose a value for \(\Psi\). This value determines the central potential \(\Phi(0)\) and total mass in the following implicit way. As we integrate equation (4.112) outward, \({\rm d}\Psi/{\rm d}r\) decreases, because initially \({\rm d}\Psi/{\rm d}r=0\) and \({\rm d}^{2}\Psi/{\rm d}r^{2}<0\). As \(\Psi\) decreases towards zero, the range \((0,\sqrt{2\Psi})\) of speeds of stars at a given radius narrows, and the density of stars drops. Eventually at some radius \(r_{\rm t}\), when \(\Psi\) becomes equal to zero, the density vanishes. We call \(r_{\rm t}\) the "tidal radius," following the term observers use to denote the outermost limit of a cluster (page 30). The mass \(M(r_{\rm t})=4\pi\int_{0}^{r_{\rm t}}{\rm d}r\,r^{2}\rho_{\rm K}\) is the system's total mass, and the potential at the tidal radius is

\[\Phi(r_{\rm t})=-\frac{GM(r_{\rm t})}{r_{\rm t}}. \tag{4.113}\]

The central potential is then \(\Phi(0)=\Phi(r_{\rm t})-\Psi(0)\). The bigger the value \(\Psi(0)\) from which we start our integration of equation (4.112), the greater will be the tidal radius, the total mass, and \(|\Phi(0)|\).

Figure 4.8a shows the density profiles of King models obtained by integrating equation (4.112) from several values of \(\Psi(0)\). The radial coordinate is marked in units of the King radius \(r_{0}\) that is defined by equations (4.106). Figure 4.8b shows the projected density profiles \(\Sigma_{\rm K}(R)\) of the King models of Figure 4.8a. Notice that for some of these models \(r_{0}\) is appreciably larger than the half-brightness, or core radius \(r_{\rm c}\), which is defined by the condition \(\Sigma_{\rm K}(r_{\rm c})/\Sigma_{\rm K}(0)=\frac{1}{2}\)--see the discussion following equation (4.106). The dashed curve in Figure 4.8b shows the modified Hubble model (4.109b), which provides a moderately good fit to the projected surface density of the King model with central potential \(\Psi(0)\simeq 8\sigma^{2}\). The squares in Figure 4.8b show the surface-brightness profile of the giant elliptical galaxy NGC 283. The King model with \(\Psi(0)\simeq 10\sigma^{2}\) fits this profile fairly well.

The ratio of the tidal radius \(r_{\rm t}\) to the King radius \(r_{0}\) defines the **concentration**\(c\) through

\[c\equiv\log_{10}(r_{\rm t}/r_{0}). \tag{4.114}\]

King models form a sequence that may be parameterized in terms of either \(c\) or \(\Psi(0)/\sigma^{2}\). Figure 4.9 gives the relationship between \(c\) and \(\Psi(0)/\sigma^{2}\). In the limit \(c\to\infty\), \(\Psi(0)/\sigma^{2}\to\infty\), the sequence of King models goes over into the isothermal sphere.

Figure 4.10 shows the dependence on the concentration of the half-mass radius \(r_{\rm h}\) (left panel) and the ratio \(r_{\rm h}/r_{\rm g}\) of the half-mass and gravitational radii (right panel). We see that although \(r_{\rm h}/r_{0}\) ranges over two orders of

magnitude along the sequence of King models, \(r_{\rm h}/r_{\rm g}\) is confined to the interval \((0.4,0.51)\).

At each point on the sequence of King models, there is a two-parameter family of systems that are related to each other by changes of scale. Thus for any value of \(c\) there are models having any given values of two of the parameters \(r_{0}\), \(\rho_{0}\) and \(\sigma\), with the third then being determined by equation (4.106).

The parameter \(\sigma\) that occurs in the relations we have given for King models must not be confused with the actual velocity dispersion \(\sigma_{r}=\sigma_{\theta}=\sigma_{\phi}\) of the stars of the system, or with the line-of-sight dispersion \(\sigma_{\parallel}\). Figure 4.11 is a plot of \(\sigma_{r}/\sigma\) and \(\sigma_{\parallel}/\sigma\) for several King models. One sees that in all these models the velocity dispersion falls monotonically from the center outward, reaching zero at \(r_{\rm t}\). The velocity dispersion of the stars at \(r_{\rm t}\) is zero because the potential energy of these stars is already equal to the largest energy allowed to any star.

The King df (4.110) is only one of several possible modified isothermal

