Figure 4.9: The relationship between the concentration \(c\) of a King model (eq. 4.114) and the central potential \(\Psi(0)\) from which equation (4.112) is integrated.

dfs. Woolley & Dickens (1961) discussed models for which \(f\) is given by (4.96) for \({\cal E}>0\) and is zero otherwise, while Wilson (1975) (see also Hunter 1977) considered models generated by dfs of the form

\[f_{\rm W}={\rm constant}\times\cases{\bigl{[}{\rm e}^{{\cal E}/\sigma^{2}}-1-({ \cal E}/\sigma^{2})\bigr{]}&for ${\cal E}>0$;\cr 0&otherwise.\cr} \tag{4.115}\]

We now examine some spherical systems generated by dfs that depend on both \({\cal E}\) and \(L\), with the result that their velocity-dispersion tensors are anisotropic.

**(d) Double-power models** In Problem 4.6 it is shown that a df of the form \(L^{\gamma}f_{1}({\cal E})\) generates a model in which the anisotropy parameter \(\beta\) (eq. 4.61) is at all radii equal to \(-{1\over 2}\gamma\). By adding two dfs of this type, say \(L^{\gamma_{1}}f_{1}({\cal E})\) and \(L^{\gamma_{2}}f_{2}({\cal E})\), we can generate a model in which \(\beta\) is a function of radius. Generalizing this idea, we are led to consider dfs of the form \(f({\cal E},L)=\sum_{\gamma}L^{\gamma}f_{\gamma}({\cal E})\). We can take the idea of power-series expansion one step further by supposing that the functions \(f_{\gamma}\) can also be expanded as power series. The df is then of the form

\[f({\cal E},L)=\sum_{\gamma\delta}\alpha_{\gamma\delta}L^{\gamma}{\cal E}^{ \delta}, \tag{4.116}\]

where the \(\alpha_{\gamma\delta}\) are arbitrary constants. The indices \(\gamma\) and \(\delta\) are not necessarily integers. The density will be real and finite only if \(\gamma>-2\), and a lower limit on \(\delta\) will be required to ensure that the density diminishes sufficiently fast as \(r\to\infty\). Kent & Gunn (1982) investigated self-gravitating models with dfs that consist of a single term in the series of (4.116).

**(e) Michie models**  A natural extension of King models to include velocity anisotropy is the family of **Michie models** defined by the df

\[f_{\rm M}({\cal E},L)=\cases{\rho_{1}(2\pi\sigma^{2})^{-3/2}{\rm e}^{-L^{2}/(2r_ {\rm a}^{2}\sigma^{2})}\bigl{(}{\rm e}^{{\cal E}/\sigma^{2}}-1\bigr{)}&({\cal E }>0)\cr 0&({\cal E}\leq 0).\cr} \tag{4.117}\]

In the limit \(r_{\rm a}\to\infty\) this df reduces to the df (4.110) of the King models, and for \({\cal E}\gg\sigma^{2}\) the variables \({\cal E}\) and \(L\) occur only through the variable \(Q\) that we defined in connection with Osipkov-Merritt models (eq. 4.73). Consequently, in a Michie model the velocity distribution is isotropic at the center, nearly radial in the outer parts, and the transition occurs near the anisotropy radius \(r_{\rm a}\). These models are fully described in Michie & Bodenheimer (1963).

### 4.4 DFs for axisymmetric density distributions

In SS3.2 we saw that most orbits in an axisymmetric potential admit three isolating integrals, \(H\), \(L_{z}\) and some third integral \(I_{3}\), for which we have an analytic expression only if the potential has the Stackel form (SS3.5.3). Since \(I_{3}\), when known, has a complicated functional form, models in which the df is a function \(f(H,L_{z})\) of the two "classical" integrals are the only ones susceptible to analytic treatment.

#### 4.4.1 DF for a given axisymmetric system

Equations (4.39) express a df of the form \(f({\cal E},L_{z})\) as the sum of parts \(f_{\pm}({\cal E},L_{z})\) that are even and odd in \(L_{z}\). We have seen that the probability density \(\nu(R,z)\) is independent of \(f_{-}\), while the azimuthal flux \(\nu\overline{v}_{\phi}\) is independent of \(f_{+}\). Lynden-Bell (1962a) extended Eddington's work on spherical systems to show that \(f_{+}({\cal E},L_{z})\) can be deduced if we are given the density \(\nu(R,z)\) and its confining potential \(\Phi(R,z)\), and that \(f_{-}({\cal E},L_{z})\) can be recovered if \(\overline{v}_{\phi}(R,z)\) is also known. To prove these results, we use cylindrical coordinates \((v_{\rm m},\psi,v_{\phi})\) for velocity space, with the polar axis in the azimuthal direction, so \(v_{R}=v_{\rm m}\cos\psi\), \(v_{z}=v_{\rm m}\sin\psi\). We have \({\rm d}^{3}{\bf v}=v_{\rm m}{\rm d}v_{\rm m}{\rm d}\psi{\rm d}v_{\phi}\) and the Jacobian determinant \(\partial({\cal E},L_{z})/\partial(v_{\phi},v_{\rm m})=Rv_{\rm m}\), so \({\rm d}^{3}{\bf v}=R^{-1}{\rm d}{\cal E}{\rm d}L_{z}{\rm d}\psi\). Hence

\[\nu(R,z)={4\pi\over R}\int_{0}^{\Psi}{\rm d}{\cal E}\int_{0}^{R\sqrt{2(\Psi-{ \cal E})}}\!\!\!{\rm d}L_{z}\,f_{+}({\cal E},L_{z}^{2}), \tag{4.118}\]

where we have taken \(f_{+}\) to be a function of \(L_{z}^{2}\) rather than of \(L_{z}\). We assume that \(\nu\) is symmetrical about the equatorial plane, and is therefore a function of \(|z|\). At fixed \(R\), \(|z|\) is a monotone function of \(\Psi\), so we can consider \(\nu\) to be a function of \((R,\Psi)\) rather than \((R,z)\). On this understanding we now differentiate (4.118) with respect to \(\Psi\):

\[\frac{\partial}{\partial\Psi}\nu(R,\Psi)=4\pi\int_{0}^{\Psi}\mathrm{d}{\cal E} \;\frac{f_{+}[{\cal E},2(\Psi-{\cal E})R^{2}]}{\sqrt{2(\Psi-{\cal E})}}. \tag{4.119}\]

This integral equation for \(f_{+}\) can be solved by taking Laplace transforms, so we multiply each side by \(\exp(-s\Psi)\) and integrate over \(\Psi\). After interchanging the order of the integrals over \(\Psi\) and \({\cal E}\) we have

\[\int_{0}^{\infty}\mathrm{d}\Psi\,\mathrm{e}^{-s\Psi}\frac{ \partial\nu}{\partial\Psi} =4\pi\int_{0}^{\infty}\mathrm{d}{\cal E}\,\mathrm{e}^{-s{\cal E}} \int_{{\cal E}}^{\infty}\mathrm{d}\Psi\,\mathrm{e}^{-s(\Psi-{\cal E})}\frac{f _{+}[{\cal E},2(\Psi-{\cal E})R^{2}]}{\sqrt{2(\Psi-{\cal E})}}\] \[=\frac{2\pi}{R}\int_{0}^{\infty}\mathrm{d}{\cal E}\,\mathrm{e}^{ -s{\cal E}}\int_{0}^{\infty}\mathrm{d}u\,\mathrm{e}^{-su/(2R^{2})}g({\cal E},u),\] (4.120a) where \[g({\cal E},u)\equiv\frac{f_{+}({\cal E},u)}{\surd u}. \tag{4.120b}\]

The integrals on the right side of (4.120a) effect Laplace transforms of \(g\) with respect to each of its arguments. Let a hat denote Laplace transformation, so

\[\hat{g}(s,t) \equiv\int_{0}^{\infty}\mathrm{d}{\cal E}\,\mathrm{e}^{-s{\cal E} }\int_{0}^{\infty}\mathrm{d}u\,\mathrm{e}^{-tu}g({\cal E},u); \tag{4.121}\] \[\hat{\nu}(R,s) \equiv\int_{0}^{\infty}\mathrm{d}\Psi\,\mathrm{e}^{-s\Psi}\nu(R, \Psi).\]

Then comparing the first of equations (4.121) with (4.120a) we see that \(t=s/(2R^{2})\). Integrating the left side of (4.120a) by parts and setting \(R=\sqrt{s/2t}\), we have finally

\[\hat{g}(s,t)=\frac{s^{3/2}}{\pi(8t)^{1/2}}\,\hat{\nu}\left(\sqrt{\frac{s}{2t} },s\right). \tag{4.122}\]

Since a function is uniquely defined by its Laplace transform, this result establishes that \(f_{+}\) is determined by \(\nu(R,z)\). The demonstration that \(f_{-}\) is determined by \(\nu\overline{v}_{\phi}\) is similar. As in the case of Eddington's inversion, there is no guarantee that the recovered df will be non-negative.

Two practical difficulties arise when attempting to use Lynden-Bell's result (4.122). First, only a very few models permit one analytically to replace \(z\) by \(\Psi\) in \(\nu(R,z)\). Second, inverting the Laplace transforms in (4.122) usually requires analytic continuation of the function \(\nu(R,\Psi)\) to the complex plane, which is problematic if \(\nu(R,\Psi)\) is derived from observational data. On account of these difficulties, equation (4.122) has yielded disappointingly few dfs in practice, although Lynden-Bell (1962a) was able to recover the df of a rotating Plummer model. This situation improved when Hunter & Qian (1993) and Qian et al. (1995) showed that the df could be obtained from a contour integral, without explicitly eliminating \(z\) between \(\nu\) and \(\Psi\), and without extensive analytic continuation.

##### Axisymmetric systems specified by \(f(H,L_{z})\)

In SS4.3.3 we investigated some spherical systems that were defined by their dfs. We now extend this approach to the axisymmetric case: we start with a simple functional form for \(f\), and then use Poisson's equation to recover the observable properties of the model. As in the spherical case, for most choices of the df Poisson's equation has to be solved numerically. We start by describing some exceptions to this rule. We adopt the convention that \(f\) is the phase-space density of mass.

**(a) Fully analytic models**  Fricke (1951) expanded the df of an axisymmetric galaxy in powers of \({\cal E}\) and \(L_{z}\). Since \(L_{z}\) can be positive or negative, it can occur only in integral powers, but we are free to include non-integral powers of \({\cal E}\). We use the term **Fricke component** to denote a df of the form \({\cal E}^{\gamma}L_{z}^{2n}\), where the 2 in the exponent of \(L_{z}\) ensures that the component is even in \(L_{z}\) and thus has non-vanishing density. With equation (4.118) the density is

\[\rho(R,z)={4\pi\over R}\int_{0}^{\Psi}{\rm d}{\cal E}\,{\cal E}^{\gamma}\int_ {0}^{R\sqrt{2(\Psi-{\cal E})}}{\rm d}L_{z}\,L_{z}^{2n}. \tag{4.123}\]

When we do the integral over \(L_{z}\) and change the second variable of integration to \(x\equiv{\cal E}/\Psi\), we obtain

\[\eqalign{\rho(R,z)&={2^{n+5/2}\over 2n+1}\pi R^{2n}\Psi^{\gamma+n+3/2}\int_ {0}^{1}{\rm d}x\,x^{\gamma}(1-x)^{n+1/2}\cr&=2^{n+3/2}{\gamma!(n-{1\over 2})! \over(\gamma+n+{3\over 2})!}\pi\,R^{2n}\Psi^{\gamma+n+3/2}.\cr}\]

Thus the density of a Fricke component is a power of \(R\) multiplied by a power of \(\Psi\). Below we shall use (4.124) in the form

\[\rho=R^{2n}\Psi^{\gamma}\quad\Leftrightarrow\quad f={2^{-(n+3/2)}\gamma!\over \pi(n-{1\over 2})!(\gamma-n-{3\over 2})!}\,L_{z}^{2n}{\cal E}^{\gamma-n-3/2}.\]

Toomre (1982) discovered a closely related one-parameter sequence of models with remarkably simple analytic properties. The dfs of Toomre's models are proportional to \(L_{z}^{2n}{\cal E}^{/\sigma^{2}}\) and their density profiles have the scale-free form \(\rho(r,\theta)\propto r^{-2}S(\theta)\), where the function \(S\) depends on the parameter \(n\) in the df. In limiting cases these systems go over into (i) the isothermal sphere, (ii) Spitzer's isothermal sheet (Problem 4.21), and (iii) cold Mestel disks (see SS4.5.1 below). Like Fricke components, for \(n>0\) Toomre's models have zero density on the \(z\) axis.

Fully analytical models that have finite central densities were discovered by Evans (1993, 1994) by assuming that the relative potential is a function \(\Psi(m^{2})\) of the spheroidal variable

\[m^{2}=R_{\rm c}^{2}+R^{2}+{z^{2}\over q_{\Phi}^{2}}.\]

Inserting this form of \(\Psi\) into Poisson's equation, we find after some algebra that the density is given by

\[-4\pi G\rho=\Bigl{(}4+{2\over q_{\Phi}^{2}}\Bigr{)}\Psi^{\prime}+{4(m^{2}-R_{ \rm c}^{2})\over q_{\Phi}^{2}}\Psi^{\prime\prime}+4\Bigl{(}1-{1\over q_{\Phi}^ {2}}\Bigr{)}\Psi^{\prime\prime}R^{2},\]

where primes denote differentiation with respect to the argument of the function. Since \(\Psi^{\prime}\), \(\Psi^{\prime\prime}\), and \(\Psi\) are all functions only of \(m^{2}\), we may straightforwardly express \(\Psi^{\prime}\) and \(\Psi^{\prime\prime}\) as functions of \(\Psi\). Once this has been done, equation (4.127) gives \(\rho\) as a function \(\rho(R,\Psi)\), and the model's df can be recovered from the formulae of Lynden-Bell (1962a) or Hunter & Qian (1993) as described in SS4.4.1.

If \(\Phi\) is either a power or the logarithm of \(m^{2}\), we can recover the df more economically using Fricke's formula (4.125): if \(\Psi=\Psi_{a}(R_{\rm c}^{2}/m^{2})^{y}\), where \(y>0\) and \(\Psi_{a}\) is a constant, then

\[\eqalign{m^{2}&=R_{\rm c}^{2}(\Psi/\Psi_{a})^{-1/y},\cr\Psi^{\prime}&=-y\Psi_{ a}R_{\rm c}^{2y}(m^{2})^{-(y+1)}=-y{\Psi_{a}\over R_{\rm c}^{2}}(\Psi/\Psi_{a})^{ 1+1/y},\cr\Psi^{\prime\prime}&=y(y+1)\Psi_{a}R_{\rm c}^{2y}(m^{2})^{-(y+2)}=y( y+1){\Psi_{a}\over R_{\rm c}^{4}}(\Psi/\Psi_{a})^{1+2/y},\cr}\]

and our expression (4.127) for \(\rho(R,\Psi)\) becomes

\[\eqalign{\rho(R,\Psi)&={y[2-(2y+1)q_{\Phi}^{-2}]\Psi_{a}\over 2\pi GR_{\rm c }^{2}}\Bigl{(}{\Psi\over\Psi_{a}}\Bigr{)}^{1+1/y}\cr&\qquad+{y(y+1)\Psi_{a} \over\pi q_{\Phi}^{2}GR_{\rm c}^{2}}\Bigl{(}{\Psi\over\Psi_{a}}\Bigr{)}^{1+2/y }\left(1+(1-q_{\Phi}^{2}){R^{2}\over R_{\rm c}^{2}}\right).\cr}\]

This is just a sum of three Fricke components, so equation (4.125) enables us to say that the df is

\[f_{\rm pow}({\cal E},L_{z})=A{\cal E}^{1/y-1/2}+B(1+CL_{z}^{2}/{\cal E}){\cal E }^{2/y-1/2},\]where

\[A\equiv{y[2-(2y+1)q_{\Phi}^{-2}](1+1/y)!\over(2\pi)^{5/2}(1/y-1/2)!\,GR _{\rm c}^{2}\Psi_{a}^{1/y}}\qquad;\quad C\equiv{1-q_{\Phi}^{2}\over R_{\rm c}^{ 2}}\Bigl{(}{2\over y}-{1\over 2}\Bigr{)}.\]

We shall call a model with this df an **Evans model**. The model is unphysical if the phase-space density is ever negative. Negative densities do not arise so long as \(1\geq q_{\Phi}^{2}\geq y+{1\over 2}\). For \(y={1\over 2}\) the only physical model is spherical--in fact it is the Plummer model. As \(y\) decreases, ever flatter models become possible.

In the limit \(y\to 0\), the potential of an Evans model tends10 to the logarithmic potential \(\Phi_{\rm L}(R,z)\) of equation (2.71a), and the self-consistent df becomes (Evans 1993)

Footnote 10: This is something of an oversimplification. We have \(\ln x=\lim_{\alpha\to 0}\int_{1}^{x}{\rm d}y/y^{1+\alpha}=\lim_{\alpha\to 0}\alpha^{-1}(1-1/x^{ \alpha})\), so \(x^{-\alpha}\) tends to one minus a (small) multiple of \(\ln x\).

\[f_{\rm log}({\cal E},L_{z})=A{\rm e}^{{\cal E}/\sigma^{2}}+B\bigl{(}1+CL_{z}^{ 2}){\rm e}^{2{\cal E}/\sigma^{2}},\]

where \(\sigma^{2}\equiv{1\over 2}v_{0}^{2}\) and

\[A\equiv{2q_{\Phi}^{2}-1\over(2\pi)^{5/2}Gq_{\Phi}^{2}\sigma}\quad;\quad B \equiv{R_{\rm c}^{2}\over\pi^{5/2}Gq_{\Phi}^{2}\sigma}\quad;\quad C\equiv{2(1- q_{\Phi}^{2})\over R_{\rm c}^{2}\sigma^{2}}.\]

Evans's df (4.131a) has an obvious application as the df of a population of dark-matter particles, since circular-speed curves tend to become flat at large radii, which implies that the potential is proportional to \(\ln R\) (SS1.1.3). The density of any luminous population falls off faster at large \(r\). Evans observed that if the df of luminous matter is

\[f_{\rm lum}=\rho_{0}R_{\rm c}^{p}\,\Bigl{(}{p\over 2\pi\sigma^{2}}\Bigr{)}^{3/2 }\,{\rm e}^{p{\cal E}/\sigma^{2}},\]

where \(p\geq 1\) is a constant, then the density of luminous matter confined by \(\Phi_{\rm L}\) is

\[\rho_{\rm lum}(R,z)={\rho_{0}R_{\rm c}^{p}\over(R_{\rm c}^{2}+R^{2}+z^{2}/q_{ \Phi}^{2})^{p/2}},\]

which is proportional to \(R^{-p}\) at large \(R\). The combined density of luminous and dark matter will generate \(\Phi_{\rm L}\) if the df of the dark matter is \(f_{\rm dark}=f_{\rm log}-f_{\rm lum}\). Evans shows that \(f_{\rm dark}\) is non-negative provided the fraction of the central mass density that is luminous is smaller than a specified function of \(q_{\Phi}\) and \(p\).

The df (4.132a) of luminous matter depends on \({\cal E}\) only, with the consequence that the surfaces of constant luminosity density coincide with equipotentials. It is straightforward to write down dfs for luminous matter that depend on \(L_{z}\) as well as \({\cal E}\) in such a way that the luminosity distribution is more flattened than the potential--see Evans (1993) for examples.

The dfs given by both (4.130a) and (4.131a) are even functions of \(L_{z}\), so they produce non-rotating models. Rotating models can be produced by adding any df\(f_{-}\) that is odd in \(L_{z}\) and small enough in magnitude to ensure that the composite df is non-negative. For example, if \(\alpha(x)\) is an odd function with absolute value less than unity, then we can choose \(f_{-}\) to be

\[f_{-}({\cal E},L_{z})=\alpha(L_{z})f_{+}({\cal E},L_{z}).\]

With this choice of \(f_{-}\) the df is equal to \((1+\alpha)f_{+}\).

A model in which \(\sigma_{\phi}=\sigma_{R}=\sigma_{z}\) everywhere is called an **isotropic rotator** and can be said to be flattened by rotation alone. For any df of the form \(f({\cal E},L_{z})\) we have \(\sigma_{R}=\sigma_{z}\), and by choosing \(\alpha\) such that \(\sigma_{\phi}=\sigma_{R}\) at some particular point, we can generate a good approximation to an isotropic rotator.

If we apply to an Evans model the prescription of equation (4.133) with \(\alpha(L_{z})={\rm sgn}(L_{z})\alpha_{0}\), where \(\alpha_{0}\) is a constant, the mean velocity becomes

\[\eqalign{\overline{v}_{\phi}&={4\pi\alpha_{0}\over\rho R^{2}}\int_{0}^{\Psi}{ \rm d}{\cal E}\int_{0}^{R\sqrt{2(\Psi-{\cal E})}}{\rm d}L_{z}\,L_{z}f_{\rm pow }({\cal E},L_{z})\cr&={16\pi\alpha_{0}y^{2}\over\rho}\Psi^{1/y+3/2}\left\{{A \over(2+y)(2+3y)}+{B\Psi^{1/y}(4-y+4yCR^{2})\over(4+3y)(16-y^{2})}\right\}.}\]

Figure 4.12 shows \(v_{\parallel}\), \(\sigma_{\parallel}\) and the isophote ellipticity \(\epsilon\) along the major axis of an edge-on Evans model that is an approximate isotropic rotator. Both \(\epsilon\) and \(v_{\parallel}/\sigma_{\parallel}\) increase with distance from the center. This model is somewhat

unrealistic because our choice of \(\alpha(x)\) has introduced a discontinuity in \(f\) at \(L_{z}=0\), which in turn causes a vortex along the symmetry axis of the model. Nevertheless, the projected velocities shown in Figure 4.12 reflect those of more realistic Evans models.

**(b) Rowley models** In general, when a df is chosen for an axisymmetric system, we have to solve for the associated density and potential distributions numerically. We now describe an effective technique for this job, which differs significantly from the method we used to solve the corresponding problem in the spherical case. Prendergast & Tomer (1970), Jarvis & Freeman (1985) and Rowley (1988) discuss this problem.

As in the spherical case, the df cannot be correctly normalized at the outset. So we write

\[f\propto{\cal F}({\cal E},L_{z}),\]

where \({\cal F}\) is our chosen functional form. The model is specified by this form and the values \(\Psi_{\rm c}\) and \(\Psi_{\rm t}\) taken by the relative potential at the center and at an outer point, for example the point \((R_{\rm t},0)\) in the equatorial plane at which the density first vanishes. We proceed iteratively. We guess what potential \(\Phi_{0}(R,z)\) our df will generate at a grid of points. We take the relative potential that appears in \({\cal E}\) to be

\[\Psi_{0}(R,z)=\psi-\lambda\Phi_{0}(R,z),\]

where \(\psi\) and \(\lambda\) are constants that we choose such that \(\Psi_{0}(0,0)=\Psi_{\rm c}\) and \(\Psi_{0}(R_{\rm t},0)=\Psi_{\rm t}\). Then at each grid point we evaluate the density

\[\rho_{1}(R,z)\equiv\int{\rm d}^{3}{\bf v}\,{\cal F}[\Psi_{0}(R,z)-{{1\over 2 }}v^{2},Rv_{\phi}].\]

and from these values solve for the corresponding potential \(\Phi_{1}(R,z)\). At the next iteration we use the relative potential \(\Psi_{1}=\psi-\lambda\Phi_{1}\), where \(\psi\) and \(\lambda\) are chosen afresh to ensure that \(\Psi_{1}\) satisfies the same conditions at the origin and \((R_{\rm t},0)\) as \(\Psi_{0}\) did. We repeat this process until \(\Psi_{n}\) and \(\rho_{n}\) change very little between iterations. The final model has df \(\lambda{\cal F}\) and density \(\lambda\rho_{n}\).

Proceeding in this way, Rowley (1988) constructed models using the functional form

\[{\cal F}({\cal E},L_{z})=\cases{{\rm e}^{\chi/\sigma^{2}}&for $\chi>0$,\cr 0&otherwise,\cr}\]

where

\[\chi\equiv{\cal E}+\omega L_{z}-{{1\over 2}}L_{z}^{2}/r_{\rm a }^{2}.\]

Here \(\sigma\), \(\omega\) and \(r_{\rm a}\) are parameters. Let \(v_{\rm m}\equiv\sqrt{v_{R}^{2}+v_{z}^{2}}\). Then \(\chi\) can be written

\[\eqalign{\chi&=\Psi-{{1\over 2}}v_{\rm m}^{2}-{{1\over 2}} \Bigl{(}1+{R^{2}\over r_{\rm a}^{2}}\Bigr{)}v_{\phi}^{2}+\omega Rv_{\phi}\cr&= \Psi-{{1\over 2}}v_{\rm m}^{2}-{{1\over 2}}\Bigl{(}1+{R^{2}\over r_{\rm a}^{2}} \Bigr{)}\Bigl{(}v_{\phi}-{\omega R\over 1+R^{2}/r_{\rm a}^{2}}\Bigr{)}^{2}+{ \omega^{2}R^{2}\over 2(1+R^{2}/r_{\rm a}^{2})}.\cr}\]Thus the df (4.138) is a truncated Gaussian in \(v_{R}\), \(v_{z}\), and \(v^{\prime}_{\phi}\equiv v_{\phi}-\overline{v}_{\phi}\), where the mean speed is

\[\overline{v}_{\phi}(R)=\frac{\omega R}{1+R^{2}/r_{\rm a}^{2}}. \tag{4.140}\]

Since \(\overline{v}_{\phi}\) depends only on \(R\), the streaming velocity is independent of distance \(|z|\) from the equatorial plane: the system is said to rotate "on cylinders". Equation (4.140) shows that we have solid-body rotation \(\overline{v}_{\phi}\simeq\omega R\) for \(R\ll r_{\rm a}\). At \(R\gg r_{\rm a}\) the mean streaming speed decays to zero as \(1/R\).

Integrating over the truncated Gaussians we find that

\[\begin{split}\rho(R,z)&=\frac{4\pi\sigma^{3}}{ \sqrt{1+R^{2}/r_{\rm a}^{2}}}\left(\sqrt{\pi/2}\,{\rm e}^{\widetilde{\Psi}}\,{ \rm erf}(\sqrt{\widetilde{\Psi}})-\sqrt{2\widetilde{\Psi}}\,\right)\\ \overline{\frac{v_{R}^{2}}{\sigma^{2}}}&=\overline{ \frac{v_{z}^{2}}{\sigma^{2}}}=\left(1+\frac{R^{2}}{r_{\rm a}^{2}}\right) \frac{\overline{\widetilde{v}_{\phi}^{2}}}{\sigma^{2}}=1-\frac{(2\widetilde{ \Psi})^{3/2}}{3\left(\sqrt{\pi/2}\,{\rm e}^{\widetilde{\Psi}}\,{\rm erf}( \sqrt{\widetilde{\Psi}})-\sqrt{2\widetilde{\Psi}}\,\right)},\end{split}\] (4.141a) where \[\widetilde{v}_{\phi}\equiv v_{\phi}-\overline{v}_{\phi}\] and \[\widetilde{\Psi}\equiv\frac{1}{\sigma^{2}}\left(\Psi+\frac{\omega^{2}R^{2}}{ 2(1+R^{2}/r_{\rm a}^{2})}\right). \tag{4.141b}\]

For large values of \(\widetilde{\Psi}\) the density grows exponentially with \(\Psi\) as in the isothermal sphere, and the velocity dispersion in the meridional plane tends to the parameter \(\sigma\). At \(r\ll r_{\rm a}\), the model is isotropic, but beyond the anisotropy radius \(r_{\rm a}\), the dispersion in the azimuthal direction falls below that in the meridional plane in close analogy with the behavior of an Osipkov-Merritt model. The mean-square velocity in the azimuthal direction is

\[\overline{v_{\phi}^{2}}=\overline{v}_{\phi}^{2}+\overline{\widetilde{v}_{ \phi}^{2}}=\frac{\overline{v_{R}^{2}}+\omega^{2}R^{2}}{1+R^{2}/r_{\rm a}^{2}} \simeq\overline{v_{R}^{2}}\frac{1+R^{2}\omega^{2}/\sigma^{2}}{1+R^{2}/r_{\rm a }^{2}}. \tag{4.142}\]

Figure 4.13: Density in the meridional plane of Rowley models that have \(\omega R_{\rm t}/\sigma=2\) (left) and 4 (right). Both models have \(\Psi(0,0)-\Psi(R_{\rm t},0)=4\sigma^{2}\) and \(r_{\rm a}=0.45R_{\rm t}\).

In SS4.8.3 we shall see that galaxies are flattened by an excess of kinetic energy in the equatorial plane relative to the meridional plane. When \(\omega r_{\rm a}/\sigma=1\), the fraction on the right of (4.142) is unity, and there would be no excess, but when this dimensionless parameter exceeds unity, there is an excess. Hence we expect the flattening to be an increasing function of \(\omega r_{\rm a}/\sigma\). The other important dimensionless parameter of Rowley models is \(\widetilde{\Psi}(0,0)\), which is analogous to the quantity that determines the concentration of a King model.

Figure 4.13 shows the density in the meridional plane of Rowley models that differ in their values of \(\omega r_{\rm a}/\sigma\). As this parameter increases the models rotate more rapidly and become more strongly flattened; the isodensity surfaces are not nearly ellipses and in projection these models have peanut shapes.

**(c) Rotation and flattening in spheroids** We have seen that mathematically the connection between the flattening of a spheroidal stellar system and its rotation rate is weak because the rotation rate is determined by \(f_{-}\)

