#### The Schwarzschild DF

The stars in a galactic disk such as that of the Milky Way travel on nearly circular and coplanar orbits. Consequently, dfs that generate cool disks in which random velocities are much smaller than the circular speed are central to understanding disk galaxies. The mean radius of a star that is on a nearly circular orbit near the equatorial plane is largely determined by its angular momentum \(L_{z}\), or equivalently by its guiding-center radius \(R_{\rm g}(L_{z})\) (eq. 3.72). In fact, in the epicycle approximation of SS3.2.3 the mean radius is equal to the guiding-center radius. Thus the radial density profile of a cool disk is largely determined by the dependence of the df upon \(L_{z}\).

The difference

\[\Delta\equiv H-E_{\rm c}(L_{z}) \tag{4.143}\]

between a star's energy and the energy \(E_{\rm c}(L_{z})\) of the circular orbit with the same angular momentum is the energy associated with the star's gyrations around the guiding center. In a disk composed of many stars these oscillations--all with random phases--lead to a dispersion in velocities at each location in the disk, superimposed on the overall rotation. The distribution of stars with respect to \(\Delta\) is what governs the velocity dispersion or temperature of the disk--in a cool disk all stars have small values of \(\Delta/E_{\rm c}(L_{z})\). These considerations suggest that we examine dfs of the form

\[f(H,L_{z})=S(L_{z})T[\Delta/\sigma^{2}(L_{z})], \tag{4.144}\]

where the function \(S\) is predominantly determined by the surface density \(\Sigma(R)\), and the function \(T\) is chosen to fit the shape of the velocity distribution; \(\sigma(L_{z})\) determines the radial dependence of the velocity dispersion.

Unfortunately the df (4.144) cannot reproduce all the properties of the solar neighborhood because observations show that \(\overline{v_{z}^{2}}/\overline{v_{R}^{2}}\simeq 0.3\neq 1\) (Table 1.2), while equation (4.40) shows that any df that depends only on \(H\) and \(L_{z}\) requires these two dispersions to be equal. This situation motivates us to consider a more complex df that also depends on the third integral \(I_{3}\) that we studied in SS3.2. We do not have an analytic expression for \(I_{3}\) in a general axisymmetric potential, so we use the approximation (3.74)

\[I_{3}\simeq H_{z}(z,\dot{z},L_{z})\equiv{{1\over 2}}\dot{z}^{2}+ \Phi_{z}(z,L_{z}), \tag{4.145}\]

where we have made the dependence of \(\Phi_{z}\) on \(L_{z}\) explicit. We take advantage of equation (4.145) to generalize (4.144) to the form

\[f(H,L_{z},I_{3})\simeq S(L_{z})T\left({\Delta\over\sigma_{R}^{2}},{H_{z}\over \sigma_{3}^{2}}\right), \tag{4.146}\]

where \(\sigma_{R}^{2}\) and \(\sigma_{3}^{2}\) are functions of \(L_{z}\). An exponential function is an obvious choice for \(T\), so we arrive at dfs of the form (Shu 1969)

\[f(H,L_{z},H_{z})=S(L_{z})\exp\left(-{\Delta\over\sigma_{R}^{2}}-{H_{z}\over \sigma_{3}^{2}}\right). \tag{4.147}\]

If we introduce the epicycle approximation for motions parallel to the plane, then \(\Delta\simeq H_{R}+H_{z}\), where \(H_{R}\) is given by equation (3.86) as

\[H_{R}\equiv{{1\over 2}}(\dot{x}^{2}+\kappa^{2}x^{2}) \tag{4.148}\]with \(x\equiv R-R_{\rm g}\) and \(\kappa\) the epicycle frequency. Thus so long as \(\sigma_{R}\) and \(\sigma_{3}\) are much smaller than the circular speed, we can write

\[f(H,L_{z},I_{3})\simeq S(L_{z})\exp\left(-\frac{H_{R}}{\sigma_{R}^{2}}-\frac{H_{ z}}{\sigma_{z}^{2}}\right),\] (4.149a) where \[\sigma_{z}^{2}\equiv\frac{\sigma_{R}^{2}\sigma_{3}^{2}}{\sigma_{R}^{2}+\sigma _{3}^{2}}. \tag{4.149b}\]

When we apply (4.149a) to the solar neighborhood, we encounter the problem that we do not directly measure the value of \(R_{\rm g}\) for stars, so it is not straightforward to determine their \(x\) values. However, an observationally accessible quantity is the difference

\[\widetilde{\bf v}\equiv{\bf v}-v_{\rm c}(R)\hat{\bf e}_{\phi} \tag{4.150}\]

between the velocity of a star and the velocity of the circular orbit at the star's current location--in the solar neighborhood this is the Local Standard of Rest (lsr) (SS1.1.2). We have from equation (3.97)

\[\widetilde{v}_{\phi}=\frac{\kappa}{\gamma}x, \tag{4.151}\]

where \(\gamma\equiv 2\Omega_{\rm g}/\kappa\) (eq. 3.93b) and \(\Omega_{\rm g}\) is the circular frequency at \(R_{\rm g}\). Substituting equation (4.151) into (4.148), we have

\[H_{R}\simeq{{1\over 2}}(v_{R}^{2}+\gamma^{2}\widetilde{v}_{\phi}^{2}). \tag{4.152}\]

Substituting this expression and equation (4.145) into equation (4.149a) we have finally

\[f\simeq f_{\rm Sch}\equiv S(L_{z})\exp\left(-\frac{v_{R}^{2}+\gamma^{2} \widetilde{v}_{\phi}^{2}}{2\sigma_{R}^{2}(L_{z})}-\frac{v_{z}^{2}+2\Phi_{z}(z, L_{z})}{2\sigma_{z}^{2}(L_{z})}\right). \tag{4.153}\]

The distribution \(f_{\rm Sch}\) is called the **Schwarzschild** df.12

Footnote 12: Karl Schwarzschild (1873–1916) pioneered photographic photometry. In 1900 he published a lower limit on the radius of curvature of space. While serving in the German army from August 1914, he gave a quantum-mechanical explanation of the anomalous Stark effect in hydrogen and obtained the first and most important exact solution of Einstein’s field equations.

We must now choose plausible forms for the three free functions \(S(L_{z})\), \(\sigma_{R}(L_{z})\) and \(\sigma_{z}(L_{x})\) that define the Schwarzschild df. Equation (4.153) predicts that the distributions of \(v_{R}\) and \(v_{z}\) are superpositions of Gaussians, one for each value of \(L_{z}\). If \(S\), \(\sigma_{R}\) and \(\sigma_{z}\) vary sufficiently slowly with \(L_{z}=R(v_{\rm c}+\widetilde{v}_{\phi})\) that they change negligibly so long as \(|\widetilde{v}_{\phi}|\) is less than a few times \(\sigma_{R}\), we may treat them as constants at a given position. Then \(\sigma_{R}(Rv_{\rm c})\) and \(\sigma_{z}(Rv_{\rm c})\) are simply the radial and vertical velocity dispersions at \(R\). The oldest disk stars of the solar neighborhood have the largest random velocities, \(\sigma_{R}\simeq 40\,{\rm km\,s^{-1}}\), and even this value is much smaller than \(v_{\rm c}\simeq 220\,{\rm km\,s^{-1}}\) (Table 1.2). Hence the approximation of constant \(\sigma_{R}\) and \(\sigma_{z}\) is a reasonable one, at least for cooler stellar populations.

We still have to choose \(S(L_{z})\), which we do by computing the surface density \(\Sigma(R)\). Integrating over velocities in the approximation of constant \(\sigma_{i}\), we find that the density of the disk is

\[\rho(R,z)=\int{\rm d}^{3}{\bf v}\,f\simeq(2\pi)^{3/2}S(Rv_{\rm c})\left(\frac{ \sigma_{R}^{2}\sigma_{z}}{\gamma}\right)_{L_{z}=Rv_{\rm c}}\exp\left(-\frac{ \Phi_{z}(z,Rv_{\rm c})}{\sigma_{z}^{2}(Rv_{\rm c})}\right). \tag{4.154}\]

At any given radius, we therefore have \(\rho(z)=\rho_{0}\exp(-\Phi_{z}/\sigma^{2})\), where \(\rho_{0}(R)\) is the density in the midplane. In Problem 4.21 it is shown that when \(\rho(z)\) takes this form and \(\Phi_{z}(z)\) is determined self-consistently by the density in the disk, the surface density is \(\Sigma=4z_{0}\rho_{0}\), where \(z_{0}=\sigma_{z}(8\pi G\rho_{0})^{-1/2}\) is the scale height of the disk. Thus

\[\Sigma(L_{z}/v_{\rm c})\simeq\Sigma(R)\simeq 4(2\pi)^{3/2}S(L_{z})\left(\frac{ \sigma_{R}^{2}\sigma_{z}z_{0}}{\gamma}\right)_{L_{z}}. \tag{4.155}\]

This approximate result relating \(S(L_{z})\) and \(\Sigma(R)\) holds only when the dispersions \(\sigma_{R},\sigma_{z}\ll v_{0}\). Nevertheless it is useful to use this result to eliminate \(S(L_{z})\) from equation (4.153), recognizing that the surface density derived from the resulting df will not be exactly equal to \(\Sigma(R)\). Thus we write

\[f_{\rm Schw}({\bf w})\simeq\frac{\gamma\Sigma(L_{z}/v_{\rm c})}{4(2\pi)^{3/2} \sigma_{R}^{2}\sigma_{z}z_{0}}\exp\left(-\frac{v_{R}^{2}+\gamma^{2}\widetilde {v}_{\phi}^{2}}{2\sigma_{R}^{2}(L_{z})}-\frac{v_{z}^{2}+2\Phi_{z}(z,L_{z})}{2 \sigma_{z}^{2}(L_{z})}\right). \tag{4.156}\]

An important application of equation (4.156) is to the case of an exponential disk, \(\Sigma(R)=\Sigma_{0}\exp(-R/R_{\rm d})\), that rotates in a potential with a constant circular speed, so we can replace \(v_{\rm c}(R)\) with \(v_{0}\) and set \(\gamma=\surd 2\). Observations of edge-on disks show that \(z_{0}\) is approximately independent of radius, so from equation (4.302c) of Problem 4.21 it follows that \(\sigma_{z}\propto\exp(-R/2R_{\rm d})\simeq\exp(-L_{z}/2R_{\rm d}v_{0})\). Let us assume that the ratio of dispersions \(\sigma_{z}/\sigma_{R}\) is independent of radius. Then the right side of equation (4.156) is completely determined and we can examine the predictions it makes. Figure 4.15 shows for the solar neighborhood the predicted distributions of azimuthal velocities \(\widetilde{v}_{\phi}\) for three populations of stars. The sharply peaked distribution is for an extremely cold population, which has \(\overline{v_{R}^{2}}^{1/2}=5\,{\rm km\,s^{-1}}\), while the broadest distribution is for a population that has \(\overline{v_{R}^{2}}^{1/2}=30\,{\rm km\,s^{-1}}\). The narrow distribution is almost Gaussian, and in fact the three-dimensional velocity distribution of such a population would conform to the triaxial Gaussian model that Schwarzschild (1907) derived from observations of solar-neighborhood stars.

\[{\rm d}n\propto\exp\left(-\frac{v_{R}^{2}+\gamma^{2}\widetilde{v}_{\phi}^{2}}{2 \sigma_{R}^{2}}-\frac{v_{z}^{2}}{2\sigma_{z}^{2}}\right)\,{\rm d}^{3}{\bf v}, \tag{4.157}\]

where now \(\sigma_{R}\) and \(\sigma_{z}\) are constants.

The broadest distribution of azimuthal velocities in Figure 4.15 is extremely skew, with a long tail to highly negative values of \(\widetilde{v}_{\phi}\) and a sharp cutoff for \(\widetilde{v}_{\phi}>0\). This asymmetry arises from two effects, both related to the exponential density profiles of stellar disks. Stars near the Sun that have \(\widetilde{v}_{\phi}>0\) have more angular momentum than the lsr and thus have guiding centers at \(R_{\rm g}>R_{0}\), while stars with \(\widetilde{v}_{\phi}<0\) have guiding centers at \(R_{\rm g}<R_{0}\). Since the surface density of stars declines exponentially with \(R\), there are more stars in the latter class than in the former, and the distribution in velocity space therefore extends further towards negative \(\widetilde{v}_{\phi}\) than in the opposite direction. The second effect is that the velocity dispersion \(\sigma_{R}\) declines with \(R\), so the fraction of the stars that are based at \(R_{\rm g}=R_{0}-\delta R\) on eccentric orbits that bring them to the Sun with \(\widetilde{v}_{\phi}<0\) is larger than the fraction of the stars based at \(R_{0}+\delta R\) that can reach the Sun with \(\widetilde{v}_{\phi}>0\). Similarly, there are more Japanese than Nepalese in Oxford in the summer,both because the population of the Japan exceeds that of Nepal, and because Japanese have larger travel budgets than Nepalese.

Figure 16 shows the prediction of equation (156) for the dependence of various averages on the temperature \(\overline{v_{R}^{2}}\) of the stellar population. The full curve in the upper panel shows that as \(\overline{v_{R}^{2}}\) increases, the mean rotation rate of the population falls more and more below the circular speed. This phenomenon is called **asymmetric drift** (BM SS10.3.1). In SS4.8.2a we shall show that for any cool-disk df we have to a good approximation that \(\overline{v_{\phi}-v_{\rm c}}\propto\overline{v_{R}^{2}}\). The dashed curve shows such a parabolic fit to the full curve. In the lower panel the full curve shows the ratio \(\overline{(v_{\phi}-v_{\rm c})^{2}}/\overline{v_{R}^{2}}\), which is predicted by epicycle theory to be \(\gamma^{-2}=0.5\) (eq. 100). This ratio does start at 0.5, but by a radial dispersion of \(10\,{\rm km\,s^{-1}}\) has risen by 10% and by a dispersion of \(20\,{\rm km\,s^{-1}}\), less than 10% of the circular speed, it has risen by nearly 80%. Thus the range of validity of equation (100) is surprisingly narrow. Problems 4.43 and 4.44 help to explain why.

Figure 17 compares the distribution of observed azimuthal velocities for F and G stars near the Sun from Nordstrom et al. (2004) with the prediction of the Schwarzschild df for a population with the same value of \(\overline{v_{R}^{2}}^{1/2}=34\,{\rm km\,s^{-1}}\). The model distribution exaggerates the skewness of the observed distribution and seriously overestimates the number of stars with \(\widetilde{v}_{\phi}\mathrel{\hbox to 0.0pt{\lower 4.0pt\hbox{ $\sim$}} \raise 1.0pt\hbox{$<$}}-60\,{\rm km\,s^{-1}}\). These shortcomings reflect the breakdown of the epicycle approximation for stars on highly eccentric orbits. We can estimate the char

