Figure 4.18 shows the density of F and G stars on four slices through velocity space, corresponding to \(v_{z}=-30\), \(-15\), \(-5\) and \(15\,{\rm km\,s^{-1}}\). At \(v_{z}=-30\,{\rm km\,s^{-1}}\) the equi-density contours are reasonably elliptical, and qualitatively in agreement with the predictions of the Schwarzschild df. At \(v_{z}=-15\,{\rm km\,s^{-1}}\) the third highest contour has a pronounced bulge around a local maximum at \((17,-43)\,{\rm km\,s^{-1}}\). This concentration, which cannot be due to noise because it involves a significant number of stars and is also visible in adjacent slices through velocity space, is qualitatively in conflict with the Schwarzschild df. It is called the **Hercules star stream**(Famaey et al. 2005). A second local maximum is visible at \((30,-12)\,{\rm km\,s^{-1}}\); this is due to the Hyades **star stream** or **moving group**. The panel for \(v_{z}=-5\,{\rm km\,s^{-1}}\) shows a neighboring local maximum at \((6,-20)\,{\rm km\,s^{-1}}\) caused by the Pleiades star stream.

The differences between the structure of Figure 4.18 and that predicted by the Schwarzschild df could have two explanations. The first possibility is that the stars are still dispersing from the associations in which they formed, with the result that the df is still evolving towards a steady state, and does

