## Chapter 5 Stability of Collisionless Systems

### 5.1 Introduction

In this chapter we examine how an equilibrium stellar system responds to external forces. Does it ring like a bell? Does the disturbance grow larger and larger, like the tilt of a pencil that is initially balanced on its point? Or does the disturbance die away? In fact, we shall find that all three types of response can be present in stellar systems.

The stability of a dynamical system is a particular aspect of its response to external forces; loosely speaking, the system is unstable if a small perturbation causes a large response. Obviously, we would not expect to find in nature a system so delicately balanced that the slightest change causes it to evolve rapidly away from its initial state. Hence we should test any proposed stellar-dynamical configuration for stability before using it to model a real stellar system. It turns out that many equilibrium stellar systems _are_ unstable. For example, the simplest model of a disk galaxy consists of a self-gravitating, razor-thin disk in which the stars move on precisely circular orbits, but we shall find that any cold disk, with no random motions, is violently unstable. It appears that a minimum level of random motion is needed to stabilize a self-gravitating disk (eq. 6.71).

Stability analyses can also aid us to interpret observations of astronomical systems. A classic example is furnished by Saturn's rings: Laplace (1829) showed that these could not be rigid bodies, as most astronomers then believed, because a solid ring would be unstable (Problem 5.1). A more recent example concerns the structure of disk galaxies. Dynamical models of low-luminosity disk galaxies often exhibit a fierce non-axisymmetric instability that results in the growth of a large bar-like structure in the central regions. Since these galaxies do not seem to be unstable, we infer that dark matter probably dominates the gravitational forces throughout the disk (see SS6.3.4).

In Chapter 3 discussed the stability of orbits in a variety of fixed gravitational potentials. The instabilities that we shall encounter here are of a different type. They are caused by cooperative or collective effects, in which a density perturbation gives rise to extra gravitational forces, which deflect the stellar orbits in such a way that the original density perturbation is enhanced.

The response of a galactic disk is strongly influenced by differential rotation--the variation of angular speed with distance from the galactic center. Differential rotation shears out disk disturbances since the material at each radius is carried around the galaxy at a different rate. We shall defer this complication to Chapter 6, which is devoted to the dynamics of differentially rotating disks and the origin of spiral structure in galaxies. In this chapter we focus on static and uniformly rotating stellar systems.

Our task is made easier by the similarities between stellar systems and two other kinds of systems:

(i) _Self-gravitating fluids_. As we saw in Chapter 4, many equilibrium stellar systems have close fluid analogs. The analogies arise because a fluid system is supported against gravity by gradients in the scalar pressure \(p\), while a stellar system is supported by gradients in the stress tensor \(-\nu\sigma_{ij}^{2}\) (see the discussion after eq. 4.209). Similarly, in this chapter we shall find it useful to draw analogies between the responses of self-gravitating fluid and stellar systems. A fluid system resists the gravitational collapse of a local density enhancement through pressure gradients, while a stellar system resists collapse because the spread in stellar velocities at every point tends to disperse any density enhancement before it has time to grow. Since the fluid systems are simpler, and important in their own right, we shall analyze fluid systems alongside the analogous stellar-dynamical ones. (ii) _Electrostatic plasmas_. Rarefied plasmas share with collisionless stellar systems the property that the mean field of the system is more important than forces from nearby particles (SS1.2). Hence many of the techniques of plasma physics can be used in stellar dynamics. However, there is a fundamental difference: plasmas have both positive and negative charges, so they are neutral on large scales and can form static homogeneous equilibria. By contrast, gravity is always an attractive force,so equilibrium gravitating systems _must_ be inhomogeneous. This essential inhomogeneity of self-gravitating systems complicates the study of their stability.

In the remainder of this section we develop the machinery of linear response theory and linear perturbation theory that we shall use to analyze the stability and response of fluid and stellar systems. In SS5.2 we examine the response of infinite, homogeneous, self-gravitating systems--this case is artificial but illuminates much of the behavior of more realistic fluid and stellar systems. In SS5.3 we outline the methods used to evaluate the response of general stellar systems; in SS5.4 we describe the energy principles that constrain the behavior of stellar systems, and in SS5.5 we apply these tools to spherical systems. Finally, in SS5.6 we examine the novel behavior seen in uniformly rotating stellar systems, as an introduction to the analysis of differentially rotating systems in Chapter 6.

#### Linear response theory

Some insight into the linear response of stellar or fluid systems can be achieved before actually solving the equations of motion. Let us examine an equilibrium system with density \(\rho_{\rm s}({\bf x})\) that is forced by an external gravitational field \(-\epsilon{\mathbf{\nabla}}\Phi_{\rm e}\), where \(|{\mathbf{\nabla}}\Phi_{\rm e}|\) is of the same order as the gravitational field in the equilibrium system, and \(\epsilon\ll 1\). The density distribution that would generate this field is \(\epsilon\rho_{\rm e}({\bf x},t)\) where

\[\nabla^{2}\Phi_{\rm e}=4\pi G\rho_{\rm e}. \tag{5.1}\]

Because the perturbation is weak, the response is linear and therefore also proportional to \(\epsilon\); thus we may write the induced density perturbation in the system as \(\epsilon\rho_{\rm s1}({\bf x},t)\). The **response function**\(R({\bf x},{\bf x}^{\prime},\tau)\) defined by

\[\rho_{\rm s1}({\bf x},t)=\int{\rm d}^{3}{\bf x}^{\prime}{\rm d}t^{\prime}\,R({ \bf x},{\bf x}^{\prime},t-t^{\prime})\rho_{\rm e}({\bf x}^{\prime},t^{\prime}) \tag{5.2}\]

relates the response density \(\rho_{\rm s1}({\bf x},t)\) to the forcing density \(\rho_{\rm e}({\bf x}^{\prime},t^{\prime})\). The response depends only on the difference between \(t\) and \(t^{\prime}\) because the equilibrium system is time-independent, so the response at time \(t\) from an instantaneous impulse at \(t^{\prime}\) can depend only on the lag \(t-t^{\prime}\). Causality requires that \(R({\bf x},{\bf x}^{\prime},\tau)=0\) for \(\tau<0\) (the effect cannot precede the cause).

Both the forcing density \(\rho_{\rm e}\) and the response density \(\rho_{\rm s1}\) contribute to the gravitational potential, and it is the _total_ perturbing potential \(\Phi_{1}\equiv\Phi_{\rm e}+\Phi_{\rm s1}\) that determines the dynamics of the system. The corresponding density is \(\rho_{1}=\rho_{\rm e}+\rho_{\rm s1}\). The **polarization function**\(P({\bf x},{\bf x}^{\prime},\tau)\) relates the response density \(\rho_{\rm s1}\) to the total density \(\rho_{1}\):

\[\rho_{\rm s1}({\bf x},t)=\int{\rm d}^{3}{\bf x}^{\prime}{\rm d}t^{\prime}\,P({ \bf x},{\bf x}^{\prime},t-t^{\prime})\rho_{1}({\bf x}^{\prime},t^{\prime}). \tag{5.3}\]Once again, \(P({\bf x},{\bf x}^{\prime},\tau)=0\) for \(\tau<0\).

It is important to understand the physical difference between the response and polarization functions. The response function describes the density response to an external perturbing force, while the polarization function describes the density response to a total perturbing force, which includes any contributions from the self-gravity of the density response. If the self-gravity of the density response is negligible, then the polarization and response functions are identical.1

Footnote 1: The term “polarization function” suggests an analogy with the electrostatics of macroscopic media (e.g., Jackson 1999). In a homogeneous medium, the electric field \({\bf E}\), macroscopic charge density \(\rho\), polarization \({\bf P}\), and displacement \({\bf D}\) are related by

\[{\bf\nabla}\cdot{\bf D}=\rho\quad;\quad{\bf P}=\epsilon_{0}\chi{\bf E}\quad; \quad{\bf D}=\epsilon_{0}{\bf E}+{\bf P}=\epsilon{\bf E}\quad;\quad\frac{ \epsilon}{\epsilon_{0}}=1+\chi, \tag{5.4}\]

 where \(\epsilon_{0}\) is the electric constant (permittivity of the vacuum), and \(\epsilon/\epsilon_{0}\) and \(\chi\) are the dielectric constant and susceptibility of the medium. Here \({\bf D}\) arises from unbound charges and hence is analogous to the external or forcing density \(\rho_{\rm e}\); \(\epsilon_{0}{\bf E}\) represents the total field and hence is analogous to \(\rho_{1}\), and \(\epsilon_{0}{\bf E}-{\bf D}=-{\bf P}\) represents the field arising from the response of the medium and is analogous to \(\rho_{\rm s1}\). For a uniform, time-independent gravitational field in a homogeneous medium, the polarization and response functions are scalars, so \(\rho_{\rm s1}=P\rho_{1}=R\rho_{\rm e}\). Thus the analogy implies that

\[P=\frac{\rho_{\rm s1}}{\rho_{1}}\Leftrightarrow\frac{-{\bf P}}{\epsilon_{0}{ \bf E}}=-\chi\quad;\quad R=\frac{\rho_{\rm s1}}{\rho_{\rm e}}\Leftrightarrow \frac{-{\bf P}}{{\bf D}}=-\frac{\epsilon_{0}\chi}{\epsilon}=-\frac{\chi}{1+ \chi}. \tag{5.5}\]

The minus sign in the analogy \(P\Leftrightarrow-\chi\) is significant: in a dielectric the movement of bound charges cancels the imposed field \({\bf D}\), whereas in a gravitational system polarization tends to enhance the applied field.

#### 5.1.2 Linearized equations for stellar and fluid systems

The dynamics of self-gravitating collisionless stellar systems is described by the collisionless Boltzmann equation (4.7),

\[\frac{\partial f}{\partial t}+[f,H]=\frac{\partial f}{\partial t}+{\bf v}\cdot \frac{\partial f}{\partial{\bf x}}-\frac{\partial\Phi}{\partial{\bf x}}\cdot \frac{\partial f}{\partial{\bf v}}=0, \tag{5.8}\]

and Poisson's equation (2.10),

\[\nabla^{2}\Phi_{\rm s}({\bf x},t)=4\pi G\int{\rm d}^{3}{\bf v}\,f({\bf x},{ \bf v},t). \tag{5.9}\]

In these equations \(\Phi({\bf x},t)\) is the total potential, \(H=\frac{1}{2}v^{2}+\Phi({\bf x},t)\) is the Hamiltonian, \([\cdot,\cdot]\) is the Poisson bracket, and \(\Phi_{\rm s}({\bf x},t)\) is the gravitational potential of the stellar system itself, which may differ from the total potential \(\Phi({\bf x},t)\) if there are external forces. Here and throughout this chapter, the df\(f({\bf x},{\bf v},t)\) is defined to be the mass density of stars in phase space, as opposed to the definition in terms of probability density used in SS4.1.

An isolated equilibrium stellar system is described by a time-independent df\(f_{0}({\bf x},{\bf v})\) and potential \(\Phi_{0}({\bf x})\) that are solutions of (5.8) and (5.9),

\[[f_{0},H_{0}]=0\quad;\quad\nabla^{2}\Phi_{0}=4\pi G\int{\rm d}^{3}{\bf v}\,f_{ 0}, \tag{5.10}\]

where \(H_{0}=\frac{1}{2}v^{2}+\Phi_{0}({\bf x})\).

Now imagine that this equilibrium system is subjected to a weak gravitational force arising from some external potential \(\epsilon\Phi_{\rm e}({\bf x},t)\), where \(|{\boldsymbol{\nabla}}\Phi_{\rm e}|\) is of order \(|{\boldsymbol{\nabla}}\Phi_{0}|\) and \(\epsilon\ll 1\). In response to this disturbance, the df of the stellar system and the gravitational potential arising from its stars are modified to

\[f({\bf x},{\bf v},t)=f_{0}({\bf x},{\bf v})+\epsilon f_{1}({\bf x},{\bf v},t) \quad;\quad\Phi_{\rm s}({\bf x},t)=\Phi_{0}({\bf x})+\epsilon\Phi_{\rm s1}({ \bf x},t), \tag{5.11}\]

and the total gravitational potential becomes

\[\Phi({\bf x},t)=\Phi_{0}({\bf x},t)+\epsilon\Phi_{1}({\bf x},t),\quad{\rm where }\quad\Phi_{1}({\bf x},t)=\Phi_{\rm s1}({\bf x},t)+\Phi_{\rm e}({\bf x},t). \tag{5.12}\]

Hence the Hamiltonian in equation (5.8) becomes \(H=H_{0}+\epsilon\Phi_{1}\). Substituting these results into equations (5.8) and (5.9), we find that the terms that are independent of \(\epsilon\) vanish by virtue of (5.10). Dropping the terms proportional to \(\epsilon^{2}\) since \(\epsilon\ll 1\), we have

\[\frac{\partial f_{1}}{\partial t}+[f_{1},H_{0}]+[f_{0},\Phi_{1}]=0\quad;\quad \nabla^{2}\Phi_{\rm s1}=4\pi G\int{\rm d}^{3}{\bf v}\,f_{1}. \tag{5.13}\]The first of these equations is called the **linearized collisionless Boltzmann equation**; the second is simply Poisson's equation (5.9), except that it relates \(\Phi_{\rm s1}\) and \(f_{1}\), instead of \(\Phi_{\rm s}\) and \(f\). Much of this chapter is devoted to analyzing solutions of these equations.

Equation (4.9) states that

\[{{\rm d}f_{1}\over{\rm d}t}\equiv{\partial f_{1}\over\partial t}+[f_{1},H_{0}]\]

is the rate of change of \(f_{1}\) as seen by an observer moving through phase space along the unperturbed orbit. Hence the first two terms in the first of equations (5.13) can be replaced by \({\rm d}f/{\rm d}t\). Integrating the equation we obtain

\[f_{1}({\bf x},{\bf v},t)=-\int_{-\infty}^{t}{\rm d}t^{\prime}\,[f_{0},\Phi_{1}] _{{\bf x}(t^{\prime}),{\bf v}(t^{\prime}),t^{\prime}}\,\]

where the Poisson bracket is evaluated along the unperturbed orbit \({\bf x}(t^{\prime}),{\bf v}(t^{\prime})\) that reaches \({\bf x},{\bf v}\) at time \(t\).

We shall examine the response of fluid systems in parallel with stellar systems. Therefore we now consider the fluid-dynamical analogs of equations (5.13). A fluid system with density \(\rho_{\rm s}({\bf x},t)\), pressure \(p({\bf x},t)\), and velocity \({\bf v}({\bf x},t)\) in a potential \(\Phi({\bf x},t)\) obeys the continuity equation (F.3),

\[{\partial\rho_{\rm s}\over\partial t}+{\bf\nabla}\cdot(\rho_{\rm s}{\bf v})=0,\]

Euler's equation (F.10),

\[{\partial{\bf v}\over\partial t}+({\bf v}\cdot{\bf\nabla}){\bf v}=-{1\over \rho_{\rm s}}{\bf\nabla}p-{\bf\nabla}\Phi,\]

and Poisson's equation,

\[{\bf\nabla}^{2}\Phi_{\rm s}=4\pi G\rho_{\rm s},\quad{\rm where}\quad\Phi=\Phi _{\rm s}+\epsilon\Phi_{\rm e}\]

is the sum of the potentials arising from the fluid and the forcing mass distribution.

We must also specify the equation of state relating \(p\) and \(\rho\). For our purposes it is sufficient to use a simple equation of state. Thus, we will consider barotropic fluids (eq. F.27), in which the pressure is a function of the density only:

\[p({\bf x},t)=p[\rho_{\rm s}({\bf x},t)].\]

When the equation of state is barotropic, Euler's equation (5.17) can be replaced by

\[{\partial{\bf v}\over\partial t}+({\bf v}\cdot{\bf\nabla}){\bf v}=-{\bf\nabla }(h+\Phi),\]where the specific enthalpy is defined by (eq. F.29)

\[h(\rho_{\rm s})\equiv\int_{0}^{\rho_{\rm s}}\frac{{\rm d}p(\rho)}{\rho}. \tag{5.21}\]

In the absence of self-gravity, weak short-wavelength disturbances propagate through a barotropic fluid at the sound speed \(v_{\rm s}({\bf x})\) (eq. F.50), defined by

\[v_{\rm s}^{2}({\bf x})\equiv\left[\frac{{\rm d}p(\rho)}{{\rm d}\rho}\right]_{ \rho_{0}({\bf x})}. \tag{5.22}\]

An isolated equilibrium fluid is described by the time-independent density, enthalpy, velocity, and potential distributions \(\rho_{0}({\bf x})\), \(h_{0}({\bf x})\), \({\bf v}_{0}({\bf x})\), and \(\Phi_{0}({\bf x})\), which are solutions of equations (5.16)-(5.20). Then we consider the response of the fluid to a weak external potential \(\epsilon\Phi_{\rm e}({\bf x},t)\):

\[\begin{array}{lcl}\rho_{\rm s}({\bf x},t)=\rho_{0}({\bf x})+\epsilon\rho_{ \rm s1}({\bf x},t)&;&h({\bf x},t)=h_{0}({\bf x})+\epsilon h_{1}({\bf x},t),\\ {\bf v}({\bf x},t)={\bf v}_{0}({\bf x})+\epsilon{\bf v}_{1}({\bf x},t)&;&\Phi ({\bf x},t)=\Phi_{0}({\bf x})+\epsilon\Phi_{1}({\bf x},t).\end{array} \tag{5.23}\]

Here, as before, \(\Phi_{1}=\Phi_{\rm s1}+\Phi_{\rm e}\) is the total perturbation in the gravitational potential, the sum of the external potential \(\Phi_{\rm e}\) and the potential \(\Phi_{\rm s1}\) arising from the density perturbation \(\rho_{\rm s1}\). Substituting equations (5.23) into the fluid equations, we find that the terms that are independent of \(\epsilon\) sum to zero, and discarding terms proportional to \(\epsilon^{2}\), we obtain

\[\frac{\partial\rho_{\rm s1}}{\partial t}+\mathbf{\nabla}\cdot(\rho_{ 0}{\bf v}_{1})+\mathbf{\nabla}\cdot(\rho_{\rm s1}{\bf v}_{0})=0, \tag{5.24a}\] \[\frac{\partial{\bf v}_{1}}{\partial t}+({\bf v}_{0}\cdot\mathbf{\nabla$ }){\bf v}_{1}+({\bf v}_{1}\cdot\mbox{\boldmath$\nabla}){\bf v}_{0}=-\mathbf{\nabla}(h_{1}+\Phi_{\rm s1}+\Phi_{\rm e}),\] (5.24b) \[\nabla^{2}\Phi_{\rm s1}=4\pi G\rho_{\rm s1},\] (5.24c) \[h_{1}=\frac{p_{1}}{\rho_{0}}=\left(\frac{{\rm d}p}{{\rm d}\rho}\right)_{\rho_ {0}}\frac{\rho_{\rm s1}}{\rho_{0}}=v_{\rm s}^{2}\frac{\rho_{\rm s1}}{\rho_{0}}. \tag{5.24d}\]

Equations (5.24a) to (5.24d) constitute a complete set of linear equations that govern the response of a barotropic fluid to small perturbations.

## 5.2 The response of homogeneous systems

### 5.2.1 Physical basis of the Jeans instability

Consider a fluid of density \(\rho_{0}\) and pressure \(p_{0}\), with no internal motions so \({\bf v}_{0}=0\). Now draw a sphere of radius \(r\) around any point and suppose that we compress this spherical region by reducing its radius to \((1-\alpha)r\), where \(\alpha\ll 1\). We will deal only with order-of-magnitude arguments in this subsection, so the details of how the fluid is compressed and the exact shape of the perturbed density distribution are not important. To order of magnitude, the density perturbation is \(\rho_{1}\approx\alpha\rho_{0}\), and the pressure perturbation is \(p_{1}\approx({\rm d}p/{\rm d}\rho)_{0}\alpha\rho_{0}=\alpha v_{\rm s}^{2}\rho_ {0}\). The pressure force per unit mass is \({\bf F}_{p}=-{\bf\nabla}p/\rho\), and our compressive perturbation therefore leads to an additional outward pressure force \({\bf F}_{p1}\), where \(|{\bf F}_{p1}|\approx p_{1}/(\rho_{0}r)\approx\alpha v_{\rm s}^{2}/r\), where we have replaced the gradient \({\bf\nabla}\) by \(1/r\). Similarly, the enhanced density of the perturbation gives rise to an extra inward gravitational force \(|{\bf F}_{g1}|\approx GM\alpha/r^{2}\), where \(M=\frac{4}{3}\pi\rho_{0}r^{3}\) is the mass originally within \(r\); in other words, \(|{\bf F}_{g1}|\approx G\rho_{0}r\alpha\). If the net force is outward, the compressed fluid re-expands and the perturbation is stable; if the net force is inward, the fluid continues to contract and the perturbation is unstable. Thus, there is instability if \(|{\bf F}_{g1}|>|{\bf F}_{p1}|\), or if

\[G\rho_{0}r\alpha\gtrsim\alpha v_{\rm s}^{2}/r,\quad\mbox{that is, if}\quad r^{2} \gtrsim\frac{v_{\rm s}^{2}}{G\rho_{0}}. \tag{5.25}\]

The same approximate criterion can be derived by comparing the gravitational potential energy and internal energy in a volume \(V\) of size \(r\) or mass \(M\approx\rho_{0}r^{3}\). The potential energy is \(W\approx-GM^{2}/r\approx-G\rho_{0}^{2}r^{5}\), and the internal energy is \(U\approx Mv_{\rm s}^{2}\approx\rho_{0}v_{\rm s}^{2}r^{3}\). If the perturbation is localized within \(V\), and there is no energy flow into \(V\), then the sum of these two energies plus the bulk kinetic energy associated with the perturbation must be zero. Since the kinetic energy is positive, the perturbation can grow only if \(W+U<0\). This requires \(r^{2}\gtrsim v_{\rm s}^{2}/(G\rho_{0})\).

We conclude that _perturbations with a scale longer than \(\approx v_{\rm s}/(G\rho_{0})^{1/2}\) are unstable_. This behavior is known as the **Jeans instability** (Jeans 1902). We now give a more careful treatment for homogeneous fluids and stellar systems; our goal is both to analyze the Jeans instability and to sharpen our tools for investigating the response of more realistic systems.

### 5.2.2 Homogeneous systems and the Jeans swindle

As we mentioned in SS5.1, an infinite homogeneous gravitating system cannot be in static equilibrium. Nevertheless, it is useful to set up artificial homogeneous systems because their linear stability properties are relatively easy to analyze. Consider, for example, the response function \(R({\bf x},{\bf x}^{\prime},\tau)\) definedin equation (5.2). In a homogeneous system this can depend on \({\bf x}\), \({\bf x}^{\prime}\) only through their difference, so the response function takes the form \(R({\bf x}-{\bf x}^{\prime},\tau)\), and equation (5.2) simplifies to a convolution in both space and time.

We showed in SS5.1.1 that equations involving convolutions in time can be analyzed with temporal Fourier transforms. Similarly, equations involving convolution in space are best analyzed using spatial Fourier transforms. We define the spatial Fourier transform of the function \(g({\bf x})\) by (eq. B.68)

\[\overline{g}({\bf k})=\int{\rm d}^{3}{\bf x}\,g({\bf x}){\rm e}^{-{\rm i}{\bf k }\cdot{\bf x}}\quad;\quad g({\bf x})=\int\frac{{\rm d}^{3}{\bf k}}{(2\pi)^{3}} \,\overline{g}({\bf k}){\rm e}^{{\rm i}{\bf k}\cdot{\bf x}}. \tag{5.26}\]

We shall assume that all relevant functions vanish at spatial infinity so their Fourier transforms exist.

We replace \(R({\bf x},{\bf x}^{\prime},t)\) in equation (5.2) by \(R({\bf x}-{\bf x}^{\prime},t)\), with a similar replacement in equation (5.3), multiply both equations by \(\exp(-{\rm i}{\bf k}\cdot{\bf x})\) and integrate over \({\rm d}^{3}{\bf x}\), to find

\[\overline{\rho}_{\rm s1}({\bf k},t)=\int{\rm d}t^{\prime}\,\overline{R}({\bf k },t-t^{\prime})\overline{\rho}_{\rm e}({\bf k},t^{\prime})=\int{\rm d}t^{ \prime}\,\overline{P}({\bf k},t-t^{\prime})\overline{\rho}_{1}({\bf k},t^{ \prime}). \tag{5.27}\]

Taking a temporal Fourier transform, we obtain:

\[\widetilde{\overline{\rho}}_{\rm s1}({\bf k},\omega)=\widetilde{\overline{R}} ({\bf k},\omega)\widetilde{\overline{\rho}}_{\rm e}({\bf k},\omega)= \widetilde{\overline{P}}({\bf k},\omega)\widetilde{\overline{\rho}}_{1}({\bf k },\omega). \tag{5.28}\]

There is a simple relation between the polarization and response functions for homogeneous systems. Using the relation \(\rho_{1}=\rho_{\rm s1}+\rho_{\rm e}\) (cf. eq. 5.12), equation (5.28) can be rewritten as

\[\widetilde{\overline{\rho}}_{\rm s1}({\bf k},\omega)=\widetilde{\overline{R} }({\bf k},\omega)\widetilde{\overline{\rho}}_{\rm e}({\bf k},\omega)= \widetilde{\overline{P}}({\bf k},\omega)[\widetilde{\overline{\rho}}_{\rm s1 }({\bf k},\omega)+\widetilde{\overline{\rho}}_{\rm e}({\bf k},\omega)]. \tag{5.29}\]

Combining these equations gives the desired relations:

\[\widetilde{\overline{R}}({\bf k},\omega)=\frac{\widetilde{\overline{P}}({\bf k },\omega)}{1-\widetilde{\overline{P}}({\bf k},\omega)}\quad;\quad\widetilde{ \overline{P}}({\bf k},\omega)=\frac{\widetilde{\overline{R}}({\bf k},\omega)} {1+\widetilde{\overline{R}}({\bf k},\omega)}. \tag{5.30}\]

We construct our artificial equilibrium for a homogeneous system by perpetrating what we shall call the **Jeans swindle**. Mathematically, the difficulty we must overcome is that if the density and pressure of the medium \(\rho_{0}\), \(p_{0}\) are constant, and the mean velocity \({\bf v}_{0}\) is zero, then Euler's equation (5.17) implies that \(\boldsymbol{\nabla}\Phi_{0}=0\). The same conclusion follows from symmetry considerations--since the system is homogeneous, there is no preferred direction for the vector \(\boldsymbol{\nabla}\Phi_{0}\) to point. On the other hand, Poisson's equation (5.18) requires that \(\nabla^{2}\Phi_{0}=4\pi G\rho_{0}\). These two requirements are inconsistent unless \(\rho_{0}=0\). Physically, there are no pressure gradients in a homogeneous medium to balance gravitational attraction. A similar inconsistency arises in an infinite homogeneous stellar system. We remove the inconsistency by the _ad hoc_ assumption that Poisson's equation describes only the relation between the _perturbed_ density and the _perturbed_ potential, while the unperturbed potential is zero. An equivalent statement is that some fixed gravitational field from an unspecified source cancels \(\mathbf{\nabla}\Phi_{0}\). This assumption constitutes the Jeans swindle; it is a swindle, of course, because there is no justification for discarding the unperturbed gravitational field. However, the swindle is vindicated by the insight it provides, so long as its limitations are kept in mind--for further discussion see SS5.2.5.2

Footnote 2: The Jeans swindle is not needed in one (unrealistic) cosmological model. This is the static model proposed, and later repudiated, by Einstein, in which the gravitational attraction of the unperturbed density \(\rho_{0}\) is canceled by vacuum energy \(\rho_{\rm A}\). See Problem 1.8.

#### The response of a homogeneous fluid system

We use the linearized fluid equations (5.24a) to (5.24d). The equilibrium state is \(\rho_{0}=\)_constant_, \({\bf v}_{0}=0\), and the Jeans swindle lets us set \(\Phi_{0}=0\). We then have

\[{\partial\rho_{\rm s1}\over\partial t}+\rho_{0}\mathbf{ \nabla}\cdot{\bf v}_{1}=0 \quad;\quad{\partial{\bf v}_{1}\over\partial t}=-\mathbf{\nabla}(h_{ 1}+\Phi_{\rm s1}+\Phi_{\rm e}), \tag{5.31a}\] \[\nabla^{2}\Phi_{\rm s1}=4\pi G\rho_{\rm s1}\quad;\quad h_{1}=v_{ \rm s}^{2}\rho_{\rm s1}/\rho_{0}, \tag{5.31b}\]

and the sound speed \(v_{\rm s}\) is a constant.

By taking the time derivative of the first of equations (5.31) and the divergence of the second, and eliminating \({\bf v}_{1}\), \(\Phi_{\rm s1}\), and \(h_{1}\) with the aid of the other equations, we can combine equations (5.31) into the single equation

\[{\partial^{2}\rho_{\rm s1}\over\partial t^{2}}-v_{\rm s}^{2}\nabla^{2}\rho_{ \rm s1}-4\pi G\rho_{0}\rho_{\rm s1}=4\pi G\rho_{0}\rho_{\rm e}. \tag{5.32}\]

We now take the spatial Fourier transform by multiplying by \(\exp(-{\rm i}{\bf k}\cdot{\bf x})\) and integrating over \({\rm d}^{3}{\bf x}\). We then apply the divergence theorem (B.45) twice to eliminate \(\nabla^{2}\), using the fact that the boundary terms are zero since all perturbed quantities are assumed to vanish at spatial infinity. We obtain

\[{\partial^{2}\overline{\rho}_{\rm s1}({\bf k},t)\over\partial t^{2}}+v_{\rm s }^{2}k^{2}\,\overline{\rho}_{\rm s1}({\bf k},t)-4\pi G\rho_{0}\overline{\rho}_ {\rm s1}({\bf k},t)=4\pi G\rho_{0}\overline{\rho}_{\rm e}({\bf k},t). \tag{5.33}\]

A **mode** is a perturbation that can be sustained without external forces. Setting \(\overline{\rho}_{\rm e}({\bf k},t)=0\) and substituting \(\overline{\rho}_{\rm s1}({\bf k},t)\propto\exp(-{\rm i}\omega t)\) into equation (5.33), we find that modes must satisfy the dispersion relation

\[\omega^{2}=\omega_{0}^{2}(k)\equiv v_{\rm s}^{2}k^{2}-4\pi G\rho_{0}=v_{\rm s} ^{2}(k^{2}-k_{\rm J}^{2}), \tag{5.34}\]where the **Jeans wavenumber**\(k_{\rm J}\) is defined by

\[k_{\rm J}^{2}\equiv\frac{4\pi G\rho_{0}}{v_{\rm s}^{2}}. \tag{5.35}\]

For \(k>k_{\rm J}\) (short wavelengths) \(\omega^{2}>0\) and the solutions are oscillatory; for \(k<k_{\rm J}\) (long wavelengths) the solutions are exponentially growing or decaying.

Now suppose that the external density is given by \(\overline{\rho}_{\rm e}({\bf k},t)=\delta(t-t_{0})\), where as usual \(\delta(\tau)\) is the delta function (Appendix C.1). Then equation (5.27) implies that \(\overline{\rho}_{\rm s1}({\bf k},t)\) is equal to the response function \(\overline{R}({\bf k},t-t_{0})\); substituting this result into equation (5.33) yields

\[\frac{\partial^{2}\overline{R}({\bf k},\tau)}{\partial\tau^{2}}+v_{\rm s}^{2} k^{2}\,\overline{R}({\bf k},\tau)-4\pi G\rho_{0}\overline{R}({\bf k},\tau)=4\pi G \rho_{0}\,\delta(\tau), \tag{5.36}\]

subject to the causality condition \(\overline{R}({\bf k},\tau)=0\) for \(\tau<0\). We now solve this to determine the response function.

First consider the case of an oscillatory perturbation (\(k>k_{\rm J}\)). For \(\tau>0\) the external potential is zero, so \(\overline{R}({\bf k},\tau)\) must satisfy the dispersion relation (5.34),

\[\overline{R}({\bf k},\tau)=A\sin[\omega_{0}(k)\tau]+B\cos[\omega_{0}(k)\tau], \tag{5.37}\]

where \(A\) and \(B\) are constants chosen to satisfy the boundary conditions at \(\tau=0\). The response function must be a continuous function of \(\tau\), since the response density cannot change discontinuously even if subjected to an impulse from an external source. Since the response function is zero for \(\tau<0\), we must have \(B=0\). Now integrate equation (5.36) from \(\tau=-\epsilon\) to \(\tau=\epsilon\), where \(\epsilon\) is a small positive number. Since the response function is continuous, the second and third terms on the left side vanish as \(\epsilon\to 0\). Since \(\int_{-\epsilon}^{\epsilon}{\rm d}\tau\,\delta(\tau)=1\) we have

\[\left.\frac{\partial\overline{R}({\bf k},\tau)}{\partial\tau}\right|_{- \epsilon}^{\epsilon}=4\pi G\rho_{0}, \tag{5.38}\]

which requires \(A\omega_{0}=4\pi G\rho_{0}\). Thus the response function for \(k>k_{\rm J}\) is

\[\overline{R}({\bf k},\tau)=\frac{4\pi G\rho_{0}}{\omega_{0}(k)}H(\tau)\sin[ \omega_{0}(k)\tau] \tag{5.39}\]

where \(H(\tau)\) is the step function (Appendix C.1). For \(k<k_{\rm J}\), \(\omega_{0}(k)\) is purely imaginary, so it proves more convenient to use

\[\gamma_{0}^{2}(k)\equiv-\omega_{0}^{2}(k)=v_{\rm s}^{2}(k_{\rm J}^{2}-k^{2}), \tag{5.40}\]and a similar derivation shows that the response function is

\[\overline{R}({\bf k},\tau)=\frac{4\pi G\rho_{0}}{\gamma_{0}(k)}H(\tau)\sinh[ \gamma_{0}(k)\tau]. \tag{5.41}\]

Taking the temporal Fourier transform (5.6) of our results we obtain

\[\widetilde{\overline{R}}({\bf k},\omega)=\left\{\begin{array}{ll}-\frac{4 \pi G\rho_{0}}{\omega^{2}-\omega_{0}^{2}(k)},&k>k_{\rm J},\;{\rm Im}(\omega)>0,\\ -\frac{4\pi G\rho_{0}}{\omega^{2}+\gamma_{0}^{2}(k)},&k<k_{\rm J}, \;{\rm Im}(\omega)>\gamma_{0}(k).\end{array}\right. \tag{5.42}\]

The polarization function for a homogeneous system can be obtained by a closely analogous derivation (Problem 5.6). However, there is a simpler route: the general relation (5.30) between the polarization and response functions in homogeneous systems gives

\[\widetilde{\overline{P}}({\bf k},\omega)=-\frac{4\pi G\rho_{0}}{\omega^{2}-v_ {\rm s}^{2}k^{2}},\qquad{\rm Im}(\omega)>0, \tag{5.43}\]

and the inverse Fourier transform gives

\[\overline{P}({\bf k},\tau)=\frac{4\pi G\rho_{0}}{v_{\rm s}k}H(\tau)\sin(v_{ \rm s}k\tau). \tag{5.44}\]

This result can also be derived by considering equation (5.39) in the limit that self-gravity is negligible, which occurs when \(k_{\rm J}/k\to 0\). As we argued in the paragraph following equation (5.3), if self-gravity is negligible the polarization and response functions are identical. As \(k_{\rm J}\to 0\), \(\omega_{0}(k)\to v_{\rm s}k\), so (5.44) follows from (5.39).

The dispersion relation for modes, equation (5.34), can be obtained by setting the external density \(\rho_{\rm e}\) in equation (5.29) to zero; thus

\[\widetilde{\overline{P}}({\bf k},\omega)=1. \tag{5.45}\]

The solutions of the dispersion relation are the singularities of the response function (5.30), since a mode has non-zero density response even though there is no external forcing.

We now describe these results in more physical terms. Equation (5.39) shows that an external impulse with wavenumber \(k>k_{\rm J}\) sets up a sinusoidal oscillation in the fluid, with frequency \(\omega_{0}(k)\). When the density \(\rho_{0}\) is small, the oscillation consists of sound waves, since the dispersion relation (5.34) reduces to that of a sound wave, \(\omega^{2}(k)=v_{\rm s}^{2}k^{2}\) (eq. F.54). As the density is increased, the frequency \(\omega\) decreases and the oscillation becomes more and more sluggish, until eventually \(\omega\) reaches zero at \(k=k_{\rm J}\). When \(k<k_{\rm J}\)the external impulse excites a growing density disturbance with temporal dependence \(\propto\exp\gamma_{0}(k)t\) at large time (eq. 5.40). The presence of the growing solution implies that the system is unstable whenever \(k^{2}<k_{\rm J}^{2}\).

In terms of the wavelength, the perturbation is unstable if \(\lambda\) exceeds the **Jeans length**\(\lambda_{\rm J}=2\pi/k_{\rm J}\), that is, if3

Footnote 3: The dispersion relation (5.34) is similar to the dispersion relation for electrostatic plasma waves, \(\omega_{0}^{2}(k)=v_{\rm s}^{2}k^{2}+\omega_{\rm P}^{2}\), where \(\omega_{\rm P}^{2}=ne^{2}/(\epsilon_{0}m)\) is the square of the plasma frequency, \(\epsilon_{0}\) is the electric constant, \(n\) is the electron number density and \(e\) and \(m\) are the electron charge and mass. In a plasma like charges repel, so the dispersion relation involves \(+\omega_{\rm P}^{2}\) instead of \(-k_{\rm J}^{2}v_{\rm s}^{2}\) and all wavelengths are stable.

\[\lambda^{2}>\lambda_{\rm J}^{2}=\frac{\pi v_{\rm s}^{2}}{G\rho_{0}}. \tag{5.46}\]

We define the **Jeans mass**\(M_{\rm J}\) as the mass originally contained within a sphere of diameter \(\lambda_{\rm J}\):

\[M_{\rm J}=\frac{4\pi}{3}\rho_{0}({\frac{1}{2}}\lambda_{\rm J})^{3}=2.92 \frac{v_{\rm s}^{3}}{G^{3/2}\rho_{0}^{1/2}}. \tag{5.47}\]

The Jeans instability in a fluid has a simple interpretation in terms of energy. The energy density of an ordinary sound wave is positive. However, the gravitational energy density of a sound wave is negative, because the enhanced potential energy in the compressed regions outweighs the reduced potential energy in the dilated regions. The Jeans instability sets in at the wavelength \(\lambda_{\rm J}\) at which the net energy density becomes negative, so kinetic energy is available to feed the growing wave (see Problem 5.7).

#### The response of a homogeneous stellar system

The equilibrium state of an infinite, homogeneous stellar system is described by the df\(f_{0}({\bf v})\), which is taken here to describe the mass density of stars in \(({\bf x},{\bf v})\) phase space. We shall usually assume that this df is Maxwellian,

\[f_{0}({\bf v})=\frac{\rho_{0}}{(2\pi\sigma^{2})^{3/2}}{\rm e}^{-v^{2}/(2\sigma ^{2})}. \tag{5.48}\]

Since the sound speed \(v_{\rm s}\) in an ideal gas is closely related to its velocity dispersion (see eq. F.55), it is natural to define the Jeans wavenumber \(k_{\rm J}\) of a homogeneous, Maxwellian stellar system by analogy with equation (5.35),

\[k_{\rm J}^{2}\equiv\frac{4\pi G\rho_{0}}{\sigma^{2}}. \tag{5.49}\]

We show below that this analogy is exact, in that perturbations to the stellar system with wavelength \(\lambda<2\pi/k_{\rm J}\) are stable, and perturbations with \(\lambda>2\pi/k_{\rm J}\) are unstable. However, the details of the behavior of fluid and stellar systems are otherwise quite different. In particular, the response of most homogeneous stellar systems to perturbations with \(\lambda<2\pi/k_{\rm J}\) is strongly damped, in contrast to the oscillatory response of fluid systems. This interesting phenomenon of damping in a time-reversible system--apparent dissipation even though the collisionless Boltzmann equation contains no friction, viscosity, or other dissipative effects--is a fundamental but paradoxical property of most stellar systems, which we shall examine with some care.

To analyze the response of the homogeneous stellar system, we use the linearized collisionless Boltzmann and Poisson equations (5.13), and invoke the Jeans swindle to set \(\Phi_{0}=0\). We have

\[\frac{\partial f_{1}}{\partial t}+{\bf v}\cdot\frac{\partial f_{1}}{\partial{ \bf x}}-\frac{\partial}{\partial{\bf x}}(\Phi_{\rm s1}+\Phi_{\rm e})\cdot\frac {\partial f_{0}}{\partial{\bf v}}=0\quad;\quad\nabla^{2}\Phi_{\rm s1}=4\pi G \int{\rm d}^{3}{\bf v}\,f_{1}, \tag{5.50}\]

where as usual \(\Phi_{\rm s1}\) is the perturbed potential due to the self-gravity of the system, and \(\Phi_{\rm e}\) is the perturbing external potential. We multiply these equations by \(\exp(-{\rm i}{\bf k}\cdot{\bf x})\), integrate over \({\rm d}^{3}{\bf x}\), and then use the divergence theorem (B.45) to eliminate spatial derivatives, assuming that \(f_{1}\), \(\Phi_{\rm e}\), and \(\Phi_{\rm s1}\) vanish at infinity. We find

\[\begin{split}\frac{\partial\overline{f}_{1}}{\partial t}+{\rm i}{ \bf k}\cdot{\bf v}\overline{f}_{1}=&\,{\rm i}\,(\overline{\Phi} _{\rm s1}+\overline{\Phi}_{\rm e}){\bf k}\cdot\frac{\partial f_{0}}{\partial{ \bf v}};\\ -k^{2}\overline{\Phi}_{\rm s1}=&\,4\pi G\int{\rm d} ^{3}{\bf v}\,\overline{f}_{1}=4\pi G\,\overline{\rho}_{\rm s1},\end{split} \tag{5.51}\]

where \(\overline{f}_{1}({\bf k},{\bf v},t)\) is the spatial Fourier transform of \(f_{1}({\bf x},{\bf v},t)\), defined as in equation (5.26), and \(\rho_{\rm s1}\) is the perturbed density of the stellar system. The first of equations (5.51) has the integrating factor \(\exp({\rm i}{\bf k}\cdot{\bf v}\,t)\); assuming that the perturbation vanishes as \(t\to-\infty\), its solution is

\[\overline{f}_{1}({\bf k},{\bf v},t)={\rm i}{\bf k}\cdot\frac{\partial f_{0}}{ \partial{\bf v}}\int_{-\infty}^{t}{\rm d}t^{\prime}\,{\rm e}^{{\rm i}{\bf k} \cdot{\bf v}(t^{\prime}-t)}\left[\overline{\Phi}_{\rm s1}({\bf k},t^{\prime}) +\overline{\Phi}_{\rm e}({\bf k},t^{\prime})\right]. \tag{5.52}\]

We now integrate over velocity, replace \(\overline{\Phi}_{\rm s1}+\overline{\Phi}_{\rm e}\) by the total perturbing potential \(\overline{\Phi}_{1}\), and replace this in turn by the perturbing density \(\overline{\rho}_{1}\) using Poisson's equation. We then have

\[\overline{\rho}_{\rm s1}({\bf k},t)=\int{\rm d}^{3}{\bf v}\,\overline{f}_{1}=- \frac{4\pi G\,{\rm i}}{k^{2}}\int{\rm d}^{3}{\bf v}\,{\bf k}\cdot\frac{ \partial f_{0}}{\partial{\bf v}}\int_{-\infty}^{t}{\rm d}t^{\prime}{\rm e}^{{ \rm i}{\bf k}\cdot{\bf v}(t^{\prime}-t)}\,\overline{\rho}_{1}({\bf k},t^{ \prime}). \tag{5.53}\]

Comparing with equation (5.27), we find that the polarization function for a homogeneous stellar system is

\[\overline{P}({\bf k},\tau)=-\frac{4\pi G\,{\rm i}}{k^{2}}H(\tau)\int{\rm d}^{3 }{\bf v}\,{\bf k}\cdot\frac{\partial f_{0}}{\partial{\bf v}}{\rm e}^{-{\rm i} {\bf k}\cdot{\bf v}\tau}. \tag{5.54}\]In particular, for the Maxwellian df (5.48),

\[\overline{P}({\bf k},\tau)=4\pi G\rho_{0}\tau H(\tau){\rm e}^{-(k\sigma\tau)^{2}/2}. \tag{5.55}\]

The polarization function decays to zero at \(\tau\to\infty\), in contrast to the polarization of a fluid, which oscillates (eq. 5.44). This decay is a manifestation of phase mixing (SS4.10.2).

We now turn to the response function. We take the temporal Fourier transform of the polarization function (5.54),

\[\widetilde{\overline{P}}({\bf k},\omega)=\int{\rm d}\tau\,\overline{P}({\bf k},\tau){\rm e}^{{\rm i}\omega\tau}=-\frac{4\pi G}{k^{2}}\int\frac{{\rm d}^{3}{ \bf v}}{{\bf k}\cdot{\bf v}-\omega}{\bf k}\cdot\frac{\partial f_{0}}{\partial {\bf v}},\quad{\rm Im}(\omega)>0. \tag{5.56}\]

The response function follows from equations (5.6) and (5.30),

\[\begin{split}\overline{R}({\bf k},\tau)&=\frac{1}{ 2\pi}\int_{{\rm i}c-\infty}^{{\rm i}c+\infty}{\rm d}\omega\,\widetilde{ \overline{R}}({\bf k},\omega){\rm e}^{-{\rm i}\omega\tau}\\ &=\frac{1}{2\pi}\int_{{\rm i}c-\infty}^{{\rm i}c+\infty}{\rm d} \omega\,\frac{\widetilde{\overline{P}}({\bf k},\omega){\rm e}^{-{\rm i} \omega\tau}}{1-\widetilde{\overline{P}}({\bf k},\omega)},\end{split} \tag{5.57}\]

where \(c\) is a sufficiently large real number. To evaluate this integral, we wish to extend the integration path to a closed contour. For \(\tau>0\), this can be done using a large semi-circle in the lower half-plane. Consequently, the value of the integral is given by the sum over the residues of any poles that the integrand has below the original integration path. Specifically,

\[\overline{R}({\bf k},\tau)=-{\rm i}\sum_{\rm P}R_{\rm p}{\rm e}^{-{\rm i} \omega_{\rm p}\tau}, \tag{5.58}\]where \(R_{\rm p}\) is the residue of \(\widetilde{R}({\bf k},\omega)\) at the pole \(\omega=\omega_{\rm p}\). The location of these poles determines the stability of the stellar system: if the maximum value of \({\rm Im}(\omega_{\rm p})\) is positive, then \(\exp(-{\rm i}\omega_{\rm p}\tau)\) grows exponentially, and the system is unstable; if zero, the system is stable but oscillates forever after a disturbance; and if negative all disturbances eventually decay exponentially, with the largest value of \({\rm Im}(\omega_{\rm p})\) dominating the long-term response.

To find the poles, we first consider the upper half-plane \({\rm Im}(\omega)>0\). The numerator \(\widetilde{P}({\bf k},\omega)\) has no poles in the upper half-plane, since the integral (5.56) is non-singular in this region. Hence, any poles in the upper half-plane must arise from zeros of the denominator, i.e., from solutions of the dispersion relation

\[\widetilde{P}({\bf k},\omega)=1,\quad{\rm Im}(\omega)>0; \tag{5.59}\]

solutions of this equation yield unstable modes.

Equation (5.56) does not define \(\widetilde{P}({\bf k},\omega)\) for \({\rm Im}(\omega)\leq 0\): in this region, the integrand of (5.57) is defined by the analytic continuation of \(\widetilde{P}({\bf k},\omega)\) to the lower half-plane. We next examine how to do this.

We set up Cartesian coordinates \((v_{1},v_{2},v_{3})\) in velocity space, with the 1-axis parallel to \({\bf k}\), and let \(F_{0}(v_{1})=\int{\rm d}v_{2}{\rm d}v_{3}\,f_{0}({\bf v})\) and \(x=kv_{1}\). Then equation (5.56) simplifies to

\[\widetilde{P}({\bf k},\omega)=-\frac{4\pi G}{k^{2}}\int_{-\infty}^{\infty}{ \rm d}x\,\frac{F_{0}^{\prime}(x/k)}{x-\omega},\qquad{\rm Im}(\omega)>0, \tag{5.60}\]

where \(F_{0}^{\prime}(v_{1})={\rm d}F_{0}/{\rm d}v_{1}\). Next, we consider \(x\) to be a complex variable, and recall that because we are integrating along the real \(x\) axis and \({\rm Im}(\omega)>0\), the integral in (5.60) is non-singular. This situation corresponds to the top panel in Figure 5.1. Now let us analytically continue the function in equation (5.60) to \({\rm Im}(\omega)=0\). Before doing so, we make the small semicircular deformation of the integration contour in the complex \(x\)-plane that is shown in the middle panel of Figure 5.1. This deformation does not change the value of the integral, but allows us to reduce \({\rm Im}(\omega)\) to zero without crossing the integration contour. Similarly, to continue analytically equation (5.60) to \({\rm Im}(\omega)<0\) we make the keyhole-shaped deformation shown in the bottom panel of Figure 5.1, which once again does not change the value of the integral. Therefore the analytic continuation of the polarization function can be written as

\[\widetilde{P}({\bf k},\omega)=-\frac{4\pi G}{k^{2}}\int_{\cal L}{\rm d}x\, \frac{F_{0}^{\prime}(x/k)}{x-\omega}, \tag{5.61}\]

where \({\cal L}\) is the **Landau contour** shown in Figure 5.1 (Landau 1946). The Landau contour allows us to continue analytically \(\widetilde{P}({\bf k},\omega)\) over the entire complex \(\omega\)-plane.

By the residue theorem

\[\int_{\cal L}{\rm d}x\,\frac{F_{0}^{\prime}(x/k)}{x-\omega}=\cases{\int_{-\infty}^{ \infty}{\rm d}x\,\frac{F_{0}^{\prime}(x/k)}{x-\omega}&({\rm Im}(\omega)>0)\cr \wp\int_{-\infty}^{\infty}{\rm d}x\,\frac{F_{0}^{\prime}(x/k)}{x-\omega}+\pi{ \rm i}F_{0}^{\prime}(\omega/k)&({\rm Im}(\omega)=0)\cr\int_{-\infty}^{\infty}{ \rm d}x\,\frac{F_{0}^{\prime}(x/k)}{x-\omega}+2\pi{\rm i}F_{0}^{\prime}(\omega /k)&({\rm Im}(\omega)<0),\cr} \tag{5.62}\]

where \(\wp\) denotes the Cauchy principal value (eq. C.6).

For the Maxwellian df (5.48), \(F_{0}(v_{1})=\rho_{0}\exp(-\frac{1}{2}v_{1}^{2}/\sigma^{2})/\sqrt{2\pi\sigma^{ 2}}\), so the integrals on the right of equation (5.62) can be written

\[-\frac{\rho_{0}}{\sqrt{2\pi}\sigma^{3}k}\int_{-\infty}^{\infty}{\rm d}x\, \frac{x\,{\rm e}^{-x^{2}/(2k^{2}\sigma^{2})}}{x-\omega}, \tag{5.63}\]

with the understanding that the principal value is to be taken if \(\omega\) is real. Then equations (5.61) and (5.62) can be combined to give

\[\widetilde{P}({\bf k},\sqrt{2}k\sigma w)=\frac{4\pi G\rho_{0}}{k^{2}\sigma^{2 }}\left[1+wZ(w)\right], \tag{5.64}\]

where the "plasma dispersion function" is

\[Z(w)=\frac{1}{\sqrt{\pi}}\int_{-\infty}^{\infty}{\rm d}s\frac{{\rm e}^{-s^{2} }}{s-w}\qquad({\rm Im}(w)>0), \tag{5.65}\]

and its analytic continuation for \({\rm Im}(w)\leq 0\). The analytic properties of the plasma dispersion function are described in Appendix C.3 and by Fried & Conte (1961).4

Footnote 4: Analogs for non-Maxwellian dfs—which can be simpler than the Maxwellian case— are discussed in Problem 5.8.

The dispersion relation follows from equation (5.59),

\[\frac{k^{2}}{k_{\rm J}^{2}}=1+wZ(w),\qquad\omega=\sqrt{2}k\sigma w, \tag{5.66}\]

where the Jeans wavenumber \(k_{\rm J}\) is defined by equation (5.49).

In examining the dispersion relation (5.66) there are three cases to consider:

**(a) Unstable solutions** In this case \({\rm Im}(\omega)>0\). In order for the dispersion relation (5.66) to be satisfied, the imaginary part of \(wZ(w)\) must vanish. Since the integration variable \(s\) in (5.65) is real,

\[wZ(w)=\frac{w}{\sqrt{\pi}}\int_{-\infty}^{\infty}{\rm d}s\frac{(s-w^{*})\,{ \rm e}^{-s^{2}}}{|s-w|^{2}} \tag{5.67}\]so

\[{\rm Im}[wZ(w)]=\frac{{\rm Im}(w)}{\sqrt{\pi}}\int_{-\infty}^{\infty}{\rm d}s \frac{{\rm se}^{-s^{2}}}{|s-w|^{2}}. \tag{5.68}\]

This integral can vanish only if \({\rm Re}(w)=0\): if, for example, \({\rm Re}(w)>0\), the absolute value of the integrand at \(s=s_{0}>0\) will always be larger than its value at \(s=-s_{0}<0\), and the integral will be positive, while if \({\rm Re}(w)<0\) the integral will be negative. Hence unstable solutions must have \(w\) and \(\omega\) imaginary--in other words, there are no **overstable** modes with \({\rm Im}(\omega)>0\) and \({\rm Re}(\omega)\neq 0\). Setting \(\omega={\rm i}\gamma\) in equation (5.66) and using equation (C.22), we obtain the dispersion relation for unstable modes,

\[\frac{k^{2}}{k_{\rm J}^{2}}=1-\frac{\sqrt{\pi}\gamma}{\sqrt{2}k\sigma}\exp \left(\frac{\gamma^{2}}{2k^{2}\sigma^{2}}\right)\left[1-{\rm erf}\left(\frac{ \gamma}{\sqrt{2}k\sigma}\right)\right]. \tag{5.69}\]

The growth rate \(\gamma\) is zero at \(k=k_{\rm J}\), which confirms that the definition (5.49) for the Jeans wavenumber in a Maxwellian stellar system does indeed separate stable and unstable wavenumbers. Unstable waves--solutions of (5.69) with \(\gamma>0\)--exist if and only if \(k^{2}<k_{\rm J}^{2}\). The dispersion relation (5.69) is plotted in Figure 5.2, along with the analogous relation (5.34) for a fluid.

**(b) Neutrally stable solutions**  Here \({\rm Im}(\omega)=0\), \({\rm Re}(\omega)\neq 0\). From equation (5.62), for real argument the plasma dispersion function is

\[Z(w)=\frac{1}{\sqrt{\pi}}\wp\int_{-\infty}^{\infty}{\rm d}s\frac{{\rm e}^{-s^ {2}}}{s-w}+{\rm i}\sqrt{\pi}{\rm e}^{-w^{2}}. \tag{5.70}\]

Figure 5.2: The dispersion relation for infinite homogeneous fluid and stellar systems, equations (5.34) and (5.59). The real and imaginary parts of the frequency are plotted separately, in units of \((4\pi G\rho_{0})^{1/2}\). The Jeans wavenumber \(k_{\rm J}\) is defined by equation (5.35) for fluids and equation (5.49) for stellar systems. Unstable branches are marked by heavy lines. As discussed on page 413, the curves with \({\rm Im}(\omega)<0\) describe Landau-damped waves rather than true modes.

The principal value is real, so \({\rm Im}\left[wZ(w)\right]=0\) only when \(\omega=0\). Consequently, there are no undamped traveling waves. In this respect the stellar system is quite different from a fluid system, whose dispersion relation (5.34) allows undamped gravity-modified sound waves at all wavelengths less than the Jeans length.

**(c) Damped solutions** Since only waves having \(k<k_{\rm J}\) are unstable, and there are no undamped waves, all waves with \(k>k_{\rm J}\) must be damped. Numerical evaluation of \(Z(w)\) (Figure 5.2) shows that there is an infinite number of solutions of (5.66) for a given wavenumber, all of them strongly damped, in the sense that \(|{\rm Im}(\omega)/{\rm Re}(\omega)|\) is of order unity or greater (the minimum value of this ratio for any \(k<k_{\rm J}\) is 0.481). This damping is called **Landau damping**.5

Footnote 5: Waves in electrostatic plasmas can exhibit much slower Landau damping, in the sense that \(|{\rm Im}(\omega)|\ll|{\rm Re}(\omega)|\).

Landau damping arises because of the singularity in the integrand of the polarization function (5.56) when

\[{\bf k}\cdot{\bf v}-\omega=0. \tag{5.71}\]

When a star's velocity \({\bf v}\) satisfies this equation, its position \({\bf x}\) at time \(t\), namely \({\bf x}(t)={\bf x}_{0}+{\bf v}t\), is such that its phase with respect to the wave \((\omega,{\bf k})\) is constant: \(\phi\equiv{\bf k}\cdot{\bf x}-\omega t={\bf k}\cdot{\bf x}_{0}+({\bf k}\cdot{ \bf v}-\omega)t\). That is, the star follows the wave in the same way that a surfer rides an ocean wave: the surfer may have a substantial velocity parallel to the crest of the wave, but perpendicular to the crest he tries to move at exactly the same speed as the crest; that is, he tries keep his phase with respect to the wave constant.

The surfer sets his phase such that the downhill direction is the direction in which the wave is running, for then the wave is doing work on him: the horizontal component of the buoyancy force \({\bf F}\) that the water imparts to the surfboard accelerates him in the direction of the wave motion, so the rate of doing work on the surfer is \({\bf F}\cdot{\bf v}>0\) (cf. eq. D.4). The surfer adjusts his speed parallel to the wave crests such that this work balances the dissipation of energy that arises from the board's motion through the water.

If the surfer crosses to the back side of the wave crest, he will do work _on_ the wave, and will slow down. But one can imagine a speedboat holding its position on this side of the crest by driving endlessly up the retreating slope of the wave. The boat would then be steadily transferring energy to the wave. Thus a particle that is in resonance with a wave can either draw energy from the wave, or give energy to the wave, depending on its phase with respect to the wave.

In a hot stellar system stars have a continuous distribution of velocities, and few stars will be close to resonance with a given wave. Non-resonant stars will in quick succession do work on the wave when they are on a retreating slope, and gain energy from the wave when they are on an advancing slope,so on the average they will exchange negligible energy with the wave. By contrast, stars that are nearly resonant can suffer net gains or losses. Stars that are initially moving slightly faster than the wave may lose enough energy as they move up a retreating slope to fall back and become trapped near a trough of the wave. Conversely, stars that are initially moving slower than the wave may gain enough energy on an advancing slope of the wave to become trapped. If the energy given up by the fast stars exceeds that gained by the slow stars, the amplitude of the wave must increase to conserve energy. When the distribution of stellar velocities is Maxwellian, as assumed in our calculation above, there are more slow stars than fast stars, and overall the near-resonant stars gain energy from the wave, so the wave decays. Trapping of stars at wave troughs is a nonlinear process, but nevertheless the rate of energy transfer through this process is captured correctly by the linear mathematical analysis that we have used (Stix 1992).

Both Landau damping and phase mixing (SS4.10.2) can damp waves in a stellar system, but the processes differ in several ways: (i) Landau damping is due to collective effects arising because of self-gravity, while phase mixing is a kinematic process that occurs even in systems with no self-gravity. (ii) In phase mixing the amplitude of the fluctuations in the df does not decay--the decay in the amplitude of the fluctuations in spatial density arises because the fluctuations in the df become more and more tightly wound in phase space. In contrast, Landau damping washes out the fluctuations in the df. (iii) Phase mixing almost always leads to decaying density fluctuations, while Landau damping can lead to either growth or decay, depending on the equilibrium df. (iv) Phase mixing is determined by the behavior of the numerator of the integrand in equation (5.57), while Landau damping is determined by the zeros of the denominator. (v) The rate of decay of the density fluctuations due to phase mixing depends on the initial perturbation and the equilibrium df, while Landau damping always leads to an exponential decay. For example, if the df is Maxwellian, the polarization function (5.55) decays _faster_ than an exponential, so Landau "damping" actually _extends_ the long-term survival of a perturbation.

A subtle but important point is that Landau-damped waves are _not_ modes. Our analysis shows only that as \(t\to\infty\), the density response is \(\propto\exp(-{\rm i}\omega_{\rm p}t)\), where \(\omega_{\rm p}\) is determined from the dispersion relation (5.66) using the Landau contour. In contrast, a mode is a solution of the linearized collisionless Boltzmann and Poisson equations (5.50) that behaves like \(\exp(-{\rm i}\omega t)\) at _all_ times. Solutions of the dispersion relation (5.59) are modes only in the part of the complex \(\omega\)-plane where the polarization function exists, which for homogeneous Maxwellian stellar systems means \({\rm Im}(\omega)>0\) (unstable modes). The properties of Landau-damped waves are determined by the analytic continuation of the polarization function to \({\rm Im}(\omega)<0\), where modes do not exist. There _are_ modes of a homogeneous stellar system with \(k>k_{\rm J}\), but these **van Kampen modes** have quite different properties from Landau-damped waves: they exist for all real \(\omega\) and \(k>k_{\rm J}\) and have singular dfs (Box 5.1).

The distinction between phase mixing and Landau damping can be illustrated by a numerical calculation of the response function for an infinite homogeneous stellar system.6 We start with equation (5.53) and replace the density \(\overline{\rho}_{\rm 1}\) by \(\overline{\rho}_{\rm s1}+\overline{\rho}_{\rm e}\) to obtain

Footnote 6: Suggested by A. Toomre.

\[\overline{\rho}_{\rm s1}({\bf k},t)=-\frac{4\pi G\,{\rm i}}{k^{2}}\int{\rm d}^ {3}{\bf v}\,{\bf k}\cdot\frac{\partial f_{0}}{\partial{\bf v}}\int_{-\infty}^ {t}{\rm d}t^{\prime}\,{\rm e}^{{\rm i}{\bf k}\cdot{\bf v}(t^{\prime}-t)}[ \overline{\rho}_{\rm s1}({\bf k},t^{\prime})+\overline{\rho}_{\rm e}({\bf k},t ^{\prime})]. \tag{5.72}\]

Now set \(\overline{\rho}_{\rm e}({\bf k},t)=\delta(t)\) and assume that \(\overline{\rho}_{\rm s1}({\bf k},t)=0\) for \(t<0\). In this case equation (5.27) shows that \(\overline{\rho}_{\rm s1}({\bf k},t)\) is just the response function \(\overline{R}({\bf k},t)\). Thus

\[\begin{split}\overline{R}({\bf k},t)&=-\frac{4\pi G \,{\rm i}}{k^{2}}\int{\rm d}^{3}{\bf v}\,{\bf k}\cdot\frac{\partial f_{0}}{ \partial{\bf v}}{\rm e}^{-{\rm i}{\bf k}\cdot{\bf v}t}\\ &\qquad-\frac{4\pi G\,{\rm i}}{k^{2}}\int{\rm d}^{3}{\bf v}\,{\bf k }\cdot\frac{\partial f_{0}}{\partial{\bf v}}\int_{0}^{t}{\rm d}t^{\prime}{\rm e }^{{\rm i}{\bf k}\cdot{\bf v}(t^{\prime}-t)}\overline{R}({\bf k},t^{\prime}), \end{split} \tag{5.73}\]

for \(t>0\), and zero for \(t<0\). For a Maxwellian df,

\[\begin{split}\overline{R}({\bf k},t)&=\frac{4\pi G \rho_{0}{\rm i}}{(2\pi)^{3/2}\sigma^{5}k^{2}}\int{\rm d}^{3}{\bf v}\,{\bf k} \cdot{\bf v}\,{\rm e}^{-v^{2}/(2\sigma^{2})-{\rm i}{\bf k}\cdot{\bf v}t}\\ &+\frac{4\pi G\rho_{0}{\rm i}}{(2\pi)^{3/2}\sigma^{5}k^{2}}\int{ \rm d}^{3}{\bf v}\,{\bf k}\cdot{\bf v}\,{\rm e}^{-v^{2}/(2\sigma^{2})}\int_{0} ^{t}{\rm d}t^{\prime}{\rm e}^{{\rm i}{\bf k}\cdot{\bf v}(t^{\prime}-t)} \overline{R}({\bf k},t^{\prime}).\end{split} \tag{5.74}\]

Now set up Cartesian coordinates \((v_{1},v_{2},v_{3})\) in velocity space, with the 1-axis parallel to \({\bf k}\). Then the integrations over \(v_{2}\) and \(v_{3}\) are immediate, the integral over \(v_{1}\) is straightforward, and we have

\[\overline{R}({\bf k},t)=4\pi G\rho_{0}t{\rm e}^{-(k\sigma t)^{2}/2}+4\pi G\rho _{0}\int_{0}^{t}{\rm d}t^{\prime}(t-t^{\prime}){\rm e}^{-k^{2}\sigma^{2}(t^{ \prime}-t)^{2}/2}\,\overline{R}({\bf k},t^{\prime}). \tag{5.75}\]

This is a Volterra integral equation, which is easily solved numerically (Press et al. 1986).7 The results are shown in Figure 5.3. The perturbation grows when \(k<k_{\rm J}\) and damps for \(k>k_{\rm J}\), as expected. In the limit \(k/k_{\rm J}\to\infty\) the damping is entirely due to phase mixing (cf. SS4.10.2), the response function is equal to the polarization function of equation (5.55), and the density response decays as a Gaussian in time. As the effects of self-gravity increase, the qualitative nature of the damping changes: the damping becomes less rapid and the density response decays at the exponential rate characteristic of Landau damping--as mentioned earlier, Landau damping actually _slows_ the decay relative to phase mixing.

Footnote 7: A simple df for which the response function can be determined analytically is described in Problem 5.8.

### 5.1 Van Kampen modes

If Landau-damped waves are not modes, then what _are_ the modes of a homogeneous stellar system with \(k>k_{\rm J}\)? We look for solutions of the linearized collisionless Boltzmann and Poisson equations (5.51) in which \(\omega\) is real, the external potential \(\overline{\Phi}_{\rm e}=0\), the perturbed df \(\overline{f}_{1}({\bf k},{\bf v},t)=\overline{f}_{1}({\bf k},{\bf v})\exp(-{ \rm i}\omega t)\), and the potential due to self-gravity \(\overline{\Phi}_{\rm s1}({\bf k},t)=\overline{\Phi}_{\rm s1}({\bf k})\exp(-{ \rm i}\omega t)\). This requires

\[({\bf k}\cdot{\bf v}-\omega)\overline{f}_{1}-\overline{\Phi}_{\rm s1}{\bf k} \cdot\frac{\partial f_{0}}{\partial{\bf v}}=0\quad;\quad-k^{2}\overline{\Phi} _{\rm s1}=4\pi G\int{\rm d}^{3}{\bf v}\,\overline{f}_{1}=4\pi G\overline{\rho} _{\rm s1}. \tag{1}\]

An obvious solution to the first equation is

\[\overline{f}_{a}({\bf k},{\bf v})=\frac{\overline{\Phi}_{\rm s1}}{{\bf k}\cdot {\bf v}-\omega}{\bf k}\cdot\frac{\partial f_{0}}{\partial{\bf v}}; \tag{2}\]

however, van Kampen (1955) pointed out that when \({\bf k}\cdot{\bf v}-\omega=0\), the first of equations (1) does not constrain \(\overline{f}_{1}\), so the most general solution is \(\overline{f}_{a}+\overline{f}_{b}\), where

\[\overline{f}_{b}({\bf k},{\bf v})=cg({\bf v}_{\perp})\delta({\bf k}\cdot{\bf v }-\omega). \tag{3}\]

Here \(c\) is a constant, \({\bf v}_{\perp}\) is the component of \({\bf v}\) that is perpendicular to \({\bf k}\), \(g({\bf v}_{\perp})\) is an arbitrary function normalized such that \(\int{\rm d}^{2}{\bf v}_{\perp}g({\bf v}_{\perp})=1\), and \(\delta(x)\) is the delta function.

Integrating \(\overline{f}_{a}+\overline{f}_{b}\) over velocity and using the second of equations (1), it is straightforward to show that the df of the mode is

\[\eqalign{\overline{f}_{1}({\bf k},{\bf v})&=\frac{a}{{\bf k}\cdot{\bf v}- \omega}{\bf k}\cdot\frac{\partial f_{0}}{\partial{\bf v}}\cr&-\frac{k^{3}a}{4 \pi G}\left[1+\frac{4\pi G}{k^{2}}\wp\int\frac{{\rm d}^{3}{\bf v}^{\prime}}{{ \bf k}\cdot{\bf v}^{\prime}-\omega}{\bf k}\cdot\frac{\partial f_{0}}{\partial {\bf v}^{\prime}}\right]g({\bf v}_{\perp})\delta({\bf k}\cdot{\bf v}-\omega),} \tag{4}\]

where \(a\) is an arbitrary constant and \(\wp\) denotes the principal value (eq. C.6). The corresponding potential is simply \(\overline{\Phi}_{\rm s1}=a\).

Singular modes of this form are known as van Kampen modes. It can be shown that the van Kampen modes are complete and that Landau-damped waves can be regarded as a superposition of van Kampen modes (van Kampen 1955; Case 1959; Stix 1992; Vandervoort 2003). The modes do not satisfy a dispersion relation since they exist for all real \(\omega\) and \(k>k_{\rm J}\). Since these solutions are neither growing not decaying, they resolve the apparent paradox of how all perturbations in a time-reversible system can decay, as appears to happen with Landau-damped waves. The van Kampen modes also demonstrate that a stellar system has a much richer--and more pathological--set of modes than a fluid.

#### Discussion

The stability of an infinite homogeneous stellar system is closely related to the stability of the analogous fluid system: in both cases there is instability if and only if the wavenumber of the disturbance is less than the Jeans wavenumber \(k_{\rm J}\), defined by equation (5.35) for fluids and (5.49) for a Maxwellian stellar system. However, the responses to perturbations with \(k>k_{\rm J}\) are quite different: the fluid supports undamped gravity-modified sound waves while the disturbance in the stellar system is strongly phase-mixed and Landau-damped.

These results rest on two linked foundations: the assumption that the system is infinite and homogeneous, and the Jeans swindle of neglecting the gravitational potential of the unperturbed system. It is time to pause and investigate the consequences of these assumptions.

The virial theorem (4.248) tells us that a stellar system of mass \(M\) and mean-square velocity \(v^{2}\) has a characteristic size \(\lambda_{0}\approx GM/v^{2}\). In terms of the mean density \(\rho\approx M/\lambda_{0}^{3}\), we have \(\lambda_{0}^{2}\approx v^{2}/G\rho\). However, from equation (5.46) the Jeans length is given by \(\lambda_{\rm J}^{2}\approx v^{2}/G\rho\), which is the same as \(\lambda_{0}^{2}\) to order of magnitude. Thus the Jeans length is comparable to the size of the system, and the assumption of homogeneity generally is not valid. Accordingly, the Jeans analysis does not establish that there is a real instability in an isolated stellar system. Nevertheless, the analysis is a cornerstone of stability theory for self-gravitating systems, for several reasons:

* The homogeneity assumption is valid and the Jeans swindle is legitimate on scales \(\lambda\ll\lambda_{0}\), because the effects of the inhomogeneity and self-gravity of the equilibrium system are small on small scales. Therefore, we can conclude from our analysis that stationary stellar systems with dfs that are approximately Maxwellian are stable on small scales.

* The Jeans analysis can be used to investigate whether non-Maxwellian dfs introduce new instabilities on small scales. For example, we can ask whether there is an analog for stellar systems of the two-stream instability, which arises in a plasma containing two interpenetrating beams of electrons or ions with different mean velocities (Stix 1992). Because the stellar system is already unstable for \(k<k_{\rm J}\), the two-stream instability is distinct from the Jeans instability only if the df\(f_{2}({\bf v})\equiv\frac{1}{2}[f_{0}({\bf v}+{\bf V})+f_{0}({\bf v}-{\bf V})]\) is unstable at wavenumbers where the df \(f_{0}({\bf v})\) is stable. It is found that there is no two-stream instability in an infinite homogeneous medium when \(f_{0}({\bf v})\) is Maxwellian (Sweet 1963; Araki 1987), although related instabilities can occur in disks containing equal populations of stars rotating in opposite directions (Sellwood & Merritt 1994; Lovelace, Jore, & Haynes 1997).
* Generalizations of Jeans's analysis to relativistic fluids in a homogeneous expanding universe are central to cosmology, where structures such as galaxies arise from gravitational instabilities (SS9.1.2b). In this case the stability analysis can be done self-consistently, without invoking the Jeans swindle.
* The physics of the Jeans instability enables us to interpret small-scale instabilities in rotating disk systems (see SS5.6.1 and SS6.2.3).
* The Jeans analysis provides insight into aspects of the behavior of more realistic stellar systems, such as the phenomenon of Landau damping, which is discussed in the context of spherical systems in SS5.5.3.

### 5.3 General theory of the response of stellar systems

The response of realistic, finite, inhomogeneous stellar systems is substantially more complicated than the response of infinite homogeneous systems that we described in the last section. Fortunately, many of the tools that we have developed for homogeneous systems remain relevant. Most fundamentally, the linear response of any stellar system is still determined by the linearized collisionless Boltzmann and Poisson equations (5.13). A key to analyzing these systems is the insight that the natural variables for solving these two equations are different: the Poisson equation is simplest in coordinates that reflect the spatial symmetry of the equilibrium system, while the linearized collisionless Boltzmann equation is simplest in the angle-action variables of the equilibrium potential. Most of the work consists of transforming between these two sets of variables, which is numerically tedious but conceptually straightforward. Palmer (1994) provides a thorough review of this approach.

#### The polarization function in angle-action variables

Let us suppose that the potential \(\Phi_{0}({\bf x})\) of the equilibrium stellar system is integrable, in other words that there exist angle-action variables \((\mathbf{\theta},{\bf J})\) in which the equilibrium Hamiltonian is independent of angles, \(H=H_{0}({\bf J})\). All spherical and two-dimensional axisymmetric disk potentials are integrable, as are the triaxial Stackel potentials discussed in SS3.5.3. The position and velocity of any star are functions of the actions and angles, which we may write as \({\bf x}(\mathbf{\theta},{\bf J})\) and \({\bf v}(\mathbf{\theta},{\bf J})\).

According to the Jeans theorem, the equilibrium df may be assumed to depend only on the actions; thus \(f_{0}=f_{0}({\bf J})\). To compute the polarization function we imagine that the stellar system is subjected to a weak gravitational force arising from some potential \(\epsilon\Phi_{1}({\bf x},t)\), where \(\epsilon\ll 1\) and \(\Phi_{1}\) includes the self-gravity of the response. The df is then modified to

\[f(\mathbf{\theta},{\bf J},t)=f_{0}({\bf J})+\epsilon f_{1}(\mathbf{\theta},{\bf J},t). \tag{5.76}\]

Since \(f_{0}\) and \(H_{0}\) are independent of the angles \(\theta\), the linearized collisionless Boltzmann equation (5.13) becomes

\[\begin{split} 0&=\frac{\partial f_{1}}{\partial t}+[f_{1},H _{0}]+[f_{0},\Phi_{1}]\\ &=\frac{\partial f_{1}}{\partial t}+\frac{\partial f_{1}}{ \partial\mathbf{\theta}}\cdot\mathbf{\Omega}-\frac{ \partial f_{0}({\bf J})}{\partial{\bf J}}\cdot\frac{\partial\Phi_{1}}{ \partial\mathbf{\theta}},\end{split} \tag{5.77}\]

where

\[\mathbf{\Omega}({\bf J})=\frac{\partial H_{0}}{\partial{\bf J}} \tag{5.78}\]

gives the rate of change \(\dot{\mathbf{\theta}}\) of the angles in the unperturbed system (eq. 3.190).

Any function of the phase-space coordinates must be a periodic function of the angles \(\theta\). Thus we can expand \(f_{1}\) and \(\Phi_{1}\) in Fourier series (cf. eqs. B.62 and B.64),

\[f_{1}(\mathbf{\theta},{\bf J},t)=\sum_{\bf m}f_{\bf m}({\bf J},t){ \rm e}^{{\rm i}{\bf m}\cdot\mathbf{\theta}}\quad;\quad\Phi_{1}[{\bf x }(\mathbf{\theta},{\bf J}),t]=\sum_{\bf m}\Phi_{\bf m}({\bf J},t){ \rm e}^{{\rm i}{\bf m}\cdot\mathbf{\theta}}, \tag{5.79}\]

where \({\bf m}\) denotes a triple of integers \((m_{1},m_{2},m_{3})\) and

\[\begin{split} f_{\bf m}({\bf J},t)&=\frac{1}{(2\pi) ^{3}}\int{\rm d}^{3}\mathbf{\theta}^{\prime}\,{\rm e}^{-{\rm i}{\bf m }\cdot\mathbf{\theta}^{\prime}}f_{1}(\mathbf{\theta}^{ \prime},{\bf J},t),\\ \Phi_{\bf m}({\bf J},t)&=\frac{1}{(2\pi)^{3}}\int{ \rm d}^{3}\mathbf{\theta}^{\prime}\,{\rm e}^{-{\rm i}{\bf m}\cdot \mathbf{\theta}^{\prime}}\Phi_{1}[{\bf x}(\mathbf{\theta}^{ \prime},{\bf J}),t].\end{split} \tag{5.80}\]

Equation (5.77) must be satisfied separately by each Fourier component. Thus

\[\frac{\partial f_{\bf m}}{\partial t}+{\rm i}{\bf m}\cdot\mathbf{ \Omega}f_{\bf m}={\rm i}{\bf m}\cdot\frac{\partial f_{0}({\bf J})}{\partial{ \bf J}}\Phi_{\bf m}. \tag{5.81}\]We multiply this result by \(\exp({\rm i}\omega t)\) and integrate from \(t=0\) to infinity. The first term can be integrated by parts, yielding

\[\left[f_{\bf m}{\rm e}^{{\rm i}\omega t}\right]_{0}^{\infty}+{\rm i}({\bf m}\cdot{ \bf\Omega}-\omega)\int_{0}^{\infty}{\rm d}t\,f_{\bf m}{\rm e}^{{\rm i}\omega t}= {\bf im}\cdot\frac{\partial f_{0}({\bf J})}{\partial{\bf J}}\int_{0}^{\infty} {\rm d}t\,\Phi_{\bf m}{\rm e}^{{\rm i}\omega t}. \tag{5.82}\]

We assume that the system was initially in its equilibrium state, so \(f_{\bf m}=\Phi_{\bf m}=0\) for \(t\leq 0\). We also assume that \({\rm Im}(\omega)>c\), where \(c\) is a positive constant chosen large enough to ensure that \(f_{\bf m}{\rm e}^{{\rm i}\omega t}\to 0\) as \(t\to\infty\). Then the first term in equation (5.82) vanishes. The integrals in the other terms are simply the temporal Fourier transforms \(\widetilde{f}_{\bf m}({\bf J},\omega)\) and \(\widetilde{\Phi}_{\bf m}({\bf J},\omega)\) (cf. eq. 5.6). Thus

\[\widetilde{f}_{\bf m}({\bf J},\omega)={\bf m}\cdot\frac{\partial f_{0}({\bf J })}{\partial{\bf J}}\,\frac{\widetilde{\Phi}_{\bf m}({\bf J},\omega)}{{\bf m} \cdot{\bf\Omega}-\omega},\qquad{\rm Im}(\omega)>c. \tag{5.83}\]

Multiplying equation (5.83) by \(\exp({\bf im}\cdot{\bf\theta})\) and summing over \({\bf m}\) gives the linearized df\(\widetilde{f}_{1}({\bf\theta},{\bf J},\omega)\). However, what we need for the polarization function is the density response \(\widetilde{\rho}_{1}({\bf x},\omega)\). The direct conversion from one to the other is straightforward--just integrate over velocity--but cumbersome, since we must first convert back from angle-action variables to Cartesian coordinates. In the next section, we obtain formulae that are both simpler and more useful, by recasting the problem in a matrix form.

#### 5.3.2 The Kalnajs matrix method

In SS2.8 we introduced the concept of a bi-orthonormal potential-density basis: two complete sets of basis functions \(\Phi_{\alpha}({\bf x})\) and \(\rho_{\alpha}({\bf x})\) such that

\[\begin{split}\nabla^{2}\Phi_{\alpha}&=4\pi G\rho_{ \alpha},\\ -\int{\rm d}^{3}{\bf x}\,\Phi_{\alpha}^{*}({\bf x})\rho_{\beta}({ \bf x})&=\delta_{\alpha\beta},\end{split} \tag{5.84}\]

where \(\alpha\) and \(\beta\) are labels for the members of the basis, and \(\delta_{\alpha\beta}\) is 1 if \(\alpha=\beta\) and zero otherwise. We expand the densities in equation (5.7) using the bi-orthonormal basis:

\[\widetilde{\rho}_{\rm s1}({\bf x},\omega)=\sum_{\beta}\widetilde{d}_{\beta}( \omega)\rho_{\beta}({\bf x})\quad;\quad\widetilde{\rho}_{1}({\bf x},\omega)= \sum_{\beta}\widetilde{g}_{\beta}(\omega)\rho_{\beta}({\bf x}). \tag{5.85}\]

Because \(\rho_{\beta}({\bf x})\) and \(\Phi_{\beta}({\bf x})\) satisfy Poisson's equation (5.84), the potentials corresponding to \(\widetilde{\rho}_{\rm s1}\) and \(\widetilde{\rho}_{1}\) are described by the same coefficients; thus

\[\widetilde{\Phi}_{\rm s1}({\bf x},\omega)=\sum_{\beta}\widetilde{d}_{\beta}( \omega)\Phi_{\beta}({\bf x})\quad;\quad\widetilde{\Phi}_{1}({\bf x},\omega)= \sum_{\beta}\widetilde{g}_{\beta}(\omega)\Phi_{\beta}({\bf x}). \tag{5.86}\]We substitute the expansions (5.85) into equation (5.7), multiply by \(\Phi_{\alpha}^{\ast}({\bf x})\), integrate with respect to \({\rm d}^{3}{\bf x}\), and use the orthogonality relation (5.84) to obtain

\[\widetilde{d}_{\alpha}(\omega)=\sum_{\beta}\widetilde{P}_{\alpha\beta}(\omega) \widetilde{g}_{\beta}(\omega),\] (5.87a) where \[\widetilde{P}_{\alpha\beta}(\omega)=-\int{\rm d}^{3}{\bf x}\,{\rm d}^{3}{\bf x }^{\prime}\,\Phi_{\alpha}^{\ast}({\bf x})\widetilde{P}({\bf x},{\bf x}^{ \prime},\omega)\rho_{\beta}({\bf x}^{\prime}). \tag{5.87b}\]

In more compact notation, we may write equation (5.87a) as

\[\widetilde{\bf d}(\omega)=\widetilde{\bf P}(\omega)\widetilde{\bf g}(\omega), \tag{5.88}\]

where \(\widetilde{\bf d}\), \(\widetilde{\bf g}\) are vectors and \(\widetilde{\bf P}\) is a matrix. Thus we have replaced the integral equation (5.7) with a matrix equation, and the polarization function with a polarization matrix. In principle, the matrix has infinite dimension, since there are infinitely many basis functions. However, if the bi-orthonormal basis is chosen sensibly we can obtain an accurate representation of the response with a manageable subset of the basis (Kalnajs 1971, 1977).

Let \(\widetilde{f}_{\beta}({\bf x},{\bf v},\omega)\exp({\rm i}\omega t)\) be the perturbation to the df induced by the potential from a total perturbing density \(\rho_{\beta}({\bf x})\exp({\rm i}\omega t)\). Then

\[\int{\rm d}^{3}{\bf v}\,\widetilde{f}_{\beta}({\bf x},{\bf v},\omega)=\int{ \rm d}^{3}{\bf x}^{\prime}\,\widetilde{P}({\bf x},{\bf x}^{\prime},\omega) \rho_{\beta}({\bf x}^{\prime}), \tag{5.89}\]

so the polarization matrix (5.87b) can be written

\[\widetilde{P}_{\alpha\beta}(\omega)=-\int{\rm d}^{3}{\bf x}\,{\rm d}^{3}{\bf v }\,\Phi_{\alpha}^{\ast}({\bf x})\widetilde{f}_{\beta}({\bf x},{\bf v},\omega). \tag{5.90}\]

Now the transformation from Cartesian phase-space coordinates \(({\bf x},{\bf v})\) to angle-action variables \(({\boldsymbol{\theta}},{\bf J})\) is canonical, so \({\rm d}^{3}{\bf x}\,{\rm d}^{3}{\bf v}={\rm d}^{3}{\boldsymbol{\theta}}\,{ \rm d}^{3}{\bf J}\) (eq. D.81). We can therefore convert the integration variables in equation (5.90) to angle-action variables,

\[\widetilde{P}_{\alpha\beta}(\omega)=-\int{\rm d}^{3}{\boldsymbol{\theta}}\, {\rm d}^{3}{\bf J}\,\Phi_{\alpha}^{\ast}[{\bf x}({\boldsymbol{\theta}},{\bf J })]\widetilde{f}_{\beta}({\boldsymbol{\theta}},{\bf J},\omega). \tag{5.91}\]

We next expand the potential basis functions in a Fourier series (cf. eq. 5.79)

\[\Phi_{\alpha}[{\bf x}({\boldsymbol{\theta}},{\bf J})]=\sum_{\bf m}\Phi_{\alpha,{\bf m}}({\bf J}){\rm e}^{{\rm i}{\bf m}\cdot{\boldsymbol{\theta}}}, \tag{5.92}\]

and employ equation (5.83) to evaluate \(\widetilde{f}_{\beta}\):

\[\widetilde{P}_{\alpha\beta}(\omega)=-\sum_{{\bf m},{\bf m}^{\prime}}\int{\rm d }^{3}{\boldsymbol{\theta}}\,{\rm d}^{3}{\bf J}\,{\bf m}\cdot\frac{\partial f _{0}({\bf J})}{\partial{\bf J}}\frac{\widetilde{\Phi}_{\alpha,{\bf m}^{\prime }}^{\ast}({\bf J})\widetilde{\Phi}_{\beta,{\bf m}}({\bf J})}{{\bf m}\cdot{ \boldsymbol{\Omega}}-\omega}{\rm e}^{{\rm i}({\bf m}-{\bf m}^{\prime})\cdot{ \boldsymbol{\theta}}}. \tag{5.93}\]The integration over \({\rm d}^{3}\mathbf{\theta}\) vanishes unless the integer triples \({\bf m}\) and \({\bf m}^{\prime}\) are equal, so the expression simplifies to

\[\widetilde{P}_{\alpha\beta}(\omega)=-(2\pi)^{3}\sum_{\bf m}\int{\rm d}^{3}{\bf J }\,{\bf m}\cdot\frac{\partial f_{0}({\bf J})}{\partial{\bf J}}\frac{ \widetilde{\Phi}_{\alpha,{\bf m}}^{*}({\bf J})\widetilde{\Phi}_{\beta,{\bf m}} ({\bf J})}{{\bf m}\cdot\mathbf{\Omega}-\omega}. \tag{5.94}\]

An equally valid form is obtained by applying the divergence theorem (B.45):8

Footnote 8: The surface term vanishes; see Problem 5.11.

\[\widetilde{P}_{\alpha\beta}(\omega)=(2\pi)^{3}\sum_{\bf m}\int{\rm d}^{3}{\bf J }\,f_{0}({\bf J})\,{\bf m}\cdot\frac{\partial}{\partial{\bf J}}\left[\frac{ \widetilde{\Phi}_{\alpha,{\bf m}}^{*}({\bf J})\widetilde{\Phi}_{\beta,{\bf m}} ({\bf J})}{{\bf m}\cdot\mathbf{\Omega}-\omega}\right]. \tag{5.95}\]

All these expressions should be restricted to \({\rm Im}(\omega)>c\).

A mode requires that \(\rho_{\rm s1}=\rho_{1}\), so by (5.85) \({\bf d}={\bf g}\), and by (5.88)

\[\widetilde{\bf P}(\omega)\widetilde{\bf d}=\widetilde{\bf d},\qquad{\rm Im}( \omega)>c; \tag{5.96}\]

in words, a mode with frequency \(\omega_{\rm p}\) exists if \(\widetilde{\bf d}\) is an eigenvector of \(\widetilde{\bf P}(\omega_{\rm p})\) with eigenvalue 1. This matrix equation is the direct analog of the dispersion relation (5.45) that we derived for homogeneous stellar systems.

#### The response matrix

The response matrix is defined by analogy to the polarization matrix (5.87b),

\[\widetilde{R}_{\alpha\beta}(\omega)=-\int{\rm d}^{3}{\bf x}\,{\rm d}^{3}{\bf x }^{\prime}\,\Phi_{\alpha}^{*}({\bf x})\widetilde{R}({\bf x},{\bf x}^{\prime}, \omega)\rho_{\beta}({\bf x}^{\prime}). \tag{5.97}\]

If we expand the external density in the bi-orthonormal basis (cf. eq. 5.85)

\[\widetilde{\rho}_{\rm e}({\bf x},\omega)=\sum_{\beta}\widetilde{h}_{\beta}( \omega)\rho_{\beta}({\bf x}), \tag{5.98}\]

then as in equation (5.88),

\[\widetilde{\bf d}(\omega)=\widetilde{\bf R}(\omega)\widetilde{\bf h}(\omega). \tag{5.99}\]

The response matrix can be determined from the polarization matrix by generalizing the arguments of SSSS5.2.2 and 5.2.4 from scalar functions of \(\omega\) to matrices (Kalnajs 1971). The total perturbing density is the sum of the external density and the perturbed density of the stellar system; thus \(\rho_{1}=\rho_{\rm s1}+\rho_{\rm e}\) and \({\bf g}={\bf d}+{\bf h}\). Using equations (5.88) and (5.99) we then have

\[\widetilde{\bf d}=\widetilde{\bf P}(\omega)\widetilde{\bf g}=\widetilde{\bf P }(\omega)\widetilde{\bf h}+\widetilde{\bf P}(\omega)\widetilde{\bf d}= \widetilde{\bf P}(\omega)\widetilde{\bf h}+\widetilde{\bf P}(\omega) \widetilde{\bf R}(\omega)\widetilde{\bf h}. \tag{5.100}\]Since this relation holds for arbitrary \(\widetilde{\bf h}\), comparison with equation (5.99) implies that

\[\widetilde{\bf R}(\omega)=\widetilde{\bf P}(\omega)+\widetilde{\bf P}(\omega) \widetilde{\bf R}(\omega)\qquad\mbox{or}\qquad[{\bf I}-\widetilde{\bf P}( \omega)]\widetilde{\bf R}(\omega)=\widetilde{\bf P}(\omega), \tag{5.101}\]

where \({\bf I}\) is the identity matrix.

Equation (5.94) shows that the matrix elements of \({\bf P}(\omega)\) shrink to zero as \({\rm Im}(\omega)\to\infty\). Thus there exists some positive constant \(c\) such that the series \({\bf I}+\widetilde{\bf P}+\widetilde{\bf P}^{2}+\cdots\) converges for \({\rm Im}(\omega)>c\); in this case the series is equal to \([{\bf I}-\widetilde{\bf P}(\omega)]^{-1}\), and we can invert equation (5.101) to obtain a relation between the polarization and response matrices,

\[\widetilde{\bf R}(\omega)=[{\bf I}-\widetilde{\bf P}(\omega)]^{-1}\widetilde{ \bf P}(\omega),\qquad{\rm Im}(\omega)>c. \tag{5.102}\]

This matrix equation is the analog of the scalar equation (5.30) for homogeneous systems.

From equations (5.99) and (5.102) the basis-function expansion of the response density is

\[\widetilde{\bf d}(\omega)=[{\bf I}-\widetilde{\bf P}(\omega)]^{-1}\widetilde{ \bf P}(\omega)\widetilde{\bf h}(\omega),\qquad{\rm Im}(\omega)>c. \tag{5.103}\]

Taking the inverse Fourier transform (5.6) yields

\[{\bf d}(t)=\frac{1}{2\pi}\int_{{\rm i}c-\infty}^{{\rm i}c+\infty}\!\!\!\!\!\! \!\!\!\!\!\!\!\!\!\!\!\!\!\!\!\!\!\!\!\!\!\!\!\!\!\!\!\!\!\!\!\!\!\!\!\!\!\!\! \!\!\!\!\!\!\!\!\!\!\!\!\!\!\!\!\!\!\!\!\!\!\!\!\!\!\!\!\!\!\!\!\!\!\!\!\!\! \!\!\!\!\!\!\!\!\!\!\!\!\!\!\!\!\!\!\!\!\!\!\!\!\!\!\!\!\!\!\!\!\!\!\!\!\!\! \!\!\!\!\!\!\!\!\!\!\!\!\!\!\!\!\!\!\!\!\!\!\!\!\!\!\!\!\!\!\!\!\!\!\!\!\!\! \!\!\!\!\!\!\!\!\!\!\!\!\!\!\!\!\!\!\!\!\!\!\!\!\!\!\!\!\!\!\!\!\!\!\!\!\!\! \!\!\!\!\!\!\!\!\!\!\!\!\!\!\!\!\!\!\!\!\!\!\!\!\!\!\!\!\!\!\!\!\!\!\!\!\!\! \!\!\!\!\!\!\!\!\!\!\!\!\!\!\!\!\!\!\!\!\!\!\!\!\!\!\!\!\!\!\!\!\!\!\!\!\!\! \!\!\!\!\!\!\!\!\!\!\!\!\!\!\!\!\!\!\!\!\!\!\!\!\!\!\!\!\!\!\!\!\!\!\!\!\!\!\! \!\!may give rise to solutions of (5.106) that are not associated with modes, but rather Landau-damped disturbances.

These matrix equations can be simplified when the stellar system has certain symmetries. For example, when the equilibrium system is spherical the density of a mode can always be assumed to have the form \(\rho_{1}({\bf x},t)=d(r){\rm Y}_{l}^{m}(\vartheta,\phi)\exp(-{\rm i}\omega t)\), where \({\rm Y}_{l}^{m}(\vartheta,\phi)\) is a spherical harmonic (Appendix C.6) and \(d(r)\) and \(\omega\) are independent of \(m\) (see Box 5.2). Thus it is sufficient to restrict the basis-function expansion to axisymmetric (\(m=0\)) basis functions, and to a single value of the angular quantum number \(l\) at a time, repeating the calculation for \(l=0,1,2,\ldots\) as required.

The Kalnajs matrix method has been used to determine the stability of a variety of stellar systems, including spherical systems, plane-parallel systems, and differentially rotating disks (see SS5.5, page 432, and SS6.3.1 respectively).

### 5.4 The energy principle and secular stability

Energy considerations often provide a powerful technique for determining the stability of dynamical systems. For example, a ball resting at the bottom of a hemispherical bowl must be stable, because all nearby positions of the ball have higher energy. In this case it is not necessary to solve the linearized equations of motion to determine that the configuration is stable. A similar criterion can be established for continuous systems: they are certain to be stable if all neighboring configurations with the same total mass have higher energy. A system that satisfies this criterion is said to enjoy **secular stability** (Lyttleton 1953; Hunter 1977). Secular stability is sufficient but not necessary for dynamical stability, because the lower energy states may not be dynamically accessible. An example of a system that is dynamically stable but secularly unstable is a ball that rolls around a bowl at constant height, maintaining a balance between centrifugal and gravitational forces. The ball's trajectory is dynamically stable (in the absence of friction) despite the existence of states of lower energy, because the ball cannot reach these states while conserving its angular momentum (see Problem 5.12).

To find energy-based stability criteria we must first find the energy change resulting from a small perturbation to the system. Once again, we shall examine self-gravitating fluid systems in parallel with stellar systems.

#### The energy principle for fluid systems

The rate at which work is done against an external force \({\bf F}=-\epsilon m{\bf\nabla}\Phi_{\rm e}\) in moving a particle of mass \(m\) is (cf. eq. D.4)

\[-{\bf F}\cdot{\bf v}=\epsilon m{\bf v}\cdot{\bf\nabla}\Phi_{\rm e}. \tag{5.107}\]

### 5.2 Response of spherical systems

The polarization function of a spherical fluid or stellar system, \(P({\bf x},{\bf x}^{\prime},\tau)\), inherits certain symmetry properties from the spherical symmetry of the equilibrium system. In particular, the polarization function can depend on \({\bf x}\) and \({\bf x}^{\prime}\) only through their magnitudes \(r=|{\bf x}|\), \(r^{\prime}=|{\bf x}^{\prime}|\), and the angle \(\gamma\) between the two vectors, defined by \(\cos\gamma={\bf x}\cdot{\bf x}^{\prime}/(rr^{\prime})\). Thus it can be expanded in a complete set of angular functions,

\[P({\bf x},{\bf x}^{\prime},\tau)=\sum_{k=0}^{\infty}K_{k}(r,r^{\prime},\tau)P_{ k}(\cos\gamma), \tag{1}\]

where \(P_{k}\) is a Legendre polynomial (Appendix C.5). Using the expansion (C.47), this can be rewritten in spherical harmonics as

\[P({\bf x},{\bf x}^{\prime},\tau)=\frac{4\pi}{2k+1}\sum_{k=0}^{\infty}\sum_{n=- k}^{k}K_{k}(r,r^{\prime},\tau){\rm Y}_{k}^{n\,*}(\vartheta^{\prime},\phi^{ \prime}){\rm Y}_{k}^{n}(\vartheta,\phi), \tag{2}\]

where \({\bf x}=(r,\vartheta,\phi)\), \({\bf x}^{\prime}=(r^{\prime},\vartheta^{\prime},\phi^{\prime})\) in spherical coordinates. (To minimize confusion between spherical coordinates and angle variables, in this chapter we reserve \(\vartheta\) for the usual polar angle, and continue to use \(\theta_{i}\) for the variable conjugate to \(J_{i}\).)

Now consider a density perturbation of the form

\[\rho_{1}({\bf x},t)=g(r){\rm Y}_{l}^{m}(\vartheta,\phi)\exp(-{\rm i}\omega t). \tag{3}\]

Substituting this result and equation (2) into the definition (5.3) of the polarization function, and using the orthonormality of the spherical harmonics (eq. C.44), we find that the response density is

\[\rho_{\rm s1}({\bf x},t)=\frac{4\pi}{2l+1}{\rm Y}_{l}^{m}(\vartheta,\phi){\rm e }^{-{\rm i}\omega t}\int{\rm d}r^{\prime}\,{r^{\prime}}^{2}f(r^{\prime})\int_ {0}^{\infty}{\rm d}\tau\,K_{l}(r,r^{\prime},\tau){\rm e}^{{\rm i}\omega\tau}. \tag{4}\]

The condition for a mode is that \(\rho_{1}=\rho_{\rm s1}\). Since both are proportional to \({\rm Y}_{l}^{m}(\vartheta,\phi)\) we may always choose the modes of spherical fluid or stellar systems to have this angular dependence. Moreover, when written in this form the equation determining the modes is independent of the **azimuthal wavenumber**\(m\); thus the frequency \(\omega\) and the radial density profile \(g(r)\) of the mode are also independent of \(m\). In other words there are \(2m+1\) degenerate modes for each \(l\). Because of this symmetry, in practice we need to calculate the response of a spherical system only to axisymmetric \(m=0\) disturbances.

This argument does not yield the behavior of the df in velocity space; for this see Barnes, Goodman, & Hut (1986).

The rate of doing work on the particle is the negative of this expression, so the total rate of doing work on a fluid system is

\[{{\rm d}E\over{\rm d}t}=-\epsilon\int{\rm d}^{3}{\bf x}\,\rho{\bf v}\cdot{\mathbf{ \nabla}}\Phi_{\rm e},\]

where \(\rho\) is the fluid density. We shall assume that the integration volume is large enough that the density is zero at its boundary, so matter and energy cannot flow into or out of the volume.

The energy principle is most useful for static fluids, in which the unperturbed velocity \({\bf v}_{0}=0\), so we shall restrict ourselves to systems of this kind. Since \({\bf v}_{0}=0\), the rate of doing work is zero to first order in the perturbation parameter \(\epsilon\): thus, the energy change must be a quadratic, not linear, function of the perturbation strength. To go to quadratic order, we write \({\bf v}({\bf x},t)=\epsilon{\bf v}_{1}({\bf x},t)\), so

\[{{\rm d}E\over{\rm d}t}=-\epsilon^{2}\int{\rm d}^{3}{\bf x}\,\rho_{0}{\bf v}_{ 1}\cdot{\mathbf{\nabla}}\Phi_{\rm e};\]

note that we have replaced the density \(\rho({\bf x},t)\) by its unperturbed value \(\rho_{0}({\bf x})\) since we are dropping terms of order \(\epsilon^{3}\).

The linearized Euler equation (5.24b) reads

\[{\partial{\bf v}_{1}\over\partial t}=-{\mathbf{\nabla}}(h_{1}+\Phi_{\rm s1}+\Phi_ {\rm e}).\]

We eliminate \(\Phi_{\rm e}\) from equations (5.109) and (5.110) to obtain

\[{{\rm d}E\over{\rm d}t}=\epsilon^{2}\int{\rm d}^{3}{\bf x}\,\rho_{0}{\bf v}_{ 1}\cdot\left[{\partial{\bf v}_{1}\over\partial t}+{\mathbf{\nabla}}(\Phi_{\rm s1} +h_{1})\right].\]

The contribution of the first term in square brackets can be rewritten as

\[\epsilon^{2}\int{\rm d}^{3}{\bf x}\,\rho_{0}{\bf v}_{1}\cdot{\partial{\bf v}_{ 1}\over\partial t}={{1\over 2}}\epsilon^{2}{{\rm d}\over{\rm d}t}\int{\rm d}^{3}{ \bf x}\,\rho_{0}{\bf v}_{1}^{2}.\]

To evaluate the second and third terms, we apply the divergence theorem (B.45)--the boundary terms vanish, since \(\rho_{0}=0\) at large distances--and use the linearized continuity equation (5.24a):

\[\eqalign{\epsilon^{2}\int{\rm d}^{3}{\bf x}\,\rho_{0}{\bf v}_{1}\cdot{\mathbf{ \nabla}}(\Phi_{\rm s1}+h_{1})&=-\epsilon^{2}\int{\rm d}^{3}{\bf x}\,(\Phi_{ \rm s1}+h_{1}){\mathbf{\nabla}}\cdot(\rho_{0}{\bf v}_{1})\cr&=\,\epsilon^{2}\int{ \rm d}^{3}{\bf x}\,(\Phi_{\rm s1}+h_{1}){\partial\rho_{\rm s1}\over\partial t }.}\]Using the linearized equation of state (5.24d) we can rewrite the term involving the enthalpy as

\[\eqalign{\epsilon^{2}\int{\rm d}^{3}{\bf x}\,h_{1}{\partial\rho_{\rm s1}\over \partial t}&=\epsilon^{2}\int{\rm d}^{3}{\bf x}\left({{\rm d}p\over{\rm d}\rho} \right)_{\rho_{0}}{\rho_{\rm s1}\over\rho_{0}}{\partial\rho_{\rm s1}\over \partial t}\cr&={{1\over 2}}\epsilon^{2}{{\rm d}\over{\rm d}t}\int{{\rm d}^{3}{\bf x} \over\rho_{0}}\left({{\rm d}p\over{\rm d}\rho}\right)_{\rho_{0}}\rho_{\rm s1}^{ 2}.}\]

The gravitational potential is related to the density by Poisson's equation,

\[\Phi_{\rm s1}({\bf x},t)=-G\int{\rm d}^{3}{\bf x}^{\prime}\,{\rho_{\rm s1}({ \bf x}^{\prime},t)\over|{\bf x}-{\bf x}^{\prime}|};\]

thus the term in equation (5.113) involving the potential can be written

\[\eqalign{\epsilon^{2}\int{\rm d}^{3}{\bf x}\,{\partial\rho_{\rm s1}\over \partial t}\Phi_{\rm s1}&=-\epsilon^{2}G\int{\rm d}^{3}{\bf x}\,{\rm d}^{3}{ \bf x}^{\prime}\,{\partial\rho_{\rm s1}({\bf x},t)\over\partial t}{\rho_{\rm s 1}({\bf x}^{\prime},t)\over|{\bf x}-{\bf x}^{\prime}|}\cr&=-\epsilon^{2}G\int {\rm d}^{3}{\bf x}\,{\rm d}^{3}{\bf x}^{\prime}\,{\partial\rho_{\rm s1}({\bf x }^{\prime},t)\over\partial t}{\rho_{\rm s1}({\bf x},t)\over|{\bf x}-{\bf x}^{ \prime}|},}\]

where in the last equation we have simply exchanged the integration variables \({\bf x}\) and \({\bf x}^{\prime}\). Averaging the two right sides of the last equation gives

\[-{{1\over 2}}\epsilon^{2}G{{\rm d}\over{\rm d}t}\int{\rm d}^{3}{\bf x}\,{ \rm d}^{3}{\bf x}^{\prime}\,{\rho_{\rm s1}({\bf x},t)\rho_{\rm s1}({\bf x}^{ \prime},t)\over|{\bf x}-{\bf x}^{\prime}|}.\]

Combining equations (5.112), (5.114), and (5.117) we have

\[{{\rm d}E\over{\rm d}t}={{1\over 2}}\epsilon^{2}{{\rm d}\over{\rm d}t} \bigg{(}\int{\rm d}^{3}{\bf x}\,\rho_{0}v_{1}^{2}+\int{{\rm d}^{3}{\bf x}\over \rho_{0}}\left|{{\rm d}p\over{\rm d}\rho}\right|_{\rho_{0}}\rho_{\rm s1}^{2}- G\int{\rm d}^{3}{\bf x}\,{\rm d}^{3}{\bf x}^{\prime}\,{\rho_{\rm s1}\rho_{\rm s1}^{ \prime}\over|{\bf x}-{\bf x}^{\prime}|}\bigg{)},\]

where we have replaced \({\rm d}p/{\rm d}\rho\) by \(|{\rm d}p/{\rm d}\rho|\) since \({\rm d}p/{\rm d}\rho\) is equal to the square of the sound speed (eq. 5.22), which must be positive for any realistic equation of state. Hydrostatic equilibrium requires \({\bf\nabla}p_{0}=-\rho_{0}{\bf\nabla}\Phi_{0}\) (eq. 5.17), so \({\rm d}p_{0}=-\rho_{0}{\rm d}\Phi_{0}\) and \(|{\rm d}p/{\rm d}\rho|_{\rho_{0}}\) can be replaced by \(\rho_{0}|{\rm d}\Phi/{\rm d}\rho|_{0}\).

Finally, assuming that the perturbations \(E\), \({\bf v}_{1}\) and \(\rho_{\rm s1}\) all vanish as \(t\to-\infty\), we can integrate with respect to time to obtain our final expression for the energy,

\[\eqalign{E={{1\over 2}}\epsilon^{2}\bigg{[}&\int{\rm d}^{3}{\bf x}\,\rho_{0}({ \bf x}){\bf v}_{1}^{2}({\bf x},t)+\int{\rm d}^{3}{\bf x}\left|{{\rm d}\Phi\over {\rm d}\rho}\right|_{0}\rho_{\rm s1}^{2}({\bf x},t)\cr&-G\int{\rm d}^{3}{\bf x} \,{\rm d}^{3}{\bf x}^{\prime}\,{\rho_{\rm s1}({\bf x},t)\rho_{\rm s1}({\bf x}^ {\prime},t)\over|{\bf x}-{\bf x}^{\prime}|}\bigg{]}.}\]Notice that we have successfully derived an expression for the energy that is correct to order using the linearized equations (5.24), which are correct only to order. Calculating the second-order perturbations in the density, velocity, etc., proved to be unnecessary for this task.

Now in any unstable mode, all of the perturbed quantities such as and grow as,. Therefore all of the terms on the right side of equation (5.119) grow as. However, the energy is constant and cannot grow; hence _the energy of any unstable mode must be zero_. Since the first term on the right side is positive, the sum of the second and third terms must be negative. Thus a sufficient condition for stability is that the sum of these two terms is non-negative for all functions. This condition can be sharpened by restricting the range of possible functions for to those for which, since mass must be conserved in any physical perturbation. Thus we arrive at

**Chandrasekhar's variational principle:**_A barotropic fluid in static equilibrium with is stable if the quantity_

(5.120)

_is non-negative for all real functions that conserve the total mass,._

The constraint that the trial functions must conserve the mass can be incorporated automatically by writing (eq. F.5) and examining arbitrary displacement vector fields. The expression (5.120) is zero for _constant_, as it must be, since this corresponds to a uniform displacement of the entire system (Problem 5.14).

This variational principle was first stated by Chandrasekhar (1963, 1964). The condition is necessary as well as sufficient for stability (Laval, Mercier, & Pellat 1965; Kulsrud & Mark 1970).

#### The energy principle for stellar systems

In stellar systems, the energy principle is most useful if the equilibrium df is ergodic, that is, if it can be written in the form, where is the Hamiltonian for motion in the unperturbed potential (SS4.2.1a). We restrict ourselves to systems of this kind; in practice these normally have spherical or plane-parallel symmetry (Box 4.1). We shall also assume that everywhere, a condition that is satisfied by most realistic models of stellar systems.

By analogy with equation (5.108), the rate at which an external potential does work on a stellar system is

(5.121)where, as usual in this chapter, the df\(f({\bf x},{\bf v},t)\) is defined to be the phase-space mass density.

Since \(\epsilon\ll 1\) we can expand the df in powers of \(\epsilon\), \(f({\bf x},{\bf v},t)=f_{0}(H_{0})+\epsilon f_{1}({\bf x},{\bf v},t)+\cdots\). The contribution of \(f_{0}\) to \({\rm d}E/{\rm d}t\) is zero, since \(H_{0}\) is an even function of \({\bf v}\) while \({\bf v}\cdot\mathbf{\nabla}\Phi_{\rm e}\) is odd. Thus the dominant energy change is

\[\frac{{\rm d}E}{{\rm d}t}=-\epsilon^{2}\int{\rm d}^{3}{\bf x}\,{\rm d}^{3}{\bf v }\,f_{1}({\bf x},{\bf v},t){\bf v}\cdot\mathbf{\nabla}\Phi_{\rm e}\ ; \tag{5.122}\]

once again the energy change depends quadratically on the perturbation strength.

To evaluate this formula we use the linearized collisionless Boltzmann equation (5.13),

\[\begin{split} 0&=\frac{\partial f_{1}}{\partial t}+[f_{1},H_{0}]+[f_{0},\Phi_{\rm s1}+\Phi_{\rm e}]\\ &=\frac{\partial f_{1}}{\partial t}+[f_{1},H_{0}]-f_{0}^{\prime} (H_{0}){\bf v}\cdot\mathbf{\nabla}(\Phi_{\rm s1}+\Phi_{\rm e}).\end{split} \tag{5.123}\]

We may now eliminate \(\Phi_{\rm e}\) from equation (5.122):

\[\frac{{\rm d}E}{{\rm d}t}=-\epsilon^{2}\int\frac{{\rm d}^{3}{\bf x}\,{\rm d}^{ 3}{\bf v}}{f_{0}^{\prime}(H_{0})}f_{1}\left(\frac{\partial f_{1}}{\partial t }+[f_{1},H_{0}]\right)+\epsilon^{2}\int{\rm d}^{3}{\bf x}\,{\rm d}^{3}{\bf v}f _{1}\,{\bf v}\cdot\mathbf{\nabla}\Phi_{\rm s1}. \tag{5.124}\]

The middle term can be written

\[\int\frac{{\rm d}^{3}{\bf x}\,{\rm d}^{3}{\bf v}}{f_{0}^{\prime}(H_{0})}f_{1}[ f_{1},H_{0}]=\tfrac{1}{2}\int\frac{{\rm d}^{3}{\bf x}\,{\rm d}^{3}{\bf v}}{f_{0}^ {\prime}(H_{0})}[f_{1}^{2},H_{0}]. \tag{5.125}\]

Furthermore,

\[\frac{1}{f_{0}^{\prime}(H_{0})}[f_{1}^{2},H_{0}]=[f_{1}^{2},h(H_{0})] \tag{5.126}\]

where \(h(H_{0})\equiv\int^{H_{0}}{\rm d}x/f_{0}^{\prime}(x)\) (eq. 5.210c). Now since \(f_{1}\) vanishes at infinity, we know that \(\int{\rm d}^{3}{\bf x}{\rm d}^{3}{\bf v}\,[f_{1}^{2},h(H_{0})]=0\) (eq. 5.210a). Thus equation (5.125) is zero, and equation (5.124) simplifies to

\[\frac{{\rm d}E}{{\rm d}t}=-\tfrac{1}{2}\epsilon^{2}\int\frac{{\rm d}^{3}{\bf x }\,{\rm d}^{3}{\bf v}}{f_{0}^{\prime}(H_{0})}\frac{\partial f_{1}^{2}}{ \partial t}-\epsilon^{2}\int{\rm d}^{3}{\bf x}\,\Phi_{\rm s1}\mathbf{ \nabla}\cdot\int{\rm d}^{3}{\bf v}\,f_{1}{\bf v}, \tag{5.127}\]

where the divergence theorem (B.45) has been used on the second term (the boundary terms vanish since the df vanishes at large distances).

We next integrate the linearized collisionless Boltzmann equation (5.123) over velocity, to obtain an equation analogous to the Jeans continuity equation (4.204),

\[\frac{\partial\rho_{\rm s1}}{\partial t}+\mathbf{\nabla}\cdot\int{ \rm d}^{3}{\bf v}f_{1}{\bf v}=0, \tag{5.128}\]where \(\rho_{\rm s1}=\int{\rm d}^{3}{\bf v}\,f_{1}\). Therefore equation (5.127) can be rewritten as

\[\frac{{\rm d}E}{{\rm d}t}=-\tfrac{1}{2}\epsilon^{2}\frac{{\rm d}}{{\rm d}t}\int \frac{{\rm d}^{3}{\bf x}\,{\rm d}^{3}{\bf v}}{f^{\prime}_{0}(H_{0})}f_{1}^{2}+ \epsilon^{2}\int{\rm d}^{3}{\bf x}\,\frac{\partial\rho_{\rm s1}}{\partial t} \Phi_{\rm s1}. \tag{5.129}\]

Since \(f^{\prime}(H_{0})<0\) by assumption, we may replace it with \(-|f^{\prime}(H_{0})|\). Following the logic that took us from equation (5.116) to (5.117), and integrating with respect to time, we finally obtain

\[E=\tfrac{1}{2}\epsilon^{2}\left[\int\frac{{\rm d}^{3}{\bf x}\,{\rm d}^{3}{\bf v }}{|f^{\prime}_{0}(H_{0})|}f_{1}^{2}({\bf x},{\bf v},t)-G\int{\rm d}^{3}{\bf x }\,{\rm d}^{3}{\bf x}^{\prime}\frac{\rho_{\rm s1}({\bf x},t)\rho_{\rm s1}({ \bf x}^{\prime},t)}{|{\bf x}-{\bf x}^{\prime}|}\right]. \tag{5.130}\]

As for a fluid system, the energy of any unstable mode must be zero. Thus a sufficient condition for stability is that \(E\) is always positive (or always negative; but this cannot occur since \(E\) is positive for any \(f_{1}\) that is an odd function of \({\bf v}\)--the first term in 5.130 is positive and the second is zero). Thus we have the

**Variational principle for stellar systems:**_A stellar system having an ergodic df\(f_{0}(H_{0})\) with \(f^{\prime}_{0}(H_{0})<0\) is stable if the quantity_

\[W[f_{1}]\equiv\int\frac{{\rm d}^{3}{\bf x}\,{\rm d}^{3}{\bf v}}{|f^{\prime}_{ 0}(H_{0})|}f_{1}^{2}({\bf x},{\bf v})-G\int\frac{{\rm d}^{3}{\bf x}\,{\rm d}^{ 3}{\bf v}\,{\rm d}^{3}{\bf x}^{\prime}{\rm d}^{3}{\bf v}^{\prime}}{|{\bf x}-{ \bf x}^{\prime}|}f_{1}({\bf x},{\bf v})f_{1}({\bf x}^{\prime},{\bf v}^{\prime}) \tag{5.131}\]

_is non-negative for all functions \(f_{1}({\bf x},{\bf v})\) that conserve the total mass, that is, \(\int{\rm d}^{3}{\bf x}\,{\rm d}^{3}{\bf v}\,f_{1}({\bf x},{\bf v})=0\). As in the case of fluid systems, the trivial perturbation \(f_{1}=-\mathbf{\xi}\cdot\mathbf{\nabla}f_{0}\) corresponds to a uniform displacement of the entire system and yields \(W[f_{1}]=0\)._

We can sharpen this result, because not all trial functions \(f_{1}\) are physically plausible. In particular, \(f_{1}\) must arise from the displacement of stars from their original phase-space positions and by analogy with equation (F.5) we may write

\[\epsilon f_{1}=-\frac{\partial}{\partial{\bf x}}\cdot(f_{0}\Delta{\bf x})- \frac{\partial}{\partial{\bf v}}\cdot(f_{0}\Delta{\bf v}). \tag{5.132}\]

Furthermore the displacements \((\Delta{\bf x},\Delta{\bf v})\) must be due to the motion of the stars under the influence of some Hamiltonian. The time-evolution operator that describes Hamiltonian motion is canonical (see discussion on page 803), so the transformation from the original phase-space position \(({\bf x},{\bf v})\) to the new position \(({\bf x}^{\prime},{\bf v}^{\prime})\equiv({\bf x}+\Delta{\bf x},{\bf v}+\Delta {\bf v})\) is a canonical transformation. The generating function for this transformation can be written \(S({\bf x},{\bf v}^{\prime})={\bf x}\cdot{\bf v}^{\prime}+\epsilon g({\bf x},{ \bf v}^{\prime})\). Using equations (D.93) we have

\[{\bf x}^{\prime}={\bf x}+\epsilon\frac{\partial g}{\partial{\bf v}^{\prime}} \quad;\quad{\bf v}={\bf v}^{\prime}+\epsilon\frac{\partial g}{\partial{\bf x}}, \tag{5.133}\]or

\[\Delta{\bf x}=\epsilon\frac{\partial g({\bf x},{\bf v})}{\partial{\bf v}}\quad; \quad\Delta{\bf v}=-\epsilon\frac{\partial g({\bf x},{\bf v})}{\partial{\bf x}}, \tag{5.134}\]

where we have replaced \({\bf v}^{\prime}\) by \({\bf v}\) on the right side of these equations since the difference between the two velocities is small.

Equation (5.132) now becomes

\[f_{1}=-\frac{\partial}{\partial{\bf x}}f_{0}\frac{\partial g}{\partial{\bf v}} +\frac{\partial}{\partial{\bf v}}f_{0}\frac{\partial g}{\partial{\bf x}}=[g,f _{0}]. \tag{5.135}\]

Moreover, all functions of this form conserve total mass, \(\int{\rm d}^{3}{\bf x}\,{\rm d}^{3}{\bf v}\,f_{1}=0\) (by eq. 5.210a). The condition (5.135) severely restricts the allowable trial functions \(f_{1}\), and with this restriction Antonov (1960) was able to show that the variational principle (5.131) provided both necessary and sufficient conditions for stability. Thus we have

**Antonov's variational principle:**_A stellar system having an ergodic df \(f_{0}(H_{0})\) with \(f_{0}^{\prime}(H_{0})<0\) is stable if and only if the quantity_

\[W_{\rm A}[g]\equiv\int\frac{{\rm d}^{3}{\bf x}\,{\rm d}^{3}{\bf v}}{|f_{0}^{ \prime}(H_{0})|}[g,f_{0}]^{2}-G\int\frac{{\rm d}^{3}{\bf x}\,{\rm d}^{3}{\bf v }\,{\rm d}^{3}{\bf x}^{\prime}{\rm d}^{3}{\bf v}^{\prime}}{|{\bf x}-{\bf x}^{ \prime}|}[g,f_{0}]_{{\bf x},{\bf v}}[g,f_{0}]_{{\bf x}^{\prime},{\bf v}^{ \prime}} \tag{5.136}\]

_is non-negative for all functions \(g({\bf x},{\bf v})\)._

We have already argued that \(W_{\rm A}[g]\geq 0\) is _sufficient_ for stability. Antonov's original proof of this result takes a different but instructive route, which is traced in Problem 5.15. The proof that \(W_{\rm A}[g]\geq 0\) is _necessary_ for stability is more delicate. Antonov's proof assumed that the stellar system has a complete set of modes, i.e., that every perturbation can be written as a sum of modes, but this assumption is difficult to justify for a stellar system (see, for example, Box 5.1). An alternative and more satisfactory proof is given by Kulsrud & Mark (1970); for a general discussion of energy-based criteria for stability of stellar systems see Bartholomew (1971) and references therein.

A simple corollary of Antonov's variational principle, proved in Problem 5.15, is that _the modes of a stellar system having an ergodic df \(f_{0}(H_{0})\) with \(f_{0}^{\prime}(H_{0})<0\) have frequency \(\omega\) such that \(\omega^{2}\) is real_. In other words the modes either have real frequencies, corresponding to undamped oscillations, or imaginary frequencies, corresponding to growing modes. There are no growing oscillations (overstabilities).

Finally, we state without proof

**Goodman's variational principle:**_An equilibrium stellar system in which the df is invariant under velocity reversal, \(f({\bf x},{\bf v})=f({\bf x},-{\bf v})\), and the potential \(\Phi_{0}({\bf x})\) is integrable is unstable if the quantity_

\[\frac{-\int{\rm d}^{3}{\bf x}\,{\rm d}^{3}{\bf x}^{\prime}\Phi^{*}({\bf x}) \widetilde{P}({\bf x},{\bf x}^{\prime},{\rm i}s)\rho({\bf x}^{\prime})}{-\int{ \rm d}^{3}{\bf x}\,\Phi^{*}({\bf x})\rho({\bf x})}>1 \tag{5.137}\]_for any \(s>0\). The restrictions on the trial functions \(\rho({\bf x})\), \(\Phi({\bf x})\) are that \(\int{\rm d}^{3}{\bf x}\,\rho({\bf x})=0\), and \(\Phi({\bf x})\) is the gravitational potential corresponding to an isolated mass distribution with density \(\rho({\bf x})\)._

The proof is given by Goodman (1988). Goodman's variational principle is mainly important for the conceptual insight it provides rather than as a practical tool for determining stability--it requires the polarization function, and once this is known it is not too hard to calculate the modes of the system directly rather than through a variational principle. Loosely speaking, Goodman's principle formalizes the intuitive feeling that a system is unstable if the response \(\rho_{\rm s1}\) is greater than the stimulus \(\rho_{1}\).

**5.4.3 The relation between the stability of fluid and stellar systems**

The analogy between Antonov's variational principle (5.136) for stellar systems and Chandrasekhar's variational principle (5.120) for fluids can be made precise by

**Antonov's first law:**_A stellar system having an ergodic df \(f_{0}(H_{0})\) with \(f_{0}^{\prime}(H_{0})<0\) is stable if the barotropic star with the same equilibrium density distribution is stable._

To prove this, consider the function

\[\rho_{1}({\bf x})=\int{\rm d}^{3}{\bf v}\,[g,f_{0}]. \tag{5.138}\]

We now use Schwarz's inequality (B.75), with \(A=|f_{0}^{\prime}(H_{0})|^{1/2}\) and \(B=[g,f_{0}]/|f_{0}^{\prime}(H_{0})|^{1/2}\), which yields

\[\int{\rm d}^{3}{\bf v}\,\frac{[g,f_{0}]^{2}}{|f_{0}^{\prime}(H_{0})|}\geq \frac{\left(\int{\rm d}^{3}{\bf v}\,[g,f_{0}]\right)^{2}}{\int{\rm d}^{3}{\bf v }\,|f_{0}^{\prime}(H_{0})|}=\frac{\rho_{1}^{2}}{\int{\rm d}^{3}{\bf v}\,|f_{0} ^{\prime}(H_{0})|}\,. \tag{5.139}\]

The denominator on the right side of (5.139) can be related to the unperturbed density \(\rho_{0}(\Phi_{0})=\int{\rm d}^{3}{\bf v}\,f_{0}(\frac{1}{2}v^{2}+\Phi_{0})\) by differentiating both sides of the latter expression with respect to \(\Phi_{0}\):

\[\left(\frac{{\rm d}\rho}{{\rm d}\Phi}\right)_{0}=\int{\rm d}^{3}{\bf v}\,f_{0} ^{\prime}(H_{0})=-\int{\rm d}^{3}{\bf v}\,\left|f_{0}^{\prime}(H_{0})\right|. \tag{5.140}\]

Combining equations (5.136)-(5.140), we obtain

\[W_{\rm A}[g]\geq\int{\rm d}^{3}{\bf x}\left|\frac{{\rm d}\Phi}{{\rm d}\rho} \right|_{0}\rho_{1}^{2}-G\int\frac{{\rm d}^{3}{\bf x}\,{\rm d}^{3}{\bf x}^{ \prime}}{|{\bf x}-{\bf x}^{\prime}|}\rho_{1}({\bf x})\rho_{1}({\bf x}^{\prime}). \tag{5.141}\]

If the barotropic fluid with density \(\rho_{0}(r)\) is stable, then the right side is non-negative by Chandrasekhar's variational principle. Hence \(W_{\rm A}[g]\geq 0\) and the stellar system is stable by Antonov's variational principle.

### 5.5 The response of spherical systems

The tools developed in SS5.3 can be used to investigate the linear response and stability of a wide variety of stellar systems, so long as the Hamiltonian that describes motion in the equilibrium potential is integrable. The simplest of these are plane-parallel and spherical stellar systems. Plane-parallel systems are mostly used to model the properties of galactic disks in the direction normal to the galactic plane; an example is the isothermal sheet described in Problem 4.21. For stability analyses of plane-parallel systems see Kulsrud & Mark (1970), Mark (1971), Antonov (1971), Kalnajs (1973a), Fridman & Polyachenko (1984), Araki (1985), Mathur (1990), and Weinberg (1991b). For the sake of brevity, in this section we focus exclusively on spherical systems.

As shown in Box 5.2, the modes of spherical fluid or stellar systems can always be chosen to have density distributions with angular dependence proportional to a spherical harmonic \({\rm Y}_{l}^{m}(\vartheta,\phi)\), and the frequency of the mode associated with \((l,m)\) is independent of \(m\). Modes with \(l=m=0\) are spherically symmetry and hence are called **radial** modes, while modes with \(l\geq 1\) are called **non-radial**.

#### The stability of spherical systems with ergodic DFs

We begin by examining the stability of spherical systems in which the equilibrium df is ergodic, \(f_{0}=f_{0}(H_{0})=f_{0}[{1\over 2}v^{2}+\Phi_{0}(r)]\). Once again we shall exploit the analogy with barotropic fluids.

Chandrasekhar's variational principle (eq. 5.120) can be used to derive a remarkably general result that was proved independently by Antonov (1962b) in the context of stellar systems and by Lebovitz (1965) for stars. This is the

**Antonov-Lebovitz theorem:**_All non-radial modes of a barotropic star with \({\rm d}p(\rho)/{\rm d}\rho>0\) are stable._

The proof is given in Appendix H.

This theorem shows that only radial modes are dangerous for the stability of a spherical star.

Now any spherical stellar system with an ergodic df that satisfies \(f_{0}^{\prime}(H_{0})<0\) must have \({\rm d}\rho_{0}/{\rm d}r<0\)--this follows from equation (5.140) and the observation that \({\rm d}\Phi_{0}/{\rm d}r>0\) since the gravitational force in a spherical system is always radially inward. Thus an immediate consequence of the Antonov-Lebovitz theorem and Antonov's first law is

**Antonov's second law:**_All non-radial modes of a stellar system having an ergodic equilibrium df \(f_{0}(H_{0})\) with \(f_{0}^{\prime}(H_{0})<0\) are stable._

Unfortunately, Antonov's first law is not very helpful when we consider the stability of stellar systems to _radial_ modes. The barotropic analogs ofrealistic stellar systems are usually unstable to radial modes, so the theorem, which provides only sufficient but not necessary conditions for stability, does not constrain the stability of the analogous stellar systems. A much more powerful tool is provided by the

**Doremus-Feix-Baumann theorem:**_All radial modes of a stellar system with an ergodic equilibrium df\(f_{0}(H_{0})\) and \(f_{0}^{\prime}(H_{0})<0\) are stable._

The proof is given in Appendix I.9

Footnote 9: Our discussion here is restricted to linear stability. Nonlinear stability is discussed by Holm et al. (1985) and Rein (2007) among others.

The Doremus-Feix-Baumann theorem, combined with Antonov's second law, shows that almost _all_ realistic spherical stellar systems with an ergodic df are stable. Thus, for example, all of the polytropic stellar systems (SS4.3.3a) with \(n>\frac{3}{2}\) are stable.10 In particular, the Plummer model (\(n=5\)) is stable; this is important because the density distribution in the Plummer model is similar to the density distribution in many real stellar systems. The isothermal sphere (SS4.3.3b) is also stable.

Footnote 10: Polytropes with \(\frac{1}{2}<n<\frac{3}{2}\) are somewhat unrealistic because they have an integrable singularity in \(f_{0}\) at the boundary \({\cal E}=0\). However, numerical experiments (Henon 1973a; Barnes, Goodman, & Hut 1986) indicate that these systems are also stable. Thus \(f_{0}^{\prime}(H_{0})<0\) is _sufficient_ but not _necessary_ for stability.

The ergodic df for the isochrone model (eq. 4.47) is given by equation (4.54); as shown in Figure 4.2 this model has \(f_{0}^{\prime}(H_{0})<0\) and hence is stable, as are the Jaffe and Hernquist models shown in the same figure. The Jaffe and Hernquist models are special cases of the Dehnen models (eq. 2.64 with \(\beta=4\)); it is straightforward to show that all Dehnen models with ergodic df have \(f_{0}^{\prime}(H_{0})<0\) and hence are stable (Tremaine et al. 1994). Finally, the df of King models (eq. 4.110) decrease with increasing energy, so all King models are stable.

#### 5.5.2 The stability of anisotropic spherical systems

In most spherical stellar systems the df depends both on energy and angular momentum, \(f_{0}=f_{0}(H_{0},L)\). In such systems, the variational principles that we have proved cannot be used to establish stability to non-radial modes. However, a modified version of these principles can still be applied to radial modes. To show this, we note that in this case \(\mathbf{\nabla}\Phi_{1}=\hat{\bf e}_{r}{\rm d}\Phi_{1}/{\rm d}r\), so

\[-\mathbf{\nabla}\Phi_{1}\cdot\frac{\partial f_{0}}{\partial{\bf v}}= -\frac{\partial\Phi_{1}}{\partial r}\frac{\partial f_{0}}{\partial v_{r}}=- \frac{\partial\Phi_{1}}{\partial r}v_{r}\frac{\partial f_{0}}{\partial H_{0}}. \tag{5.142}\]

When we substitute this expression into the left side of the linearized collisionless Boltzmann equation (5.13), no partial derivatives of the form \(\partial f_{0}/\partial L\) appear. Hence all of the steps leading to the variational principles (5.131) and (5.136) can be carried out by simply replacing \({\rm d}f_{0}/{\rm d}H_{0}\) by \(\partial f_{0}/\partial H_{0}\) wherever it appears, and the Doremus-Feix-Baumann theorem can be extended to:_All radial modes of a spherical stellar system with \(\partial f_{0}/\partial H_{0}<0\) are stable._

For example, all Michie models (eq. 4.117) are stable to radial modes, as are the constant-anisotropy Hernquist models with \(\beta=\pm\frac{1}{2}\) (eqs. 4.69 and 4.72).

The stability of anisotropic spherical systems to non-radial modes is more complicated. In contrast to ergodic systems, which are generally stable to non-radial perturbations because of Antonov's second law, anisotropic systems with predominantly radial orbits are susceptible to the **radial-orbit instability**. As an example, Figure 5.4 shows the evolution of an initially spherical Hernquist model with anisotropy radius \(r_{\rm a}/a=0.3\). There is a strong instability, which leads to a triaxial or bar-like final state, and persists in models with \(r_{\rm a}/a\simeq 1\).

**Physical basis of the radial-orbit instability** In a smooth, spherical galactic potential, stars can oscillate back and forth through the center on radial orbits. A time exposure of a star on such an orbit would show a bright, straight wire or rod, symmetric about the origin and reaching out to the star's apocenter. The image would be faintest at the center of the galaxy, since the star travels fastest there, and brightest at apocenter, since the stellar velocity is temporarily zero at this point. Because of this geometry, rather than thinking of the orbit as having a single apocenter that rotates by \(\pi\) in one radial period, it is convenient to regard the orbit as having two apocenters on opposite sides of the galaxy, each of them stationary. The orientation can be specified by the azimuthal angle \(\psi_{\rm a}\) of either apocenter.

Next consider a star of energy \(E\) on a nearly radial orbit, with small but

Figure 5.4: Instability in a spherical stellar system with the Hernquist density distribution (eq. 2.64). The df has the Osipkov–Merritt form (§4.3.2c) with anisotropy radius \(r_{\rm a}=0.3a\). The radial-orbit instability converts the initial spherical system on the left to the triaxial one on the right in a few crossing times. From Meza & Zamorano (1997).

non-zero angular momentum \(L\). Each apocenter precesses slowly, at a rate

\[{{\rm d}\psi_{\rm a}\over{\rm d}t}={\Delta\psi-\pi\over T_{r}}=\Omega_{\phi}-{ {1\over 2}}\Omega_{r},\]

where \(\Delta\psi\) is the angle between successive apocenters, defined by equation (3.18b), \(T_{r}\) is the radial period (eq. 3.39b), and \(\Omega_{r}=2\pi/T_{r}\) and \(\Omega_{\phi}\) are the radial and azimuthal frequencies. A sequence of images of the trajectory, with exposure time long compared to \(T_{r}\) but short compared to the precession time \((\psi_{\rm a})^{-1}\), would show a nearly straight wire that rotated slowly around its center at the rate \(\dot{\psi}_{\rm a}\). For nearly radial orbits in non-singular potentials11

Footnote 11: This property does not hold for singular potentials; for example, in the Kepler potential the angle between successive apocenters is \(2\pi\), independent of \(L\). The behavior of \(\Delta\psi\) in singular power-law potentials is discussed in Problem 3.19.

\[\Delta\psi=\pi+p(E)L+{\rm higher\-order\ terms\ in\ }L.\]

Thus the rate of precession of nearly radial orbits is

\[{{\rm d}\psi_{\rm a}\over{\rm d}t}={p(E)\over T_{r}(E)}L+{\rm O}(L^{2}),\]

where \(T_{r}(E)\) is the radial period at zero angular momentum.

For example, in the isochrone potential (2.47), the angle between successive apocenters for an orbit with angular momentum \(L\) is12

Footnote 12: This result follows from equation (3.40), after replacing \(\pi\,{\rm sgn}(L)\) by \(\pi\) since \(\pi\) and \(-\pi\) are equivalent azimuths.

\[\Delta\psi=\pi+{\pi L\over\sqrt{L^{2}+4GMb}},\]

so \(p(E)={{1\over 2}}\pi(GMb)^{-1/2}\).

Now imagine that the star is subjected to a weak non-radial gravitational field. Its angular momentum will change at an average rate \(\dot{L}=N\), where \(N\) is the torque per unit mass exerted by this field, averaged over one radial period. Changes in the angular momentum affect the precession rate through equation (5.145); we have

\[{{\rm d}^{2}\psi_{\rm a}\over{\rm d}t^{2}}\simeq{{\rm d}\over{\rm d}t}{p(E) \over T_{r}(E)}L\simeq{p(E)\over T_{r}(E)}N.\]

We have neglected the effect of the torque on the energy; this has a much smaller influence on the precession rate because \(\dot{\psi}_{\rm a}\) is proportional to \(L\) and \(L\) is small.

For comparison, the orientation of a rigid body subjected to the same torque would change at a rate given by

\[\frac{\mathrm{d}^{2}\psi}{\mathrm{d}t^{2}}=\frac{N}{I}, \tag{5.148}\]

where \(I\) is the moment of inertia per unit mass (eq. D.43). Thus we deduce that a near-radial orbit has an effective moment of inertia \(I=T_{r}(E)/p(E)\); for example, in the isochrone potential \(I(E)=2\pi^{-1}T_{r}(E)\sqrt{GMb}\). In contrast to rigid bodies, the effective moment of inertia of nearly radial orbits can be negative, although it is positive for most galactic potentials (i.e., nearly radial orbits precess in the same direction that they revolve).13

Footnote 13: It is precisely because \(I>0\) for the logarithmic potential \(\Phi_{\mathrm{L}}(x,y)\) (eq. 3.103) that long-axis orbits are stable while short-axis orbits are unstable.

The mass distribution in a spherical galaxy composed of stars on nearly radial orbits can be regarded as a collection of wires, resembling a hedgehog or porcupine. The wires have various lengths and orientations, and precess at different rates, but on average the mass distribution is spherical so there are no significant non-radial gravitational forces. Now suppose that for some reason the wires in a small solid angle are squeezed together, forming a clump with slightly higher density than the rest of the sphere. The enhanced gravitational force from the clump tends to attract more wires, whose additional mass promotes the growth of this clump. This tendency is counterbalanced by the precession of the wires in random directions, which tends to disperse the clump before it has time to grow. This competition between self-gravity and dispersion is the same one that appears in the Jeans instability.

We argued after equation (5.25) that the Jeans instability set in when the sum of the gravitational potential energy and the kinetic energy in a volume of homogeneous fluid became negative. Similarly, an approximate criterion for the radial-orbit instability in a spherical stellar system can be derived by comparing the potential and kinetic energies associated with precessional motion in a cone of opening angle \(\vartheta\). For simplicity we shall assume that the effective moment of inertia \(I>0\). The mass of the cone is \(M\approx\rho r^{3}\vartheta^{2}\) where \(\rho\) and \(r\) are the mean density and radius of the system, and the gravitational potential energy associated with the cone is \(W\approx-GM^{2}/r\approx-G\rho^{2}r^{5}\vartheta^{4}\). The kinetic energy per unit mass associated with precession of the orbits in the cone is \(\frac{1}{2}L^{2}/I\) (cf. eq. D.43), so the total kinetic energy is \(K\approx ML^{2}/I\approx\rho r^{3}\vartheta^{2}L^{2}/I\). Instability sets in when \(K+W\lesssim 0\), or when \(\vartheta^{2}\gtrsim L^{2}/(G\rho r^{2}I)\).

An important difference between the Jeans instability and the radial-orbit instability is that infinite homogeneous systems are _always_ unstable if the scale \(\lambda\) is sufficiently large. In contrast, the angle \(\vartheta\) cannot exceed a value of order unity, so we expect that the system is stable if the typical angular momentum \(L^{2}\gtrsim G\rho r^{2}I\), that is, tangential velocity dispersion suppresses the instability.

The existence of the radial-orbit instability was pointed out by Antonov (1973), who argued that any spherical system composed entirely of stars on radial orbits was unstable, and similar arguments were made by Lynden-Bell (1979) in the context of bar formation in disk galaxies. Polyachenko (1981) demonstrated the presence of the instability in N-body simulations. However, its importance was widely recognized only after it was rediscovered by Barnes (1985). Merritt & Aguilar (1985) and Meza & Zamorano (1997) have investigated the onset of the radial-orbit instability in simulations of Osipkov-Merritt models (SS4.3.2b) with the Dehnen density distribution (2.64); they find that these models are unstable if the anisotropy radius \(r_{\rm a}\) is less than about 40% of the half-mass radius \(r_{\rm h}\). The stability boundary can be determined more precisely by evaluating the complex frequencies of the modes of anisotropic spherical systems, using the Kalnajs matrix method (Polyachenko & Shukhman 1981; Palmer & Papaloizou 1987; Saha 1991; Weinberg 1991a; Saha 1992; see Merritt 1999 for a review).

The radial-orbit instability is sensitive to the details of the df near \(L=0\) and the potential near the center: _any_ spherical system in which the df is unbounded as \(L\to 0\) is unstable if the potential is smooth (Palmer & Papaloizou 1987); conversely, a small central point mass can suppress the instability in otherwise unstable systems (Palmer & Papaloizou 1988).

The radial-orbit instability is important because the dark halos of galaxies are believed to form hierarchically from the dissipationless collapse and merging of smaller sub-units (SS9.2), and this process tends to produce systems with radially biased orbits. Thus it is likely that the radial-orbit instability operates during the collapse process. As this argument would suggest, N-body simulations of dark-halo formation in a cosmological context usually yield triaxial final states (Dubinski & Carlberg 1991; Bullock 2002; Bailin & Steinmetz 2005).

#### Landau damping and resonances in spherical systems

Stability theory addresses only one aspect of how a stellar system responds to external forces. A natural next question is whether a stable stellar system can sustain undamped oscillations, that is, can it ring like a bell? The answer is not obvious, since two simple systems we have relied on for guidance give quite different answers: the response of the infinite homogeneous stellar system that we analyzed in SS5.2.4 is strongly damped at all wavenumbers greater than the Jeans wavenumber \(k_{\rm J}\), while barotropic self-gravitating systems (e.g., stars) exhibit a rich spectrum of undamped oscillations.

To examine this question, let us focus initially on radial oscillations of a spherical stellar system. The stars in such a system have radial frequencies \(\Omega_{r}\) that lie in the range \(\Omega_{r,{\rm min}}\) to \(\Omega_{r,{\rm max}}\). The minimum radial frequency is non-zero so long as the system has finite extent, and the maximum radial frequency will be finite so long as the system has a smooth core.

Assume that the perturbed gravitational potential oscillates radially at some real frequency \(\omega\). The orbital radius of a star with actions \({\bf J}\) oscillates with frequency \(\Omega_{r}({\bf J})\) and its harmonics \(k\Omega_{r}({\bf J})\), where \(k\) is an integer. Consequently the oscillation of the stellar system resonates with stars that satisfy the condition

\[k\Omega_{r}({\bf J})=\omega. \tag{5.149}\]

Resonant stars damp the oscillations of their host stellar system, because energy is transferred from the oscillation to the resonant stars, just as oscillations in an infinite homogeneous stellar system decay through Landau damping when the resonance condition (5.71) is satisfied. The collection of resonant stars can be thought of as a singular van Kampen mode, in which the perturbation is restricted to the surface of action space on which the resonant condition (5.149) is satisfied (Vandervoort 2003).

We define the **resonant spectrum** to be the set of all frequencies \(\omega\) at which resonant stars are present. All oscillations with frequencies in the resonant spectrum are damped. Normally, the resonant spectrum is continuous. However, there may be frequencies that are not in the resonant spectrum. In particular, there is a gap in the spectrum for \(-\Omega_{r,{\rm min}}<\omega<\Omega_{r,{\rm min}}\), and other gaps may exist if \(|k|\Omega_{r,{\rm max}}<(|k|+1)\Omega_{r,{\rm min}}\) for integer \(k\). Oscillations with frequencies in these gaps will not be damped, since they are not in resonance with any stars in the system.

A more careful version of this argument is provided by Mathur (1990), who also proves that at least some spherical systems have radial modes with frequencies in the gaps of the continuous resonant spectrum. Thus spherical stellar systems _can_ ring like a bell, although in most cases they do not.

These arguments can be extended to non-radial oscillations. In this case the general resonance condition is

\[{\bf m}\cdot{\bf\Omega}({\bf J})=\omega, \tag{5.150}\]

where \({\bf m}\) is an integer triple and \({\bf\Omega}({\bf J})=\partial H_{0}/\partial{\bf J}\) (eq. 3.190). In spherical systems we may choose the actions \({\bf J}=(J_{1},J_{2},J_{3})\) to be respectively the \(z\)-component of angular momentum, the total angular momentum, and the radial action (Table 3.1). In this case \(\Omega_{1}=0\), \(\Omega_{2}=\Omega_{\vartheta}\) is the azimuthal frequency, and \(\Omega_{3}=\Omega_{r}\) is the radial frequency.

The gravitational potential arising from a mode of a spherical system can be written in the form \(\Phi_{1}({\bf x},t)=f(r){\rm Y}_{l}^{m}(\vartheta,\phi)\exp(-{\rm i}\omega t)\) (see Box 5.2). When this potential is written as a Fourier series in angle-action variables, \(\Phi_{1}({\bf x},t)=\sum_{\bf m}\Phi_{\bf m}({\bf J})\exp[{\rm i}({\bf m}\cdot \mathbf{\theta}-\omega t)]\), the only non-zero terms have \(m_{1}=m\) and \(|m_{2}|\leq l\).14 Thus the resonant condition (5.150) for perturbations of order \(l\) can be written

Footnote 14: The relation \(m_{1}=m\) follows because an azimuthal rotation by \(\Delta\phi\) changes \(\theta_{1}\) by \(\Delta\phi\) but leaves \(\theta_{2}\) and \(\theta_{3}\) unaffected; thus the two forms of \(\Phi_{1}\) change by \(\exp({\rm i}m\Delta\phi)\) and \(\exp({\rm i}m_{1}\Delta\phi)\). Since these two changes must be the same, \(m=m_{1}\). The relation \(|m_{2}|\leq l\) is not in the resonant spectrum.

\[m_{2}\Omega_{\vartheta}+m_{3}\Omega_{r}=\omega\qquad(|m_{2}|\leq l). \tag{5.151}\]For \(l=0\) the oscillations are radial, \(m_{1}=m_{2}=0\), and we recover the resonant condition (5.149) with \(m_{3}=k\). Oscillations with \(l=1\) shift the core of the galaxy relative to its outer parts, and are sometimes called "sloshing" or "seiche" oscillations (see Problem 5.17).

Using the Kalnajs matrix method (SS5.3.2), Weinberg (1994b) has shown that many spherical stellar systems exhibit low-frequency seiche oscillations that decay very slowly, having damping times of tens to hundreds of crossing times. Thus seiche oscillations may dominate the appearance of a galaxy long after the other effects of a disturbance have died away. Moreover the simple geometry and low frequency of seiche oscillations imply that they are easily excited by the strong tidal forces that are present during galaxy mergers (SS8.5).

### 5.6 The stability of uniformly rotating systems

Stability analyses are more difficult for rotating systems than for spherical systems, for two main reasons. First, since rotating systems are usually flattened rather than spherically symmetric, both the equilibrium gravitational field corresponding to a given density distribution and the behavior of orbits in that field are more difficult to determine than in the spherical case. Second, and equally fundamental, the system now has a reservoir of rotational kinetic energy to feed any possible unstable modes.

Because of these complications, there are few general stability theorems for rotating stellar systems. Instead, we must develop our insight using simple models, N-body simulations, and numerical mode calculations. In this chapter we restrict ourselves to uniformly rotating systems, deferring the discussion of differentially rotating systems to the next chapter.

There is a rich literature, dating back to Newton, on uniformly rotating, self-gravitating fluid systems. As in the case of spherical systems, these provide useful analogs to rotating stellar systems. A brief summary of this classic topic in applied mathematics is given in SS5.6.3.

#### The uniformly rotating sheet

We begin by investigating a fluid model that exhibits the effects of rotation and a flattened geometry in the simplest possible way. Our model consists of an infinite disk or sheet of zero thickness and constant surface density \(\Sigma_{0}\)The sheet occupies the plane \(z=0\), is uniform in the \(x\)- and \(y\)-directions, and rotates with constant angular velocity \(\mathbf{\Omega}=\Omega\hat{\mathbf{e}}_{z}\).

We consider the response of the sheet to disturbances in its own plane. We do not examine bending or corrugation modes, deferring these to SS6.6. The analysis is simplified if we work in a frame that rotates with the unperturbed sheet at \(\mathbf{\Omega}\); thus the continuity equation (5.16), Euler's equation (5.17), and Poisson's equation (5.18) read

\[\frac{\partial\Sigma_{\mathrm{d}}}{\partial t}+\mathbf{\nabla}\cdot(\Sigma_{ \mathrm{d}}\mathbf{v})=0, \tag{5.152a}\] \[\frac{\partial\mathbf{v}}{\partial t}+(\mathbf{v}\cdot\mathbf{\nabla})\mathbf{ v}=-\frac{\mathbf{\nabla}p}{\Sigma_{\mathrm{d}}}-\mathbf{\nabla}\Phi-2\mathbf{ \Omega}\times\mathbf{v}+\Omega^{2}(x\hat{\mathbf{e}}_{x}+y\hat{\mathbf{e}}_{y}),\] (5.152b) \[\nabla^{2}\Phi=4\pi G\Sigma\delta(z), \tag{5.152c}\]

where \(\delta(z)\) is the delta function, \(\mathbf{v}(x,y,t)=v_{x}(x,y,t)\hat{\mathbf{e}}_{x}+v_{y}(x,y,t)\hat{\mathbf{ e}}_{y}\) is the velocity in the rotating frame, and the last two terms on the right side of equation (5.152b) are the Coriolis and centrifugal forces (eq. 3.116). The function \(\Sigma(x,y,t)\) is the total surface density, composed of the surface density \(\Sigma_{\mathrm{d}}(x,y,t)\) of the disk and the surface density \(\Sigma_{\mathrm{e}}(x,y,t)\) of an external perturber that interacts with the disk only through gravitational forces. Note that the first two of equations (5.152) are defined only in the \((x,y)\) plane but the third must hold throughout three-dimensional space. In a two-dimensional system of this kind, the pressure \(p\) is assumed to act only in the plane of the sheet and has dimensions of force per unit length. We assume that the equation of state is barotropic and write (cf. eq. 5.19)

\[p(x,y,t)=p[\Sigma_{\mathrm{d}}(x,y,t)]. \tag{5.153}\]

In the unperturbed state \(\Sigma=\Sigma_{\mathrm{d}}=\Sigma_{0}\), \(\mathbf{v}=0\), and \(p=p_{0}=p(\Sigma_{0})\). Equation (5.152a) is satisfied trivially, and equations (5.152b) and (5.152c) read

\[\mathbf{\nabla}\Phi_{0}=\Omega^{2}(x\hat{\mathbf{e}}_{x}+y\hat{\mathbf{e}}_{y}), \tag{5.154}\]

\[\nabla^{2}\Phi_{0}=4\pi G\Sigma_{0}\delta(z). \tag{5.155}\]

Since the sheet is uniform there is no preferred direction in the \((x,y)\) plane. Hence the gravitational field \(-\mathbf{\nabla}\Phi_{0}\) must point in the \(z\)-direction, and it is easy to show that \(\Phi_{0}=2\pi G\Sigma_{0}|z|\) (Problem 2.3). Thus equation (5.154) cannot be satisfied as it stands: there are no pressure gradients or gravitational forces to balance the centrifugal force. To proceed further we must perpetrate a version of the Jeans swindle: we assume that the centrifugal force is balanced by a gravitational force that is produced by some unspecified mass distribution. The nature of this mass distribution does not concern us, since its only function is to ensure centrifugal balance in the equilibrium state.

We now consider a small perturbation of the form \(\Sigma(x,y,t)=\Sigma_{0}+\epsilon\Sigma_{1}(x,y,t)\), \({\bf v}(x,y,t)=\epsilon{\bf v}_{1}(x,y,t)\), etc., where \(\epsilon\) is sufficiently small. Keeping only terms linear in \(\epsilon\) in equations (5.152) and (5.153), we have

\[{\partial\Sigma_{\rm d1}\over\partial t}+\Sigma_{0}{\bf\nabla}\cdot{\bf v}_{1 }=0,\]

\[{\partial{\bf v}_{1}\over\partial t}=-{v_{\rm s}^{2}\over\Sigma_{0}}{\bf\nabla }\Sigma_{\rm d1}-{\bf\nabla}\Phi_{1}-2{\bf\Omega}\times{\bf v}_{1},\]

\[\nabla^{2}\Phi_{1}=4\pi G\Sigma_{1}\delta(z),\]

where we have introduced the sound speed \(v_{\rm s}\) defined by (cf. eq. 5.22)

\[v_{\rm s}^{2}=\left[{{\rm d}p(\Sigma)\over{\rm d}\Sigma}\right]_{\Sigma_{0}}.\]

These equations are similar to equations (5.31) except that there is a Coriolis term in Euler's equation arising from the rotation, and Poisson's equation is modified to apply to a two-dimensional system.

To solve equations (5.156) we write \(\Sigma_{1}(x,y,t)=\Sigma_{a}\exp[{\rm i}({\bf k}\cdot{\bf x}-\omega t)]\), \(\Sigma_{\rm d1}(x,y,t)=\Sigma_{\rm d{a}}\exp[{\rm i}({\bf k}\cdot{\bf x}- \omega t)]\), \({\bf v}_{1}(x,y,t)=(v_{ax}{\hat{\bf e}}_{x}+v_{ay}{\hat{\bf e}}_{y})\exp[{\rm i }({\bf k}\cdot{\bf x}-\omega t)]\), and \(\Phi_{1}(x,y,z=0,t)=\Phi_{a}\exp[{\rm i}({\bf k}\cdot{\bf x}-\omega t)]\). With no loss of generality, we can choose the \(x\) axis to be parallel to \({\bf k}\), so \({\bf k}=k{\hat{\bf e}}_{x}\). First consider Poisson's equation (5.156c). For \(z\neq 0\) we have \(\nabla^{2}\Phi_{1}=0\) but when \(z=0\), \(\Phi_{1}=\Phi_{a}\exp[{\rm i}(kx-\omega t)]\). The only continuous function that satisfies both constraints and that approaches zero far from the sheet has the form

\[\Phi_{1}(x,y,z,t)=\Phi_{a}{\rm e}^{{\rm i}(kx-\omega t)-|kz|}.\]

To relate \(\Phi_{a}\) to \(\Sigma_{a}\) we integrate equation (5.156c) from \(z=-\zeta\) to \(z=\zeta\), where \(\zeta\) is a positive constant, and then let \(\zeta\to 0\). Since \(\partial^{2}\Phi_{1}/\partial x^{2}\) and \(\partial^{2}\Phi_{1}/\partial y^{2}\) are continuous at \(z=0\), but \(\partial^{2}\Phi_{1}/\partial z^{2}\) is not, the left side gives

\[\lim_{\zeta\to 0}\int_{-\zeta}^{\zeta}{\rm d}z\,\nabla^{2}\Phi_{1}=\lim_{ \zeta\to 0}\int_{-\zeta}^{\zeta}{\rm d}z\,{\partial^{2}\Phi_{1}\over \partial z^{2}}=\lim_{\zeta\to 0}\left.{\partial\Phi_{1}\over\partial z} \right|_{-\zeta}^{\zeta}=-2|k|\Phi_{a}{\rm e}^{{\rm i}(kx-\omega t)}.\]

The right side of (5.156c) gives

\[4\pi G\lim_{\zeta\to 0}\int_{-\zeta}^{\zeta}{\rm d}z\,\Sigma_{1}\delta(z)=4\pi G \Sigma_{a}{\rm e}^{{\rm i}(kx-\omega t)}.\]

Hence \(-2|k|\Phi_{a}=4\pi G\Sigma_{a}\) or

\[\Phi_{1}(x,y,z,t)=-{2\pi G\Sigma_{a}\over|k|}{\rm e}^{{\rm i}(kx-\omega t)-| kz|}.\]Substituting for \(\Sigma_{\rm d1}\), \(\Sigma_{1}\), \({\bf v}_{1}\), and \(\Phi_{1}\) in equations (5.156) we obtain

\[\omega\Sigma_{\rm d{a}} =k\Sigma_{0}v_{ax},\] \[\omega v_{ax} =\frac{v_{\rm s}^{2}k\Sigma_{\rm d{a}}}{\Sigma_{0}}-\frac{2\pi G \Sigma_{a}k}{|k|}+2{\rm i}\Omega v_{ay}, \tag{5.162}\] \[\omega v_{ay} =-2{\rm i}\Omega v_{ax}.\]

This set of equations can be solved for \(\Sigma_{\rm d{a}}\) to yield

\[\Sigma_{\rm d{a}}=\widetilde{\overline{P}}({\bf k},\omega)\Sigma_{a},\quad \mbox{where}\quad\widetilde{\overline{P}}({\bf k},\omega)=\frac{2\pi G\Sigma_{ 0}|k|}{4\Omega^{2}-\omega^{2}+v_{\rm s}^{2}k^{2}}, \tag{5.163}\]

where \(\widetilde{\overline{P}}({\bf k},\omega)\) is the Fourier transform of the polarization function (eq. 5.7) that relates the response density \(\Sigma_{\rm d{a}}\) to the total density \(\Sigma_{a}\).

In the absence of an external perturber, we require \(\widetilde{\overline{P}}({\bf k},\omega)=1\), which yields the dispersion relation for the uniformly rotating sheet,

\[\omega^{2}=4\Omega^{2}-2\pi G\Sigma_{0}|k|+v_{\rm s}^{2}k^{2}. \tag{5.164}\]

The sheet is stable if \(\omega^{2}\geq 0\) and unstable if \(\omega^{2}<0\).

Equation (5.164) is central to understanding the stability of disk systems. First consider the case in which the sheet is not rotating. If \(\Omega=0\), the sheet is unstable when \(v_{\rm s}^{2}k^{2}-2\pi G\Sigma_{0}|k|<0\), in other words if

\[|k|<k_{\rm J}\equiv\frac{2\pi G\Sigma_{0}}{v_{\rm s}^{2}}, \tag{5.165}\]

where \(k_{\rm J}\) may be thought of as the Jeans wavenumber for the sheet. There is evidently a direct analog to the classical Jeans instability (eq. 5.34) for a three-dimensional homogeneous medium: in both cases long wavelengths are subject to a gravitational instability.

Next consider the case in which the sheet rotates, but has zero sound speed. Now the sheet is unstable if \(|k|>2\Omega^{2}/(\pi G\Sigma_{0})\). As \(k\to\infty\) the perturbation grows as \(\exp(\gamma t)\), where \(\gamma^{2}=-\omega^{2}=2\pi G\Sigma_{0}|k|\). Thus the growth rate \(\gamma\to\infty\) as \(\lambda\to 0\): a cold disk is violently unstable on small scales. This result is easy to understand from the three-dimensional case: the growth rate of the Jeans instability in a three-dimensional fluid with zero sound speed is \(\gamma=(4\pi G\rho_{0})^{1/2}\) (eq. 5.34); the average density of the sheet within a sphere of radius \(\lambda\) is \(\overline{\rho}_{0}\sim\Sigma_{0}/\lambda\), so the growth rate of the Jeans instability in the sheet is \(\gamma\sim(G\overline{\rho}_{0})^{1/2}\sim(G\Sigma_{0}/\lambda)^{1/2}\sim(G \Sigma_{0}|k|)^{1/2}\).

Neither rotation nor pressure is able by itself to stabilize the sheet: a rotating sheet with zero sound speed is unstable at small wavelengths, and a non-rotating sheet with non-zero sound speed is unstable at large wavelengths. However, rotation and pressure working together _can_ stabilize the sheet: if both effects are present, the right side of the dispersion relation (5.164) is quadratic in \(k\), with a minimum at the "most unstable wavenumber" \(|k|=\pi G\Sigma_{0}/v_{\rm s}^{2}=\frac{1}{2}k_{\rm J}\). The sheet is stable at all wavelengths if this minimum is positive, which requires that

\[\frac{v_{\rm s}\Omega}{G\Sigma_{0}}\geq{{1\over 2}}\pi=1.5708. \tag{5.166}\]

Toomre (1964) has given a physical interpretation of this stability criterion, using arguments analogous to those used to interpret the Jeans instability in SS5.2.1. Consider a small circular patch of the sheet. The radius of the patch is \(h\) and its mass \(M=\pi\Sigma_{0}h^{2}\). Now suppose that the patch radius is reduced to a fraction \((1-\alpha)\) of its original value, where \(\alpha\ll 1\). The resulting pressure perturbation will be \(p_{1}\approx\alpha p_{0}\approx\alpha v_{\rm s}^{2}\Sigma_{0}\). The pressure force per unit mass is \({\bf F}_{p}=-{\boldsymbol{\nabla}}p/\Sigma\), so the extra outward pressure force has magnitude \(|{\bf F}_{p1}|\approx p_{1}/(\Sigma_{0}h)\approx\alpha v_{\rm s}^{2}/h\). Similarly, the compression leads to an extra inward gravitational force per unit mass \({\bf F}_{g1}\), where

\[|{\bf F}_{g1}|\approx GM\alpha/h^{2}\approx G\Sigma_{0}\alpha. \tag{5.167}\]

In the absence of other effects, the sheet is expected to be stable if \(|{\bf F}_{p1}|\) exceeds \(|{\bf F}_{g1}|\), that is, if

\[h\lesssim\frac{v_{\rm s}^{2}}{G\Sigma_{0}}\equiv h_{\rm l}. \tag{5.168}\]

There are also internal motions in the patch, which arise from the rotation of the sheet. If we neglect external influences, the compressed region will tend to conserve spin angular momentum around its own center. The typical spin angular momentum per unit mass is \(S\approx\Omega h^{2}\), where \(\Omega\) is the angular speed of the sheet. The outward centrifugal force per unit mass is given by \(|{\bf F}_{\rm c}|\approx\Omega^{2}h\approx S^{2}/h^{3}\). If \(S\) is conserved, the centrifugal force felt by each element is increased by the compression; the amount of the increase is \(|{\bf F}_{c1}|\approx\alpha S^{2}/h^{3}\approx\alpha\Omega^{2}h\). Stability requires that \(|{\bf F}_{c1}|\) exceeds \(|{\bf F}_{g1}|\), or

\[h\gtrsim\frac{G\Sigma_{0}}{\Omega^{2}}\equiv h_{\rm u}. \tag{5.169}\]

Equations (5.168) and (5.169) show that both small and large regions are stable, one through pressure and the other through centrifugal force. The instability is suppressed at intermediate radii if \(h_{\rm u}\lesssim h_{\rm l}\), which requires

\[\frac{v_{\rm s}\Omega}{G\Sigma_{0}}\gtrsim 1, \tag{5.170}\]

an order of magnitude statement of the stability criterion (5.166).

Although equation (5.166) was derived for a fluid sheet, a very similar stability criterion applies to the analogous stellar system. A razor-thin sheet of stars with a Maxwellian velocity distribution is stable if (Toomre 1964)

\[{\sigma\Omega\over G\Sigma_{0}}\geq 1.68,\]

where \(\sigma\) is the one-dimensional velocity dispersion of the stars. We defer the derivation of this important result to SS6.2.3, where it appears as a special case of Toomre's stability criterion for differentially rotating disks. The coefficient on the right of equation (5.171) differs by less than 7% from the coefficient in the fluid stability criterion (5.166), illustrating once again the close analogies between stellar systems and fluids.

The approximation that the sheet is razor-thin has greatly simplified the stability analysis. Nevertheless, it is possible to investigate the stability of more realistic sheets with three-dimensional structure. These sheets or disks are still uniform in the \(x\) and \(y\) directions, but have an equilibrium vertical structure \(\rho_{0}(z)\) that is determined by the equation of state and the equation of hydrostatic equilibrium. The stability criteria derived by such analyses are generally very similar to equation (5.166). For example, Goldreich & Lynden-Bell (1965b) have analytically determined the stability of a uniformly rotating isothermal disk (equation of state \(p=v_{\rm s}^{2}\rho\), where \(v_{\rm s}\) is a constant). They find that the disk is stable if

\[{v_{\rm s}\Omega\over G\Sigma_{0}}\geq 1.06,\]

a result that differs by only about 30% from equation (5.166). The reason that the idealized two-dimensional sheet works so well is that the most unstable wavelength is several times the characteristic disk thickness, and the behavior of perturbations with such relatively long wavelengths is insensitive to the details of the vertical structure.

In conclusion, the uniformly rotating sheet exhibits three important features: (i) a cold sheet is violently unstable; (ii) the sheet can be stabilized by a sound speed \(v_{\rm s}\) or velocity dispersion \(\sigma\) that satisfies the stability criteria (5.166) or (5.171) for fluid or stellar systems, respectively; (iii) the stability properties of fluid and stellar sheets are very similar. We shall encounter all of these features again in more realistic models of uniformly and differentially rotating disks.

#### 5.6.2 Kalnajs disks

This family, described in SS4.5.2, comprises the simplest self-gravitating stellar disks and one of the few self-consistent stellar systems whose modes can be studied analytically. Kalnajs disks have potential and surface density (eqs. 4.166 and 4.167)

\[\Sigma_{0}(R)=\Sigma_{\rm c}\sqrt{1-\frac{R^{2}}{a^{2}}}\quad;\quad\Phi_{0}(R)= \tfrac{1}{2}\Omega_{0}^{2}R^{2}, \tag{5.173}\]

where

\[\Omega_{0}^{2}=\frac{\pi^{2}G\Sigma_{\rm c}}{2a} \tag{5.174}\]

and \(a\) is the radius of the disk edge. The mean angular speed \(\Omega\) of the stars in a Kalnajs disk is independent of position, and relative to this mean speed the stars have isotropic velocity dispersion in the disk plane, given by equation (4.175).

The modes of razor-thin disks such as these can be separated into horizontal oscillations or **density waves**, in which the disk's surface density varies but it remains in its original plane, and vertical oscillations or **bending waves**, in which the surface density remains unchanged and the disk oscillates in the direction normal to its original plane. The horizontal and vertical modes of the Kalnajs disks were analyzed by Kalnajs (1972a) and Polyachenko (1977) respectively. The horizontal modes, on which we focus here, have potentials that can be written as

\[\Phi_{l}^{m}(R,\phi,t)=P_{l}^{|m|}(\eta){\rm e}^{{\rm i}(m\phi-\omega t)}\qquad \left(\begin{array}{c}l>0,\ |m|\leq l\\ l-m\ {\rm even}\end{array}\right). \tag{5.175}\]

Here \(\eta=\sqrt{1-R^{2}/a^{2}}\) and \(P_{l}^{m}\) is an associated Legendre function (Appendix C.5). The corresponding surface density can be determined from equations (2.202) and (2.204a); after adjusting to the notation of the present section, we have

\[\Sigma_{l}^{m}(R,\phi,t)=-\frac{2}{\pi^{2}Gag_{lm}\eta}P_{l}^{|m|}(\eta){\rm e }^{{\rm i}(m\phi-\omega t)}\qquad\left(\begin{array}{c}l>0,\ |m|\leq l\\ l-m\ {\rm even}\end{array}\right), \tag{5.176}\]

where \(g_{lm}\) is defined by equation (2.204b).

There are three trivial zero-frequency modes of the Kalnajs disks. The two modes with \(l=1\), \(m=\pm 1\) correspond to a uniform translation of the disk.15 The third zero-frequency mode has \(l=2\), \(m=0\) and corresponds to a rescaling of the outer radius \(a\) of the disk. There is also a non-trivialmode with \(l=2\), \(m=0\) and non-zero frequency, corresponding to a stable pulsation in which the outer radius oscillates but the surface-density profile of the disk retains the form (5.173).

More interesting behavior is exhibited by the modes with quantum numbers \(l=2\), \(m=\pm 2\), corresponding to a bar-like distortion of the disk. These modes can be analyzed relatively simply, without even using the collisionless Boltzmann equation (Kalnajs & Athanassoula-Georgala 1974).16

Footnote 16: Even the finite-amplitude oscillations corresponding to these quantum numbers can be described analytically: for real \(\omega\) these are simply the Freeman bars discussed in Box 4.2.

The surface density and potential of the mode with \(l=m=2\) are given by equations (5.175) and (5.176)17

Footnote 17: We do not have to consider modes with \(m<0\) since \(\mathop{\rm Re}\nolimits\{\Phi_{a}(R)\exp[{\rm i}(m\phi-\omega t)]\}=\mathop{ \rm Re}\nolimits\{\Phi_{a}^{*}(R)\exp[{\rm i}(-m\phi+\omega^{*}t)]\}\). Thus every mode with \(m<0\) and frequency \(\omega\) is also a mode with \(m>0\) and frequency \(-\omega^{*}\).

\[\Phi_{1}(R,\phi,t) =R^{2}{\rm e}^{{\rm i}(2\phi-\omega t)}=(x+{\rm i}y)^{2}{\rm e}^{- {\rm i}\omega t} \tag{5.178}\] \[\Sigma_{1}(R,\phi,t) =-\frac{8}{3\pi^{2}Ga}\frac{(x+{\rm i}y)^{2}{\rm e}^{-{\rm i} \omega t}}{\sqrt{1-R^{2}/a^{2}}}.\]

The unperturbed equations of motion are

\[\ddot{\bf R}_{0}=-\mathbf{\nabla}\Phi_{0}=-\Omega_{0}^{2}{\bf R}_{0}. \tag{5.179}\]

If we now add a weak potential perturbation equal to \(\epsilon\) times (5.178), the perturbed orbit \({\bf R}(t)\) is governed by the equations

\[\ddot{\bf R}=-\mathbf{\nabla}(\Phi_{0}+\epsilon\Phi_{1}), \tag{5.180}\]

where the right side must be evaluated at \({\bf R}(t)\). We now assume that \(\epsilon\) is sufficiently small that we may work to first order in \(\epsilon\) and set \({\bf R}(t)={\bf R}_{0}(t)+\epsilon{\bf R}_{1}(t)\). To first order in \(\epsilon\) the right side of (5.180) is given by

\[\left[\mathbf{\nabla}(\Phi_{0}+\epsilon\Phi_{1})\right] _{{\bf R}_{0}(t)+\epsilon{\bf R}_{1}(t)}=\left(\mathbf{\nabla}\Phi_{ 0}\right)_{{\bf R}_{0}(t)+\epsilon{\bf R}_{1}(t)}+\epsilon\left(\mathbf{\nabla}\Phi_{1}\right)_{{\bf R}_{0}(t)}+{\rm O}(\epsilon^{2}) \tag{5.181}\] \[=\left(\mathbf{\nabla}\Phi_{0}\right)_{{\bf R}_{0}}+ \epsilon\left[({\bf R}_{1}\cdot\mathbf{\nabla})\mathbf{\nabla$ }\Phi_{0}\right]_{{\bf R}_{0}}+\epsilon\left(\mbox{\boldmath$\nabla}\Phi_{1} \right)_{{\bf R}_{0}}+{\rm O}(\epsilon^{2}).\]

Since \(\mathbf{\nabla}\Phi_{0}=\Omega_{0}^{2}{\bf R}\), we have \(({\bf R}_{1}\cdot\mathbf{\nabla})\mathbf{\nabla}\Phi_{0}= \Omega_{0}^{2}{\bf R}_{1}\). When we subtract the unperturbed equations of motion (5.179), the perturbed equations become

\[\ddot{\bf R}_{1}+\Omega_{0}^{2}{\bf R}_{1}=-\left(\mathbf{\nabla}\Phi _{1}\right)_{{\bf R}_{0}(t)}, \tag{5.182}\]

which is the equation of motion for a driven harmonic oscillator. We now write \({\bf R}_{1}(t)\equiv[x_{1}(t),y_{1}(t)]\) and seek the solution of this equation for the unperturbed trajectory that passes through \((x_{i},y_{i})\) with velocity \((v_{xi},v_{yi})\) at time \(t_{i}\). We find

\[\eqalign{x_{1}(t)&={x_{i}+{\rm i}y_{i}-{\rm i}(v_{xi}+{\rm i}v_{yi})/\Omega_{0} \over\omega^{2}-2\Omega_{0}\omega}{\rm e}^{{\rm i}(\Omega_{0}t-\Omega_{0}t_{i} -\omega t)}\cr&\qquad+{x_{i}+{\rm i}y_{i}+{\rm i}(v_{xi}+{\rm i}v_{yi})/\Omega_ {0}\over\omega^{2}+2\Omega_{0}\omega}{\rm e}^{{\rm i}(-\Omega_{0}t+\Omega_{0}t _{i}-\omega t)},}\]

in the analogous equation for \(y_{1}(t)\) the right side is multiplied by \({\rm i}\). If we now set \(t=t_{i}\) we obtain a relation between the displacement \((x_{1},y_{1})\) and the unperturbed phase-space position of a star,

\[x_{1}(t)=-{\rm i}y_{1}(t)=2{\omega(x+{\rm i}y)-2{\rm i}(v_{x}+{\rm i}v_{y}) \over\omega(\omega^{2}-4\Omega_{0}^{2})}{\rm e}^{-{\rm i}\omega t}.\]

To first order in the perturbation, the equation of continuity may be written in the form (eq. F.5)

\[\Sigma_{1}+\hbox{\boldmath$\nabla$}\cdot(\Sigma_{0}\hbox{\boldmath$\xi$})=0,\]

where \(\xi\) is the mean displacement of the stars at a given position. We may write \(\hbox{\boldmath$\xi$}=\overline{x_{1}}\hat{\bf e}_{x}+\overline{y_{1}}\hat{ \bf e}_{y}\), where the bar, as usual, denotes the average over velocities of the stars at a given position.

The unperturbed mean velocity at \((x,y)\) is \(\overline{\dot{x}_{0}}=-\Omega y\), \(\overline{\dot{y}_{0}}=\Omega x\). The mean displacement is obtained by averaging equation (5.184) over all velocities at a given point,

\[\overline{x_{1}}(t)=-{\rm i}\overline{y_{1}}(t)=2{(\omega+2\Omega)(x+{\rm i}y )\over\omega(\omega^{2}-4\Omega_{0}^{2})}{\rm e}^{-{\rm i}\omega t}.\]

We now substitute into the continuity equation (5.185) using equation (5.173) for \(\Sigma_{0}\). It is straightforward to show that

\[\Sigma_{1}={2\Sigma_{\rm c}\over a^{2}}{\omega+2\Omega\over\omega(\omega^{2}- 4\Omega_{0}^{2})}{(x+{\rm i}y)^{2}{\rm e}^{-{\rm i}\omega t}\over\sqrt{1-R^{2} /a^{2}}}.\]

The frequency of the mode is determined by the requirement that equation (5.178) be consistent with (5.187). Eliminating \(\Sigma_{1}\) from these equations and using equation (5.174), we find the equation for the frequencies of the modes with quantum numbers \((l,m)=(2,2)\):

\[\omega^{3}-{{5\over 2}}\Omega_{0}^{2}\omega+3\Omega\Omega_{0}^{2}=0.\]

To analyze stability we rewrite this as \(\omega^{3}-{{5\over 2}}\Omega_{0}^{2}\omega=-3\Omega\Omega_{0}^{2}\). The cubic polynomial on the left has a local minimum of \(-2({{5\over 6}})^{3/2}\Omega_{0}^{3}\) at \(\omega=({{5\over 6}})^{1/2}\Omega_{0}\)\[\frac{\Omega}{\Omega_{0}}<\sqrt{\frac{125}{486}}=0.507. \tag{5.189}\]

A convenient measure of the importance of rotation for the equilibrium structure of any self-gravitating body is the ratio \(t\) of the rotational kinetic energy \(T\) to the body's self-gravitational potential energy \(W\):

\[t\equiv\frac{T}{|W|}. \tag{5.190}\]

The virial theorem (4.248) states that \(K/|W|=\frac{1}{2}\), where \(K=T+\frac{1}{2}\Pi\) is the sum of the rotational kinetic energy \(T\) and the kinetic energy in random motions \(\frac{1}{2}\Pi\). Thus we have \(0\leq t\leq\frac{1}{2}\). For a Kalnajs disk, \(t=\frac{1}{2}(\Omega/\Omega_{0})^{2}\). Thus the Kalnajs disks are unstable to a bar-like mode if

\[t>0.1286. \tag{5.191}\]

It is natural to speculate that this instability is related to the bars found in many disk galaxies, but uniformly rotating stellar systems such as Kalnajs disks can behave quite differently from the differentially rotating disks found in galaxies, and we shall find in Chapter 6 that bar-like instabilities in differentially rotating disks arise from a quite different mechanism.

Figure 5.5 shows the regions of instability for modes with quantum number \(l\leq 5\), as determined by Kalnajs (1972a) using the linearized collisionless

Boltzmann equation. As \(l\) increases, the behavior becomes increasingly complicated: for a given pair of indices \((l,m)\), a Kalnajs disk has up to \(l+1\) different modes with different frequencies \(\omega\). The figure shows that all Kalnajs disks are unstable to one or more modes; however, there are stable composite systems consisting of superimposed Kalnajs disks.

#### Maclaurin spheroids and disks

Throughout this chapter we have stressed the analogies between the dynamics of stellar and fluid systems. This analogy extends to the bar-like instability of rapidly rotating Kalnajs disks, which is also present in uniformly rotating, self-gravitating bodies of incompressible fluid.

The study of rotating fluid bodies was initiated by Newton, who treated the Earth as a homogeneous, incompressible fluid body in order to estimate the expected flattening of the earth due to its rotation. His treatment was valid only for small rotation speeds, but in 1742 C. Maclaurin found an exact solution for the equilibrium of an incompressible, uniformly rotating fluid body (Problem 5.19). These **Maclaurin spheroids** form a one-parameter family of oblate (flattened) spheroids (SS2.5.2), which can be parametrized by their angular momentum or angular speed, by the ratio of polar to equatorial axes, or by the parameter \(t\) defined in equation (5.190).

A surprise came in 1834 when C. G. Jacobi showed that the Maclaurin spheroids were not the only possible equilibrium shape for a uniformly rotating, incompressible fluid body: there were also equilibria in which the fluid surface was ellipsoidal, with all three principal axes different. In other words, while a non-rotating fluid body supported by its own self-gravity _must_ be spherical by Lichtenstein's theorem (Box 4.1), a rotating fluid body supported by its own self-gravity need _not_ be axisymmetric. It is a measure of the unexpectedness of this result that Jacobi's discovery came almost a century after Maclaurin's. The relationship of the **Jacobi ellipsoids**, as these configurations are now called, to the simpler Maclaurin spheroids remained obscure until the end of the nineteenth century, when Poincare and others showed that the Jacobi ellipsoids are actually the preferred configurations of rapidly rotating fluid bodies because they have lower energy for fixed angular momentum and mass. In the language of SS5.4, rapidly rotating Maclaurin spheroids with \(0.1375<t<0.2738\) are dynamically stable but secularly unstable: if the fluid has even the tiniest viscosity, they will gradually evolve into Jacobi ellipsoids.

There is a simple physical basis for this instability. The kinetic energy of a uniformly rotating body is \({1\over 2}L^{2}/I\), where \(L\) is the angular momentum and \(I\) is the moment of inertia (eq. D.43). The deformation of the spheroid into an ellipsoid increases the moment of inertia and thus lowers the rotational kinetic energy. The deformation also raises the gravitational potential energy; however, for sufficiently large angular momentum the loss of rotational kinetic energy is greater than the gain of potential energy, so the spheroid is secularly unstable.

For the most rapidly rotating Maclaurin spheroids, \(0.2738<t<0.5\), the secular instability is overwhelmed by a more rapid dynamical instability to a bar-like mode, reminiscent of the dynamical instability that plagues the Kalnajs disks with \(0.1286<t<0.5\) (eq. 5.191).

The complete spectrum of modes of the Maclaurin spheroids was first analyzed by Bryan (1888). More recent discussions appear in Lyttleton (1953), Chandrasekhar (1969), and Tassoul (1978). An even closer fluid analog to the Kalnajs disk is the **Maclaurin disk**, a razor-thin disk of two-dimensional fluid, in which the pressure acts only in the disk plane and the surface-density distribution is the same as in the Kalnajs disks. The Maclaurin disks become secularly unstable to a bar-like mode at \(t=\frac{1}{8}=0.125\), and dynamically unstable at \(t=\frac{1}{4}=0.25\) (Problem 5.20; Takahara 1976; Weinberg 1983).

Finally, in 1860 Riemann showed that even the Jacobi ellipsoids are only special members of a much larger family of triaxial equilibrium configurations of self-gravitating fluid; the **Riemann ellipsoids** not only rotate but have internal streaming motions (Chandrasekhar 1969). The Riemann ellipsoids draw attention to the distinction between the **material speed** at which the _matter_ of a rotating triaxial body streams and the pattern speed, the angular speed at which the _figure_ of the body rotates. This distinction is important for bars in disk galaxies because the pattern speed governs the gravitational interaction of the bar with the rest of the galaxy, while the material speed determines the observed velocities.

