## 3 Galaxy Formation

Why is the universe populated by galaxies, rather than a uniform sea of stars? Why are most stars in galaxies with luminosities near \(L_{\star}\simeq 3\times 10^{10}\,L_{\odot}\) (eq. 18)? What is the physical origin of the fundamental plane of elliptical galaxies (eq. 20) and the Tully-Fisher law for disk galaxies (eq. 24)? These are the kinds of questions that a complete theory of galaxy formation should answer. The answers to these questions are still incomplete, and what answers we do have are based on a wide range of physics that extends well beyond stellar dynamics. Nevertheless, stellar dynamics does play a central role in the formation of galaxies and in determining their characteristics, and that role is the subject of this chapter.

In SS1.3 (eq. 73) we saw that only 15% of the cosmic matter density is contributed by baryonic matter, the remaining \(\sim 85\%\) of the density being non-baryonic matter that has no strong or electromagnetic interactions. Thus it is a useful first approximation to neglect the contribution of the baryonic matter to the gravitational forces involved in galaxy formation. In this approximation the dark matter is not affected by the baryons. Without the complications of baryonic physics (radiation, fluid dynamics, star formation, etc.) the dynamics of dark matter becomes a well-defined--though difficult--stellar-dynamical problem that has been extensively studied, and largely solved, by cosmologists over the last several decades. In this chapter we review the results of this study.

The emergence of stars, galaxies, groups and clusters of galaxies, andeven larger inhomogeneities from the nearly homogeneous early universe is referred to as **structure formation**. In SS9.1, which is concerned with the earliest phase in the emergence of structure, we shall derive results that apply to baryons and dark matter alike. In SSSS9.2 and 9.3 we shall restrict ourselves to discussion of the dynamics of dark matter in order to avoid the complexities of nonlinear gas dynamics. This restriction is unfortunate since the sophisticated theoretical framework that emerges describes only the distribution of matter that we cannot see, but the results are essential for understanding the behavior of the baryons that we do see. Finally, in SS9.4 we discuss in a speculative vein the complex dynamics of ordinary matter that leads to the formation of visible stars and galaxies near the centers of dark halos.

### 9.1 Linear structure formation

As we described in SS1.3 the universe is homogeneous and isotropic on large scales, and therefore described by the metric (1.44) with the evolution of the scale factor \(a(t)\) given by the Friedmann-Robertson-Walker (FRW) equations (1.47)-(1.50). A fundamental assumption of modern cosmology is that early in its history the universe was almost perfectly homogeneous, and that stars, galaxies, clusters, and other large-scale structures developed by the growth of gravitationally unstable fluctuations in the density \(\rho({\bf x})\) of the baryonic and non-baryonic matter. Rather than working directly with \(\rho({\bf x})\), we use the dimensionless **overdensity**

\[\delta({\bf x})\equiv{\rho({\bf x})\over\rho_{0}}-1,\]

where \(\rho_{0}\) denotes the average matter density over a volume \(V\) that is sufficiently large that the universe can be considered homogeneous. We shall usually assume that \({\bf x}\) is a comoving coordinate, so the physical distance corresponding to the comoving distance \(x\) is \(a(t)x\), where \(a(t)\) is the cosmic scale factor (SS1.3.1). In the linear regime \(|\delta|\ll 1\).

In the real universe \(\delta\) has a well-defined value at each location \({\bf x}\). However, we do not know what this value was early in the life of the universe, and it is natural to imagine it to be the outcome of pure chance. That is, we imagine \(\delta({\bf x})\) to be a random variable, and we say that the function \(\delta\) defines a random field. Thus we replace the concept of a homogeneous universe with that of a statistically homogeneous universe: one in which the statistical properties of \(\rho({\bf x})\) in any volume \(V\) of given size and shape are independent of the location of its centroid.

If \(\delta\) is to be a continuous function, the (random) values that it takes at two nearby points \({\bf x}^{\prime}\) and \({\bf x}^{\prime}+{\bf x}\) must be correlated: given that the field is continuous and we know the value of \(\delta({\bf x}^{\prime})\), then the uncertainty in \(\delta({\bf x}^{\prime}+{\bf x})\) must become smaller and smaller as \({\bf x}\to 0\). The degree to which \(\delta({\bf x}^{\prime})\) and \(\delta({\bf x}^{\prime}+{\bf x})\) are mutually dependent on one another is quantified by the **correlation function**

\[\xi({\bf x})\equiv\langle\delta({\bf x}^{\prime})\delta({\bf x}^{\prime}+{\bf x })\rangle. \tag{9.2}\]

Here the angle brackets imply that the expectation value is to be taken. The assumption of statistical homogeneity allows us to interpret this as the average over all points \({\bf x}^{\prime}\). If the universe is statistically isotropic, \(\xi\) cannot depend on the direction of the displacement \({\bf x}\), but only on its magnitude \(x=|{\bf x}|\). Thus we can assume that the correlation function has the form

\[\xi(x)\equiv\langle\delta({\bf x}^{\prime})\delta({\bf x}^{\prime}+{\bf x})\rangle. \tag{9.3}\]

For simplicity, we restrict our averages to a large but finite volume \(V\), which we take to be a cube of side \(V^{1/3}\). We apply periodic boundary conditions on this cube--since we can make \(V\) very large, this assumption has no effect on the answer. Then the overdensity is periodic and can be expanded as a Fourier series (Appendix B.4),

\[\delta({\bf x})={1\over V}\sum_{\bf k}\delta_{\bf k}{\rm e}^{{\rm i}{\bf k} \cdot{\bf x}}\quad{\rm where}\quad\delta_{\bf k}=\int_{V}{\rm d}^{3}{\bf x} \,\delta({\bf x})\,{\rm e}^{-{\rm i}{\bf k}\cdot{\bf x}}, \tag{9.4}\]

\({\bf k}=2\pi{\bf n}/V^{1/3}\) with \({\bf n}=(n_{1},n_{2},n_{3})\) a triple of integers, and the sum is over all \({\bf n}\). Note that \(\delta_{\bf 0}=0\) because \(\delta({\bf x})\) has zero mean by definition. Like \(\delta({\bf x})\), \(\delta_{\bf k}\) is a random variable with zero mean. The reality of \(\delta({\bf x})\) is assured by including both \({\bf k}\) and \(-{\bf k}\) in the sum, with

\[\delta_{-{\bf k}}=\delta_{\bf k}^{\ast}. \tag{9.5}\]

When we insert the Fourier expansion (9.4) into the definition (9.3) of the correlation function, we find

\[\xi(x)={1\over V^{2}}\sum_{{\bf k}^{\prime}{\bf k}}\langle\delta_{{\bf k}^{ \prime}}\delta_{\bf k}\rangle{\rm e}^{{\rm i}({\bf k}^{\prime}+{\bf k})\cdot{ \bf x}^{\prime}}{\rm e}^{{\rm i}{\bf k}\cdot{\bf x}}. \tag{9.6}\]

Since the right side cannot depend on \({\bf x}^{\prime}\), we infer that

\[\langle\delta_{{\bf k}^{\prime}}\delta_{\bf k}\rangle=0\quad{\rm if}\quad{ \bf k}^{\prime}\neq-{\bf k}. \tag{9.7}\]

Hence equation (9.6) simplifies to

\[\xi(x)={1\over V^{2}}\sum_{\bf k}\langle\delta_{-{\bf k}}\delta_{\bf k} \rangle{\rm e}^{{\rm i}{\bf k}\cdot{\bf x}}={1\over V^{2}}\sum_{\bf k}\langle| \delta_{\bf k}|^{2}\rangle{\rm e}^{{\rm i}{\bf k}\cdot{\bf x}}\equiv{1\over V }\sum_{\bf k}P({\bf k}){\rm e}^{{\rm i}{\bf k}\cdot{\bf x}}, \tag{9.8}\]

where \(P({\bf k})\equiv\langle|\delta_{\bf k}|^{2}\rangle/V\) is the **power spectrum** of the random field. Equation (9.8) states that the power spectrum is the Fourier transform of the correlation function. Since the universe is isotropic, the power spectrum can only depend on \(k=|{\bf k}|\). The variance of the overdensity is

\[\langle\delta^{2}({\bf x})\rangle=\xi(0)={1\over V}\sum_{\bf k}P(k). \tag{9.9}\]

#### 9.1.1 Gaussian random fields

We say that \(\delta({\bf x})\) is a **Gaussian random field** if, except for the obvious constraint implied by the reality condition (9.5), the \(\delta_{\bf k}\) are independent random variables.1 Equation (9.4) can be written

Footnote 1: Mathematically we require that if \(\delta_{\bf k}=a+{\rm i}b\), with \(a,b\) real, and \(p_{\bf k}(\delta_{\bf k})\,{\rm d}a\,{\rm d}b\) is the probability that \(\delta_{\bf k}\) lies in the infinitesimal area of the complex plane \({\rm d}a\,{\rm d}b\), then the probability that \(\delta_{\bf k}\) lies in this area _and_\(\delta_{{\bf k}^{\prime}}\) lies in \({\rm d}a^{\prime}{\rm d}b^{\prime}\) is \(p_{\bf k}(\delta_{\bf k})p_{{\bf k}^{\prime}}(\delta_{{\bf k}^{\prime}})\,{\rm d }a\,{\rm d}b\,{\rm d}a^{\prime}{\rm d}b^{\prime}\) so long as \({\bf k}\neq\pm{\bf k}^{\prime}\).

\[\delta({\bf x})=\frac{1}{V}{\sum_{\bf k}}^{\prime}\left(\delta_{\bf k}{\rm e}^ {{\rm i}{\bf k}\cdot{\bf x}}+\delta_{-{\bf k}}{\rm e}^{-{\rm i}{\bf k}\cdot{ \bf x}}\right), \tag{9.10}\]

where \(\sum^{\prime}\) is the sum over only half of \({\bf k}\)-space.2 In a Gaussian field, each term \(\delta_{\bf k}\exp({\rm i}{\bf k}\cdot{\bf x})+\delta_{-{\bf k}}\exp(-{\rm i}{ \bf k}\cdot{\bf x})\) is an independent real random variable. According to the central limit theorem (Appendix B.10), a sum of a large number of independent random variables has a Gaussian distribution: that is, the probability that \(\delta({\bf x})\) lies in the small interval \((\delta,\delta+{\rm d}\delta)\) is

Footnote 2: For example the volume \(k_{1}>0\) plus the half-plane \(k_{1}=0\), \(k_{2}>0\), plus the half-line \(k_{1}=k_{2}=0\), \(k_{3}>0\). Recall that \(\delta_{\bf 0}=0\).

\[{\rm d}p=\frac{1}{(2\pi\sigma^{2})^{1/2}}\exp\left(-\frac{\delta^{2}}{2\sigma^ {2}}\right){\rm d}\delta, \tag{9.11}\]

where \(\sigma^{2}=\langle\delta^{2}({\bf x})\rangle\) is the position-independent variance of the overdensity field. It is from this property that Gaussian random fields derive their name.

Any quantity that is a linear function of the values taken by the overdensity field at several positions will also have a Gaussian distribution. For example, the gradient of the overdensity field is \({\bf\nabla}\delta={\rm i}V^{-1}\sum_{\bf k}\delta_{\bf k}{\bf k}\exp({\rm i}{ \bf k}\cdot{\bf x})\), and the central limit theorem can be applied to this sum to show that its distribution is Gaussian.

The central limit theorem can also be used to establish a much more powerful result on the joint distribution of the overdensities at several locations. Let \({\bf u}\equiv(\delta_{1},\ldots,\delta_{D})\) be the vector describing the overdensity \(\delta_{i}\equiv\delta({\bf x}_{i})\) at the \(D\) positions \({\bf x}_{1},\ldots,{\bf x}_{D}\). Then, by an extension of equation (9.10),

\[{\bf u}=\frac{1}{V}{\sum_{\bf k}}^{\prime}\left[({\rm e}^{{\rm i}{\bf k}\cdot{ \bf x}_{1}},\ldots,{\rm e}^{{\rm i}{\bf k}\cdot{\bf x}_{D}})\delta_{\bf k}+({ \rm e}^{-{\rm i}{\bf k}\cdot{\bf x}_{1}},\ldots,{\rm e}^{-{\rm i}{\bf k}\cdot{ \bf x}_{D}})\delta_{-{\bf k}}\right]. \tag{9.12}\]

Each term in the sum is an independent vector random variable, so by equation (B.99) the joint probability distribution of \(\delta_{1},\ldots,\delta_{D}\) is given by

\[{\rm d}p=\frac{{\rm d}\delta_{1}\cdots{\rm d}\delta_{D}}{(2\pi)^{D/2}|{\bf C}|^{ 1/2}}\exp\bigg{(}-\mbox{$\frac{1}{2}$}\sum_{i,j=1}^{D}\delta_{i}C_{ij}^{-1} \delta_{j}\bigg{)}, \tag{9.13}\]where \({\bf C}^{-1}\) is the inverse of the matrix \({\bf C}\) defined by

\[C_{ij}=\langle\delta({\bf x}_{i})\delta({\bf x}_{j})\rangle=\xi(|{\bf x}_{j}-{\bf x }_{i}|), \tag{9.14}\]

\(|{\bf C}|\) is the determinant of this matrix, and \(\xi(x)\) is defined by equation (9.2). Since the matrix \({\bf C}\) is determined by the correlation function \(\xi(x)\), which in turn is determined by the power spectrum \(P({\bf k})\) through equation (9.8), _all of the statistical properties of a Gaussian random field are determined by its power spectrum_ (e.g., Bardeen et al. 1986).

**(a) Filtering** To understand the physical meaning of the power spectrum, imagine smoothing the density field with a filter that averages out all density fluctuations on scales less than the **smoothing length**\(L\). More precisely, we convolve the overdensity field \(\delta({\bf x})\) with a filter or **window function**\(W({\bf x})\) to generate a smoothed field \(\delta_{L}({\bf x})\):

\[\delta_{L}({\bf x})\equiv\int{\rm d}^{3}{\bf x}^{\prime}\,W({\bf x}-{\bf x}^{ \prime})\delta({\bf x}^{\prime}), \tag{9.15}\]

where \(\int{\rm d}^{3}{\bf x}\,W({\bf x})=1\) and \(W({\bf x})\simeq 0\) for \(|{\bf x}|\mathrel{\hbox to 0.0pt{\raise 2.1973pt\hbox{$>$}}{\lower 2.1973pt \hbox{$\sim$}}}L\). In terms of its Fourier components, the smoothed overdensity is \(\delta_{L}({\bf x})=V^{-1}\sum_{\bf k}\delta_{L,{\bf k}}\exp({\rm i}{\bf k} \cdot{\bf x})\). It is straightforward to show that the relation between the smoothed and unsmoothed Fourier components (eq. 9.4) is

\[\delta_{L,{\bf k}}=\widetilde{W}({\bf k})\delta_{\bf k}\quad\hbox{where}\quad \widetilde{W}({\bf k})\equiv\int{\rm d}^{3}{\bf x}\,W({\bf x})\,{\rm e}^{-{ \rm i}{\bf k}\cdot{\bf x}}. \tag{9.16}\]

Thus smoothing reduces the Fourier amplitudes by a factor \(|\widetilde{W}({\bf k})|\), which is unity for \({\bf k}={\bf 0}\) and tends to zero for \(k\mathrel{\hbox to 0.0pt{\raise 2.1973pt\hbox{$>$}}{\lower 2.1973pt\hbox{$\sim$}}} K\equiv 2\pi/L\). For brevity we shall say that a filter of this kind has scale \(K^{-1}\) although the actual smoothing length is closer to \(2\pi/K\).

Although several different filters are used in cosmology, we shall work with fields that have been smoothed by simply setting to zero all amplitudes with \(k\) greater than \(K\)--that is, we shall work with the **sharp k-space filter**. This filter's window function \(W_{K}({\bf x})\) is determined by taking the inverse of the filter's Fourier transform, \(\widetilde{W}_{K}({\bf k})=H(1-k/K)\), where \(H\) is the step function (Appendix C.1 and eq. B.68). Denoting \(x=|{\bf x}|\) we find that

\[W_{K}({\bf x})={1\over 2\pi^{2}x^{3}}\left(\sin Kx-Kx\cos Kx\right). \tag{9.17}\]

Any given smoothing filter defines a characteristic mass that is equal to the mean density times the volume contained within one smoothing length. One natural way to define this mass is

\[M\equiv\rho_{0}\int{\rm d}^{3}{\bf x}\,{W({\bf x})\over W(0)}={\rho_{0}\over W (0)}. \tag{9.18}\]For the sharp \(k\)-space filter \(W_{K}(0)=K^{3}/(6\pi^{2})\), so

\[M_{K}=\frac{6\pi^{2}\rho_{0}}{K^{3}}. \tag{9.19}\]

By equations (9.9) and (9.16), the variance of an overdensity field that has been smoothed with the filter \(W_{K}\) is

\[\sigma_{K}^{2}=\frac{1}{V}\sum_{\bf k}\widetilde{W}_{K}^{2}({\bf k})P(k)=\frac {1}{V}\sum_{|{\bf k}|<K}P(k). \tag{9.20}\]

If we now proceed to the limit \(V\to\infty\) of a large box, the interval between values of \(k_{i}=2\pi n_{i}/V^{1/3}\) over which we are summing becomes infinitesimal, and we have

\[\frac{1}{V}\sum_{\bf k}\to\frac{1}{V}\int{\rm d}^{3}{\bf n}=\frac{1}{(2\pi)^{ 3}}\int{\rm d}^{3}{\bf k}. \tag{9.21}\]

Then equation (9.20) becomes

\[\sigma_{K}^{2}=\frac{1}{2\pi^{2}}\int_{0}^{K}{\rm d}k\,k^{2}P(k). \tag{9.22}\]

**(b) The Harrison-Zeldovich power spectrum** The simplest power spectra are power laws,

\[P(k)\propto k^{n}. \tag{9.23}\]

For a power spectrum of this form,

\[\sigma_{K}^{2}\propto\int_{0}^{K}{\rm d}k\,k^{2+n}\propto K^{3+n}. \tag{9.24}\]

For example, suppose that the density field consists of points laid down at random, with average density \(n\); in other words, the density field is a Poisson process (see Appendix B.8). In a box of side \(K^{-1}\), the mean number of points will be \(N=nK^{-3}\), the variance in this number will be \(\sigma_{N}^{2}=N\) (eq. B.84), the fractional standard deviation will be \(\sigma_{N}/N=N^{-1/2}\propto K^{3/2}\), and this is just \(\sigma_{K}\). Comparing this result with equation (9.24) shows that the power spectrum of a Poisson process has \(n=0\). Power spectra with \(n>0\) are smoother than Poisson processes on large scales.

The most important power-law spectrum in cosmology is the **Harrison-Zeldovich** or **scale-invariant spectrum**, which has \(n=1\). Different theories of inflation predict spectra that deviate little from the Harrison-Zeldovich spectrum but Zeldovich's3 interest in this particular case was motivated by a much simpler argument. In general relativity, the fluctuationsin the metric tensor are of order \(\Phi/c^{2}\), where \(\Phi\) is the gravitational potential arising from the density fluctuations. If the metric-tensor fluctuations are too large on large scales, the universe will not be homogeneous and isotropic; if too large on small scales, small structures will be relativistic. Therefore it is natural to be interested in a spectrum for which the rms fluctuation in \(\Phi\) is independent of scale. From equation (9.24), the rms density fluctuation on a scale \(K^{-1}\) is \(\rho_{\rm RMS}(K)\approx\rho_{0}\sigma_{K}\propto K^{(3+n)/2}\). The rms mass fluctuation is \(M_{\rm RMS}(K)\approx\rho_{\rm RMS}(K)K^{-3}\propto K^{(n-3)/2}\), and the corresponding fluctuation in the gravitational potential is \(\Phi_{\rm RMS}(K)\approx GM_{\rm RMS}(K)K\propto K^{(n-1)/2}\). Thus the rms potential fluctuation is independent of scale if and only if \(n=1\).

##### 9.1.2 Gravitational instability in the expanding universe

At early times \(|\delta({\bf x})|\ll 1\) everywhere and we can derive linear equations for the evolution of the overdensity. For the moment we assume that the cosmic material behaves like a fluid, so consider the form taken by the continuity and Euler equations (eqs. F.3 and F.10) in comoving coordinates \({\bf x}\). In principle this can be found by use of the chain rule, but in practice it is simpler to go back to the underlying physics. There are two limiting cases to consider, depending on whether relativistic or non-relativistic fluid dominates the inhomogeneities.

**(a) Non-relativistic fluid** Let \(V\) be a volume that is fixed in comoving coordinates. With \(\rho\) the usual fluid density, the mass contained in \(V\) is \(M=a^{3}\int_{V}{\rm d}^{3}{\bf x}\,\rho\). With \({\bf v}=\dot{\bf x}\) the rate of change of the comoving coordinate of the fluid element that is at \({\bf x}\), the physical velocity of the fluid relative to the boundary of \(V\) is \(a{\bf v}\), so the rate at which mass flows out of \(V\) is \(a^{2}\oint_{V}{\rm d}^{2}{\bf S}\cdot(a{\bf v})\,\rho\), where \(a^{2}{\rm d}^{2}{\bf S}\) is the vector whose magnitude is the area of an element of the surface of \(V\), and whose direction is the outward normal to the surface. Equating this to minus the derivative of the integral that gives \(M\), and bearing in mind that \(V\) is arbitrary, we conclude that

\[{\partial\over\partial t}(a^{3}\rho)+a^{3}{\bf\nabla}\cdot(\rho{\bf v})=0,\]

where \({\bf\nabla}=\partial/\partial{\bf x}\). Expanding the derivative on the left and identifying \(H(t)=\dot{a}/a\) as the Hubble parameter at time \(t\), the continuity equation in comoving coordinates becomes

\[{\partial\rho\over\partial t}+3H\rho+{\bf\nabla}\cdot(\rho{\bf v})=0.\]

Consider next the form of the Euler equation in comoving coordinates. The kinetic energy per unit mass of a particle that is located at \({\bf x}\) and has comoving velocity \({\bf v}\equiv{\rm d}{\bf x}/{\rm d}t\) is \({1\over 2}(\dot{a}{\bf x}+a{\bf v})^{2}\), so the particle's Lagrangian is

\[{\cal L}={1\over 2}(\dot{a}{\bf x}+a{\bf v})^{2}-\Phi.\]From equation (D.48) we deduce that its equation of motion is

\[\frac{\mathrm{d}}{\mathrm{d}t}\left[(\dot{a}\mathbf{x}+a\mathbf{v})a\right]-( \dot{a}\mathbf{x}+a\mathbf{v})\dot{a}+\boldsymbol{\nabla}\Phi=0. \tag{9.28}\]

We can rearrange this to

\[\frac{\mathrm{d}\mathbf{v}}{\mathrm{d}t}+2H\mathbf{v}+\frac{\ddot{a}}{a} \mathbf{x}+\frac{1}{a^{2}}\boldsymbol{\nabla}\Phi=0. \tag{9.29}\]

If we now consider the particle to be the element of fluid located at \(\mathbf{x}\), and bear in mind that in addition to the gravitational force this element may be subject to a pressure force, we see that in comoving coordinates Euler's equation (F.10) reads

\[\frac{\partial\mathbf{v}}{\partial t}+(\mathbf{v}\cdot\boldsymbol{\nabla}) \mathbf{v}+2H\mathbf{v}+\frac{\ddot{a}}{a}\mathbf{x}=-\frac{1}{a^{2}}\left( \frac{1}{\rho}\boldsymbol{\nabla}p+\boldsymbol{\nabla}\Phi\right). \tag{9.30}\]

We now linearize the continuity equation (9.26) around the undisturbed Hubble flow in which \(\mathbf{v}=0\) and \(\rho\) is independent of \(\mathbf{x}\). Using subscripts \(0\) and \(1\) to denote the undisturbed and perturbed parts of quantities, we have

\[\frac{\partial\rho_{0}}{\partial t}+3H\rho_{0}=0\quad;\quad\frac{\partial\rho _{1}}{\partial t}+3H\rho_{1}+\rho_{0}\boldsymbol{\nabla}\cdot\mathbf{v}=0. \tag{9.31}\]

The first of these simply implies that \(\rho_{0}\propto a^{-3}\) (eq. 1.59 with \(w=0\)). Dividing the second equation by \(\rho_{0}(t)\) yields

\[\begin{split} 0&=\frac{\partial\delta}{\partial t}+ \frac{\partial\ln\rho_{0}}{\partial t}\,\delta+3H\delta+\boldsymbol{\nabla} \cdot\mathbf{v}\\ &=\frac{\partial\delta}{\partial t}+\boldsymbol{\nabla}\cdot \mathbf{v},\end{split} \tag{9.32}\]

where the second equality uses the first of equations (9.31).

Similarly linearizing equation (9.30) we obtain

\[\ddot{a}a\mathbf{x}=-\boldsymbol{\nabla}\Phi_{0}\quad;\quad\frac{\partial \mathbf{v}}{\partial t}+2H\mathbf{v}=-\frac{1}{a^{2}}\left(\frac{1}{\rho_{0}} \boldsymbol{\nabla}p_{1}+\boldsymbol{\nabla}\Phi_{1}\right). \tag{9.33}\]

In the first equation, \(\boldsymbol{\nabla}\Phi_{0}=-GM\mathbf{x}/(a^{2}x^{3})\), where \(M\) is the gravitational mass within a sphere of comoving radius \(x\), and this equation is equivalent to equation (1.47). Taking the curl of the second equation, we find that the vorticity \(\boldsymbol{\omega}\equiv\boldsymbol{\nabla}\times\mathbf{v}\) satisfies \(\partial\boldsymbol{\omega}/\partial t=-2H\boldsymbol{\omega}\), so any vorticity initially present decays with time. Hence we may assume that \(\boldsymbol{\omega}=\mathbf{0}\). Since any vector field that has vanishing curl can written as the gradient of a scalar field, we can write \({\bf v}=\mathbf{\nabla}\psi\). When we use this expression to eliminate \({\bf v}\) from (9.33) we obtain

\[\mathbf{\nabla}\left[\frac{\partial\psi}{\partial t}+2H\psi+\frac{1}{a^{ 2}}\left(\frac{p_{1}}{\rho_{0}}+\Phi_{1}\right)\right]=0. \tag{9.34}\]

Evidently the expression in square brackets is independent of \({\bf x}\), so it can only depend on \(t\). Nothing of physical significance is changed if we add to \(\psi\) any function of \(t\) only, and we exploit this freedom to ensure that the square bracket always vanishes, so we have

\[\frac{\partial\psi}{\partial t}+2H\psi+\frac{1}{a^{2}}\left(\frac{p_{1}}{\rho_ {0}}+\Phi_{1}\right)=0. \tag{9.35}\]

Taking the Fourier transform of equation (9.32) yields \(\partial\delta_{\bf k}/\partial t=k^{2}\psi_{\bf k}\). Similarly Fourier transforming equation (9.35) and eliminating \(\psi_{\bf k}\) between these equations we obtain

\[\frac{\partial^{2}\delta_{\bf k}}{\partial t^{2}}+2H\frac{\partial\delta_{\bf k }}{\partial t}+\frac{k^{2}}{a^{2}}\left(\frac{p_{1{\bf k}}}{\rho_{0}}+\Phi_{1 {\bf k}}\right)=0. \tag{9.36}\]

To proceed further we require a relation between \(p_{1{\bf k}}\) and \(\delta_{\bf k}\). Simple inflationary theories predict that the cosmic fluid is everywhere on the same adiabat, so \(p_{1{\bf k}}=v_{\rm s}^{2}\rho_{0}\delta_{\bf k}\) (Peacock 1999), where \(v_{\rm s}\) is the sound speed (eq. F.50). Poisson's equation \(a^{-2}\nabla^{2}\Phi_{1}=4\pi G\rho_{1}\) links \(\Phi_{1}\) to the perturbed density. Taking the Fourier transform of Poisson's equation we eliminate \(\Phi_{1{\bf k}}\) from equation (9.36) and have finally

\[\frac{\partial^{2}\delta_{\bf k}}{\partial t^{2}}+2H\frac{\partial\delta_{\bf k }}{\partial t}+\left(\frac{k^{2}}{a^{2}}v_{\rm s}^{2}-4\pi G\rho_{0}\right) \delta_{\bf k}=0. \tag{9.37}\]

In this equation \(\rho_{0}\) is the undisturbed value of the density of the non-relativistic fluid, whose disturbed value is measured by \(\delta_{\bf k}\). Our derivation has excluded relativistic fluids, which can couple to the non-relativistic fluid, either through \(\Phi_{1}\) or through electromagnetic interactions such as Thomson scattering. However, the derivation of equation (9.37) _is_ valid in the presence of vacuum energy, which does not cluster, if \(\rho_{0}\) is interpreted as the matter density \(\rho_{\rm m}\) rather than the total density \(\rho_{\rm m}+\rho_{\Lambda}\). In any given cosmological model \(H\), \(v_{\rm s}\) and \(\rho_{0}\) are known functions of time, so we can solve this equation for the evolution of the overdensity.

We first examine the solution in the important case of large-scale, long-wavelength perturbations, for which \(k\) is small. Then the term that is proportional to \(k^{2}\) can be neglected. In this case the solutions are given in Problem 9.1 for a universe that contains only non-relativistic matter and vacuum energy. In the special case of matter domination, which holds over the interval \(3100\gtrsim z\gtrsim 0.5\) (eqs. 1.70 and 1.74) in which most structure forms, \(H=2/(3t)\) and \(4\pi G\rho_{0}=2/(3t^{2})\) (eq. 1.63). The solutions of (9.37) are then linear combinations of the growing and decaying power laws,

\[\delta_{\bf k}\propto t^{2/3}\qquad\mbox{and}\qquad\delta_{\bf k}\propto t^{-1}. \tag{9.38}\]

After some time the growing solution will dominate, so we may neglect the decaying one. In the current and future epoch of domination by vacuum energy, \(H\) is a constant and \(4\pi G\rho_{0}=\frac{3}{2}H^{2}\Omega_{\rm m}\) (cf. eq. 1.65) rapidly becomes negligible, so equation (9.37) with \(k=0\) may be approximated by

\[\frac{\partial^{2}\delta_{\bf k}}{\partial t^{2}}+2H\frac{\partial\delta_{\bf k }}{\partial t}=0. \tag{9.39}\]

The general solution of this equation is \(\delta_{\rm m}(t)=\alpha+\beta\exp(-2Ht)\), where \(\alpha\) and \(\beta\) are constants; in words, \(\delta_{\bf k}\) is asymptotically constant. To summarize, density fluctuations grow as \(t^{2/3}\) when the universe is matter-dominated, but this growth freezes out once the universe becomes dominated by vacuum energy at \(z_{\rm m\Lambda}\simeq 0.5\).

Let \(t_{\rm i}\) be a time early in the era of matter domination, for example the time of decoupling. Then we define the **growth factor**\(g_{\rm i}(t)\) to be the growth in the amplitude of long-wavelength perturbations between \(t_{\rm i}\) and a subsequent time \(t\):

\[g_{\rm i}\equiv\frac{\delta_{\bf 0}(t)}{\delta_{\bf 0}(t_{\rm i})}. \tag{9.40}\]

Figure 9.1: Full curve: \((t/t_{\rm i})^{-2/3}\) times the growth factor \(g_{\rm i}(t)\) defined by equation (9.40) with \(t_{\rm i}\) corresponding to \(1+z=1000\). The values of \(g_{\rm i}\) were obtained by numerical evaluation of the integral (9.92) for a flat universe with \(\Omega_{\rm m0}=1-\Omega_{\Lambda 0}=0.27\) (eq. 1.73) Dashed curve: \((t/t_{\rm i})^{2/3}a(t_{\rm i})/a(t)\) for the same cosmology.

The full curve in Figure 9.1 is a plot of \((t/t_{\rm i})^{-2/3}g_{\rm i}(t)\) for a flat universe similar to our own (eq. 1.73), while the dashed curve shows \((t/t_{\rm i})^{2/3}a(t_{\rm i})/a(t)\). Throughout the matter-dominated era both plotted quantities are indistinguishable from unity, attesting to the accuracy of the approximations \(g_{\rm i}(t)\propto t^{2/3}\) and \(a(t)\propto t^{2/3}\). The curves turn below unity around \(z=2\), as \(g_{\rm i}\) starts to grow more slowly, and \(a\) grows faster, than in a matter-dominated cosmology. However, even at the present epoch \(g_{\rm i}\) is only 10% below the value it would have in the absence of vacuum energy.

**(b) Relativistic fluid** Equation (9.37) is invalid during the radiation-dominated era, \(z\gtrsim z_{\gamma{\rm m}}\simeq 3100\) (eq. 1.70), because the mass-energy density of radiation does not satisfy the continuity equation (9.26): during expansion the total energy of a body of radiation decreases. However there is a different conservation law we can use. The mean free path of photons is short, so each volume of the photon fluid conserves its entropy. Thus the radiation's entropy density _does_ satisfy equation (9.26). The energy and entropy densities of black-body radiation of temperature \(T\) are proportional to \(T^{4}\) and \(T^{3}\), respectively (Problem 9.2), so from equation (9.26) we conclude that conservation of entropy implies that

\[\frac{\partial\rho^{3/4}}{\partial t}+3H\rho^{3/4}+\mathbf{\nabla} \cdot(\rho^{3/4}{\bf v})=0. \tag{9.41}\]

After multiplying through by \(\frac{4}{3}\rho^{1/4}\) this can be rewritten

\[\frac{\partial\rho}{\partial t}+4H\rho+{\bf v}\cdot\mathbf{\nabla} \rho+\mbox{$\frac{4}{3}$}\rho\mathbf{\nabla}\cdot{\bf v}=0. \tag{9.42}\]

Linearizing, we find that for radiation the analog of equation (9.32) is

\[\frac{\partial\delta}{\partial t}+\mbox{$\frac{4}{3}$}\mbox{\boldmath$\nabla$ }\cdot{\bf v}=0. \tag{9.43}\]

Taking the Fourier transform of this equation we find that \(\partial\delta_{\bf k}/\partial t=\frac{4}{3}k^{2}\psi_{\bf k}\). Before proceeding, we need to modify the analysis for non-relativistic matter in two ways. First, the linearized Euler equation (9.35) is modified by special relativity to (Problem 9.4)

\[\frac{\partial\psi}{\partial t}+2H\psi+\frac{1}{a^{2}}\left(\frac{p_{1}}{ \rho_{0}+p_{0}/c^{2}}+\Phi_{1}\right)=0. \tag{9.44}\]

For radiation \(p=\frac{1}{3}\rho c^{2}\), so \(p_{1{\bf k}}/(\rho_{0}+p_{0}/c^{2})=\frac{1}{4}c^{2}\delta_{\bf k}\). Second, according to general relativity, in Poisson's equation we must replace the density \(\rho\) by \(\rho+3p/c^{2}\) (Problem 9.5). For radiation this implies that \((k/a)^{2}\Phi_{1{\bf k}}=-8\pi G\rho_{0}\delta_{\bf k}\). When these relations are used to eliminate \(\psi_{\bf k}\), \(p_{1{\bf k}}\) and \(\Phi_{1{\bf k}}\) from the Fourier transform of equation (9.44), we obtain

\[\frac{\partial^{2}\delta_{\bf k}}{\partial t^{2}}+2H\frac{\partial\delta_{\bf k }}{\partial t}+\left(\frac{k^{2}c^{2}}{3a^{2}}-\mbox{$\frac{32}{3}$}\pi G\rho _{0}\right)\delta_{\bf k}=0. \tag{9.45}\]In the radiation-dominated era we have \(2H=1/t\) and \(\frac{32}{3}\pi G\rho_{0}=1/t^{2}\) (eq. 1.64), so equation (9.45) becomes

\[\frac{\partial^{2}\delta_{\bf k}}{\partial t^{2}}+\frac{1}{t}\frac{\partial \delta_{\bf k}}{\partial t}+\left(\frac{k^{2}c^{2}}{3a^{2}}-\frac{1}{t^{2}} \right)\delta_{\bf k}=0. \tag{9.46}\]

For \(k/a\mathrel{\hbox to 0.0pt{\raise 2.1973pt\hbox{$>$}}{\lower 2.1973pt\hbox{$ \sim$}}}1/ct\) the first term in the bracket dominates the second, and causes \(\delta_{\bf k}\) to oscillate: radiation pressure stabilizes perturbations with wavelengths \(2\pi a/k\) smaller than the horizon size \(\sim ct\), just as pressure was found to stabilize perturbations with wavelength less than the Jeans length in SS5.2: the short-wavelength fluctuations are simply sound waves. On the other hand, when the wavelength is significantly larger than the horizon size, we can neglect this term and the solutions are linear combinations of the power laws

\[\delta_{\bf k}(t)\propto t\qquad\hbox{and}\qquad\delta_{\bf k}(t)\propto t^{- 1}. \tag{9.47}\]

These results tell us how fluctuations in the radiation density evolve in the radiation-dominated era. During this epoch a small fraction of the total density is contributed by non-relativistic matter, in the form of baryons and collisionless dark matter. This density is too small to affect the evolution of perturbations in the relativistic fluid. However, we need to understand how perturbations in these trace constituents evolve in the radiation-dominated era.

The baryon fluid is fully ionized and its free electrons scatter radiation efficiently. Consequently, the baryon fluid is forced to move with the same velocity as the radiation fluid. From equations (9.32) and (9.43), it follows that \(\delta({\rm matter})=\frac{3}{4}\delta({\rm radiation})\), so \(\delta({\rm matter})\propto t\) for wavelengths greater than the horizon size.

Next consider the evolution of fluctuations in the collisionless dark matter. In a collisionless fluid, random particle velocities cause overdensities to disperse, just as density perturbations in a stellar system with wavelength shorter than the Jeans wavelength are damped (SS5.2.4). This process erases fluctuations on scales \(\mathrel{\hbox to 0.0pt{\raise 2.1973pt\hbox{$<$}}{\lower 2.1973pt\hbox{$\sim$}}}\sigma t\) where \(\sigma\) is the velocity dispersion of the particles at time \(t\). At early times most candidate particles for the dark matter are relativistic,4 so \(\sigma\sim c\) and the fluctuations are damped on all scales less than the horizon \(a(t)x_{\rm h}(t)\sim ct\) (eq. 1.68). Once the dark-matter particles become non-relativistic, their velocity dispersion declines and the damping scale becomes smaller than the current horizon. Comparison with observations shows no evidence of suppression of the density fluctuations on small scales, which implies that the unknown dark-matter particles must have mass \(\mathrel{\hbox to 0.0pt{\raise 2.1973pt\hbox{$>$}}{\lower 2.1973pt\hbox{$\sim$}}}1\,{ \rm keV}\) (Blumenthal et al. 1984). Such particles become non-relativistic at \(z\gtrsim 5\times 10^{6}\) and their random velocities at decoupling are \(\lesssim 50\,{\rm km\,s^{-1}}\); dark matter satisfying these constraints is known as **cold dark matter** (**CDM**).

Precise calculations analogous to the simple ones that we have done can be carried out for a realistic universe that contains baryons, radiation and collisionless dark matter. The results of these calculations are encapsulated in the **transfer function**

\[T^{2}(k)=\frac{\langle\delta_{k}^{2}\rangle_{z=0}}{\langle\delta_{k}^{2} \rangle_{z\rightarrow\infty}}\bigg{/}\frac{\langle\delta_{0}^{2}\rangle_{z=0} }{\langle\delta_{0}^{2}\rangle_{z\rightarrow\infty}}. \tag{9.48}\]

Here \(\delta_{0}\) is the overdensity on large scales, which obeys equations such as (9.37) and (9.46) with \(k=0\). Thus \(T(k)\) represents the factor by which the linear fluctuations with wavenumber \(k\) are enhanced or suppressed relative to large-scale fluctuations, which are easily calculated for any given cosmological model--see Problem 9.1. By definition, \(T(0)=1\).

The transfer function is plotted in Figure 9.2 for a universe with parameters given by equation (1.73). Its structure is straightforward to understand qualitatively. While the universe is radiation-dominated, fluctuations in both matter and radiation initially grow as \(\delta\propto t\) (eq. 9.47), so long as the comoving wavelength of the fluctuation is larger than the growing horizon size (\(a/k\gtrsim ct\)). Once the fluctuation is contained within the horizon, its growth is stopped or greatly slowed: fluctuations in the radiation density cannot grow because the wavenumber exceeds the Jeans wavenumber as described after (9.46), and fluctuations in the baryons cannot grow because they are strongly coupled to the radiation. The growth of fluctuations in the non-baryonic dark matter also slows, but for a different reason: because the universe is radiation-dominated, the characteristic expansion time \(a/\dot{a}\sim(G\rho_{\gamma})^{-1/2}\) is much shorter than the characteristic growth time for gravitational instability in the matter, \(\sim(G\rho_{\rm m})^{-1/2}\), so the instability does not have time to grow.

Eventually, at redshift \(z_{\gamma{\rm m}}\simeq 3100\) (eq. 1.70 and Problem 1.13), the universe becomes matter-dominated, and the dark-matter fluctuations begin to grow as \(\delta\propto t^{2/3}\) (eq. 9.38). The baryons remain frozen to the radiation, and thus their fluctuations cannot grow, until decoupling at redshift \(z_{\rm d}\simeq 1100\) (eq. 1.71). During decoupling, the population of free electrons fades and the mean free path of photons increases. Hence there is an interval in which the baryons are still coupled to the photons, but the mean free path of the photons is not small. In this interval the photon-baryon fluid has large coefficients of viscosity and thermal conductivity, so fluctuations with scales smaller than the horizon, which are sound waves, are rapidly damped--this process is called **Silk damping** (Silk 1968). In fact, they are essentially eliminated on all scales smaller than that of rich clusters of galaxies \(\approx 10\,{\rm Mpc}\). If there were no dark matter, structures with scales smaller than \(10\,{\rm Mpc}\) could not form, so dark matter is _required_ for galaxy formation.5 However, small-scale fluctuations in the dark matter persist after decoupling, so the baryons fall into the potential wells that they generate, and from then on the dark-matter and baryon fluctuations evolve together. Thus notwithstanding Silk damping, the transfer function is essentially the same for dark matter and baryons in a given cosmological model.

Footnote 5: If we were to dispense with dark matter, the rotation curves of galaxies would oblige us to subscribe to a theory of gravity that differs materially from that used here, and in such a theory galaxies might be able to form without dark matter.

Let \(t_{\gamma{\rm m}}\) and \(x_{\gamma{\rm m}}=x_{\rm h}(t_{\gamma{\rm m}})\) be the time and the comoving horizon size when the universe becomes matter-dominated; for the model of equation (1.73), \(t_{\gamma{\rm m}}\simeq 6\times 10^{4}\,{\rm yr}\) (eq. 1.70) and \(x_{\gamma{\rm m}}\simeq 125\,{\rm Mpc}\) (Problem 1.13). Fluctuations with comoving wavelength \(2\pi/k\) larger than \(x_{\gamma{\rm m}}\) grow as \(t\) for \(t\lesssim t_{\gamma{\rm m}}\) and as \(t^{2/3}\) later, as predicted by equations (9.38) and (9.47); thus the transfer function \(T(k)\) is near unity for \(kx_{\gamma{\rm m}}\lesssim 1\). For shorter wavelengths, the growth \(\delta\propto t\) is suppressed by a factor \(t_{\rm h}/t_{\gamma{\rm m}}\), where \(t_{\rm h}\) is the time when this wavelength enters the horizon, defined by \(kx_{\rm h}(t_{\rm h})\sim 1\). Since \(x_{\rm h}\propto t^{1/2}\) in the radiation-dominated era (eq. 1.68), \(t_{\rm h}/t_{\gamma{\rm m}}\sim 1/(kx_{\gamma{\rm m}})^{2}\). Thus the transfer function is roughly

\[T(k)\approx\cases{1&$(kx_{\gamma{\rm m}}\lesssim 1)$\cr(kx_{\gamma{\rm m}})^{-2 }&$(kx_{\gamma{\rm m}}\gtrsim 1)$.} \tag{9.49}\]

The exact transfer function for a given cosmological model can be calculated using publicly available codes such as cmbfast(Seljak & Zaldarriaga 1996). For the important case of a flat universe containing cold dark matter, in

