### Appendix B: Mathematical background

The text presupposes an acquaintance with mathematical physics at the level of Jackson (1999) or Arfken & Weber (2005). This appendix contains a summary of some of the material and formulae that will be needed.

### B.1 Vectors

The location of the point with Cartesian coordinates \((x,y,z)\) may be described by a **position vector**,

\[{\bf x}=x{\hat{\bf e}}_{x}+y{\hat{\bf e}}_{y}+z{\hat{\bf e}}_{z},\] (B.1)

where \({\hat{\bf e}}_{x}\), \({\hat{\bf e}}_{y}\), and \({\hat{\bf e}}_{z}\) are fixed unit vectors that point along the \(x\), \(y\) and \(z\) axes. The distance of the point from the origin is written \(r\) or \(|{\bf x}|\) and is equal to \((x^{2}+y^{2}+z^{2})^{1/2}\).

Similarly, we represent an arbitrary vector \({\bf A}\) in component form as

\[{\bf A}=A_{x}{\hat{\bf e}}_{x}+A_{y}{\hat{\bf e}}_{y}+A_{z}{\hat{\bf e}}_{z}.\] (B.2)The magnitude of a vector \(\mathbf{A}\) is \(A\equiv|\mathbf{A}|\equiv(A_{x}^{2}+A_{y}^{2}+A_{z}^{2})^{1/2}\).

The **scalar** or **dot product** of two vectors \(\mathbf{A}\) and \(\mathbf{B}\) is

\[\mathbf{A}\cdot\mathbf{B}\equiv|\mathbf{A}||\mathbf{B}|\cos\psi,\] (B.3)

where \(\psi\) is the angle between the two vectors, placed tail to tail. Note that \(\mathbf{A}\cdot\mathbf{B}=\mathbf{B}\cdot\mathbf{A}\) and \(\mathbf{A}\cdot\mathbf{A}=\left|\mathbf{A}\right|^{2}\). Since \(\hat{\mathbf{e}}_{x}\cdot\hat{\mathbf{e}}_{x}=\hat{\mathbf{e}}_{y}\cdot\hat{ \mathbf{e}}_{y}=\hat{\mathbf{e}}_{z}\cdot\hat{\mathbf{e}}_{z}=1\), and \(\hat{\mathbf{e}}_{x}\cdot\hat{\mathbf{e}}_{y}=\hat{\mathbf{e}}_{x}\cdot\hat{ \mathbf{e}}_{z}=\hat{\mathbf{e}}_{y}\cdot\hat{\mathbf{e}}_{z}=0\), we may write the dot product in component form as

\[\mathbf{A}\cdot\mathbf{B}=\sum_{i=1}^{3}A_{i}B_{i},\] (B.4)

where the subscripts \(1\), \(2\), and \(3\) stand for \(x\), \(y\), and \(z\), respectively. For simplicity we generally adopt the **summation convention**: we automatically sum from \(1\) to \(3\) over any dummy subscript that appears repeatedly in one term of an equation. Thus equation (B.4) may be written

\[\mathbf{A}\cdot\mathbf{B}=A_{i}B_{i}.\] (B.5)

The **vector** or **cross product** of two vectors is

\[\mathbf{A}\times\mathbf{B}\equiv AB\sin\psi\,\hat{\mathbf{p}},\] (B.6)

where \(\hat{\mathbf{p}}\) is a unit vector that is perpendicular to the plane containing \(\mathbf{A}\) and \(\mathbf{B}\) and points in the direction of movement of a right-hand screw when \(\mathbf{A}\) is rotated about the origin into \(\mathbf{B}\). Note that \(\mathbf{A}\times\mathbf{B}=-\mathbf{B}\times\mathbf{A}\), that \(\mathbf{A}\times\mathbf{A}=0\), and that \(\hat{\mathbf{e}}_{x}\times\hat{\mathbf{e}}_{y}=\hat{\mathbf{e}}_{z}\), \(\hat{\mathbf{e}}_{y}\times\hat{\mathbf{e}}_{z}=\hat{\mathbf{e}}_{x}\), \(\hat{\mathbf{e}}_{z}\times\hat{\mathbf{e}}_{x}=\hat{\mathbf{e}}_{y}\). In component form the cross product may be written

\[\mathbf{A}\times\mathbf{B}=\epsilon_{ijk}\hat{\mathbf{e}}_{i}A_{j}B_{k},\] (B.7)

where a sum over \(i\), \(j\), and \(k\) is implied by the summation convention. Here \(\epsilon_{ijk}\) is the **permutation tensor** which is defined to be zero if any two or more of the indices \(i\), \(j\), and \(k\) are equal, \(+1\) if \((i,j,k)\) is an even permutation of \((1,2,3)\) [the even permutations are \((2,3,1)\) and \((3,1,2)\)] and \(-1\) if \((i,j,k)\) is an odd permutation of \((1,2,3)\).

Three important identities that involve the dot and cross product are:

\[\mathbf{A}\cdot(\mathbf{B}\times\mathbf{C})=\mathbf{C}\cdot(\mathbf{A}\times \mathbf{B})=\mathbf{B}\cdot(\mathbf{C}\times\mathbf{A}),\] (B.8)

\[\mathbf{A}\times(\mathbf{B}\times\mathbf{C})=(\mathbf{A}\cdot\mathbf{C}) \mathbf{B}-(\mathbf{A}\cdot\mathbf{B})\mathbf{C},\] (B.9)

\[(\mathbf{A}\times\mathbf{B})\cdot(\mathbf{C}\times\mathbf{D})=(\mathbf{A} \cdot\mathbf{C})(\mathbf{B}\cdot\mathbf{D})-(\mathbf{A}\cdot\mathbf{D})( \mathbf{B}\cdot\mathbf{C}).\] (B.10)

In proving these identities it is useful to employ the relation

\[\epsilon_{ijk}\epsilon_{klm}=\delta_{il}\delta_{jm}-\delta_{im}\delta_{jl},\] (B.11)

where the summation convention has been used (over index \(k\)) and \(\delta_{pq}\) is defined to be \(1\) if \(p=q\) and zero otherwise.

The velocity and acceleration of a particle may be written in Cartesian components as

\[\mathbf{v}\equiv\dot{\mathbf{x}}\equiv\frac{\mathrm{d}\mathbf{x}}{\mathrm{d}t} =\dot{x}\hat{\mathbf{e}}_{x}+\dot{y}\hat{\mathbf{e}}_{y}+\dot{z}\hat{ \mathbf{e}}_{z}\quad;\quad\mathbf{a}\equiv\ddot{\mathbf{x}}\equiv\frac{ \mathrm{d}^{2}\mathbf{x}}{\mathrm{d}t^{2}}=\ddot{x}\hat{\mathbf{e}}_{x}+\ddot{y }\hat{\mathbf{e}}_{y}+\ddot{z}\hat{\mathbf{e}}_{z}.\] (B.12)

### Curvilinear coordinate systems

Let \((q_{1},q_{2},q_{3})\) denote the coordinates of a point in an arbitrary coordinate system. A fundamental quantity of any coordinate system is the **metric tensor**\(h_{ij}({\bf q})\), defined such that the distance \({\rm d}s\) between the points \((q_{1},q_{2},q_{3})\) and \((q_{1}+{\rm d}q_{1},\,q_{2}+{\rm d}q_{2},\,q_{3}+{\rm d}q_{3})\) is given by

\[{\rm d}s^{2}=h_{ij}{\rm d}q_{i}{\rm d}q_{j};\] (B.13)

summation over \(i\) and \(j\) from 1 to 3 is implied by the summation convention. The coordinate systems used in this book are **orthogonal**, that is, \(h_{ij}=0\) if \(i\neq j\). In this case we write \(h_{ii}\equiv h_{i}^{2}\), so

\[{\rm d}s^{2}=h_{i}^{2}{\rm d}q_{i}^{2}.\] (B.14)

The velocity is

\[\dot{\bf x}=\sum_{i}h_{i}\frac{{\rm d}q_{i}}{{\rm d}t}\hat{\bf e}_{i},\] (B.15)

where \(\hat{\bf e}_{1}\) is a unit vector pointing in the direction from \((q_{1},q_{2},q_{3})\) to \((q_{1}+{\rm d}q_{1},q_{2},q_{3})\), etc. The volume element in orthogonal coordinates is

\[{\rm d}^{3}{\bf x}=h_{1}h_{2}h_{3}{\rm d}q_{1}{\rm d}q_{2}{\rm d}q_{3}.\] (B.16)

For Cartesian coordinates, \((q_{1},q_{2},q_{3})=(x,y,z)\) and \(h_{1}=h_{2}=h_{3}=1\).

**Cylindrical coordinate system**  In this system the location of a point is denoted by the triple \((R,\phi,z)\), where \(R\) is the perpendicular distance from the \(z\) axis to the point, and \(\phi\) is the azimuthal angle between the \(x\) axis and the projection of the position vector onto the \((x,y)\) plane (Figure B.1). Thus the relation to Cartesian coordinates is

\[x=R\cos\phi\quad;\quad y=R\sin\phi\quad;\quad z=z.\] (B.17)

In cylindrical coordinates the position vector is

\[{\bf x}=R\hat{\bf e}_{R}+z\hat{\bf e}_{z}.\] (B.18)

Any vector may be written \({\bf A}=A_{R}\hat{\bf e}_{R}+A_{\phi}\hat{\bf e}_{\phi}+A_{z}\hat{\bf e}_{z}\), where \(\hat{\bf e}_{\phi}=\hat{\bf e}_{z}\times\hat{\bf e}_{R}\) and

\[A_{x}=A_{R}\cos\phi-A_{\phi}\sin\phi\quad;\quad A_{y}=A_{R}\sin\phi+A_{\phi} \cos\phi\quad;\quad A_{z}=A_{z}.\] (B.19)The expressions for dot and cross products in cylindrical coordinates are simply equations (B.5) and (B.7), with the subscripts \((1,2,3)\) denoting \((R,\phi,z)\) instead of \((x,y,z)\); however, the decomposition into components must be carried out at the same position for both vectors in the product, since the directions of \(\hat{\mathbf{e}}_{R}\) and \(\hat{\mathbf{e}}_{\phi}\) depend on position.

The velocity in cylindrical coordinates is

\[\mathbf{v}=\frac{\mathrm{d}\mathbf{x}}{\mathrm{d}t}=\dot{R}\hat{\mathbf{e}}_{R }+R\dot{\hat{\mathbf{e}}}_{R}+\dot{z}\hat{\mathbf{e}}_{z}.\] (B.20)

To compute \(\dot{\hat{\mathbf{e}}}_{R}\) we use equation (B.19) with \(A_{R}=1\), \(A_{\phi}=0\), \(A_{z}=0\). Thus \(\dot{\hat{\mathbf{e}}}_{R}=\cos\phi\,\hat{\mathbf{e}}_{x}+\sin\phi\,\hat{ \mathbf{e}}_{y}\), and \(\mathrm{d}\dot{\hat{\mathbf{e}}}_{R}=(-\sin\phi\,\hat{\mathbf{e}}_{x}+\cos \phi\,\hat{\mathbf{e}}_{y})\mathrm{d}\phi.\) The expression in parentheses is just \(\hat{\mathbf{e}}_{\phi}\). After carrying out a similar analysis for \(\hat{\mathbf{e}}_{\phi}\) we have

\[\frac{\mathrm{d}\hat{\mathbf{e}}_{R}}{\mathrm{d}\phi}=\hat{\mathbf{e}}_{\phi} \quad;\quad\frac{\mathrm{d}\hat{\mathbf{e}}_{\phi}}{\mathrm{d}\phi}=-\hat{ \mathbf{e}}_{R},\] (B.21)

and

\[\dot{\hat{\mathbf{e}}}_{R}=+\dot{\phi}\,\hat{\mathbf{e}}_{\phi}\quad;\quad \dot{\hat{\mathbf{e}}}_{\phi}=-\dot{\phi}\,\hat{\mathbf{e}}_{R}.\] (B.22)

Thus the velocity is

\[\mathbf{v}=\dot{R}\hat{\mathbf{e}}_{R}+R\dot{\phi}\hat{\mathbf{e}}_{\phi}+ \dot{z}\hat{\mathbf{e}}_{z}.\] (B.23)

The acceleration is

\[\mathbf{a}=\frac{\mathrm{d}\mathbf{v}}{\mathrm{d}t}=(\ddot{R}-R\dot{\phi}^{2} )\hat{\mathbf{e}}_{R}+(2\dot{R}\dot{\phi}+R\ddot{\phi})\hat{\mathbf{e}}_{\phi }+\ddot{z}\hat{\mathbf{e}}_{z}.\] (B.24)

For cylindrical coordinates the metric tensor is given by (B.14) with

\[h_{R}=1\quad;\quad h_{\phi}=R\quad;\quad h_{z}=1.\] (B.25)

The volume element is \(\mathrm{d}^{3}\mathbf{x}=R\,\mathrm{d}R\,\mathrm{d}\phi\,\mathrm{d}z\).

**Spherical coordinate system** The position of a point in these coordinates is denoted by \((r,\theta,\phi)\) (Figure B.1). The coordinate \(r\) is the radial distance from the origin to the point; \(\theta\) is the angle between the position vector and the \(z\) axis; and \(\phi\) is the same azimuthal angle used in cylindrical coordinates. The relation to Cartesian coordinates is

\[x=r\sin\theta\cos\phi\quad;\quad y=r\sin\theta\sin\phi\quad;\quad z=r\cos\theta.\] (B.26)

In spherical coordinates the position vector is simply

\[\mathbf{x}=r\hat{\mathbf{e}}_{r}.\] (B.27)

Any vector may be written \(\mathbf{A}=A_{r}\hat{\mathbf{e}}_{r}+A_{\theta}\hat{\mathbf{e}}_{\theta}+A_{ \phi}\hat{\mathbf{e}}_{\phi}\), where \(\hat{\mathbf{e}}_{\theta}=\hat{\mathbf{e}}_{\phi}\times\hat{\mathbf{e}}_{r}\) and

\[A_{x} =A_{r}\sin\theta\cos\phi+A_{\theta}\cos\theta\cos\phi-A_{\phi} \sin\phi,\] \[A_{y} =A_{r}\sin\theta\sin\phi+A_{\theta}\cos\theta\sin\phi+A_{\phi} \cos\phi,\] (B.28) \[A_{z} =A_{r}\cos\theta-A_{\theta}\sin\theta.\]

Once again, the expressions for dot and cross products in spherical coordinates are equations (B.5) and (B.7), with the subscripts \((1,2,3)\) denoting \((r,\theta,\phi)\) instead of \((x,y,z)\), and with the understanding that the decomposition into components must be carried out at the same position for both vectors in the product.

The rate of change of the unit vectors is

\[\dot{\hat{\mathbf{e}}}_{r}=\dot{\theta}\hat{\mathbf{e}}_{\theta}+\dot{\phi} \sin\theta\hat{\mathbf{e}}_{\phi}\,:\,\dot{\hat{\mathbf{e}}}_{\theta}=-\dot{ \theta}\hat{\mathbf{e}}_{r}+\dot{\phi}\cos\theta\hat{\mathbf{e}}_{\phi}\,:\, \dot{\hat{\mathbf{e}}}_{\phi}=-\dot{\phi}\sin\theta\hat{\mathbf{e}}_{r}-\dot{ \phi}\cos\theta\hat{\mathbf{e}}_{\theta}.\] (B.29)Thus the velocity is

\[{\bf v}=\hat{r}\hat{\bf e}_{r}+r\hat{\theta}\hat{\bf e}_{\theta}+r\sin\theta\dot{ \phi}\hat{\bf e}_{\phi}.\] (B.30)

The acceleration is

\[\begin{split}{\bf a}=\frac{{\rm d}{\bf v}}{{\rm d}t}&= (\ddot{r}-r\dot{\theta}^{2}-r\sin^{2}\theta\dot{\phi}^{2})\hat{\bf e}_{r}+(2\dot {r}\dot{\theta}+r\ddot{\theta}-r\sin\theta\cos\theta\dot{\phi}^{2})\hat{\bf e}_ {\theta}\\ &\quad+(r\sin\theta\ddot{\phi}+2\sin\theta\dot{r}\dot{\phi}+2r \cos\theta\dot{\theta}\dot{\phi})\hat{\bf e}_{\phi}.\end{split}\] (B.31)

For spherical coordinates the metric tensor is given by

\[h_{r}=1\quad;\quad h_{\theta}=r\quad;\quad h_{\phi}=r\sin\theta.\] (B.32)

The volume element is \({\rm d}^{3}{\bf x}=r^{2}\sin\theta\,{\rm d}r{\rm d}\theta{\rm d}\phi\).

For brevity the two angular coordinates are sometimes written as \({\bf\Omega}\equiv(\theta,\phi)\), and the element of area on the unit sphere is written \({\rm d}^{2}\Omega\equiv\sin\theta\,{\rm d}\theta\,{\rm d}\phi\).

### **B.3 Vector calculus**

GradientIn Cartesian coordinates we define the **gradient** of a scalar function of position \(f({\bf x})\) to be

\[{\bf\nabla}f\equiv\hat{\bf e}_{x}\frac{\partial f}{\partial x}+\hat{\bf e}_{y }\frac{\partial f}{\partial y}+\hat{\bf e}_{z}\frac{\partial f}{\partial z}= \hat{\bf e}_{i}\frac{\partial f}{\partial x_{i}},\] (B.33)

where \((x_{1},x_{2},x_{3})=(x,y,z)\) and we have used the summation convention. The symbol \({\bf\nabla}\) is called grad, del, or nabla and is considered to be a vector operator defined by

\[{\bf\nabla}=\hat{\bf e}_{i}\frac{\partial}{\partial x_{i}}.\] (B.34)

Where it is necessary to distinguish the variable used in the gradient, we write

\[{\bf\nabla}_{\bf x}\quad{\rm or}\quad\frac{\partial}{\partial{\bf x}}.\] (B.35)

The change in the value of \(f\) between the points \({\bf x}\) and \({\bf x}+{\rm d}{\bf x}\) is

\[{\rm d}f=\frac{\partial f}{\partial x_{i}}{\rm d}x_{i}={\bf\nabla}f\cdot d{\bf x}.\] (B.36)

If \({\bf x}\) and \({\bf x}+{\rm d}{\bf x}\) lie on a surface \(S\) on which \(f\) is constant, that is, if \(f({\bf x})=f({\bf x}+{\rm d}{\bf x})\), then \({\rm d}f=0\) and \({\bf\nabla}f\cdot d{\bf x}=0\), so \({\bf\nabla}f\) is orthogonal to \({\rm d}{\bf x}\). Since \({\rm d}{\bf x}\) can be chosen to be any infinitesimal vector lying in \(S\), \({\bf\nabla}f\) must be normal to \(S\) itself. Hence the gradient of \(f\) is normal to surfaces of constant \(f\).

In cylindrical coordinates the expression for the gradient must be modified, since \({\rm d}{\bf x}\) is not \({\rm d}R\,\hat{\bf e}_{R}+{\rm d}\phi\,\hat{\bf e}_{\phi}+{\rm d}z\,\hat{\bf e }_{z}\) but \({\rm d}R\,\hat{\bf e}_{R}+R{\rm d}\phi\,\hat{\bf e}_{\phi}+{\rm d}z\,\hat{\bf e }_{z}\). Hence for consistency with equation (B.36) we must have

\[{\bf\nabla}=\hat{\bf e}_{R}\frac{\partial}{\partial R}+\frac{\hat{\bf e}_{ \phi}}{R}\frac{\partial}{\partial\phi}+\hat{\bf e}_{z}\frac{\partial}{ \partial z}.\] (B.37)

In spherical coordinates

\[{\bf\nabla}=\hat{\bf e}_{r}\frac{\partial}{\partial r}+\frac{\hat{\bf e}_{ \theta}}{r}\frac{\partial}{\partial\theta}+\frac{\hat{\bf e}_{\phi}}{r\sin \theta}\frac{\partial}{\partial\phi}.\] (B.38)The general form valid in any orthogonal coordinate system is

\[\boldsymbol{\nabla}=\frac{\hat{\mathbf{e}}_{i}}{h_{i}}\frac{\partial}{\partial q_ {i}}.\] (B.39)

We shall use the identity

\[\boldsymbol{\nabla}_{\mathbf{x}}[\mathbf{a}\cdot(\mathbf{x}\times\mathbf{b})]= \boldsymbol{\nabla}_{\mathbf{x}}[\mathbf{x}\cdot(\mathbf{b}\times\mathbf{a})] =\mathbf{b}\times\mathbf{a},\] (B.40)

where \(\mathbf{a}\) and \(\mathbf{b}\) are constants.

**Divergence**  In Cartesian coordinates we define the divergence of a vector-valued function \(\mathbf{F}(\mathbf{x})\) to be the scalar

\[\boldsymbol{\nabla}\cdot\mathbf{F}\equiv\frac{\partial F_{x}}{\partial x}+ \frac{\partial F_{y}}{\partial y}+\frac{\partial F_{z}}{\partial z}=\frac{ \partial F_{i}}{\partial x_{i}}.\] (B.41)

To clarify the physical meaning of the divergence, consider a volume \(V\) enclosed by a surface \(S\). For simplicity, assume initially that the volume is cubical, occupying the region \(x_{ia}\leq x_{i}\leq x_{ib}\), \(i=1,2,3\). Then

\[\int_{V}\mathrm{d}^{3}\mathbf{x}\,\boldsymbol{\nabla}\cdot \mathbf{F}=\int_{x_{1a}}^{x_{1b}}\mathrm{d}x_{1}\int_{x_{2a}}^{x_{2b}}\mathrm{ d}x_{2}\int_{x_{3a}}^{x_{3b}}\mathrm{d}x_{3}\bigg{(}\frac{\partial F_{1}}{ \partial x_{1}}+\frac{\partial F_{2}}{\partial x_{2}}+\frac{\partial F_{3}}{ \partial x_{3}}\bigg{)}\] (B.42) \[=\int_{x_{2a}}^{x_{2b}}\mathrm{d}x_{2}\int_{x_{3a}}^{x_{3b}} \mathrm{d}x_{3}\left[F_{1}(x_{1b},x_{2},x_{3})-F_{1}(x_{1a},x_{2},x_{3}) \right]+\text{two similar terms}.\]

This expression may be written more concisely as the area integral \(\oint_{S}\mathrm{d}^{2}\mathbf{S}\cdot\mathbf{F}\), where \(\mathrm{d}^{2}S\) is the area of a small element of the surface and \(\mathrm{d}^{2}\mathbf{S}\) is a vector normal to the surface, pointing outward, with magnitude \(\mathrm{d}^{2}S\). (Note that in this notation \(\mathrm{d}^{3}\mathbf{x}\) is a scalar but \(\mathrm{d}^{2}\mathbf{S}\) is a vector.) We may generalize this result to an arbitrary volume by dividing the volume into many small cubes and noting that the surface integrals from the inside faces of the cubes cancel. Hence for an arbitrary volume \(V\),

\[\int_{V}\mathrm{d}^{3}\mathbf{x}\,\boldsymbol{\nabla}\cdot\mathbf{F}=\oint_{S }\mathrm{d}^{2}\mathbf{S}\cdot\mathbf{F}.\] (B.43)

This result is known as the **divergence theorem** or Gauss's theorem (we reserve the latter term for the application of the divergence theorem to Poisson's equation; see eq. 2.12). In Cartesian coordinates we have

\[\int_{V}\mathrm{d}^{3}\mathbf{x}\,\frac{\partial F_{j}}{\partial x_{j}}=\oint _{S}\mathrm{d}^{2}S_{j}F_{j}.\] (B.44)

An immediate consequence of the divergence theorem is that for arbitrary scalar and vector functions \(g\) and \(\mathbf{F}\),

\[\int\mathrm{d}^{3}\mathbf{x}\,g\boldsymbol{\nabla}\cdot\mathbf{F}=\oint_{S}g \mathbf{F}\cdot\mathrm{d}^{2}\mathbf{S}-\int\mathrm{d}^{3}\mathbf{x}\,(\mathbf{ F}\cdot\boldsymbol{\nabla})g,\] (B.45)

which is a three-dimensional analog of integration by parts.

If \(\mathbf{F}=f\hat{\mathbf{e}}_{i}\), then we have \(\int_{V}\mathrm{d}^{3}\mathbf{x}\,(\partial f/\partial x_{i})=\oint_{S} \mathrm{d}^{2}S_{i}\,f\), which can be written more compactly after multiplication by \(\hat{\mathbf{e}}_{i}\) and summing over indices as

\[\int_{V}\mathrm{d}^{3}\mathbf{x}\,\boldsymbol{\nabla}f=\oint_{S}\mathrm{d}^{ 2}\mathbf{S}\,f.\] (B.46)

In cylindrical coordinates

\[\boldsymbol{\nabla}\cdot\mathbf{F}=\frac{1}{R}\frac{\partial}{\partial R}(RF_{ R})+\frac{1}{R}\frac{\partial F_{\phi}}{\partial\phi}+\frac{\partial F_{z}}{ \partial z}.\] (B.47)This result can be derived from the divergence theorem, or by writing \(\boldsymbol{\nabla}\cdot\mathbf{F}=(\hat{\mathbf{e}}_{R}\partial/\partial R+\hat{ \mathbf{e}}_{\phi}\partial/R\partial\phi+\hat{\mathbf{e}}_{z}\partial/ \partial z)\cdot(F_{R}\hat{\mathbf{e}}_{R}+F_{\phi}\hat{\mathbf{e}}_{\phi}+F_{ z}\hat{\mathbf{e}}_{z})\) and expanding the expression using equation (B.21). In spherical coordinates

\[\boldsymbol{\nabla}\cdot\mathbf{F}=\frac{1}{r^{2}}\frac{\partial}{\partial r}( r^{2}F_{r})+\frac{1}{r\sin\theta}\frac{\partial}{\partial\theta}(\sin\theta F _{\theta})+\frac{1}{r\sin\theta}\frac{\partial F_{\phi}}{\partial\phi}.\] (B.48)

In arbitrary orthogonal coordinates

\[\boldsymbol{\nabla}\cdot\mathbf{F}=\frac{1}{h_{1}h_{2}h_{3}}\left[\frac{ \partial}{\partial q_{1}}(h_{2}h_{3}F_{1})+\frac{\partial}{\partial q_{2}}(h_ {3}h_{1}F_{2})+\frac{\partial}{\partial q_{3}}(h_{1}h_{2}F_{3})\right].\] (B.49)

LaplacianThe divergence of the gradient of a scalar function is called the **Laplacian** of that function. Thus the Laplacian of \(F(\mathbf{x})\) is

\[\nabla^{2}F\equiv\boldsymbol{\nabla}\cdot(\boldsymbol{\nabla}F).\] (B.50)

In different coordinate systems we have

\[\nabla^{2}F=\frac{\partial^{2}F}{\partial x^{2}}+\frac{\partial^{2}F}{\partial y ^{2}}+\frac{\partial^{2}F}{\partial z^{2}};\] (B.51)

\[\nabla^{2}F=\frac{1}{R}\frac{\partial}{\partial R}\bigg{(}R\frac{\partial F}{ \partial R}\bigg{)}+\frac{1}{R^{2}}\frac{\partial^{2}F}{\partial\phi^{2}}+ \frac{\partial^{2}F}{\partial z^{2}};\] (B.52)

\[\nabla^{2}F=\frac{1}{r^{2}}\frac{\partial}{\partial r}\bigg{(}r^{2}\frac{ \partial F}{\partial r}\bigg{)}+\frac{1}{r^{2}\sin\theta}\frac{\partial}{ \partial\theta}\bigg{(}\sin\theta\frac{\partial F}{\partial\theta}\bigg{)}+ \frac{1}{r^{2}\sin^{2}\theta}\frac{\partial^{2}F}{\partial\phi^{2}};\] (B.53)

\[\nabla^{2}F=\frac{1}{h_{1}h_{2}h_{3}}\left[\frac{\partial}{\partial q_{1}} \bigg{(}\frac{h_{2}h_{3}}{h_{1}}\frac{\partial F}{\partial q_{1}}\bigg{)}+ \frac{\partial}{\partial q_{2}}\bigg{(}\frac{h_{3}h_{1}}{h_{2}}\frac{ \partial F}{\partial q_{2}}\bigg{)}+\frac{\partial}{\partial q_{3}}\bigg{(} \frac{h_{1}h_{2}}{h_{3}}\frac{\partial F}{\partial q_{3}}\bigg{)}\right].\] (B.54)

Convective operatorWe shall use the convective operator \((\mathbf{A}\cdot\boldsymbol{\nabla})\mathbf{B}\). In Cartesian coordinates we have

\[(\mathbf{A}\cdot\boldsymbol{\nabla})\mathbf{B}=\hat{\mathbf{e}}_{i}A_{j} \frac{\partial B_{i}}{\partial x_{j}}.\] (B.55)

In cylindrical coordinates

\[\begin{split}(\mathbf{A}\cdot\boldsymbol{\nabla})\mathbf{B}& =\left(A_{R}\frac{\partial B_{R}}{\partial R}+\frac{A_{\phi}}{R} \frac{\partial B_{R}}{\partial\phi}+A_{z}\frac{\partial B_{R}}{\partial z}- \frac{A_{\phi}B_{\phi}}{R}\right)\hat{\mathbf{e}}_{R}\\ &\quad+\left(A_{R}\frac{\partial B_{\phi}}{\partial R}+\frac{A_{ \phi}}{R}\frac{\partial B_{\phi}}{\partial\phi}+A_{z}\frac{\partial B_{\phi}} {\partial z}+\frac{A_{\phi}B_{R}}{R}\right)\hat{\mathbf{e}}_{\phi}\\ &\quad+\left(A_{R}\frac{\partial B_{z}}{\partial R}+\frac{A_{ \phi}}{R}\frac{\partial B_{z}}{\partial\phi}+A_{z}\frac{\partial B_{z}}{ \partial z}\right)\hat{\mathbf{e}}_{z}.\end{split}\] (B.56)

In spherical coordinates

\[\begin{split}&(\mathbf{A}\cdot\boldsymbol{\nabla})\mathbf{B}= \left(A_{r}\frac{\partial B_{r}}{\partial r}+\frac{A_{\theta}}{r}\frac{ \partial B_{r}}{\partial\theta}+\frac{A_{\phi}}{r\sin\theta}\frac{\partial B_{r }}{\partial\phi}-\frac{A_{\theta}B_{\theta}+A_{\phi}B_{\phi}}{r}\right)\hat{ \mathbf{e}}_{r}\\ &+\left(A_{r}\frac{\partial B_{\theta}}{\partial r}+\frac{A_{\theta }}{r}\frac{\partial B_{\theta}}{\partial\theta}+\frac{A_{\phi}}{r\sin\theta} \frac{\partial B_{\theta}}{\partial\phi}+\frac{A_{\theta}B_{r}}{r}-\frac{A_{ \phi}B_{\phi}\cot\theta}{r}\right)\hat{\mathbf{e}}_{\theta}\\ &+\left(A_{r}\frac{\partial B_{\phi}}{\partial r}+\frac{A_{\theta }}{r}\frac{\partial B_{\phi}}{\partial\theta}+\frac{A_{\phi}}{r\sin\theta} \frac{\partial B_{\phi}}{\partial\phi}+\frac{A_{\phi}B_{r}}{r}+\frac{A_{\phi}B _{\theta}\cot\theta}{r}\right)\hat{\mathbf{e}}_{\phi}.\end{split}\] (B.57)In arbitrary orthogonal coordinates

\[({\bf A}\cdot{\bf\nabla}){\bf B}={\hat{\bf e}}_{j}\left[\frac{A_{i}}{h_{i}}\frac{ \partial B_{j}}{\partial q_{i}}+\frac{B_{i}}{h_{i}h_{j}}\left(A_{j}\frac{ \partial h_{j}}{\partial q_{i}}-A_{i}\frac{\partial h_{i}}{\partial q_{j}} \right)\right].\] (B.58)

**Green's theorem** Let \(\gamma\) be a closed curve in the \((x,y)\) plane, and \(S\) the area enclosed by this curve. Then **Green's theorem** states that for any functions \(f(x,y)\), \(g(x,y)\)

\[\oint_{\gamma}[f(x,y)\,{\rm d}x+g(x,y)\,{\rm d}y]=\int\!\!\!\int_{S}{\rm d}x\, {\rm d}y\,\left(\frac{\partial g}{\partial x}-\frac{\partial f}{\partial y} \right),\] (B.59)

where the line integral is taken anti-clockwise around the curve. This is demonstrated by first proving the theorem for the small square area \(x\to x+\Delta x\), \(y\to y+\Delta y\), then adding together many of these areas to fill up \(S\).

In particular, let \(f(x,y)=-y\), \(g(x,y)=x\). Then

\[\oint_{\gamma}(x\,{\rm d}y-y\,{\rm d}x)=2\int\!\!\!\int_{S}{\rm d}x\,{\rm d}y.\] (B.60)

Now1\(\oint_{\gamma}y\,{\rm d}x=-\oint_{\gamma}x\,{\rm d}y\) so

Footnote 1: To see this, let \(t\) be a parameter that varies from \(0\) to \(1\) as we travel around the curve \(\gamma\), so the curve is defined by \([x(t),y(t)]\), \(0\leq t<1\). Then integrating by parts we have \(\oint_{\gamma}x\,{\rm d}y=\int_{0}^{1}{\rm d}t\,x(t)\,{\rm d}y(t)/{\rm d}t=- \int_{0}^{1}{\rm d}t\,y(t)\,{\rm d}x(t)/{\rm d}t=-\oint_{\gamma}y\,{\rm d}x\).

\[\oint_{\gamma}x\,{\rm d}y=\int\!\!\!\int_{S}{\rm d}x\,{\rm d}y,\] (B.61)

which is the area enclosed by the curve \(\gamma\).

### Fourier series and transforms

Any function \(f(x)\) that is periodic with period \(L\), so that \(f(x+L)=f(x)\), can be written as a **Fourier series**,

\[f(x)=\frac{1}{c}\sum_{n=-\infty}^{\infty}F_{n}\exp\left(\frac{2\pi{\rm i}nx}{ L}\right);\] (B.62)

normally the constant \(c\) is set to unity, but for some purposes (see below) it is more convenient to set \(c=L\).

To find the coefficients \(F_{n}\), we multiply this equation by \(\exp(-2\pi{\rm i}mx/L)\), where \(m\) is an integer, and integrate from \(-L/2\) to \(L/2\):

\[\int_{-L/2}^{L/2}{\rm d}x\,f(x)\exp\left(-\frac{2\pi{\rm i}mx}{L}\right)=\sum _{n=-\infty}^{\infty}\frac{F_{n}}{c}\int_{-L/2}^{L/2}{\rm d}x\,\exp\left(\frac {2\pi{\rm i}(n-m)x}{L}\right).\] (B.63)

The integral on the right side is zero for integer \(n\) and \(m\) unless \(n=m\), in which case it equals \(L\). Thus

\[F_{n}=\frac{c}{L}\int_{-L/2}^{L/2}{\rm d}x\,f(x)\exp\left(-\frac{2\pi{\rm i}nx }{L}\right).\] (B.64)

If \(f(x)\) is real, then \(F_{n}^{*}=F_{-n}\).

When \(x\) is the azimuthal angle \(\phi\), \(L=2\pi\) and it is convenient to write \(F_{n}\equiv a_{n}\exp(-{\rm i}n\phi_{n})\) where \(a_{n}\) is real and positive. Then for \(c=1\) equation (B.62) becomes

\[f(\phi)=\sum_{n=-\infty}^{\infty}a_{n}\exp\left[{\rm i}n(\phi-\phi_{n})\right].\] (B.65)

If \(f(\phi)\) is real, then \(a_{-n}=a_{n}\), \(\phi_{-n}=\phi_{n}\), and

\[f(\phi)=a_{0}+2\sum_{m=1}^{\infty}a_{n}\cos m(\phi-\phi_{n}).\] (B.66)

To derive analogous formulae for non-periodic functions, we consider the limit in which both \(|n|\) and \(L\) become large. We replace \(n\) by a continuous variable \(k\equiv 2\pi n/L\), replace \(LF_{n}/c\) by a continuous function \(F(k)\), and replace the sum \(\sum_{n}\) in (B.62) by the integral \(\int{\rm d}n=L\int{\rm d}k/(2\pi)\). Thus we arrive at the **Fourier transform**,

\[F(k)=\int_{-\infty}^{\infty}{\rm d}x\,f(x){\rm e}^{-{\rm i}kx}\quad;\quad f(x )=\int_{-\infty}^{\infty}\frac{{\rm d}k}{2\pi}\,F(k){\rm e}^{{\rm i}kx}.\] (B.67)

Here the advantage of the choice \(c=L\) is that \(F_{n}\) and \(F(k=2\pi n/L)\) are identical (see, for example, SS9.1).

The Fourier transform is easily generalized to \(D\)-dimensional vectors:

\[F({\bf k})=\int{\rm d}^{D}{\bf x}\,f({\bf x}){\rm e}^{-{\rm i}{\bf k}\cdot{\bf x }}\quad;\quad f({\bf x})=\int\frac{{\rm d}^{D}{\bf k}}{(2\pi)^{D}}\,F({\bf k} ){\rm e}^{{\rm i}{\bf k}\cdot{\bf x}},\] (B.68)

where the integral is over the entire \(D\)-dimensional space. In this book, the variable \(x\) or \({\bf x}\) in the Fourier transform often denotes position, and then \(k\) or \({\bf k}\) is called the **wavenumber** or **wavevector**.

We shall also use Fourier transforms in the time domain. Here we restrict our attention to functions \(f(t)\) that vanish for \(t<0\). The temporal Fourier transform of \(f(t)\) is then given by

\[F(\omega)=\int_{0}^{\infty}{\rm d}t\,f(t){\rm e}^{{\rm i}\omega t}\quad;\quad f (t)=\int_{-\infty}^{\infty}\frac{{\rm d}\omega}{2\pi}\,F(\omega){\rm e}^{-{ \rm i}\omega t},\] (B.69)

where \(\omega\) is the **frequency**. Note that the sign convention differs from (B.67) (there are good reasons for this). Note also that \(\omega\) can be a complex number, in contrast to \(k\) or \({\bf k}\) which is real. The use of complex \(\omega\) enables us to generalize the temporal Fourier transform \(F(\omega)\) to a wide class of functions \(f(t)\) that diverge as \(t\to\infty\). Suppose there exists a real constant \(c>0\) such that \(f(t)\exp(-ct)\to 0\) as \(t\to\infty\). Set \(g(t)=f(t)\exp(-ct)\). Then

\[G(\omega)=\int_{0}^{\infty}{\rm d}t\,f(t){\rm e}^{({\rm i}\omega-c)t}\quad; \quad g(t)=\int_{-\infty}^{\infty}\frac{{\rm d}\omega}{2\pi}\,{\rm e}^{-{\rm i }\omega t}G(\omega).\] (B.70)

Now set \(\omega^{\prime}\equiv\omega+{\rm i}c\) and \(F(\omega^{\prime})\equiv G(\omega)\). The preceding equation becomes

\[F(\omega^{\prime})=\int_{0}^{\infty}{\rm d}t\,f(t){\rm e}^{{\rm i}\omega^{ \prime}t}\quad;\quad f(t)=\int_{{\rm i}c-\infty}^{{\rm i}c+\infty}\frac{{\rm d }\omega^{\prime}}{2\pi}\,F(\omega^{\prime}){\rm e}^{-{\rm i}\omega^{\prime}t},\] (B.71)

and \(F(\omega^{\prime})\) is defined whenever \({\rm Im}(\omega^{\prime})\geq c\).

### Abel integral equation

Let

\[f(x)=\int_{x}^{\infty}\frac{\mathrm{d}t\,g(t)}{(t-x)^{\alpha}}\qquad(0<\alpha<1).\] (B.72a) Then \[\begin{split} g(t)&=-\frac{\sin\pi\alpha}{\pi}\frac{ \mathrm{d}}{\mathrm{d}t}\int_{t}^{\infty}\frac{\mathrm{d}x\,f(x)}{(x-t)^{1- \alpha}}\\ &=-\frac{\sin\pi\alpha}{\pi}\int_{t}^{\infty}\frac{\mathrm{d}x}{ (x-t)^{1-\alpha}}\frac{\mathrm{d}f}{\mathrm{d}x}.\end{split}\] (B.72b)

The first line can be proved by substituting equation (B.72a) into (B.72b), interchanging the order of integration, and using the integral

\[\int_{0}^{1}\frac{\mathrm{d}u}{u^{\alpha}(1-u)^{1-\alpha}}=\frac{\pi}{\sin\pi \alpha}.\] (B.73)

The second line can be proved by replacing the integration variable \(x\) in the first expression for \(g(t)\) by \(u=x-t\) and then carrying out the differentiation with respect to \(t\).

Another useful result, which can be proved similarly, is

\[f(x)=\int_{0}^{x}\frac{\mathrm{d}t\,g(t)}{(x-t)^{\alpha}}\qquad(0<\alpha<1),\] (B.74a) \[g(t)=\frac{\sin\pi\alpha}{\pi}\frac{\mathrm{d}}{\mathrm{d}t}\int_{0}^{t}\frac{ \mathrm{d}x\,f(x)}{(t-x)^{1-\alpha}}=\frac{\sin\pi\alpha}{\pi}\left[\int_{0}^ {t}\frac{\mathrm{d}x}{(t-x)^{1-\alpha}}\frac{\mathrm{d}f}{\mathrm{d}x}+\frac{ f(0)}{t^{1-\alpha}}\right].\] (B.74b)

### Schwarz's inequality

For any two real functions \(A(x)\) and \(B(x)\),

\[\int\mathrm{d}x\,A^{2}\int\mathrm{d}x\,B^{2}\geq\left(\int\mathrm{d}x\,AB \right)^{2},\] (B.75)

with equality if and only if \(A(x)=cB(x)\) for some constant \(c\).

To prove this, let \(C(x)=A(x)-\lambda B(x)\). Then \(\int\mathrm{d}x\,C^{2}\) is non-negative, so

\[\int\mathrm{d}x\,C^{2}=\int\mathrm{d}x\,A^{2}-2\lambda\int\mathrm{d}x\,AB+ \lambda^{2}\int\mathrm{d}x\,B^{2}\geq 0.\] (B.76)

Now set \(\lambda=\int\mathrm{d}x\,AB/\int\mathrm{d}x\,B^{2}\). Equation (B.76) becomes

\[\int\mathrm{d}x\,A^{2}-\frac{\left(\int\mathrm{d}x\,AB\right)^{2}}{\int\mathrm{ d}x\,B^{2}}\geq 0,\] (B.77)

which proves (B.75).

### B.7 Calculus of variations

Consider a curve \({\bf x}={\bf x}(t)\), \(t_{0}\leq t\leq t_{1}\), which we label by \(\gamma\). We define a function

\[I(\gamma)\equiv\int_{t_{0}}^{t_{1}}{\rm d}t\,L[{\bf x}(t),\dot{\bf x}(t),t].\] (B.78)

Now consider a nearby curve \(\gamma^{\prime}\) defined by \({\bf x}={\bf x}(t)+\epsilon{\bf h}(t)\), where \({\bf h}(t_{0})={\bf h}(t_{1})=0\). As \(\epsilon\to 0\) we have

\[\eqalign{I(\gamma^{\prime})-I(\gamma)&=\int_{t_{0}}^{t_{1}}{\rm d}t\,[L({\bf x }+\epsilon{\bf h},\dot{\bf x}+\epsilon\dot{\bf h},t)-L({\bf x},\dot{\bf x},t)] \cr&=\epsilon\int_{t_{0}}^{t_{1}}{\rm d}t\left[{\bf h}\cdot\frac{\partial L}{ \partial{\bf x}}+\dot{\bf h}\cdot\frac{\partial L}{\partial\dot{\bf x}}\right] +{\rm O}(\epsilon^{2}),}\] (B.79)

where the integral on the second line is evaluated along the unperturbed curve \(\gamma\). On this curve \(L\) can be considered to be a function only of time, \(L(t)=L[{\bf x}(t),\dot{\bf x}(t),t]\). Hence we may integrate by parts to obtain

\[I(\gamma^{\prime})-I(\gamma)=-\epsilon\int_{t_{0}}^{t_{1}}{\rm d}t\,{\bf h} \cdot\left[\frac{{\rm d}}{{\rm d}t}\!\left(\frac{\partial L}{\partial\dot{\bf x }}\right)-\frac{\partial L}{\partial{\bf x}}\right]+\epsilon\!\left({\bf h} \cdot\frac{\partial L}{\partial\dot{\bf x}}\right)^{t_{1}}_{t_{0}}+{\rm O}( \epsilon^{2}).\] (B.80)

The boundary term is seen to be zero since \({\bf h}(t_{0})={\bf h}(t_{1})=0\).

The curve \(\gamma\) is an **extremal** if \(I(\gamma)-I(\gamma^{\prime})={\rm O}(\epsilon^{2})\) for all variations \({\bf h}\). Extremal curves have the largest or smallest values of the function \(I\) on any continuous curves connecting the fixed endpoints \({\bf x}(t_{0})\) and \({\bf x}(t_{1})\). At an extremal curve, the integral in equation (B.80) must vanish for all variations \({\bf h}(t)\) for which \({\bf h}(t_{0})={\bf h}(t_{1})=0\); thus the condition for an extremal curve is

\[\frac{{\rm d}}{{\rm d}t}\!\left(\frac{\partial L}{\partial\dot{\bf x}}\right) -\frac{\partial L}{\partial{\bf x}}=0.\] (B.81)

This is the **Euler-Lagrange equation** for an extremal curve \(I\).

### B.8 Poisson distribution

This distribution describes the statistical properties of a wide range of physical phenomena, but for concreteness we shall derive it in the context of the distribution of stars in phase space.

Suppose that the phase-space density of stars, \(f\), is uniform over some phase-space volume \({\cal V}\), and that the distribution of stars is separable (SS7.2.4) so the probability of finding a star at any phase-space position is unaffected by the presence or absence of other stars nearby. On average, we expect to find \(r=f{\cal V}\) stars in this volume, but we want more detailed information: the probability \(p_{n}\) that exactly \(n\) stars are present.

Let us divide \({\cal V}\) into a large number \(K\) of cells, each of volume \(\Delta{\cal V}={\cal V}/K\). If \(K\) is sufficiently large, the probability of finding two or more stars in any cell is negligible, and the probability of finding one star in a cell is just \(f\Delta{\cal V}=r/K\). Hence the probability of finding \(n\) stars in \({\cal V}\) is simply the probability that \(n\) cells are occupied by a star and \(K-n\) are vacant. This is given by the binomialdistribution,

\[\begin{split} p_{n}&=\frac{K!}{n!(K-n)!}(f\Delta{\cal V })^{n}(1-f\Delta{\cal V})^{K-n}\\ &=\frac{K(K-1)\cdots(K-n+1)}{n!}\frac{r^{n}}{K^{n}}\left(1-\frac{r }{K}\right)^{K-n}\\ &=\left(1-\frac{1}{K}\right)\cdots\left(1-\frac{n-1}{K}\right) \frac{r^{n}}{n!}\left(1-\frac{r}{K}\right)^{K-n}.\end{split}\] (B.82)

Now let \(K\to\infty\); each of the factors \((1-j/K)\) approaches unity, \((1-r/K)^{K}\to\exp(-r)\) and \((1-r/K)^{-n}\to 1\). Thus

\[p_{n}=\frac{r^{n}}{n!}\mathrm{e}^{-r},\] (B.83)

which is the **Poisson distribution**. It is straightforward to show that \(\sum_{n=1}^{\infty}p_{n}=1\), as it must, and that the mean and variance of the Poisson distribution are

\[\langle n\rangle=\sum_{n=1}^{\infty}np_{n}=r\quad;\quad\langle(n-\mu)^{2} \rangle=r.\] (B.84)

More generally, the Poisson distribution describes the probability of obtaining \(n\) successes in a **Poisson process**, which is a large number \(K\) of trials in which the probability of success in a single trial is \(r/K\), and the probability of success in different trials is independent.

### Conditional probability and Bayes's theorem

The joint probability distribution \(p(x,y)\) of two variables \(x\) and \(y\) is defined so that \(p(x,y)\,\mathrm{d}x\,\mathrm{d}y\) is the probability that the first variable is found in the interval \((x,x+\mathrm{d}x)\)_and_ the second variable is in the interval \((y,y+\mathrm{d}y)\). For many purposes we are interested in the conditional probability distribution \(p(x|y)\), where \(p(x|y)\,\mathrm{d}x\) is the probability that the first variable is found in the interval \((x,x+\mathrm{d}x)\)_given that_ the second variable has the value \(y\).

The relation between these two functions is fundamental to probability theory and statistical inference. Let \(X\) be the event "first variable is found in the interval \((x,x+\mathrm{d}x)\)," and \(Y\) the event "second variable is in the interval \((y,y+\mathrm{d}y)\)." The probability that both events occur is \(P_{XY}=p(x,y)\mathrm{d}x\,\mathrm{d}y\). The probability that \(Y\) occurs, whatever the value of \(x\) may be, is \(P_{Y}=\mathrm{d}y\int\mathrm{d}x^{\prime}\,p(x^{\prime},y)\equiv p_{y}(y)\, \mathrm{d}y\). The probability that \(X\) occurs, given that \(Y\) occurs, is \(P_{X|Y}=P_{XY}/P_{Y}\). Thus

\[p(x|y)=\frac{p(x,y)}{\int\mathrm{d}x^{\prime}\,p(x^{\prime},y)}=\frac{p(x,y)} {p_{y}(y)}.\] (B.85)

Similarly,

\[p(y|x)=\frac{p(x,y)}{\int\mathrm{d}y^{\prime}\,p(x,y^{\prime})}=\frac{p(x,y)} {p_{x}(x)};\] (B.86)

eliminating \(p(x,y)\) between equations (B.85) and (B.86) gives

\[p(y|x)=p(x|y)\frac{p_{y}(y)}{p_{x}(x)}=\frac{p(x|y)p_{y}(y)}{\int\mathrm{d}y^{ \prime}\,p(x|y^{\prime})p_{y}(y^{\prime})}.\] (B.87)

This is the celebrated **Bayes's theorem**, proved by Bayes and by Laplace in the eighteenth century. The formula is equally valid if \(x\) and \(y\) are vectors.

The most important application of Bayes's theorem is in statistical inference. Here \(y\) represents a set of model parameters and \(x\) represents a set of experimental or observational data. The model to be tested is encapsulated in \(p(x|y)\), which predicts the probability of observing a given set of data \(x\) given a particular value for the model parameters \(y\). The function \(p_{y}(y)\) is the prior probability of \(y\), which is our best estimate of the probability distribution of the model parameters before the data are taken--usually \(p_{y}(y)\propto\)_constant_ or \(p_{y}(y)\propto y^{-1}\). Then \(p(y|x)\) is the probability distribution of the model parameters given the data, from which the best estimate of \(y\) and associated error bars can be derived (e.g., Saha 2003).

### **B.10 Central limit theorem**

This theorem governs the behavior of sums of large numbers of independent random variables. For a thorough treatment see Feller (1971).

Let \(u_{1},u_{2},\ldots,u_{n}\) be independent random variables with distinct probability distributions \(p_{j}(u)\); that is, the probability that \(u_{j}\) lies in the interval \((u_{j},u_{j}+{\rm d}u_{j})\) is \(p_{j}(u_{j}){\rm d}u_{j}\). Since \(p_{j}(u)\) is a probability distribution, we must have \(p_{j}(u)\geq 0\) and \(\int{\rm d}u\,p_{j}(u)=1\).

The first two moments of the distributions are

\[\int{\rm d}u\,up_{j}(u)\equiv\mu_{j}\quad;\quad\int{\rm d}u\,(u-\mu_{j})^{2}p_ {j}(u)\equiv\sigma_{j}^{2};\] (B.88)

these define the **mean**\(\mu_{j}\) and the **variance**\(\sigma_{j}^{2}\) or the **standard deviation**\(\sigma_{j}\). For simplicity we assume that \(\mu_{j}=0\), although our results are easily generalized to distributions with non-zero mean, by working with the variables \(u_{j}^{\prime}\equiv u_{j}-\mu_{j}\). We define the normalized sum

\[s\equiv\frac{1}{\sqrt{n}}(u_{1}+\cdots+u_{n}).\] (B.89)

The central limit theorem states that _as \(n\to\infty\) the probability distribution of \(s\) approaches_

\[g(s)\equiv\frac{1}{\sqrt{2\pi\sigma^{2}}}\exp\left(-\frac{s^{2}}{2\sigma^{2}} \right),\quad{\rm where}\quad\sigma^{2}\equiv\frac{1}{n}\sum_{j=1}^{n}\sigma_ {j}^{2},\] (B.90)

which is the **normal** or **Gaussian** probability distribution. Thus the sum \(\sum_{i=1}^{n}u_{i}\) is also normally distributed, with variance \(n\sigma^{2}\) equal to the sum of the individual variances.

_Proof:_ Since the \(u_{j}\) are statistically independent, the probability that they lie in the volume \({\rm d}u_{1}\cdots{\rm d}u_{n}\) around \((u_{1},\ldots,u_{n})\) is \(p_{1}(u_{1})\cdots p_{n}(u_{n}){\rm d}u_{1}\cdots{\rm d}u_{n}\). Thus the probability that the normalized sum lies in the interval \((s,s+{\rm d}s)\) is \(q(s){\rm d}s\), where

\[q(s)=\int{\rm d}u_{1}\cdots{\rm d}u_{n}\,p_{1}(u_{1})\cdots p_{n}(u_{n})\delta \left(s-\frac{u_{1}+\cdots+u_{n}}{\sqrt{n}}\right);\] (B.91)

here \(\delta\) denotes the delta function and we have used equation (C.7). Taking the Fourier transform (B.67), we have

\[\eqalign{Q(\omega)&\equiv\int{\rm d}s\,g(s){\rm e}^{-{\rm i}\omega s}\cr&=\int{ \rm d}u_{1}\cdots{\rm d}u_{n}\,p_{1}(u_{1})\cdots p_{n}(u_{n})\exp\left[{-{\rm i }\omega(u_{1}+\cdots+u_{n})\over\sur n}\right]\cr&=\prod_{j=1}^{n}\int{\rm d}u \,p_{j}(u){\rm e}^{-{\rm i}\omega u/\sur n}.\cr}\]

As \(n\to\infty\) at fixed \(u\), the exponent in the last equation tends to zero, so we can expand the exponential in a Taylor series,

\[\eqalign{\int{\rm d}u\,p_{j}(u){\rm e}^{-{\rm i}\omega u/\sur n}&=\int{\rm d}u \,p_{j}(u)\left[1-{\rm i}\omega u/\sqrt{n}-{{1\over 2}}(\omega u)^{2}/n+{\rm O }(n^{-3/2})\right]\cr&=1-{\omega^{2}\sigma_{j}^{2}\over 2n}+{\rm O}(n^{-3/2}),\cr}\]

where the last equation employs our assumption that the mean \(\mu_{j}=0\). Substituting equation (B.93) into (B.92) and taking the logarithm, we have

\[\ln Q(\omega)=\sum_{j=1}^{n}\ln\left[1-{{1\over 2}}\omega^{2} \sigma_{j}^{2}/n+{\rm O}(n^{-3/2})\right].\]

We expand the right side in its Taylor series to find

\[\ln Q(\omega)=-{\omega^{2}\over 2n}\sum_{j=1}^{n}\sigma_{j}^{2}+{\rm O}(n^{-1/ 2})\]

so as \(n\to\infty\)

\[Q(\omega)=\exp\left(-{\omega^{2}\over 2n}\sum_{j=1}^{n}\sigma_{j}^{2}\right).\]

Taking the inverse Fourier transform (B.67),

\[q(s)=\int{{\rm d}\omega\over 2\pi}Q(\omega){\rm e}^{{\rm i}\omega s}={1\over \sur 2\pi\sigma^{2}}{\rm e}^{-{{1\over 2}}s^{2}/\sigma^{2}},\]

where \(\sigma^{2}=n^{-1}\sum_{j=1}^{n}\sigma_{j}^{2}\). This is the normal distribution (B.90), as required.\(\triangleleft\)

The central limit theorem can be generalized to vectors. Let \({\bf u}^{1},\ldots,{\bf u}^{n}\) be independent random variables with dimension \(D\) and probability distribution \(p^{j}({\bf u}^{j})\). The moments of the distribution are

\[\int{\rm d}^{D}{\bf u}\,u_{i}p^{j}({\bf u})\equiv\mu_{i}^{j}\quad;\quad\int{ \rm d}^{D}{\bf u}\,(u_{i}-\mu_{i}^{j})(u_{k}-\mu_{k}^{j})p^{j}({\bf u})\equiv C _{ik}^{j},\]

where \(i,k=1,\ldots,D\), \(j=1,\ldots,n\), and \({\bf C}^{j}\) is the **covariance matrix**.

For simplicity we assume again that the means \(\mathbf{\mu}^{j}=0\). Then the normalized sum \({\bf s}\equiv\sum_{j=1}^{n}{\bf u}^{j}/\sur n\) approaches the multivariate Gaussian distribution

\[g({\bf s})={1\over(2\pi)^{D/2}|{\bf C}|^{1/2}}\exp\left(-{{1\over 2}}{ \bf s}\cdot{\bf M}\cdot{\bf s}\right),\]where \(|{\bf C}|\) is the determinant of \({\bf C}\) and

\[{\bf C}\equiv\frac{1}{n}\sum_{j=1}^{n}{\bf C}^{j}\quad;\quad{\bf M}={\bf C}^{-1}.\] (B.100)

_Appendix C:_ **Special functions**

Complete descriptions of the special functions of mathematical physics are given in many books (Morse & Feshbach 1953; Abramowitz & Stegun 1964; Jackson 1999; Gradshteym & Ryzhik 2000; Arfken & Weber 2005) and on web sites such as mathworld.wolfram.com.

Throughout this appendix, \(x\) and \(z\) represent real and complex variables respectively.

### Delta function and step function

The **delta function** is a singular function defined by the properties

\[\delta(x)=0\ \ \mbox{for}\ \ x\neq 0\quad;\quad\int_{-\infty}^{\infty}{\rm d}x \,f(x)\delta(x)=f(0),\] (C.1)

where \(f(x)\) is an arbitrary function continuous at \(x=0\). The delta function is sometimes called the Dirac delta function, after its inventor P.A.M. Dirac. The definition (C.1) implies that

\[\delta[f(x)]=\sum_{j}\frac{\delta(x-x_{j})}{|f^{\prime}(x_{j})|},\] (C.2)

where \(x_{j}\) are the solutions of \(f(x_{j})=0\).

The three-dimensional delta function is a function of \({\bf x}=(x,y,z)\) defined by

\[\delta({\bf x})=\delta(x)\delta(y)\delta(z).\] (C.3)

The delta function can be written as

\[\delta(x)=\frac{1}{\pi}\lim_{\epsilon\to 0}\frac{|\epsilon|}{\epsilon^{2}+x^{2} }=\frac{1}{\pi}\lim_{a\to\infty}\frac{\sin ax}{x}=\frac{1}{2\pi}\int_{-\infty} ^{\infty}{\rm d}x\,{\rm e}^{{\rm i}xt}.\] (C.4)

The **Plemelj identity** is

\[\lim_{\epsilon\to 0}\int_{a}^{b}\frac{{\rm d}x\,f(x)}{x-y+{\rm i}|\epsilon|}= \wp\int_{a}^{b}\frac{{\rm d}x\,f(x)}{x-y}-{\rm i}\pi f(y),\] (C.5)

where \(f(x)\) is an arbitrary continuous function, \(x\) and \(y\) are real, \(a<y<b\), and \(\wp\) denotes the Cauchy principal value,

\[\begin{split}\wp\int_{a}^{b}\frac{{\rm d}x\,f(x)}{x-y}& \equiv\lim_{\epsilon\to 0}\left(\int_{a}^{y-|\epsilon|}\frac{{\rm d}x \,f(x)}{x-y}+\int_{y+|\epsilon|}^{b}\frac{{\rm d}x\,f(x)}{x-y}\right)\\ &=\lim_{\epsilon\to 0}\int_{a}^{b}{\rm d}x\,f(x)\frac{x-y}{(x-y)^{2 }+\epsilon^{2}}.\end{split}\] (C.6)

The Plemelj identity is easy to prove using the last line in (C.6) and the first of the expressions in (C.4).

If \(p(x_{1},\ldots,x_{N})\,{\rm d}x_{1}\cdots{\rm d}x_{N}\) is the number of objects with parameters in the range \(x_{1}\to x_{1}+{\rm d}x_{1},\ldots,x_{N}\to x_{N}+{\rm d}x_{N}\), and \(G\) is some function of \(x_{1},\ldots,x_{N}\), then the number of objects with \(G\) in the range \(g\to g+{\rm d}g\) is \(n(g)\,{\rm d}g\), where

\[n(g)=\int{\rm d}x_{1}\cdots{\rm d}x_{N}\,p(x_{1},\ldots,x_{N})\delta[g-G(x_{1}, \ldots,x_{N})].\]

The **step function** is defined by

\[H(x)=\cases{0&$(x<0)$,\cr 1&$(x>0)$.\cr}\]

Obviously,

\[{{\rm d}H(x)\over{\rm d}x}=\delta(x).\]

**C.2 Factorial or gamma function**

\[z!\equiv\Gamma(z+1)\equiv\int_{0}^{\infty}{\rm d}t\,t^{z}{\rm e}^{-t}\qquad({ \rm Re}\,z>-1).\]

By analytic continuation the function \(z!\) can be defined for all complex numbers \(z\), except for simple poles at \(-1,-2,\ldots\). Some useful relations are

\[z!=z(z-1)!,\]

\[(z-1)!\,(-z)!=\pi\,{\rm csc}\,\pi z,\]

\[(2z)!={2^{2z}\over\sqrt{\pi}}z!\,(z-{{1\over 2}})!,\]

\[z!^{*}=(z^{*})!,\]

\[x!=\sqrt{2\pi}x^{x+1/2}{\rm e}^{-x}\left[1+{\rm O}(x^{-1})\right]\quad{\rm as }\,\,x\to\infty;\]

the last of these is **Stirling's approximation** to \(x!\) for large \(x\).

Special values of \(x!\) include

\[\eqalign{n!=1\cdot 2\cdot 3\cdots n&;\quad 0!=1!=1;\cr(-{{1\over 2}})!= \sqrt{\pi}=1.77245&;\quad({{1\over 2}})!={{1\over 2}}\sqrt{\pi}=0.88623.\cr}\]

**C.3 Error function, Dawson's integral, and plasma dispersion function**

The **error function** is defined by

\[{\rm erf}\,z\equiv{2\over\sqrt{\pi}}\int_{0}^{z}{\rm d}t\,{\rm e}^{-t^{2}}={2 \over\sqrt{\pi}}\sum_{n=0}^{\infty}{(-1)^{n}z^{2n+1}\over n!\,(2n+1)}.\]

We have

\[{\rm erf}(0)=0\ ;\ {\rm erf}(\infty)=1\ ;\ {\rm erf}(-z)=-\,{\rm erf}(z)\ ;\ { \rm erf}(z^{*})=\left[{\rm erf}(z)\right]^{*}.\]

\[{\rm As}\,\,x\to\infty,\ \ \ 1-{\rm erf}\,x\to{{\rm e}^{-x^{2}}\over\sqrt{\pi}x} \left[1+{\rm O}(x^{-2})\right].\]The error function is closely related to **Dawson's integral**,

\[F_{\pm}(x)\equiv{\rm e}^{\mp x^{2}}\int_{0}^{x}{\rm d}y\,{\rm e}^{\pm y^{2}};\] (C.20)

in particular,

\[F_{-}(z)={{1\over 2}}\sqrt{\pi}{\rm e}^{z^{2}}\,{\rm erf}(z)\quad;\quad F _{+}(z)=-{{1\over 2}}\sqrt{\pi}{\rm i}{\rm e}^{-z^{2}}\,{\rm erf}({\rm i}z).\] (C.21)

On the interval \((0,\infty)\), the function \(F_{+}(x)\) has a maximum of \(0.54104\) at \(x=0.92414\). As \(x\to\infty\), \(F_{+}(x)\to 1/(2x)\).

The **plasma dispersion function** (Fried & Conte 1961) is

\[Z(z)={\rm i}\sqrt{\pi}{\rm e}^{-z^{2}}[1+{\rm erf}({\rm i}z)].\] (C.22)

An alternative expression is

\[Z(z)={1\over\sqrt{\pi}}\int_{-\infty}^{\infty}{\rm d}s{{\rm e}^{-s^{2}}\over s -z}\qquad({\rm Im}\,z>0),\] (C.23)

and its analytic continuation for \({\rm Im}\,z\leq 0\). For real and imaginary argument,

\[Z(x)={\rm i}\sqrt{\pi}{\rm e}^{-x^{2}}-2F_{+}(x)\quad;\quad Z({\rm i}y)={\rm i }\sqrt{\pi}{\rm e}^{y^{2}}(1-{\rm erf}\,y).\] (C.24)

For \(y>0\),

\[Z(x-{\rm i}y)=Z^{*}(x+{\rm i}y)+2{\rm i}\sqrt{\pi}{\rm e}^{-(x-{\rm i}y)^{2}}.\] (C.25)

We have also

\[Z(0)={\rm i}\sqrt{\pi}\quad;\quad Z(z^{*})=-[Z(-z)]^{*}\quad;\quad{{\rm d}Z \over{\rm d}z}=-2-2zZ(z).\] (C.26)

As \(|z|\to\infty\),

\[Z(z)={\rm i}\sqrt{\pi}\sigma{\rm e}^{-z^{2}}-\sum_{n=0}^{\infty}{(n-{1\over 2 })!\over\sqrt{\pi}z^{2n+1}},\] (C.27)

where \(\sigma=0,1,2\) if \({\rm Im}(z)\) is positive, zero or negative.

### Elliptic integrals

The incomplete elliptic integrals of the first and second kinds are

\[F(\theta,k)\equiv\int_{0}^{\theta}{{\rm d}\phi\over\sqrt{1-k^{2}\sin^{2}\phi} }\quad;\quad E(\theta,k)\equiv\int_{0}^{\theta}{\rm d}\phi\,\sqrt{1-k^{2}\sin ^{2}\phi}.\] (C.28)

The complete elliptic integrals of the first and second kinds are

\[\begin{array}{l} K(k)\equiv F({{1\over 2}}\pi,k)=\int_{0}^{\pi/2}{{\rm d }\phi\over\sqrt{1-k^{2}\sin^{2}\phi}}=\int_{0}^{1}{{\rm d}t\over\sqrt{(1-t^{2}) (1-k^{2}t^{2})}},\\ E(k)\equiv E({{1\over 2}}\pi,k)=\int_{0}^{\pi/2}{\rm d}\phi\,\sqrt{1-k^{2}\sin ^{2}\phi}=\int_{0}^{1}{\rm d}t\,\sqrt{{1-k^{2}t^{2}\over 1-t^{2}}}.\end{array}\] (C.29)

Note that \(K(0)=E(0)=\pi/2\), \(E(1)=1\), and \(K(k)\) diverges logarithmically as \(k\to 1\), in that \(K(k)-{{1\over 2}}\ln[16/(1-k^{2})]\to 0\).

### C.5 Legendre functions

The Legendre functions of the first and second kinds, \(P^{\mu}_{\lambda}(z)\) and \(Q^{\mu}_{\lambda}(z)\), are linearly independent solutions of the differential equation

\[\frac{{\rm d}}{{\rm d}z}\left[(1-z^{2})\frac{{\rm d}w}{{\rm d}z}\right]-\frac{ \mu^{2}}{1-z^{2}}w+\lambda(\lambda+1)w=0.\] (C.30)

For \({\rm Re}(\lambda)>0\), the Legendre functions of the first kind diverge (\(\propto z^{\lambda}\)) as \(|z|\to\infty\), while the functions of the second kind vanish [\(\propto z^{-(\lambda+1)}\)]. As \(z\to 0\)

\[\begin{array}{l}\left[\frac{d\ln P^{\mu}_{\lambda}(z)}{dz}\right]_{z=0}=2 \tan[\frac{1}{2}\pi(\lambda+\mu)]\frac{[\frac{1}{2}(\lambda+\mu)]![\frac{1}{2} (\lambda-\mu)]!}{[\frac{1}{2}(\lambda+\mu-1)]![\frac{1}{2}(\lambda-\mu-1)]!}, \\ \left[\frac{d\ln Q^{\mu}_{\lambda}(z)}{dz}\right]_{z=0}=2\exp\{\frac{1}{2}\pi{ \rm i}\,{\rm sgn}[{\rm Im}(z)]\}\frac{[\frac{1}{2}(\lambda+\mu)]![\frac{1}{2} (\lambda-\mu)]!}{[\frac{1}{2}(\lambda+\mu-1)]![\frac{1}{2}(\lambda-\mu-1)]!}. \end{array}\] (C.31)

Here \({\rm sgn}(x)=+1\) if \(x>0\) and \(-1\) if \(x<0\).

For many applications we are interested in the Legendre functions with real arguments, \(z=x\), in the interval \(-1\leq x\leq 1\). Unless \(\mu\) is an even integer, \(P^{\mu}_{\lambda}(x+{\rm i}\epsilon)\) and \(P^{\mu}_{\lambda}(x-{\rm i}\epsilon)\) are different for real \(x\) and \(\epsilon\) as \(\epsilon\to 0\). Thus it is conventional to redefine the Legendre functions for \(-1\leq x\leq 1\) by

\[\begin{array}{l}P^{\mu}_{\lambda}(x)\equiv\frac{1}{2}\lim_{\epsilon\to 0 }\left[{\rm e}^{\pi{\rm i}\mu/2}P^{\mu}_{\lambda}(x+{\rm i}|\epsilon|)+{\rm e} ^{-\pi{\rm i}\mu/2}P^{\mu}_{\lambda}(x-{\rm i}|\epsilon|)\right]\\ Q^{\mu}_{\lambda}(x)\equiv\frac{1}{2}{\rm e}^{-{\rm i}\pi\mu}\lim_{\epsilon \to 0}\left[{\rm e}^{-\pi{\rm i}\mu/2}Q^{\mu}_{\lambda}(x+{\rm i}|\epsilon|)+{ \rm e}^{\pi{\rm i}\mu/2}Q^{\mu}_{\lambda}(x-{\rm i}|\epsilon|)\right].\end{array}\] (C.32)

For \(\mu=0\) and \(\lambda\) a non-negative integer, the Legendre functions are polynomials given by the formula

\[P_{l}(x)\equiv P^{0}_{l}(z)=\frac{1}{2^{l}l!}\frac{{\rm d}^{l}}{{\rm d}x^{l}} (x^{2}-1)^{l}.\] (C.33)

These **Legendre polynomials** are also generated by the relation

\[\frac{1}{\sqrt{1-2xt+t^{2}}}=\sum_{l=0}^{\infty}P_{l}(x)t^{l}\ \ \ |t|<1,\ |x|\leq 1,\] (C.34)

which leads to an expression for the inverse distance between the points \({\bf x}\) and \({\bf x}^{\prime}\),

\[\frac{1}{|{\bf x}-{\bf x}^{\prime}|}=\sum_{l=0}^{\infty}\frac{r^{l}_{<}}{r^{l+ 1}_{>}}P_{l}(\cos\gamma),\] (C.35)

where \(r_{<}=\min(|{\bf x}|,|{\bf x}^{\prime}|)\), \(r_{>}=\max(|{\bf x}|,|{\bf x}^{\prime}|)\), and \(\gamma\) is the angle between the two vectors.

For integer \(m>0\) and integer \(l\geq 0\) the Legendre functions are sometimes called **associated Legendre functions**, and are given by1

Footnote 1: Our convention for associated Legendre functions with real \(x\) between \(-1\) and \(+1\) follows Abramowitz & Stegun (1964), Press et al. (1986), Gradshteyn & Ryzhik (2000), and software such as IDL, Maple, and Mathematica, but differs from Morse & Feshbach (1953), Arfken & Weber (2005), and the first edition of this book by a factor \((-1)^{m}\).

\[P^{m}_{l}(x)=(-1)^{m}(1-x^{2})^{m/2}\frac{{\rm d}^{m}P^{0}_{l}(x)}{{\rm d}x^{m }}=(-1)^{m}\frac{(1-x^{2})^{m/2}}{2^{l}l!}\frac{{\rm d}^{l+m}P^{0}_{l}(x)}{{ \rm d}x^{l+m}}.\] (C.36)Note that \(P_{l}^{m}(x)\) vanishes for \(m>l\), and that \(P_{l}^{m}(x)\) is even in \(x\) if \(l-m\) is even, and odd if \(l-m\) is odd. We have

\[P_{l}^{m}(0)=(-1)^{(l+m)/2}\frac{(l+m)!}{2^{l}[\frac{1}{2}(l-m)]!\,[\frac{1}{2} (l+m)]}\quad(l-m\ {\rm even}),\] (C.37)

and zero if \((l-m)\) is odd. For integer \(m>0\),

\[P_{l}^{-m}(x)=(-1)^{m}\frac{(l-m)!}{(l+m)!}P_{l}^{m}(x).\] (C.38)

The associated Legendre functions are orthogonal in the sense that

\[\int_{-1}^{1}{\rm d}x\,P_{l}^{m}(x)P_{n}^{m}(x)=\frac{2}{2l+1}\frac{(l+m)!}{(l- m)!}\delta_{ln}.\] (C.39)

\[\int_{-1}^{1}\frac{{\rm d}x}{1-x^{2}}\,P_{l}^{m}(x)P_{l}^{k}(x)=\frac{1}{m} \frac{(l+m)!}{(l-m)!}\delta_{mk}.\] (C.40)

The associated Legendre functions can be written most compactly using the substitution \(x=\cos\theta\); since \(-1\leq x\leq 1\) we take \(0\leq\theta\leq\pi\) and let \(c=\cos\theta\), \(s=\sin\theta\):

\[\begin{array}{llll}P_{0}(c)=1&\\ P_{1}(c)=c&P_{1}^{1}(c)=-s&\\ P_{2}(c)=\frac{1}{2}(3c^{2}-1)&P_{2}^{1}(c)=-3cs&P_{2}^{2}(c)=3s^{2}\\ P_{3}(c)=\frac{1}{2}(5c^{3}-3c)&P_{3}^{1}(c)=-\frac{3}{2}s(5c^{2}-1)&P_{3}^{2} (c)=15cs^{2}&P_{3}^{3}(c)=-15s^{3}.\end{array}\] (C.41)

### **C.6 Spherical harmonics**

A spherical harmonic is defined by the expression

\[{\rm Y}_{l}^{m}(\theta,\phi)=\sqrt{\frac{2l+1}{4\pi}\frac{(l-m)!}{(l+m)!}}P_{l }^{m}(\cos\theta){\rm e}^{{\rm i}m\phi}\quad(m\geq 0).\] (C.42)

For most purposes, the indices of the spherical harmonics can be restricted to \(l=0,1,2,\ldots\) and \(m=-l,-l+1,\ldots,l-1,l\). The variables lie in the range \(0\leq\theta\leq\pi\) and \(0\leq\phi\leq 2\pi\) and usually represent the angular coordinates in a spherical coordinate system (see Figure B.1). Note that

\[{\rm Y}_{l}^{-m}(\theta,\phi)=(-1)^{m}{\rm Y}_{l}^{m\,*}(\theta,\phi),\] (C.43)

where the asterisk denotes complex conjugation.

The most important feature of the spherical harmonics, which is easily proved using equation (C.39), is that they are orthonormal in the sense that

\[\oint{\rm d}^{2}\Omega\,{\rm Y}_{k}^{n\,*}(\mathbf{\Omega}){\rm Y}_{l}^{m}( \mathbf{\Omega})=\int_{0}^{\pi}\!\!{\rm d}\theta\,\sin\theta\int_{0}^{2\pi}\! \!{\rm d}\phi\,{\rm Y}_{k}^{n\,*}(\theta,\phi){\rm Y}_{l}^{m}(\theta,\phi)= \delta_{kl}\delta_{nm}.\] (C.44)

An arbitrary function of position \(f(\mathbf{r})\) can be written in spherical coordinates as a series of spherical harmonics,

\[f(\mathbf{r})=f(r,\theta,\phi)=\sum_{n=0}^{\infty}\sum_{m=-l}^{l}f_{lm}(r){\rm Y }_{l}^{m}(\theta,\phi).\] (C.45)Multiplying by \({\rm Y}_{n}^{k\,\,\ast}(\theta,\phi)\), integrating over solid angle, and using equation (C.44), we find

\[f_{nk}(r)=\int{\rm d}^{2}\Omega\,{\rm Y}_{k}^{n\,\,\ast}(\theta,\phi)f({\bf r}).\] (C.46)

The addition theorem for spherical harmonics states that if the directions \((\theta,\phi)\) and \((\theta^{\prime},\phi^{\prime})\) are separated by an angle \(\gamma\), then

\[P_{l}(\cos\gamma)=\frac{4\pi}{2l+1}\sum_{m=-l}^{l}{\rm Y}_{l}^{m\,\,\ast}( \theta^{\prime},\phi^{\prime}){\rm Y}_{l}^{m}(\theta,\phi).\] (C.47)

Together with equation (C.35), this leads to an expression for the inverse distance between the points \({\bf x}=(r,\theta,\phi)\) and \({\bf x}^{\prime}=(r^{\prime},\theta^{\prime},\phi^{\prime})\):

\[\frac{1}{|{\bf x}-{\bf x}^{\prime}|}=\sum_{l=0}^{\infty}\sum_{m=-l}^{l}\frac{ 4\pi}{2l+1}\frac{r_{<}^{l}}{r_{>}^{l+1}}{\rm Y}_{l}^{m\,\,\ast}(\theta^{\prime },\phi^{\prime}){\rm Y}_{l}^{m}(\theta,\phi),\] (C.48)

where \(r_{<}=\min(r,r^{\prime})\) and \(r_{>}=\max(r,r^{\prime})\).

Using equations (B.53) and (C.30) we can show that

\[\nabla^{2}[f(r){\rm Y}_{l}^{m}(\theta,\phi)]=\biggl{[}\frac{1}{r^{2}}\frac{{ \rm d}}{{\rm d}r}\Bigl{(}r^{2}\frac{{\rm d}f}{{\rm d}r}\Bigr{)}-\frac{l(l+1)}{ r^{2}}f(r)\biggr{]}{\rm Y}_{l}^{m}(\theta,\phi).\] (C.49)

The first few spherical harmonics are:

\[\begin{array}{ll}{\rm Y}_{0}^{0}(\theta,\phi)=\frac{1}{\sqrt{4\pi}}\\ {\rm Y}_{1}^{0}(\theta,\phi)=\sqrt{\frac{3}{4\pi}}\cos\theta&{\rm Y}_{1}^{\pm 1 }(\theta,\phi)=\mp\sqrt{\frac{3}{8\pi}}\sin\theta\,{\rm e}^{\pm{\rm i}\phi}\\ {\rm Y}_{2}^{0}(\theta,\phi)=\sqrt{\frac{5}{16\pi}}(3\cos^{2}\theta-1)&{\rm Y }_{2}^{\pm 1}(\theta,\phi)=\mp\sqrt{\frac{15}{8\pi}}\sin\theta\cos\theta\,{\rm e}^{ \pm{\rm i}\phi}\\ &{\rm Y}_{2}^{\pm 2}(\theta,\phi)=\sqrt{\frac{15}{32\pi}}\sin^{2}\theta\,{ \rm e}^{\pm 2{\rm i}\phi}.\end{array}\] (C.50)

### Bessel functions

The most complete reference is Watson (1995).

The Bessel functions of the first and second kind, \(J_{\nu}(z)\) and \(Y_{\nu}(z)\), are linearly independent solutions of the differential equation

\[\frac{1}{z}\frac{{\rm d}}{{\rm d}z}\Bigl{(}z\frac{{\rm d}w}{{\rm d}z}\Bigr{)} +\Bigl{(}1-\frac{\nu^{2}}{z^{2}}\Bigr{)}w=0.\] (C.51)

In series form,

\[J_{\nu}(z)=\sum_{k=0}^{\infty}\frac{(-1)^{k}}{k!\,(\nu+k)!}({\frac{1}{2}}z)^{ \nu+2k},\] (C.52)

and \(Y_{\nu}(z)\) is defined by the relation

\[Y_{\nu}(z)=\frac{\cos\nu\pi J_{\nu}(z)-J_{-\nu}(z)}{\sin\nu\pi},\] (C.53)

or by its limiting value if \(\nu\) is an integer. The function \(Y_{\nu}(z)\) diverges as \(z^{-|\nu|}\) when \(z\to 0\). As \(x\to\infty\)

\[J_{\nu}(x)\to\sqrt{\frac{2}{\pi x}}\cos(x-{\frac{1}{2}}\nu\pi-{ \frac{1}{4}}\pi)+{\rm O}(x^{-1}).\] (C.54)If \(\nu\equiv n\) is an integer

\[J_{n}(-z)=(-1)^{n}J_{n}(z)\quad;\quad J_{-n}(z)=(-1)^{n}J_{n}(z)\quad;\quad Y_{- n}(z)=(-1)^{n}Y_{n}(z),\] (C.55)

\[J_{n}(z)=\frac{1}{\pi}\int_{0}^{\pi}\mathrm{d}\theta\,\cos(z\sin\theta-n\theta).\] (C.56)

If \(C_{\nu}\) denotes either \(J_{\nu}\) or \(Y_{\nu}\),

\[C_{\nu-1}(z)+C_{\nu+1}(z)=\frac{2\nu}{z}C_{\nu}(z)\quad;\quad C_{\nu-1}(z)-C_{ \nu+1}(z)=2\frac{\mathrm{d}C_{\nu}(z)}{\mathrm{d}z}.\] (C.57)

For \(\nu=0\) these relations imply

\[J_{0}^{\prime}(z)=-J_{1}(z).\] (C.58)

An important integral identity is

\[\int_{0}^{\infty}\mathrm{d}k\,k\int_{0}^{\infty}\mathrm{d}R\,RF(R)J_{\nu}(kR) J_{\nu}(kr)=F(r)\quad(\nu\geq-\tfrac{1}{2}),\] (C.59)

where \(F(R)\) is an arbitrary function. If

\[g(k)=\int_{0}^{\infty}\mathrm{d}r\,rf(r)J_{\nu}(kr)\] (C.60a) then \[g\] is called the **Hankel transform** of \[f\], and equation (C.59) yields

\[f(r)=\int_{0}^{\infty}\mathrm{d}k\,kg(k)J_{\nu}(kr).\] (C.60b)

The Hankel transform is defined for any integer \(\nu\) and all real \(\nu\geq-\tfrac{1}{2}\).

The **modified Bessel functions** are

\[I_{\nu}(z)=\mathrm{i}^{-\nu}J_{\nu}(\mathrm{i}z)\quad;\quad K_{\nu}(z)=K_{- \nu}(z)=\frac{\pi}{2}\frac{I_{-\nu}(z)-I_{\nu}(z)}{\sin\nu\pi};\] (C.61)

the second equation is replaced by its limiting value if \(\nu\) is an integer. As \(z\to 0\),

\[\begin{split} I_{\nu}(z)&\to\frac{1}{\nu!}\left( \tfrac{1}{2}z\right)^{\nu}\quad(\nu\neq-1,-2,\ldots);\\ K_{\nu}(z)&\to\frac{(\nu-1)!}{2}\left(\tfrac{1}{2} z\right)^{-\nu}\quad(\nu>0);\end{split}\] (C.62)

At large \(x\),

\[I_{\nu}(x)\to\frac{\mathrm{e}^{x}}{\sqrt{2\pi x}}\quad;\quad K_{\nu}(x)\to \sqrt{\frac{\pi}{2x}}\mathrm{e}^{-x}.\] (C.63)

If \(\nu\equiv n\) is an integer,

\[I_{n}(-z)=(-1)^{n}I_{n}(z)\quad;\quad I_{n}(z)=I_{-n}(z)=\frac{1}{\pi}\int_{0} ^{\pi}\mathrm{d}\theta\,\mathrm{e}^{z\cos\theta}\cos(n\theta).\] (C.64)

If \(Z_{\nu}\) denotes either \(I_{\nu}\) or \(\mathrm{e}^{\mathrm{i}\pi\nu}K_{\nu}\),

\[Z_{\nu-1}(z)-Z_{\nu+1}(z)=\frac{2\nu}{z}Z_{\nu}(z)\quad;\quad Z_{\nu-1}(z)+Z_ {\nu+1}(z)=2\frac{\mathrm{d}Z_{\nu}(z)}{\mathrm{d}z};\] (C.65)

for \(\nu=0\) these imply

\[I_{0}^{\prime}(z)=I_{1}(z)\quad;\quad K_{0}^{\prime}(z)=-K_{1}(z).\] (C.66)We shall use the results

\[{\rm e}^{z\cos\theta}=I_{0}(z)+2\sum_{n=1}^{\infty}I_{n}(z)\cos n\theta=\sum_{n=- \infty}^{\infty}I_{n}(z)\cos n\theta;\] (C.67)

\[\int_{0}^{\infty}{\rm d}x\,x^{\mu}J_{\nu}(x)=2^{\mu}\frac{[\frac{1}{2}(\nu+\mu+ 3)]!}{[\frac{1}{2}(\nu-\mu+3)]!}\quad{\rm Re}(\nu+\mu)>-1,\ {\rm Re}(\mu)<\tfrac{1}{2}.\] (C.68)

\[\int_{u}^{\infty}{\rm d}x\,\frac{x\,{\rm e}^{-\mu x}}{(x^{2}-u^{2}) ^{1-\nu}}\] (C.69) \[\qquad=\frac{2^{\nu-1/2}(\nu-1)!}{\sqrt{\pi}}\frac{u^{\nu+1/2}}{ \mu^{\nu-1/2}}K_{\nu+1/2}(u\mu)\qquad(u>0,\ {\rm Re}\,\mu,\nu>0);\] (C.70) \[\int_{0}^{b}{\rm d}y\,y^{\nu}(b^{2}-y^{2})^{\nu-3/2}K_{\nu}(y)=2^ {\nu-3}\sqrt{\pi}(\nu-\tfrac{3}{2})!\,b^{2\nu-1}\] \[\qquad\times\left[I_{\nu-1}(\tfrac{1}{2}b)K_{\nu}(\tfrac{1}{2}b) -I_{\nu}(\tfrac{1}{2}b)K_{\nu-1}(\tfrac{1}{2}b)\right]\qquad({\rm Re}\,\nu>- \tfrac{1}{2}).\]

### _Appendix D:_ Mechanics

We assume a background in classical mechanics at the advanced undergraduate level, including basic Hamiltonian mechanics. Useful reference texts include Landau & Lifshitz (1989), Jose & Saletan (1998), and Sussman & Wisdom (2001). The most elegant and mathematical treatment of the subject is found in Arnold (1989). This appendix contains a brief summary of the concepts employed in this book.

### _D.1 Single particles_

The momentum of a particle is \({\bf p}=m{\bf v}\), where \(m\) is its mass and \({\bf v}\) is its velocity. Its motion is described by **Newton's second law**,

\[{\bf F}=\frac{{\rm d}{\bf p}}{{\rm d}t},\] (D.1)

where \({\bf F}\) is the force acting on the particle. Thus, if the mass of the particle is constant,

\[\frac{{\rm d}{\bf v}}{{\rm d}t}=\frac{{\rm d}^{2}{\bf x}}{{\rm d}t^{2}}=\frac{ {\bf F}}{m}.\] (D.2)

The **work** done against the force \({\bf F}\) in moving a particle from \({\bf x}_{1}\) to \({\bf x}_{2}\) is

\[W_{12}=-\int_{{\bf x}_{1}}^{{\bf x}_{2}}{\rm d}{\bf x}\cdot{\bf F},\] (D.3)

a line integral that is to be taken along the particle's trajectory from \({\bf x}_{1}\) to \({\bf x}_{2}\). The rate at which work is done on the force is

\[\frac{{\rm d}W}{{\rm d}t}=-\frac{{\rm d}}{{\rm d}t}\int_{{\bf x}_{1}}^{{\bf x} _{2}(t)}{\rm d}{\bf x}\cdot{\bf F}=-\frac{{\rm d}{\bf x}}{{\rm d}t}\cdot{\bf F }=-{\bf F}\cdot{\bf v},\] (D.4)

evaluated at \({\bf x}_{2}\). For a particle of fixed mass,

\[\begin{array}{l}W_{12}=-m\int_{{\bf x}_{1}}^{{\bf x}_{2}}{\rm d}{\bf x} \cdot\frac{{\rm d}^{2}{\bf x}}{{\rm d}t^{2}}=-m\int_{{\bf x}_{1}}^{{\bf x}_{2} }{\rm d}t\,\frac{{\rm d}{\bf x}}{{\rm d}t}\cdot\frac{{\rm d}^{2}{\bf x}}{{\rm d }t^{2}}=-m\int_{{\bf x}_{1}}^{{\bf x}_{2}}{\rm d}t\,{\bf v}\cdot\frac{{\rm d}{ \bf v}}{{\rm d}t}\\ \phantom{W_{12}}=\tfrac{1}{2}m[v^{2}({\bf x}_{1})-v^{2}({\bf x}_{2})].\end{array}\] (D.5)The **kinetic energy** of a particle is \(K\equiv\frac{1}{2}mv^{2}\), so

\[W_{12}=K_{1}-K_{2}.\] (D.6)

Many forces encountered in nature are **conservative**, that is, the work \(W_{12}\) is independent of the path taken between the endpoints \({\bf x}_{1}\) and \({\bf x}_{2}\). In this case, we may choose some fixed point \({\bf x}_{0}\) and define the potential energy \(V({\bf x})\) by

\[V({\bf x})\equiv-\int_{{\bf x}_{0}}^{{\bf x}}{\rm d}{\bf x}\cdot{\bf F}\quad{\rm then }\quad W_{12}=V({\bf x}_{2})-V({\bf x}_{1}),\] (D.7)

A common convention that fixes the otherwise arbitrary zero point of \(V\) is to place the point \({\bf x}_{0}\) at "infinity," that is, far from all interacting bodies, where \({\bf F}\) is negligibly small. All such points yield the same zero point, since the work done in moving from one point at "infinity" to another is negligible.

There are two immediate consequences of equations (D.7). Taking the gradient of \(V\), we obtain

\[{\bf F}=-\mathbf{\nabla}V({\bf x})=-\frac{\partial V}{\partial{\bf x }};\] (D.8)

and substituting the second of equations (D.7) into (D.6) yields

\[K_{1}+V({\bf x}_{1})=K_{2}+V({\bf x}_{2}).\] (D.9)

Thus if the **energy** of a particle is defined to be \(E=K+V=\frac{1}{2}mv^{2}+V({\bf x})\), we find that _if the forces acting on a particle are conservative, then its energy is conserved_. We shall also encounter cases in which the forces are conservative if the trajectory is traversed instantaneously, but the force at a given position is time-dependent. In these cases we can still write \({\bf F}=-\mathbf{\nabla}V({\bf x},t)\), but the energy \(E\) is no longer conserved; in fact,

\[\frac{{\rm d}E}{{\rm d}t}=\frac{\partial V({\bf x},t)}{\partial t}.\] (D.10)

Forces due to gravity are conservative (the proof is given at the beginning of Chapter 2); for gravity the potential energy of a particle is proportional to its mass and we may write \(V({\bf x})=m\Phi({\bf x})\), where \(\Phi\) is the gravitational potential. Thus the energy per unit mass \(\frac{1}{2}v^{2}+\Phi({\bf x})\) is conserved. Since we deal almost exclusively with gravitational forces in this book, we shall usually shorten the term "energy per unit mass" to simply "energy," and refer to \(\frac{1}{2}v^{2}\) as the kinetic energy and \(\Phi\) as the potential energy. When the distinction is not clear from the context, we use the notation \(E\) for energy per unit mass and \(\widetilde{E}=mE\) for energy.

The **angular momentum** of a particle relative to some origin O is

\[{\bf L}\equiv{\bf x}\times{\bf p},\] (D.11)

where the position vector \({\bf x}\) is measured from O. The torque is

\[{\bf N}={\bf x}\times{\bf F}.\] (D.12)

We have

\[\frac{{\rm d}{\bf L}}{{\rm d}t}=\frac{{\rm d}{\bf x}}{{\rm d}t}\times{\bf p}+{ \bf x}\times\frac{{\rm d}{\bf p}}{{\rm d}t}={\bf v}\times{\bf p}+{\bf x}\times{ \bf F}.\] (D.13)

The first term is proportional to \({\bf p}\times{\bf p}=0\), and thus

\[{\bf N}=\frac{{\rm d}{\bf L}}{{\rm d}t};\] (D.14)

in words, _the torque is equal to the rate of change of angular momentum_.

### D.2 Systems of particles

Consider an isolated system of \(N\) particles of masses \(m_{\alpha}\) and positions \({\bf x}_{\alpha}\), \(\alpha=1,\ldots,N\). The total mass of the system and the total force on particle \(\alpha\) are

\[M=\sum_{\alpha=1}^{N}m_{\alpha}\quad;\quad{\bf F}_{\alpha}=\sum_{\beta=1\atop \beta\neq\alpha}^{N}{\bf F}_{\alpha\beta},\] (D.15)

where \({\bf F}_{\alpha\beta}\) is the force exerted on particle \(\alpha\) by particle \(\beta\). According to **Newton's third law**

\[{\bf F}_{\alpha\beta}=-{\bf F}_{\beta\alpha}.\] (D.16)

The **center of mass** is located at

\[{\bf x}_{\rm cm}={\sum_{\alpha=1}^{N}m_{\alpha}{\bf x}_{\alpha}\over M}.\] (D.17)

Thus

\[{{\rm d}^{2}{\bf x}_{\rm cm}\over{\rm d}t^{2}}={1\over M}\sum_{\alpha=1}^{N}m_ {\alpha}{{\rm d}^{2}{\bf x}_{\alpha}\over{\rm d}t^{2}}={1\over M}\sum_{\alpha= 1}^{N}\sum_{\beta\neq\alpha}{\bf F}_{\alpha\beta}.\] (D.18)

The sum over \({\bf F}_{\alpha\beta}\) vanishes since each pair \({\bf F}_{\alpha\beta}+{\bf F}_{\beta\alpha}\) sums to zero. Thus

\[{{\rm d}^{2}{\bf x}_{\rm cm}\over{\rm d}t^{2}}=0,\] (D.19)

and we conclude that _the center of mass of an isolated system moves at uniform velocity_.

Similarly, the total angular momentum is

\[{\bf L}=\sum_{\alpha=1}^{N}{\bf x}_{\alpha}\times{\bf p}_{\alpha},\] (D.20)

and

\[{{\rm d}{\bf L}\over{\rm d}t}=\sum_{\alpha=1}^{N}\sum_{\beta\neq\alpha}{\bf x} _{\alpha}\times{\bf F}_{\alpha\beta}.\] (D.21)

The right side is a sum of pairs of the form \({\bf x}_{\alpha}\times{\bf F}_{\alpha\beta}+{\bf x}_{\beta}\times{\bf F}_{ \beta\alpha}=({\bf x}_{\alpha}-{\bf x}_{\beta})\times{\bf F}_{\alpha\beta}\). In the case of gravity and most other phenomena, the interparticle force acts along the line joining the two particles, so this term also vanishes. In words, _the total angular momentum of an isolated system is conserved if the interparticle forces act along the line joining the particles_.

As for a single particle, we compute the work done against the forces \({\bf F}_{\alpha}\) on particle \(\alpha\) in moving the system from configuration 1 to configuration 2,

\[W_{12}=-\sum_{\alpha=1}^{N}\int_{1}^{2}{\rm d}{\bf x}_{\alpha}\cdot{\bf F}_{ \alpha},\] (D.22)

and as in equation (D.5) we may write

\[W_{12}=K_{{\rm tot},1}-K_{{\rm tot},2},\] (D.23)

where \(K_{{\rm tot}}={1\over 2}\sum_{\alpha=1}^{N}m_{\alpha}v_{\alpha}^{2}\) is the total kinetic energy of the system. Note that

\[K_{{\rm tot}}={1\over 2}Mv_{\rm cm}^{2}+{1\over 2}\sum_{\alpha=1}^{N}m_{ \alpha}{v_{\alpha}^{\prime}}^{2},\] (D.24)where \({\bf v}^{\prime}_{\alpha}={\bf v}_{\alpha}-{\bf v}_{\rm cm}\) and \({\bf v}_{\rm cm}=\hat{\bf x}_{\rm cm}\) is the velocity of the center of mass. Thus the total kinetic energy is the sum of the kinetic energy of motion relative to the center of mass, and the kinetic energy of a single particle of mass \(M\) moving at the velocity of the center of mass.

In many cases the interparticle forces are conservative, and can be written as

\[{\bf F}_{\alpha\beta}=-\frac{\partial}{\partial{\bf x}_{\alpha}}V(|{\bf x}_{ \alpha}-{\bf x}_{\beta}|).\] (D.25)

Note that this form automatically guarantees the validity of Newton's third law, and that the interparticle forces act along the line joining the particles. We have

\[W_{12}=\sum_{\alpha=1}^{N}\sum_{\beta\neq\alpha}\int_{1}^{2}{\rm d}{\bf x}_{ \alpha}\cdot\frac{\partial}{\partial{\bf x}_{\alpha}}V(|{\bf x}_{\alpha}-{\bf x }_{\beta}|).\] (D.26)

We define the potential energy of the system to be

\[W_{\rm tot}={{1\over 2}}\sum_{\alpha=1}^{N}\sum_{\beta\neq\alpha}V(|{\bf x}_{ \alpha}-{\bf x}_{\beta}|).\] (D.27)

Then, as a result of any small change \({\bf x}_{\alpha}\to{\bf x}_{\alpha}+{\rm d}{\bf x}_{\alpha}\), \(\alpha=1,\ldots,N\),

\[{\rm d}W_{\rm tot}=\sum_{\alpha=1}^{N}\sum_{\beta\neq\alpha}{\rm d}{\bf x}_{ \alpha}\cdot\frac{\partial V(|{\bf x}_{\alpha}-{\bf x}_{\beta}|)}{\partial{ \bf x}_{\alpha}},\] (D.28)

where the factor \({{1\over 2}}\) has disappeared because the term involving a given pair of particles--say, \(V(|{\bf x}_{1}-{\bf x}_{2}|)\)--appears twice in equation (D.27). Thus equations (D.26) and (D.28) yield \(W_{12}=W_{{\rm tot},2}-W_{{\rm tot},1}\), and equation (D.23) yields

\[K_{{\rm tot},1}+W_{{\rm tot},1}=K_{{\rm tot},2}+W_{{\rm tot},2};\] (D.29)

in words, _the total energy of the system \(K_{{\rm tot}}+W_{{\rm tot}}\) is conserved_.

The behavior of an isolated two-body system with conservative interparticle forces is particularly simple. We have

\[m_{1}\frac{{\rm d}^{2}{\bf x}_{1}}{{\rm d}t^{2}}={\bf F}_{12}=-m_{2}\frac{{\rm d }^{2}{\bf x}_{2}}{{\rm d}t^{2}}.\] (D.30)

The center of mass is at \({\bf x}_{\rm cm}=(m_{1}{\bf x}_{1}+m_{2}{\bf x}_{2})/M\) and moves at uniform velocity. The relative separation vector \({\bf r}={\bf x}_{2}-{\bf x}_{1}\) obeys the equation

\[\frac{{\rm d}^{2}{\bf r}}{{\rm d}t^{2}}=-\frac{{\bf F}_{12}}{\mu}=\frac{1}{ \mu}\frac{\partial V(|{\bf r}|)}{\partial{\bf x}_{1}},\] (D.31)

where the **reduced mass** is

\[\mu\equiv\frac{m_{1}m_{2}}{m_{1}+m_{2}}.\] (D.32)

Since \(\partial V/\partial{\bf x}_{1}=-\partial V/\partial{\bf r}\), we have

\[\frac{{\rm d}^{2}{\bf r}}{{\rm d}t^{2}}=-\frac{1}{\mu}\frac{\partial V(|{\bf r }|)}{\partial{\bf r}},\] (D.33)

which is the equation of motion of a fictitious single particle (the **reduced particle**) of mass \(\mu\) in the fixed potential \(V(|{\bf r}|)\). Thus the two-body problem has been reduced to a one-body problem. The total energy is

\[E={{1\over 2}}Mv_{\rm cm}^{2}+{{1\over 2}}(m_{1}{v_{1}^{\prime}}^{2}+m_{2}{v_{2}^{ \prime}}^{2})+V={{1\over 2}}Mv_{\rm cm}^{2}+{{1\over 2}}\mu{\dot{\bf r }}^{2}+V(|{\bf r}|),\] (D.34)in which we have used the relations \({\bf v}^{\prime}_{1}=-m_{2}{\bf\dot{r}}/M\), \({\bf v}^{\prime}_{2}=m_{1}{\bf\dot{r}}/M\).

**Rigid bodies** Although stellar systems are not rigid bodies, the simple behavior of rigid bodies provides a useful comparison to the behavior of rotating stellar systems.

Consider a rigid body that rotates about its center of mass at \({\bf x}=0\), with angular velocity \({\bf\Omega}\). Its angular momentum and kinetic energy are given by equations (D.20) and (D.24),

\[{\bf L}=\sum_{\alpha=1}^{N}m_{\alpha}{\bf x}_{\alpha}\times{\bf v}_{\alpha} \quad;\quad K={{1\over 2}}\sum_{\alpha=1}^{N}m_{\alpha}v_{\alpha}^{2},\] (D.35)

where the sum is over all of the particles in the body. Since the body is rigid, the velocity at each point is

\[{\bf v}_{\alpha}={\bf\Omega}\times{\bf x}_{\alpha}.\] (D.36)

Using the vector identity (B.8), we have

\[K={{1\over 2}}\sum_{\alpha=1}^{N}m_{\alpha}{\bf v}_{\alpha}\cdot({\bf \Omega}\times{\bf x}_{\alpha})={{1\over 2}}\sum_{\alpha=1}^{N}m_{\alpha}{\bf \Omega}\cdot({\bf x}_{\alpha}\times{\bf v}_{\alpha})={{1\over 2}}{\bf \Omega}\cdot{\bf L}.\] (D.37)

Moreover

\[{\bf L}=\sum_{\alpha=1}^{N}m_{\alpha}{\bf x}_{\alpha}\times({\bf\Omega} \times{\bf x}_{\alpha})={\bf I}^{\prime}\cdot{\bf\Omega}\quad{\rm or}\quad L_ {j}=\sum_{k=1}^{3}I^{\prime}_{jk}\Omega_{k}.\] (D.38)

Here \({\bf I}^{\prime}\) is the **moment of inertia tensor**, a symmetric tensor having components1

Footnote 1: Unfortunately, this term is sometimes used to denote the tensor

\[I_{jk}\equiv\sum_{\alpha=1}^{N}m_{\alpha}x_{\alpha j}x_{\alpha k}\] (D.39)

(cf. eqs. 4.243 and 7.15). The relation between the two definitions is \[I^{\prime}_{jk}={\rm trace}\,({\bf I})\delta_{jk}-I_{jk}.\] (D.40)

\[I^{\prime}_{jk}\equiv\sum_{\alpha=1}^{N}m_{\alpha}(|{\bf x}_{\alpha}|^{2} \delta_{jk}-x_{\alpha j}x_{\alpha k}).\] (D.41)

The kinetic energy is given by

\[K={{1\over 2}}{\bf\Omega}\cdot{\bf I}^{\prime}\cdot{\bf\Omega}={ {1\over 2}}{\bf L}\cdot({\bf I}^{\prime})^{-1}\cdot{\bf L}.\] (D.42)

If the coordinate axes coincide with the principal axes of the moment of inertia tensor, then the tensor is diagonal. If, in addition, the angular velocity vector lies in one of the principal axes (say, the \(z\) axis), we have

\[{\bf L}=L\hat{\bf e}_{z}\quad;\quad{\bf\Omega}=\Omega\hat{\bf e}_{z}\quad;\quad K ={L^{2}\over 2I},\] (D.43)

where

\[I\equiv I^{\prime}_{zz}=\sum_{\alpha=1}^{N}m_{\alpha}(x_{\alpha}^{2}+y_{ \alpha}^{2}).\] (D.44)

### D.3 Lagrangian dynamics

Consider a particle moving in a conservative force field defined by the potential energy \(V({\bf x},t)\). We define the **Lagrangian**

\[{\cal L}({\bf x},\dot{\bf x},t)\equiv K-V=\tfrac{1}{2}m\dot{\bf x}^{2}-V({\bf x},t).\] (D.45)

The **principle of least action** or **Hamilton's principle** states that _the motion of the particle from time \(t_{0}\) to \(t_{1}\) is along a curve \({\bf x}(t)\) that is an extremal of the_ **action**

\[I\equiv\int_{t_{0}}^{t_{1}}{\rm d}t\,{\cal L}.\] (D.46)

The proof is simple. According to the Euler-Lagrange equation (B.81), the trajectory is an extremal of \(I\) if and only if

\[0=\frac{{\rm d}}{{\rm d}t}\left(\frac{\partial{\cal L}}{\partial\dot{\bf x}} \right)-\frac{\partial{\cal L}}{\partial{\bf x}}=m\ddot{\bf x}+\frac{\partial V }{\partial{\bf x}},\] (D.47)

which is simply a restatement of Newton's second law.

The attraction of this approach is that the Lagrangian \({\cal L}\) is a _scalar_ function. Hence it is straightforward to compute \({\cal L}\) as a function \({\cal L}({\bf q},\dot{\bf q},t)\) of arbitrary--sometimes called **generalized**--coordinates \({\bf q}\) and their time derivatives \(\dot{\bf q}\). Extremizing the action with \({\cal L}\) expressed in this form yields **Lagrange's equations**

\[\frac{{\rm d}}{{\rm d}t}\left(\frac{\partial{\cal L}}{\partial\dot{\bf q}} \right)-\frac{\partial{\cal L}}{\partial{\bf q}}=0,\] (D.48)

which are the equations of motion in the generalized coordinates. This approach avoids the heavy algebra that is often required to express vector equations in curvilinear coordinates.

### D.4 Hamiltonian dynamics

For a given set of generalized coordinates \({\bf q}\) we define the **generalized momentum**\({\bf p}\) to be

\[{\bf p}\equiv\left(\frac{\partial{\cal L}}{\partial\dot{\bf q}}\right)_{{\bf q },t}.\] (D.49)

The **Hamiltonian** is

\[H({\bf q},{\bf p},t)\equiv{\bf p}\cdot\dot{\bf q}-{\cal L}({\bf q},\dot{\bf q },t),\] (D.50)

where it is understood that \(\dot{\bf q}\) is to be eliminated in favor of \({\bf q}\), \({\bf p}\), and \(t\) using equation (D.49).

#### D.4.1 Hamilton's equations

The total derivative of the Hamiltonian is

\[{\rm d}H=\left(\frac{\partial H}{\partial{\bf q}}\right)_{{\bf p},t}\!\!\!\! \!\!\!\!\!\!\!\!\!\!\!\!\!\!\!\!\!\!\!\!\!\!\!\!\!\!\!\!\!\!\!\!\!\!\!\!\!\! \!\!\!\!\!\!\!\!\!\!\!\!\!\!\!\!\!\!\!\!\!\!\!\!\!\!\!\!\!\!\!\!\!\!\!\!\! \!\!\!\!\!\!\!\!\!\!\!\!\!\!\!\!\!\!\!\!\!\!\!\!\!\!\!\!\!\!\!\!\!\!\!\!\! \!\!\!\!\!\!\!\!\!\!\!\!\!\!\!\!\!\!\!\!\!\!\!\!\!\!\!\!\!\!\!\!\!\!\!\!\! \!\!\!\!\!\!\!\!\!\!\!\!\!\!\!\!\!\!\!\!\!\!\!\!\!\!\!\!\!\!\!\!\!\!\!\!\! \!\!\!\!\!\!\!\!\!\!\!\!\!\!\!\!\!\!\!\!\!\!\!\!\!\!\!\!\!\!\!\!\!\!\!\! \!\!\!\!\!\!\!\!\!\!\!\!\!\!\!\!\!\!\!\!\!\!\!\!\!\!\!\!\!\!\!\!\!\!\!\!\! \!\!\!\!\!\!\!\!\!\!\!\!\!\!\!\!\!\!\!\!\!\!\!\!\!\!\!\!\!\!\!\!\!\!\!\!\!\! \!\!\!\!\!\!\!\!\!\!\!\!\!\!\!\!\!\!\!\!\!\!\!\!\!\!\!\!\!\!\!\!\!\!\!\!\!\!\! \!\!\!\!\!\!\!\!\!\!\!\!\!\!\!\!\!\!\!\!\!\!\!\!\!\!\!\!\!\!\!\!\!\!\!\!\!\!\! \!\!\!\!\!\!\!\!\!\!\!\!\!\!\!\!\!\!\!\!\!\!\!\!\!\!\!\!\!\!\!\!\!\!\!\!\!\!\!\!\!\!\! \!\!where the first and fourth terms in the first line have canceled because of equation (D.49). Since equations (D.51) and (D.52) must be the same, we have

\[\dot{\bf q}=\left(\frac{\partial H}{\partial{\bf p}}\right)_{{\bf q},t};\quad \left(\frac{\partial H}{\partial{\bf q}}\right)_{{\bf p},t}=-\left(\frac{ \partial{\cal L}}{\partial{\bf q}}\right)_{{\bf q},t};\quad\left(\frac{ \partial H}{\partial t}\right)_{{\bf q},{\bf p}}=-\left(\frac{\partial{\cal L }}{\partial t}\right)_{{\bf q},{\bf\dot{q}}}.\] (D.53)

Using Lagrange's equations (D.48) and simplifying the notation, the first two of these equations lead us to **Hamilton's equations**

\[\dot{\bf q}=\frac{\partial H}{\partial{\bf p}}\quad;\quad\dot{\bf p}=-\frac{ \partial H}{\partial{\bf q}}.\] (D.54)

The **configuration space** of a system is the \(n\)-dimensional space with coordinates \((q_{1},\ldots,q_{n})\). The corresponding **momentum space** has coordinates \((p_{1},\ldots,p_{n})\). A system with \(n\)-dimensional configuration and momentum spaces is said to have \(n\)**degrees of freedom**. Phase space is the \(2n\)-dimensional space with coordinates \((q_{1},\ldots,q_{n},p_{1},\ldots,p_{n})\equiv({\bf q},{\bf p})\equiv{\bf w}\). Since Hamilton's equations (D.54) are first-order differential equations, if we are given the Hamiltonian and a particle's phase-space coordinates \({\bf w}_{0}\) at time \(t=0\), we can solve for the coordinates \({\bf w}_{t}\) at any later (or earlier) time \(t\). Thus through each point \({\bf w}_{0}\) in phase space there passes a unique phase-space trajectory \({\bf w}_{t}({\bf w}_{0})\), which gives the future and past phase-space coordinates of the particle that has coordinates \({\bf w}_{0}\) at \(t=0\). No two of these trajectories can ever intersect: if they did, the trajectory of a particle that started from the intersection point would not be unique. We define the **time-evolution operator**\({\bf H}_{t}\) by

\[{\bf w}_{t}\equiv{\bf H}_{t}({\bf w}_{0}),\] (D.55)

and say that the operator \({\bf H}_{t}\) defines a **Hamiltonian flow** in phase space.

Along a trajectory \({\bf w}(t)\), the Hamiltonian \(H[{\bf w}(t),t]\) changes at a rate

\[\frac{{\rm d}H}{{\rm d}t}=\frac{\partial H}{\partial{\bf q}}\cdot\dot{\bf q} +\frac{\partial H}{\partial{\bf p}}\cdot\dot{\bf p}+\frac{\partial H}{ \partial t}=\frac{\partial H}{\partial t},\] (D.56)

where we have used (D.54) to eliminate \(\dot{\bf q}\) and \(\dot{\bf p}\). Hence, if \(\partial{\cal L}/\partial t=0\), it follows from (D.53) that the Hamiltonian is conserved along all dynamical trajectories.

Thus, for example, consider motion in the time-independent potential \(V({\bf x})\). If we work in Cartesian coordinates, the Lagrangian \({\cal L}=\frac{1}{2}m\dot{\bf x}^{2}-V({\bf x})\) depends only on \({\bf x}\) and \(\dot{\bf x}\), so \(\partial{\cal L}/\partial t=0\). Hence the Hamiltonian \(H\) is conserved. The physical meaning of this conservation law is easily found. We have \({\bf p}=\partial{\cal L}/\partial\dot{\bf x}=m\dot{\bf x}\) and

\[H({\bf x},{\bf p})={\bf p}\cdot\dot{\bf x}-{\cal L}=\frac{p^{2}}{2m}+V({\bf x});\] (D.57)

this is simply the total energy \(E=K+V\), which we know to be conserved for motion in a time-independent potential. Thus _for motion in a fixed potential the Hamiltonian is equal to the total energy_.

In most cases, the velocity \({\bf v}\) of a particle of mass \(m\) is just \(1/m\) times the particle's momentum \({\bf p}\), so by a slight abuse of language, we shall also use the term phase space to denote the six-dimensional space with coordinates \(({\bf x},{\bf v})\).

#### d.4.2 Poincare invariants

Let \(\mathcal{S}_{0}\) be any two-dimensional surface in phase space, and let us specify points on \(\mathcal{S}_{0}\) by some set of coordinates \((u,v)\). The time-evolution operator \(\mathbf{H}_{t}\) maps each point of \(\mathcal{S}_{0}\) into a new surface \(\mathcal{S}_{t}\) and also maps the curves of constant \(u\), \(v\) on \(\mathcal{S}_{0}\) into curves that define \((u,v)\) coordinates on \(\mathcal{S}_{t}\). With this definition, each pair \((u,v)\) defines a trajectory.

We define

\[\begin{split} A(t)&\equiv\iint_{\mathcal{S}_{t}} \mathrm{d}\mathbf{q}\cdot\mathrm{d}\mathbf{p}=\sum_{i=1}^{n}\iint_{\mathcal{S}_ {t}}\mathrm{d}q_{i}\mathrm{d}p_{i}\\ &=\sum_{i=1}^{n}\iint_{\mathcal{S}_{t}}\mathrm{d}u\mathrm{d}v \,\frac{\partial(q_{i},p_{i})}{\partial(u,v)},\end{split}\] (D.58)

and calculate \(\mathrm{d}A/\mathrm{d}t\). We set \(t^{\prime}\equiv t+\delta t\), where \(\delta t\) is small, and let \(q(u,v)\), \(q^{\prime}(u,v)\) be points on the same trajectory at times \(t\) and \(t^{\prime}\), with similar definitions for \(p\) and \(p^{\prime}\). To first order in the small interval \(\delta t\), Hamilton's equations (D.54) yield

\[(\mathbf{q}^{\prime},\mathbf{p}^{\prime})=\mathbf{H}_{\delta t}(\mathbf{q}, \mathbf{p})=\bigg{(}\mathbf{q}+\frac{\partial H}{\partial\mathbf{p}}\delta t, \,\mathbf{p}-\frac{\partial H}{\partial\mathbf{q}}\delta t\bigg{)}.\] (D.59)

Differentiating these equations with respect to \(u\) and \(v\), we find

\[\begin{split}&\frac{\partial(q_{i}^{\prime},p_{i}^{\prime})}{ \partial(u,v)}=\left|\frac{\partial q_{i}}{\partial u}+\frac{\partial^{2}H}{ \partial u\partial p_{i}}\delta t\quad\frac{\partial q_{i}}{\partial v}+\frac{ \partial^{2}H}{\partial v\partial p_{i}}\delta t\right|\\ &\frac{\partial p_{i}}{\partial u}-\frac{\partial^{2}H}{\partial u \partial q_{i}}\delta t\quad\frac{\partial p_{i}}{\partial v}-\frac{\partial^ {2}H}{\partial v\partial q_{i}}\delta t\right|\\ &=\frac{\partial(q_{i},p_{i})}{\partial(u,v)}+\bigg{(}\frac{ \partial q_{i}}{\partial v}\frac{\partial^{2}H}{\partial u\partial q_{i}}- \frac{\partial p_{i}}{\partial u}\frac{\partial^{2}H}{\partial v\partial p_{i }}+\frac{\partial p_{i}}{\partial v}\frac{\partial^{2}H}{\partial u\partial p _{i}}-\frac{\partial q_{i}}{\partial u}\frac{\partial^{2}H}{\partial v\partial q _{i}}\bigg{)}\delta t+\mathrm{O}(\delta t)^{2}.\end{split}\] (D.60)

Thus

\[\begin{split}&\frac{\mathrm{d}A}{\mathrm{d}t}=\lim_{\delta t\to 0} \frac{1}{\delta t}\iint\mathrm{d}u\mathrm{d}v\sum_{i}\bigg{[}\frac{\partial(q_{i }^{\prime},p_{i}^{\prime})}{\partial(u,v)}-\frac{\partial(q_{i},p_{i})}{ \partial(u,v)}\bigg{]}\\ &=\sum_{i}\iint\mathrm{d}u\mathrm{d}v\bigg{(}\frac{\partial q_{i }}{\partial v}\frac{\partial^{2}H}{\partial u\partial q_{i}}-\frac{\partial p_{ i}}{\partial u}\frac{\partial^{2}H}{\partial v\partial p_{i}}+\frac{\partial p_{i}}{ \partial v}\frac{\partial^{2}H}{\partial u\partial p_{i}}-\frac{\partial q_{ i}}{\partial u}\frac{\partial^{2}H}{\partial v\partial q_{i}}\bigg{)}.\end{split}\] (D.61)

One may show that the sum of the brackets in the second of equations (D.61) vanishes, by replacing every occurrence of \(\partial/\partial u\) in the second derivatives by \(\sum_{k}\Big{(}\frac{\partial q_{k}}{\partial u}\frac{\partial}{\partial q_{k} }+\frac{\partial p_{k}}{\partial u}\frac{\partial}{\partial p_{k}}\Big{)}\) and similarly for \(\partial/\partial v\). Hence \(\mathrm{d}A/\mathrm{d}t=0\) and we have:

Poincare invariant theorem_If \(\mathcal{S}(0)\) is any two-surface in phase space, and \(\mathcal{S}(t)\) is the surface into which \(\mathcal{S}(0)\) is mapped by the time-evolution operator \(\mathbf{H}_{t}\), then_

\[\iint_{\mathcal{S}(0)}\mathrm{d}\mathbf{q}\cdot\mathrm{d}\mathbf{p}=\iint_{ \mathcal{S}(t)}\mathrm{d}\mathbf{q}\cdot\mathrm{d}\mathbf{p}.\] (D.62)

This conserved quantity is known as a **Poincare invariant**.

**Corollary** If \(\gamma(0)\) is any closed path through phase space, and \(\gamma(t)\) is the path to which \(\gamma(0)\) is mapped by the time-evolution operator, then

\[\oint_{\gamma(0)}\mathrm{d}\mathbf{q}\cdot\mathbf{p}=\oint_{\gamma(t)}\mathrm{d} \mathbf{q}\cdot\mathbf{p}.\] (D.63)_Proof:_ By Green's theorem (B.61),

\[\oint_{\gamma(t)}{\rm d}{\bf q}\cdot{\bf p}=\sum_{i}\oint_{\gamma(t)}{\rm d}q_{i} \,p_{i}=\sum_{i}\iint_{{\cal S}(t)}{\rm d}q_{i}{\rm d}p_{i}=\iint_{{\cal S}(t)}{ \rm d}{\bf q}\cdot{\rm d}{\bf p},\] (D.64)

where \({\cal S}(t)\) is any surface that has \(\gamma(t)\) as its boundary. The result now follows from the Poincare invariant theorem.\(\triangleleft\)

#### D.4.3 Poisson brackets

Let \(A({\bf w})\) and \(B({\bf w})\) be any two scalar functions of the phase-space coordinates. Then the **Poisson bracket** is defined by

\[[A,B]\equiv\frac{\partial A}{\partial{\bf q}}\cdot\frac{\partial B}{\partial{ \bf p}}-\frac{\partial A}{\partial{\bf p}}\cdot\frac{\partial B}{\partial{ \bf q}}.\] (D.65)

An equivalent definition is

\[[A,B]=\sum_{\alpha,\beta=1}^{2n}J_{\alpha\beta}\frac{\partial A}{\partial w_{ \alpha}}\frac{\partial B}{\partial w_{\beta}},\] (D.66)

where the **symplectic matrix** is

\[{\bf J}\equiv\left(\begin{array}{cc}{\bf 0}&{\bf I}\\ -{\bf I}&{\bf 0}\end{array}\right),\] (D.67)

and \({\bf 0}\) and \({\bf I}\) are the \(n\times n\) zero and unit matrix. The symplectic matrix has the useful properties

\[{\bf J}^{-1}={\bf J}^{\rm T}=-{\bf J}\quad;\quad{\bf J}^{2}=-{\bf I}\quad; \quad|{\bf J}|=1,\] (D.68)

where \(|{\bf J}|\) denotes the determinant of \({\bf J}\), and the superscript "T" denotes the transpose of a matrix, \(A_{ij}^{\rm T}=A_{ji}\).

It is straightforward to verify the following properties of Poisson brackets:

1. \([A,B]=-[B,A]\) and \([A+B,C]=[A,C]+[B,C]\);
2. \([AB,C]=A[B,C]+B[A,C]\);
3. \([[A,B],C]+[[B,C],A]+[[C,A],B]=0\)  (**Jacobi identity**);
4. Hamilton's equations may be written \[\dot{w}_{\alpha}=[w_{\alpha},H]\quad\mbox{or}\quad\dot{\bf w}=[{\bf w},H] \quad\mbox{or}\quad\dot{\bf w}={\bf J}\cdot\frac{\partial H}{\partial{\bf w}};\] (D.69)
5. The phase-space coordinates \({\bf w}=({\bf q},{\bf p})\) satisfy the **canonical commutation relations**\([p_{i},p_{j}]=[q_{i},q_{j}]=0\) and \([q_{i},p_{j}]=\delta_{ij}\), or simply \[[w_{\alpha},w_{\beta}]=J_{\alpha\beta}.\] (D.70)

#### D.4.4 Canonical coordinates and transformations

The power of Lagrange's equations (D.48) is that they can describe the motion of a dynamical system in _any_ system of coordinates \({\bf q}\). It is therefore natural to ask what are the most general phase-space coordinates in which motion can be described by Hamilton's equations.

Any set of phase-space coordinates \({\bf W}\equiv\{W_{\alpha},\ \alpha=1,\ldots,2n\}\) is said to be **canonical** if

\[[W_{\alpha},W_{\beta}]=J_{\alpha\beta}.\] (D.71)Equation (D.70) shows that ordinary phase-space coordinates are canonical. If \({\bf W}\) and \({\bf w}\) are two sets of canonical coordinates, then the function relating them, \({\bf W}({\bf w})\), is a **canonical transformation** or **map**.

Let \({\bf W}\) be a set of canonical coordinates; then with equation (D.70) and the chain rule we have

\[\eqalign{[A,B]&=\sum_{\alpha,\beta=1}^{2n}J_{\alpha\beta}{\partial A\over \partial w_{\alpha}}{\partial B\over\partial w_{\beta}}=\sum_{\kappa\lambda} \bigg{(}\sum_{\alpha\beta}J_{\alpha\beta}{\partial W_{\kappa}\over\partial w _{\alpha}}{\partial W_{\lambda}\over\partial w_{\beta}}\bigg{)}{\partial A \over\partial W_{\kappa}}{\partial B\over\partial W_{\lambda}}\cr&=\sum_{ \kappa\lambda}[W_{\kappa},W_{\lambda}]{\partial A\over\partial W_{\kappa}}{ \partial B\over\partial W_{\lambda}}=\sum_{\kappa\lambda}J_{\kappa\lambda}{ \partial A\over\partial W_{\kappa}}{\partial B\over\partial W_{\lambda}}.}\]

Thus the derivatives involved in the definition (D.65) of the Poisson bracket can be taken with respect to _any_ set of canonical coordinates, just as the vector formula \({\bf\nabla}\cdot{\bf a}=\sum_{i}(\partial a_{i}/\partial x_{i})\) is valid in any Cartesian coordinate system.

The rate of change of an arbitrary canonical coordinate \(W_{\alpha}\) along an orbit is

\[\dot{W}_{\alpha}=\sum_{\beta=1}^{2n}{\partial W_{\alpha}\over\partial w_{ \beta}}\dot{w}_{\beta}=\sum_{\beta\gamma}{\partial W_{\alpha}\over\partial w_ {\beta}}J_{\beta\gamma}{\partial H\over\partial w_{\gamma}},\]

where we have used Hamilton's equations (D.69). We now write \(H\) in terms of the new coordinates \(W_{\alpha}\), so

\[\dot{W}_{\alpha}=\sum_{\beta\gamma\delta}{\partial W_{\alpha}\over\partial w_ {\beta}}J_{\beta\gamma}{\partial W_{\delta}\over\partial w_{\gamma}}{\partial H \over\partial W_{\delta}}=\sum_{\delta}[W_{\alpha},W_{\delta}]{\partial H \over\partial W_{\delta}}.\]

Since the coordinates are canonical, this simplifies to

\[\dot{W}_{\alpha}=\sum_{\delta}J_{\alpha\delta}{\partial H\over\partial W_{ \delta}},\]

which is simply Hamilton's equations (D.69) in the coordinate system \({\bf W}\). Thus _Hamilton's equations are valid in any canonical coordinate system,_ whatever the Hamiltonian may be.

The Jacobian matrix relating two coordinate systems \({\bf w}\) and \({\bf W}\) is a \(2n\times 2n\) matrix \({\bf g}({\bf w})\) defined by

\[g_{\alpha\beta}\equiv{\partial W_{\alpha}\over\partial w_{\beta}}.\]

In terms of this matrix, equation (D.66) can be written

\[[W_{\alpha},W_{\beta}]=\sum_{\gamma\delta}J_{\gamma\delta}g_{\alpha\gamma}g_{ \beta\delta}.\]

If the coordinates \({\bf W}\) are canonical, \([W_{\alpha},W_{\beta}]=J_{\alpha\beta}\), then this equation can be rewritten in matrix notation as

\[{\bf g}\cdot{\bf J}\cdot{\bf g}^{\rm T}={\bf J}.\]

Conversely, if equation (D.78) holds the coordinates \({\bf W}\) are canonical.

An equivalent condition is obtained by left-multiplying this result by \({\bf J}\) and using equation (D.68) to obtain \({\bf J}\cdot{\bf g}\cdot{\bf J}\cdot{\bf g}^{\rm T}=-{\bf I}\); then left-multiplying by \({\bf g}^{\rm T}\) to obtain \({\bf g}^{\rm T}\cdot{\bf J}\cdot{\bf g}\cdot{\bf J}\cdot{\bf g}^{\rm T}=-{\bf g }^{\rm T}\); then right-multiplying by the inverse of \({\bf g}^{\rm T}\) to obtain \({\bf g}^{\rm T}\cdot{\bf J}\cdot{\bf g}\cdot{\bf J}=-{\bf I}\); then right-multiplying by \({\bf J}\) to obtain

\[{\bf g}^{\rm T}\cdot{\bf J}\cdot{\bf g}={\bf J}.\]A matrix \({\bf g}\) that satisfies the condition (D.78) or (D.79) is said to be **symplectic**. Thus _a transformation \({\bf W}({\bf w})\) is canonical if and only if its Jacobian matrix is symplectic_. Sometimes the terms "canonical" and "symplectic" are used interchangeably.

Consider a small phase-space volume element, which may be written \({\rm d}^{2n}{\bf w}\) and \({\rm d}^{2n}{\bf W}\) in two different canonical coordinate systems. The relation between these two expressions is

\[{\rm d}^{2n}{\bf W}=\left|\left|\frac{\partial{\bf W}}{\partial{\bf w}}\right| \right|\,{\rm d}^{2n}{\bf w}=||{\bf g}||\,{\rm d}^{2n}{\bf w},\] (D.80)

where \(||{\bf g}||\) denotes the absolute value of the determinant \(|{\bf g}|\). From equation (D.78), \(|{\bf J}|=|{\bf g}|\times|{\bf J}|\times|{\bf g}^{\rm T}|=|{\bf J}|\times|{\bf g }|^{2}\), where we have used the fact that the determinant of a matrix and its transpose are identical. Since \(|{\bf J}|\) is non-zero (eq. D.68), \(|{\bf g}|=\pm 1\), so \(||{\bf g}||=1\) and

\[{\rm d}^{2n}{\bf W}={\rm d}^{2n}{\bf w};\] (D.81)

in words, _the phase-space volume element is the same in any canonical coordinates_.

Finally, we consider how the Poincare invariant is changed by an canonical transformation. In the canonical coordinates \({\bf W}\), the analog of equation (D.58) is

\[\begin{split} A^{\prime}(t)\equiv\iint_{{\cal S}_{t}}{\rm d}{ \bf Q}\cdot{\rm d}{\bf P}&=\sum_{i=1}^{n}\iint_{{\cal S}_{t}}{ \rm d}u{\rm d}v\,\frac{\partial(Q_{i},P_{i})}{\partial(u,v)}\\ &=\sum_{i=1}^{n}\iint_{{\cal S}_{t}}{\rm d}u{\rm d}v\,\left(\frac {\partial Q_{i}}{\partial u}\frac{\partial P_{i}}{\partial v}-\frac{\partial Q _{i}}{\partial v}\frac{\partial P_{i}}{\partial u}\right).\end{split}\] (D.82)

The expression in brackets can be rewritten using the symplectic matrix,

\[\begin{split} A^{\prime}(t)&=\sum_{\alpha,\beta=1}^ {2n}\iint_{{\cal S}_{t}}{\rm d}u{\rm d}v\,\frac{\partial W_{\alpha}}{\partial u }J_{\alpha\beta}\frac{\partial W_{\beta}}{\partial v}\\ &=\sum_{\alpha\beta\gamma\delta}\iint_{{\cal S}_{t}}{\rm d}u{\rm d }v\,\frac{\partial W_{\alpha}}{\partial w_{\gamma}}\frac{\partial w_{\gamma}}{ \partial u}J_{\alpha\beta}\frac{\partial W_{\beta}}{\partial w_{\delta}}\frac{ \partial w_{\delta}}{\partial v}\\ &=\sum_{\gamma\delta}\iint_{{\cal S}_{t}}{\rm d}u{\rm d}v\,\frac {\partial w_{\gamma}}{\partial u}({\bf g}^{\rm T}\cdot{\bf J}\cdot{\bf g})_{ \gamma\delta}\frac{\partial w_{\delta}}{\partial v}\\ &=\sum_{\gamma\delta}\iint_{{\cal S}_{t}}{\rm d}u{\rm d}v\,\frac {\partial w_{\gamma}}{\partial u}J_{\gamma\delta}\frac{\partial w_{\delta}}{ \partial v},\end{split}\] (D.83)

where the last line follows from equation (D.79) because \({\bf W}\) is canonical. The last line is simply the Poincare invariant \(A(t)\) in the original coordinates \({\bf w}\). We conclude that Poincare invariants are conserved in a canonical transformation. Thus, if \({\bf w}\) and \({\bf W}\) are any two sets of canonical coordinates,

\[\iint_{{\cal S}}{\rm d}{\bf q}\cdot{\rm d}{\bf p}=\iint_{{\cal S}}{\rm d}{ \bf Q}\cdot{\rm d}{\bf P}\quad;\quad\oint_{\gamma}{\rm d}{\bf q}\cdot{\bf p}= \oint_{\gamma}{\rm d}{\bf Q}\cdot{\bf P}\] (D.84)

for all surfaces \({\cal S}\) and closed curves \(\gamma\) in phase space. The converse argument shows that any transformation that conserves all integrals of the form \(\iint{\rm d}{\bf q}\cdot{\rm d}{\bf p}\) and \(\oint{\rm d}{\bf q}\cdot{\bf p}\) is canonical. Thus _a transformation is canonical if and only if it conserves the Poincare invariants of all surfaces in phase space_.

We have shown that the mapping defined by the time-evolution operator \({\bf H}_{t}\) (eq. D.55) conserves Poincare invariants; hence _the transformation defined by a Hamiltonian flow over any time interval \(t\) is canonical._ Since canonical transformations preserve the phase-space volume element, the phase-space volume element is conserved in the flow. Since any finite volume is the sum of volume elements, _the volume of any arbitrary region in phase space is conserved by a Hamiltonian flow_. This result leads immediately to the collisionless Boltzmann equation in the form (4.10).

#### D.4.5 Extended phase space

A Hamiltonian that does not depend explicitly on time is said to be **autonomous**. Although most of the Hamiltonians that we shall deal with in this book are autonomous, it is important to understand the behavior of dynamical systems governed by time-dependent or non-autonomous Hamiltonians \(H({\bf q},{\bf p},t)\). The trajectories in such systems still satisfy Hamilton's equations (D.54), but by (D.56) the energy of the system \(E(t)=H[{\bf q}(t),{\bf p}(t),t]\) is no longer conserved along a trajectory.

Let us define \(p_{0}\equiv-E\), \(q_{0}=t\), and an **extended phase space** with coordinates \(({\bf Q},{\bf P})\), where \({\bf Q}\equiv(q_{0},q_{1},\ldots,q_{n})\) and \({\bf P}\equiv(p_{0},p_{1},\ldots,p_{n})\). We introduce a new variable \(\tau\) called the **fictitious time**, which serves as the time coordinate in the extended phase space. Now consider the trajectories in the extended phase space governed by the Hamiltonian

\[{\cal H}({\bf Q},{\bf P})\equiv H({\bf q},{\bf p},t)-E=H({\bf q},{\bf p},q_{0} )+p_{0}.\] (D.85)

These are given by Hamilton's equations

\[\frac{{\rm d}Q_{i}}{{\rm d}\tau}=\frac{\partial{\cal H}}{\partial P_{i}}\quad; \quad\frac{{\rm d}P_{i}}{{\rm d}\tau}=-\frac{\partial{\cal H}}{{\rm d}Q_{i}} \qquad i=0,\ldots,n.\] (D.86)

For \(i=0\) the first of these becomes \({\rm d}t/{\rm d}\tau=1\) which implies that the fictitious time and the actual time coincide along a trajectory; and the second becomes \({\rm d}E/{\rm d}\tau=\partial H/\partial t\) which is identical to equation (D.56). For \(i=1,\ldots,n\) the equations of motion (D.86) are identical to Hamilton's equations in the original phase space. Thus _the behavior of a dynamical system governed by a time-dependent Hamiltonian with \(n\) degrees of freedom can be described by an autonomous Hamiltonian with \((n+1)\) degrees of freedom._

#### D.4.6 Generating functions

Consider two points \({\bf w}_{0}\) and \({\bf w}\) in phase space, and two distinct paths \(\gamma_{0}\) and \(\gamma_{1}\) from \({\bf w}_{0}\) to \({\bf w}_{1}\). Let \(\Gamma\) denote the closed path from \({\bf w}_{0}\) to \({\bf w}\) and back that is created by first traversing \(\gamma_{0}\) and then traversing \(\gamma_{1}\) in the reverse direction. If \(({\bf q},{\bf p})\) and \(({\bf q}^{\prime},{\bf p}^{\prime})\) are canonical coordinate systems then the conservation of Poincare invariants in canonical transformations implies that

\[\oint_{\Gamma}({\rm d}{\bf q}\cdot{\bf p}-{\rm d}{\bf q}^{\prime}\cdot{\bf p}^ {\prime})=0;\] (D.87)

splitting the path \(\Gamma\) into its components we have

\[\int_{\gamma_{0}}({\rm d}{\bf q}\cdot{\bf p}-{\rm d}{\bf q}^{\prime}\cdot{\bf p }^{\prime})=\int_{\gamma_{1}}({\rm d}{\bf q}\cdot{\bf p}-{\rm d}{\bf q}^{\prime }\cdot{\bf p}^{\prime}).\] (D.88)We conclude that the integral does not depend on the path of integration, so for a fixed initial point \({\bf w}_{0}\) we may write

\[S({\bf w})=\int_{{\bf w}_{0}}^{{\bf w}}({\rm d}{\bf q}\cdot{\bf p}-{\rm d}{\bf q }^{\prime}\cdot{\bf p}^{\prime}),\] (D.89)

or

\[{\rm d}S={\rm d}{\bf q}\cdot{\bf p}-{\rm d}{\bf q}^{\prime}\cdot{\bf p}^{\prime},\] (D.90)

where \({\rm d}S\) is an exact differential. \(S\) is called a **generating function** of the canonical transformation from \(({\bf q},{\bf p})\) to \(({\bf q}^{\prime},{\bf p}^{\prime})\).

Now let us assume that we use \(({\bf q},{\bf q}^{\prime})\) as phase-space coordinates instead of \(({\bf q},{\bf p})\). Then \({\rm d}S({\bf q},{\bf q}^{\prime})={\rm d}{\bf q}\cdot{\bf p}-{\rm d}{\bf q}^{ \prime}\cdot{\bf p}^{\prime}\), where \({\bf p}\) and \({\bf p}^{\prime}\) are regarded as functions of \({\bf q}\) and \({\bf q}^{\prime}\). Since \({\rm d}S\) is an exact differential, we must have

\[{\bf p}=\frac{\partial S({\bf q},{\bf q}^{\prime})}{\partial{\bf q}}\quad; \quad{\bf p}^{\prime}=-\frac{\partial S({\bf q},{\bf q}^{\prime})}{\partial{ \bf q}^{\prime}}.\] (D.91)

Every sufficiently smooth and non-degenerate function \(S({\bf q},{\bf q}^{\prime})\) defines a canonical transformation through these relations.

Similarly, let \(S_{2}({\bf q},{\bf p}^{\prime})\equiv{\bf q}^{\prime}\cdot{\bf p}^{\prime}+S\), where \({\bf p}\) and \({\bf q}^{\prime}\) are now regarded as functions of \({\bf q}\) and \({\bf p}^{\prime}\). Then equation (D.90) becomes

\[{\rm d}S_{2}({\bf q},{\bf p}^{\prime})={\bf q}^{\prime}\cdot{\rm d}{\bf p}^{ \prime}+{\rm d}{\bf q}^{\prime}\cdot{\bf p}^{\prime}+{\rm d}S={\bf q}^{\prime} \cdot{\rm d}{\bf p}^{\prime}+{\rm d}{\bf q}\cdot{\bf p}\] (D.92)

and

\[{\bf q}^{\prime}=\frac{\partial S_{2}({\bf q},{\bf p}^{\prime})}{\partial{\bf p }^{\prime}}\quad;\quad{\bf p}=\frac{\partial S_{2}({\bf q},{\bf p}^{\prime})}{ \partial{\bf q}}.\] (D.93)

If \(S_{3}({\bf q}^{\prime},{\bf p})\equiv{\bf q}\cdot{\bf p}-S\), where \({\bf q}\) and \({\bf p}^{\prime}\) are regarded as functions of \({\bf q}^{\prime}\) and \({\bf p}\), then

\[{\bf p}^{\prime}=\frac{\partial S_{3}({\bf q}^{\prime},{\bf p})}{\partial{\bf q }^{\prime}}\quad;\quad{\bf q}=\frac{\partial S_{3}({\bf q}^{\prime},{\bf p})}{ \partial{\bf p}}.\] (D.94)

Canonical transformations can also be defined by generating functions of the form \(S_{4}({\bf p},{\bf p}^{\prime})\). Notice that all these generating functions depend on one old and one new variable so the canonical transformation defined by a generating function is, inconveniently, always implicit.

The generating function \(S({\bf q},{\bf q}^{\prime})={\bf q}\cdot{\bf q}^{\prime}\) yields the canonical transformation \(({\bf q}^{\prime},{\bf p}^{\prime})=({\bf p},-{\bf q})\); this transformation simply exchanges position and momentum and highlights the fact that configuration space and momentum space have equal status in Hamiltonian mechanics. The generating function \(S_{2}({\bf q},{\bf p}^{\prime})={\bf q}\cdot{\bf p}^{\prime}\) yields the identity transformation \(({\bf q}^{\prime},{\bf p}^{\prime})=({\bf q},{\bf p})\).

These results can be extended to time-dependent generating functions \(S({\bf w},t)\), using the extended phase space \(({\bf Q},{\bf P})=(q_{0},{\bf q},p_{0},{\bf p})\) defined earlier. Let us define a canonical transformation in the extended phase space by the generating function

\[{\cal S}_{2}({\bf Q},{\bf P}^{\prime})=q_{0}p_{0}^{\prime}+S_{2}({\bf q},{\bf p }^{\prime},q_{0}).\] (D.95)

The analogs to equations (D.93) in the extended phase space become

\[q_{0}^{\prime}=\frac{\partial{\cal S}_{2}}{\partial p_{0}^{\prime}}=q_{0}\quad; \quad p_{0}=\frac{\partial{\cal S}_{2}}{\partial q_{0}}=p_{0}^{\prime}+\frac{ \partial S_{2}}{\partial q_{0}}\quad;\quad{\bf q}^{\prime}=\frac{\partial S_{2} }{\partial{\bf p}^{\prime}}\quad;\quad{\bf p}=\frac{\partial S_{2}}{ \partial{\bf q}}.\] (D.96)

The third and fourth equations are simply a restatement of the original canonical transformation between \({\bf w}\) and \({\bf w}^{\prime}\) as defined by equations (D.93). The Hamiltonian \({\cal H}({\bf Q},{\bf P})\) in the extended phase space (eq. D.85) is transformed to

\[{\cal H}^{\prime}({\bf Q}^{\prime},{\bf P}^{\prime})={\cal H}({\bf Q},{\bf P})= H({\bf q},{\bf p},q_{0})+p_{0}=H({\bf q},{\bf p},q_{0})+p_{0}^{\prime}+\frac{ \partial S_{2}}{\partial q_{0}},\] (D.97)where \(q_{0}\), \({\bf q}\), and \({\bf p}\) are functions of \(q_{0}^{\prime}\), \({\bf q}^{\prime}\), and \({\bf p}^{\prime}\) defined by equations (D.96). Now we transform back from the extended phase space \(({\bf Q}^{\prime},{\bf P}^{\prime})\) to ordinary phase space with coordinates \(({\bf q}^{\prime},{\bf p}^{\prime})\). The time variable is unchanged, since \(q_{0}=q_{0}^{\prime}\). However, the motion is now described by a Hamiltonian

\[H^{\prime}({\bf q}^{\prime},{\bf p}^{\prime},t)=H({\bf q},{\bf p},t)+\frac{ \partial S_{2}}{\partial q_{0}}=H({\bf q},{\bf p},t)+\frac{\partial S_{2}}{ \partial t}.\] (D.98)

Similarly, the generating function \(S_{3}({\bf q}^{\prime},{\bf p},t)\) transforms the Hamiltonian to

\[H^{\prime}({\bf q}^{\prime},{\bf p}^{\prime},t)=H({\bf q},{\bf p},t)-\frac{ \partial S_{3}}{\partial t}.\] (D.99)

### Appendix E: Delaunay variables for Kepler orbits

Angle-action variables for Kepler orbits are fundamental tools of celestial mechanics. They follow immediately from formulae in SS3.5.2 for the isochrone potential by taking the limit \(b\to 0\). From equations (3.226), the Kepler Hamiltonian and frequencies are

\[H_{\rm K}=-\frac{(GM)^{2}}{2(L+J_{r})^{2}}\quad;\quad\Omega_{r}=\Omega_{ \vartheta}=\frac{(GM)^{2}}{(L+J_{r})^{3}}\quad;\quad\Omega_{\phi}={\rm sgn}(J _{\phi})\Omega_{\vartheta}.\] (E.1)

Since \(H_{\rm K}\) depends on the actions in the combination \(L+J_{r}\), it is convenient to make this combination an action. **Delaunay variables**\((J_{a},J_{b},J_{c})\) are defined by the generating function

\[S_{\rm d}=\theta_{\phi}J_{a}+\theta_{\vartheta}(J_{b}-|J_{a}|)+\theta_{r}(J_{ c}-J_{b}).\] (E.2)

Differentiating with respect to the old angles we discover the connection between the new and old actions:

\[J_{\phi}=J_{a}\quad;\quad J_{\vartheta}=J_{b}-|J_{a}|\quad;\quad J_{r}=J_{c}-J _{b}.\] (E.3)

Thus the Delaunay actions are \((J_{a},J_{b},J_{c})=(L_{z},L,L+J_{r})\). In these variables the Kepler Hamiltonian and frequencies take on an extremely simple form:

\[H_{\rm K}=-\frac{(GM)^{2}}{2J_{c}^{2}}\quad;\quad\Omega_{a}=\Omega_{b}=0\quad ;\quad\Omega_{c}=\frac{(GM)^{2}}{J_{c}^{3}}.\] (E.4)

Since \(\Omega_{a}\) and \(\Omega_{b}\) both vanish, the Delaunay angles \(\theta_{a}\) and \(\theta_{b}\) are both integrals of motion. Thus the Kepler potential has five isolating integrals--three actions and two angles. Differentiating \(S_{\rm d}\) with respect to \(J_{a}\), etc., we find that the Delaunay angles are

\[\theta_{a}=\theta_{\phi}-{\rm sgn}(J_{a})\theta_{\vartheta}=\theta_{1}\quad; \quad\theta_{b}=\theta_{\vartheta}-\theta_{r}\quad;\quad\theta_{c}=\theta_{r}.\] (E.5)

Physically, the constancy of \(\theta_{a}\) and \(\theta_{b}\) implies that both the orientation of the orbital plane and the direction of the line of apsides are fixed, or, equivalently, that both the angular-momentum vector \({\bf L}\) and the eccentricity vector \({\bf e}\) (Box 3.2) are time-independent.1

Footnote 1: Conservation of the three components of \({\bf L}\) and the three components of \({\bf e}\) yields only five independent constraints because \({\bf L}\cdot{\bf e}=0\).

A Kepler orbit is conventionally described by its semi-major axis \(a\) and eccentricity \(e\), which were defined in SS3.1b, and by three angular orbital elements, \(i\)\(\Omega\) and \(\omega\), as shown in Figure E.1. In the limit \(b\to 0\) the parameter \(c\) of equations (3.240) becomes \(-GM/(2H)\), so by equation (3.32) it is equal to \(a\). The variable \(e\) in equation (3.240) becomes \((1-L^{2}/GMa)^{1/2}\) so by (3.25b) it is equal to the Kepler eccentricity having the same symbol. With equations (3.230) we have that \(a\), \(e\), and \(i\) are given by the Delaunay actions:

\[a=\frac{J_{c}^{2}}{GM}\quad;\quad e=\sqrt{1-\frac{J_{b}^{2}}{J_{c}^{2}}}\quad; \quad i=\cos^{-1}(J_{a}/J_{b}).\] (E.6)

The inverse relations are

\[J_{a}=J_{b}\cos i\quad;\quad J_{b}=J_{c}\sqrt{1-e^{2}}\quad;\quad J_{c}=\sqrt{ GMa}.\] (E.7)

In the limit \(b\to 0\), equations (3.240) reduce to equation (3.28) between \(r\) and \(\eta\), so the latter is the eccentric anomaly. By the first of equations (3.241), the angle \(\theta_{c}=\theta_{3}\) is simply \(2\pi t/T_{r}\), where \(T_{r}=2\pi/\Omega_{c}\) is the radial period and \(t\) is the time elapsed since the last pericenter passage (eq. 3.28b). In celestial mechanics this angle is known as the mean anomaly and is usually denoted by \(\ell\). The second of equations (3.241) shows that

\[\theta_{2}=\psi+\theta_{3}-2\tan^{-1}\bigg{(}\sqrt{\frac{1+e}{1-e}}\tan( \tfrac{1}{2}\eta)\bigg{)}.\] (E.8)

Equation (3.29) shows that the last term is simply \(\psi_{0}-\psi\), where \(\psi_{0}\) is the angle measured in the orbit plane from the ascending node to the pericenter; this angle is the **argument of pericenter** and is usually denoted by \(\omega\) (Figure E.1). Thus for a Kepler potential

\[\theta_{2}=\omega+\theta_{3}=\omega+\ell.\] (E.9)

The analogous relations for the other angle-action variables are given in Table E.1.

### Basic equations

