### Fluid mechanics

The basic principles of fluid mechanics play an important role in galaxy dynamics, both because fluid and stellar systems behave in similar ways and because gas dynamics is central to the formation of galaxies. We review here some of the concepts of fluid mechanics that are used in the book. For further reading see Landau & Lifshitz (2000).

### Basic equations

The state of a fluid is specified by its density \(\rho(\mathbf{x},t)\), pressure \(p(\mathbf{x},t)\), and velocity field \(\mathbf{v}(\mathbf{x},t)\), and possibly by other thermodynamic functions such as the temperature \(T(\mathbf{x},t)\) and **specific entropy** or entropy per unit mass \(s(\mathbf{x},t)\).

#### F.1.1 Continuity equation

Consider an arbitrary closed volume \(V\) that is fixed in space and bounded by a surface \(S\). The mass of fluid in this volume is \(M(t)=\int_{V}\mathrm{d}^{3}\mathbf{x}\,\rho(\mathbf{x},t)\), and \(M(t)\) changeswith time at a rate \({\rm d}M/{\rm d}t=\int_{V}{\rm d}^{3}{\bf x}\,\partial\rho/\partial t\). The mass flowing out through the surface area element \({\rm d}^{2}S\) per unit time is \(\rho{\bf v}\cdot{\rm d}^{2}{\bf S}\), where \({\rm d}^{2}{\bf S}\) is an outward-pointing vector, normal to the surface, with magnitude \({\rm d}^{2}S\). Thus \({\rm d}M/{\rm d}t=-\oint_{S}{\rm d}^{2}{\bf S}\cdot(\rho{\bf v})\) and hence

\[\int_{V}{\rm d}^{3}{\bf x}\,\frac{\partial\rho}{\partial t}+\oint_{S}{\rm d}^{ 2}{\bf S}\cdot(\rho{\bf v})=0.\] (F.1)

Using the divergence theorem (B.43),

\[\int_{V}{\rm d}^{3}{\bf x}\left[\frac{\partial\rho}{\partial t}+{\mathbf{\nabla}} \cdot(\rho{\bf v})\right]=0;\] (F.2)

since this result must hold for any volume, we arrive at the **continuity equation**

\[\frac{\partial\rho}{\partial t}+{\mathbf{\nabla}}\cdot(\rho{\bf v})=0.\] (F.3)

In Cartesian coordinates this can be written

\[\frac{\partial\rho}{\partial t}+\frac{\partial}{\partial x_{j}}(\rho v_{j})=0,\] (F.4)

where we have used the summation convention (page 772).

In perturbation theory, we sometimes need to know the change in density resulting from a small displacement of the fluid. Let the fluid element at position \({\bf x}\) be displaced to \({\bf x}+\epsilon{\mathbf{\xi}}({\bf x})\), where \(\epsilon\) is sufficiently small. If the displacement occurs at \(t=t_{0}\), we may write \({\bf v}({\bf x},t)={\mathbf{\xi}}({\bf x})\delta(t-t_{0})\), where \(\delta\) denotes the delta function (Appendix C.1). If we now integrate equation (F.3) in time from just before to just after \(t_{0}\), we have

\[\rho_{1}=-{\mathbf{\nabla}}\cdot(\rho_{0}{\mathbf{\xi}}),\] (F.5)

where \(\epsilon\rho_{1}({\bf x})\) is the change in density at \({\bf x}\). We have replaced the density by its unperturbed value in the divergence, since it is multiplied by the small quantity \({\mathbf{\xi}}\). For more details see, for example, Chandrasekhar (1969).

#### F.1.2 Euler's equation

In a fluid that has no viscosity, the total pressure force acting on a volume is \(-\oint_{S}{\rm d}^{2}{\bf S}\,p\). In addition, there may be some external force, in particular from a gravitational potential \(\Phi({\bf x},t)\). Thus Newton's second law reads

\[M\frac{{\rm d}{\bf v}}{{\rm d}t}=-\oint_{S}{\rm d}^{2}{\bf S}\,p-M{\mathbf{\nabla} }\Phi.\] (F.6)

According to the divergence theorem in the form (B.46), \(\oint_{S}{\rm d}^{2}{\bf S}\,p=\int_{V}{\rm d}^{3}{\bf x}\,{\mathbf{\nabla}}p\), and since equation (F.6) must hold for any volume \(V\),

\[\rho\frac{{\rm d}{\bf v}}{{\rm d}t}=-{\mathbf{\nabla}}p-\rho{\mathbf{\nabla}}\Phi.\] (F.7)

The quantity \({\rm d}{\bf v}/{\rm d}t\) in equation (F.7) is the acceleration of the fluid element at \({\bf x}\). This is not necessarily the same as \(\partial{\bf v}/\partial t\), the rate of change of velocity at the point \({\bf x}\), since different fluid elements occupy this point at different times: for example, in a waterfall \(\partial{\bf v}/\partial t=0\) but \({\rm d}{\bf v}/{\rm d}t={\bf g}\), the acceleration due to the Earth's gravity. More generally, if \(w({\bf x},t)\) is any intrinsic property of the fluid--pressure, density, velocity, etc.--we define \({\rm d}w/{\rm d}t\) to be the rate of change of \(w\) as seen by an observer traveling with the fluid. The quantity \({\rm d}w/{\rm d}t\) is sometimes referred to as the **Lagrangian** or **convective derivative** of, to distinguish it from the **Eulerian derivative**. The relation between these two derivatives is straightforward to derive. The change during the interval is the sum of the change at a given point in space,, and the difference in between two points separated by at the same instant. The latter change is. Thus

