#### F.1.3 Energy equation

A fluid carries kinetic energy, internal energy, and gravitational potential energy. We ignore other energy forms, such as magnetic fields (Kulsrud 2005).

The kinetic energy per unit volume is \(K({\bf x},t)={1\over 2}\rho v^{2}\). Using the continuity equation (F.3), Euler's equation (F.10), and the vector identity \({\bf v}\cdot[({\bf v}\cdot{\bf\nabla}){\bf v}]=v_{i}v_{j}(\partial v_{i}/ \partial x_{j})={1\over 2}{\bf v}\cdot{\bf\nabla}v^{2}\), the rate of change of kinetic energy per unit volume can be written

\[\eqalign{{\partial K\over\partial t}&={{1\over 2}}{\partial\rho \over\partial t}v^{2}+\rho{\bf v}\cdot{\partial{\bf v}\over\partial t}\cr&=-{ {1\over 2}}v^{2}{\bf\nabla}\cdot(\rho{\bf v})-{{1\over 2}} \rho{\bf v}\cdot{\bf\nabla}v^{2}-{\bf v}\cdot{\bf\nabla}p-\rho{\bf v}\cdot{ \bf\nabla}\Phi\cr&=-{{1\over 2}}{\bf\nabla}\cdot(\rho{\bf v}v^{2})-{\bf v}\cdot{\bf \nabla}p-\rho{\bf v}\cdot{\bf\nabla}\Phi.\cr}\]

The internal energy per unit volume is \(U({\bf x},t)=\rho u\), where \(u\) is the specific internal energy (internal energy per unit mass), which is determined by the equation of state of the fluid. The rate of change of internal energy per unit volume is

\[{\partial U\over\partial t}={\partial\rho\over\partial t}u+\rho{\partial u \over\partial t}=-u{\bf\nabla}\cdot(\rho{\bf v})+\rho{\partial u\over\partial t}.\]

The potential energy per unit volume due to an external gravitational field is \(W_{\rm e}=\rho\Phi_{\rm e}\), and the energy due to the self-gravity of the fluid is \(W_{\rm s}={1\over 2}\rho\Phi_{\rm s}\), where \(\nabla^{2}\Phi_{\rm s}=4\pi G\rho\) (eq. 2.18). Setting \(W\equiv W_{\rm e}+W_{\rm s}\) and \(\Phi\equiv\Phi_{\rm e}+\Phi_{\rm s}\), we have

\[\eqalign{{\partial W\over\partial t}&={\partial\over\partial t}\rho(\Phi_{\rm e }+{{1\over 2}}\Phi_{\rm s})={\partial\rho\over\partial t}(\Phi_{\rm e}+{ {1\over 2}}\Phi_{\rm s})+\rho{\partial(\Phi_{\rm e}+{{1\over 2}}\Phi_{\rm s}) \over\partial t}\cr&=-(\Phi_{\rm e}+{{1\over 2}}\Phi_{\rm s}){\bf\nabla}\cdot(\rho{\bf v})+\rho{ \partial(\Phi_{\rm e}+{{1\over 2}}\Phi_{\rm s})\over\partial t}.\cr}\]

The total rate of change of energy is

\[\eqalign{{\partial\over\partial t}(K+U+W)&=-{\bf\nabla}\cdot[\rho{\bf v}({ {1\over 2}}v^{2}+u+\Phi)]-{\bf v}\cdot{\bf\nabla}p+\rho{\bf v}\cdot{\bf\nabla}u\cr& +\rho{\partial u\over\partial t}+{{1\over 2}}\Phi_{\rm s}{\bf\nabla}\cdot(\rho{\bf v})+ \rho{\partial(\Phi_{\rm e}+{{1\over 2}}\Phi_{\rm s})\over\partial t}\cr&=-{\bf\nabla} \cdot[\rho{\bf v}({{1\over 2}}v^{2}+u+\Phi)]-{\bf v}\cdot{\bf\nabla}p+\rho{ \partial u\over\partial t}+{{1\over 2}}\Phi_{\rm s}{\bf\nabla}\cdot(\rho{\bf v})+ \rho{\partial(\Phi_{\rm e}+{{1\over 2}}\Phi_{\rm s})\over\partial t},\cr}\]

where we have used equation (F.8) to replace the Eulerian derivative of \(u\) by the convective derivative.

To proceed further, we use the second law of thermodynamics, which states that the specific entropy of a fluid element changes if heat flows into or out of it, or if heat is generated within the element by viscous dissipation, nuclear reactions, or other mechanisms. Let \({\bf q}\) be the heat flux, so the rate of heat flow out of a volume is \(\oint_{S}{\rm d}^{2}{\bf S}\cdot{\bf q}\), which is equal to \(\int_{V}{\rm d}^{3}{\bf x}\,{\bf\nabla}\cdot{\bf q}\) by the divergence theorem, equation (B.43). Let \(\epsilon\) be the rate of internal energy production per unit mass. Then according to the second law, the specific entropy changes at a rate given by

\[\rho T{{\rm d}s\over{\rm d}t}=-{\bf\nabla}\cdot{\bf q}+\rho\epsilon.\]The specific entropy is related to the volume per unit mass, the internal energy, and the specific enthalpy by by

