\[v_{\rm g}\equiv\left({{\rm d}\omega(k)\over{\rm d}k}\right)_{k_{0}}.\] (F.72)

When we write \(k=k_{0}+u\), equation (F.71) becomes

\[f(x,t)\simeq{\rm e}^{{\rm i}[k_{0}x-\omega(k_{0})t]}\int_{-\infty}^{\infty}{{ \rm d}u\over 2\pi}F(k_{0}+u){\rm e}^{{\rm i}u(x-v_{\rm g}t)}.\] (F.73)

We can rewrite this expression as

\[f(x,t)\simeq{\rm e}^{{\rm i}[k_{0}x-\omega(k_{0})t]}A(x-v_{\rm g}t),\] (F.74)

where

\[A(x)\equiv\int_{-\infty}^{\infty}{{\rm d}u\over 2\pi}F(k_{0}+u){\rm e}^{{\rm i }ux}.\] (F.75)

Because \(F(k)\) is non-zero only for \(k\) near \(k_{0}\), the integrand in this equation is non-zero only for \(|u|\ll k_{0}\). Thus \(A(x)\) varies much more slowly than \(\exp\{{\rm i}[k_{0}x-\omega(k_{0})t]\}\), and we can picture the wave at any instant as having a rapidly varying phase \(\propto\exp({\rm i}k_{0}x)\) modulated by a slowly varying amplitude \(|A(x-v_{\rm g}t)|\) (the dashed line in Figure F.1b). This argument shows that the envelope of the wave propagates with a velocity \(v_{\rm g}\), which is known as the **group velocity**. The group velocity represents the velocity of any true physical disturbance, since a disturbance arising from any physical source is always localized.

The group velocity is distinct from the **phase velocity**\(v_{\rm p}\), the velocity of a given crest of the wave. On a crest \(kx-\omega t=\)_constant_; thus

\[v_{\rm p}={\omega\over k}.\] (F.76)

The phase velocity and group velocity are equal in a non-dispersive medium, for then \(\omega(k)\) is linear in \(k\). It can be shown that the energy of a dispersive wave propagates at the group velocity (Whitham 1974). For the sound waves investigated in Appendix F.3.1, \(v_{\rm p}=v_{\rm g}=v_{\rm s}\), the sound speed.

### _Appendix G:_ Discrete Fourier transforms

The discrete Fourier transform of a set of \(K\) numbers, \(\{x_{k}\}\) (\(k=0,\ldots,K-1\)), is

\[\hat{x}_{m}\equiv\sum_{k=0}^{K-1}x_{k}{\rm e}^{-2\pi{\rm i}km/K}\ \ \ \ (m=0,\ldots,K-1).\] (G.1a)

We have:

**Discrete Fourier transform theorem**_If the \(K\) numbers \(\hat{x}_{m}\) are defined by equation (G.1a), then_

\[x_{k^{\prime}}={1\over K}\sum_{m=0}^{K-1}\hat{x}_{m}{\rm e}^{2\pi{\rm i}k^{ \prime}m/K}.\] (G.1b)

_Proof_: We multiply both sides of equation (G.1a) by \((1/K){\rm e}^{2\pi{\rm i}k^{\prime}m/K}\) and sum over \(m\), to obtain

\[{1\over K}\!\sum_{m=0}^{K-1}\hat{x}_{m}{\rm e}^{2\pi{\rm i}k^{\prime}m/K}={1 \over K}\!\sum_{m=0}^{K-1}\sum_{k=0}^{K-1}x_{k}{\rm e}^{2\pi{\rm i}(k^{\prime}- k)m/K}={1\over K}\!\sum_{k=0}^{K-1}x_{k}\!\sum_{m=0}^{K-1}{\rm e}^{2\pi{ \rm i}(k^{\prime}-k)m/K}.\] (G.2)The inner sum on the extreme right of equation (G.2) is a geometric series, with sum \(K\) if \(k^{\prime}=k\), or (since \(k\) and \(k^{\prime}\) are integers and \(|k^{\prime}-k|<K\))

\[{1-{\rm e}^{2\pi{\rm i}(k^{\prime}-k)}\over 1-{\rm e}^{2\pi{\rm i}(k^{\prime}-k)/ K}}=0\ \ {\rm if}\ \ k^{\prime}\neq k.\] (G.3)

Thus the only contributing term in the outer sum on the right of equation (G.3) is that for which \(k=k^{\prime}\).\(\triangleleft\)

In 1965 Cooley and Tukey published an algorithm, known as the **fast Fourier transform**, by which the discrete transform \(\{\hat{x}_{m}\}\) of \(K\) numbers \(x_{k}\) can be evaluated in only \(\sim K\ln(K)\) multiplications and additions.1 For large \(K\) this is far fewer operations than the \(K^{2}\) operations required by a direct evaluation of the sums of equation (G.1a) (Press et al. 1986).

Footnote 1: The Cooley–Tukey algorithm requires that \(K=p^{m}\), where \(m\) is an integer and \(p\) is a prime number; usually \(p=2\).

Discrete Fourier transforms share many of the properties of continuous Fourier transforms. To demonstrate these properties, it is necessary to define the quantities \(x_{k}\) for \(k\) outside the range \((0,K-1)\) by the rule

\[x_{k}=x_{k+mK}\qquad{\rm for\ all\ integer\ }m,\] (G.4)

i.e., to assume that \(x_{k}\) is periodic with period \(K\). Note that \(\hat{x}_{m}\) is already periodic with period \(K\) (see eq. G.1a). We may now prove:

Discrete Fourier convolution theorem_If the three sets of \(K\) numbers \(\{x_{k}\}\), \(\{y_{k}\}\), \(\{z_{k}\}\) satisfy (G.4) and are related by_

\[z_{k}=\sum_{k^{\prime}=0}^{K-1}y_{k-k^{\prime}}x_{k^{\prime}},\quad{\rm then} \quad\hat{z}_{m}=\hat{y}_{m}\hat{x}_{m}.\] (G.5)

_Proof:_ We take the discrete Fourier transform of both sides of equation (G.5) and then rearrange the resulting double sum. We have

\[\hat{z}_{m}=\sum_{k=0}^{K-1}{\rm e}^{-2\pi{\rm i}km/K}\sum_{k^{\prime}=0}^{K-1 }y_{k-k^{\prime}}x_{k^{\prime}}=\sum_{k^{\prime}=0}^{K-1}x_{k^{\prime}}{\rm e }^{-2\pi{\rm i}k^{\prime}m/K}\sum_{k=0}^{K-1}y_{k-k^{\prime}}{\rm e}^{-2\pi{ \rm i}(k-k^{\prime})m/K}.\] (G.6)

If we now define \(k^{\prime\prime}\equiv k-k^{\prime}\), the inner sum in equation (G.6) becomes \(\hat{y}_{m}\). This is independent of \(k^{\prime}\), so it may be taken out of the outer sum, which then yields \(\hat{x}_{m}\).\(\triangleleft\)

Application to James's Fourier potential solverGiven \(K-1\) real numbers \(x_{k}\)\((k=1,\ldots,K-1)\) we define the sine transform of the set to be the numbers

\[x^{\alpha}(S)\equiv\sum_{k=1}^{K-1}x_{k}\sin\Big{(}{\pi\alpha k\over K}\Big{)} \quad(j=1,\ldots,K-1).\] (G.7a) Multiplying both sides of this equation by \[\sin(\pi\alpha j/K)\] and summing over \[\alpha\] we find that the inverse transformation is \[x_{j}={2\over K}\sum_{\alpha=1}^{K-1}x^{\alpha}(S)\sin\Big{(}{\pi\alpha j\over K }\Big{)}.\] (G.7b)Analogously we define the cosine transform of a set of \(K\) numbers \(x_{k}\) (\(k=0,\dots,K-1\)) to be2

Footnote 2: The connection between discrete complex Fourier transforms and discrete sine and cosine transforms is established by: (i) taking the period of the data \(x_{k}\) to be \(2K\); (ii) breaking the sum \(\hat{x}_{m}=\sum_{k=0}^{2K-1}x_{k}{\tenrm e}^{-\pi{\tenrm i}km/K}\) into a sum from \(0\) to \(K-1\) and another from \(K\) to \(2K-1\) and then using the periodicity of the \(x_{k}\) to express the latter as a sum from \(-K\) to \(-1\); (iii) writing \(x_{k}=x_{k}^{+}+x_{k}^{-}\), where \(x_{k}^{\pm}\equiv{1\over 2}(x_{k}\pm x_{-k})\), and then combining terms associated with \(k\) and \(-k\) to produce

\[\eqalign{x^{\alpha}(C)\equiv\sum_{k=0}^{K-1}x_{k}{\tenrm e}^{-\pi{\tenrm i} km/K}&\cr}\]

There is no elegant formula for recovering the \(x_{k}\) from the \(x^{\alpha}(C)\), but the matrix that is the inverse of \(A_{\alpha k}\equiv\cos(\pi\alpha k/K)\) can be calculated numerically if required.

Sine transforms help us to convolve values of the density on a grid with a softening kernel (SS2.9). For simplicity we consider the two-dimensional case, but the results generalize straightforwardly to three dimensions. If the density at grid point \((i,j)\) is \(\rho_{ij}\), the potential values are

\[\Phi_{ij}=\sum_{kl}S_{i-k,j-l}\,\rho_{kl},\]

where \(S\) is the softening kernel. Since this is an even function of its indices, i.e., \(S_{ij}=S_{-i,j}\) etc., and it will be evaluated only for \(|i-j|\leq K\), it can be written as a sum of cosines:

\[S_{ij}=\sum_{\alpha\beta}S_{\rm C}^{\alpha\beta}\cos\Big{(}{\pi\alpha i\over K }\Big{)}\cos\Big{(}{\pi\beta j\over K}\Big{)}.\]

Using this expression to eliminate \(S_{i-k,j-l}\) from (G.9), we have

\[\eqalign{\Phi_{ij}&=\sum_{kl}\sum_{\alpha\beta}S_{\rm C}^{\alpha\beta}\cos \Big{(}{\pi\alpha(i-k)\over K}\Big{)}\cos\Big{(}{\pi\beta(j-l)\over K}\Big{)} \rho_{kl}\cr&=\sum_{kl}\sum_{\alpha\beta}S_{\rm C}^{\alpha\beta}\Big{[}\cos \Big{(}{\pi\alpha i\over K}\Big{)}\cos\Big{(}{\pi\alpha k\over K}\Big{)}+\sin \Big{(}{\pi\alpha i\over K}\Big{)}\sin\Big{(}{\pi\alpha k\over K}\Big{)} \Big{]}\cr&\qquad\times\Big{[}\cos\Big{(}{\pi\beta j\over K}\Big{)}\cos \Big{(}{\pi\beta l\over K}\Big{)}+\sin\Big{(}{\pi\beta j\over K}\Big{)}\sin \Big{(}{\pi\beta l\over K}\Big{)}\Big{]}\rho_{kl},}\]

where we have twice used \(\cos(A-B)=\cos A\cos B+\sin A\sin B\). When we multiply out the two big square brackets, we get a sum of four terms of the type

\[\eqalign{\sum_{\alpha\beta}S_{\rm C}^{\alpha\beta}\sum_{kl}\cos\Big{(}{\pi \alpha i\over K}\Big{)}\cos\Big{(}{\pi\alpha k\over K}\Big{)}&\sin\Big{(}{ \pi\beta j\over K}\Big{)}\sin\Big{(}{\pi\beta l\over K}\Big{)}\rho_{kl}\cr& =\sum_{\alpha\beta}S_{\rm C}^{\alpha\beta}\cos\Big{(}{\pi\alpha i\over K} \Big{)}\sin\Big{(}{\pi\beta j\over K}\Big{)}\rho^{\alpha\beta}({\tenrm CS}),}\]where

\[\rho^{\alpha\beta}(\text{CS})\equiv\sum_{kl}\cos\Big{(}\frac{\pi\alpha k}{K} \Big{)}\sin\Big{(}\frac{\pi\beta l}{K}\Big{)}\rho_{kl}\] (G.13)

is \(\rho_{kl}\) with its first index cosine transformed and its second index sine transformed. When these and the analogous results are inserted into (G.11), we find

\[\begin{split}\Phi_{ij}&=\sum_{\alpha\beta}S_{\text{ C}}^{\alpha\beta}\bigg{[}\rho^{\alpha\beta}(\text{CC})\cos\Big{(}\frac{\pi \alpha i}{K}\Big{)}\cos\Big{(}\frac{\pi\beta j}{K}\Big{)}+\rho^{\alpha\beta}( \text{CS})\cos\Big{(}\frac{\pi\alpha i}{K}\Big{)}\sin\Big{(}\frac{\pi\beta j}{K }\Big{)}\\ &\quad+\rho^{\alpha\beta}(\text{SC})\sin\Big{(}\frac{\pi\alpha i} {K}\Big{)}\cos\Big{(}\frac{\pi\beta j}{K}\Big{)}+\rho^{\alpha\beta}(\text{SS} )\sin\Big{(}\frac{\pi\alpha i}{K}\Big{)}\sin\Big{(}\frac{\pi\beta j}{K}\Big{)} \bigg{]}.\end{split}\] (G.14)

Thus, \(\Phi_{ij}\) is the sum of four contributions, one from each of the four sine or cosine transforms of \(\rho_{ij}\). The generalization to three dimensions is clear: in three dimensions there are eight possible transforms, \(\rho^{\alpha\beta\gamma}(\text{CSS})\) etc., and each one will give rise to a contribution to \(\Phi_{ijk}\). The necessity of calculating eight sine/cosine transforms of \(\rho\) in order to use the convolution theorem to calculate the potential corresponds to the doubling of the range of each index mentioned below equation (2.237) in the context of complex DFTs.

Box 2.5 describes a technique for obtaining the gravitational potential of a general mass distribution without convolving the softening kernel with the full density distribution. Instead a potential is calculated from the triple sine transform of \(\rho\), and a correction to this potential made by convolving the softening kernel with a distribution of masses that is confined to the walls of the box. Here we explain how the hollowness of the mass distribution dramatically simplifies the convolution.

We break the boundary points of the grid into six isolated pieces, being the top and bottom, front and back, etc., of the grid's bounding box. We ensure that each of these bounding planes is a complete \((K+1)\times(K+1)\) plane of grid points by duplicating nodes of the grid that lie along edges of the grid, and splitting into three copies the nodes that lie at the corners of the grid. We divide any mass assigned to these replicated nodes in equal parts to the copies. Now the triple cosine transform of a density distribution that is confined to the top and bottom planes, \(k=0\) and \(k=K\), is

\[\begin{split}\rho^{\alpha\beta\gamma}_{\text{top/bot}}(\text{CCC })&=\sum_{ij}\big{(}\rho_{ij0}+(-1)^{\gamma}\rho_{ijK}\big{)}\cos \Big{(}\frac{\pi\alpha i}{K}\Big{)}\cos\Big{(}\frac{\pi\beta j}{K}\Big{)},\\ &\equiv\rho^{\alpha\beta\cdot}_{0}(\text{CC})+(-1)^{\gamma}\rho^{ \alpha\beta\cdot}_{K}(\text{CC}),\end{split}\] (G.15)

where the second line introduces a convenient notation for keeping track of the objects generated. The key point is that the right side of equation (G.15) is just a two-dimensional cosine transform, which is inexpensive to calculate. The other two pairs of bounding planes generate similar contributions to \(\rho^{\alpha\beta\gamma}(\text{CCC})\), so for a hollow mass distribution \(\rho^{\alpha\beta\gamma}(\text{CCC})\) may be expressed as a sum of two-dimensional cosine transforms. The other transforms required, such as \(\rho^{\alpha\beta\gamma}(\text{CCS})\), \(\rho^{\alpha\beta\gamma}(\text{CSS})\), and \(\rho^{\alpha\beta\gamma}(\text{SSS})\) are even easier to evaluate because, for example, \(\sum_{k}\rho_{ijk}\sin(\pi\gamma k/K)\) vanishes for a mass distribution that is confined to the top and bottom bounding surfaces.

_Appendix H:_ **The Antonov-Lebovitz theorem**

This theorem states that all non-radial modes of a barotropic star with \({\rm d}p(\rho)/{\rm d}\rho>0\) are stable. The original proofs are by Antonov (1962b) and Lebovitz (1965), but here we follow Aly & Perez (1992).

To prove the theorem, we must show that \(W[\rho_{1}]\) in equation (5.120) is never negative. We begin by rewriting the second term using Poisson's equation (5.115), so

\[W[\rho_{1}]=\int{\rm d}^{3}{\bf x}\left|\frac{{\rm d}\Phi}{{\rm d}\rho}\right| _{0}\rho_{1}^{2}({\bf x})+\int{\rm d}^{3}{\bf x}\,\rho_{1}({\bf x})\Phi_{1}({ \bf x}).\] (H.1)

Now we apply Schwarz's inequality (B.75), setting \(A=|{\rm d}\Phi/{\rm d}\rho|_{0}^{1/2}\rho_{1}\) and \(B=\Phi_{1}/|{\rm d}\Phi/{\rm d}\rho|_{0}^{1/2}\). We have

\[W[\rho_{1}]=\int{\rm d}^{3}{\bf x}(A^{2}+AB)\geq\frac{X^{2}}{Y}+X=\frac{X}{Y} (X+Y),\] (H.2)

where

\[X\equiv\int{\rm d}^{3}{\bf x}\,AB=\int{\rm d}^{3}{\bf x}\,\rho_{1}\Phi_{1} \quad;\quad Y\equiv\int{\rm d}^{3}{\bf x}\,B^{2}=\int{\rm d}^{3}{\bf x}\, \left|\frac{{\rm d}\rho}{{\rm d}\Phi}\right|_{0}\Phi_{1}^{2}.\] (H.3)

As shown in Box 5.2, any non-radial mode can be chosen to be the real part of a function having angular dependence proportional to \({\rm Y}_{l}^{m}(\theta,\phi)\) with \(l\geq 1\). Thus if \(\Phi_{1}({\bf x})\) is the potential of a mode, we may write

\[\Phi_{1}({\bf x})={\rm Re}[\Phi_{\rm c}({\bf x})],\quad{\rm where}\quad\Phi_{ \rm c}({\bf x})\equiv\frac{{\rm d}\Phi_{0}}{{\rm d}r}s_{lm}(r){\rm Y}_{l}^{m}( \theta,\phi);\] (H.4)

the common factor \({\rm d}\Phi_{0}/{\rm d}r\) has been introduced to simplify later equations. Using equation (C.49), Poisson's equation can be written

\[\rho_{1}({\bf x})={\rm Re}[\rho_{\rm c}({\bf x})]\] (H.5a) where \[\rho_{\rm c}({\bf x})=\frac{1}{4\pi G}\nabla^{2}\Phi_{\rm c}=\frac{1}{4\pi G }\,{\rm Y}_{l}^{m}(\theta,\phi)\left(\frac{1}{r^{2}}\frac{{\rm d}}{{\rm d}r} r^{2}\frac{{\rm d}}{{\rm d}r}s_{lm}\frac{{\rm d}\Phi_{0}}{{\rm d}r}-\frac{l(l+1)}{r^{2 }}s_{lm}\frac{{\rm d}\Phi_{0}}{{\rm d}r}\right).\] (H.5b)

Then \(X=\int{\rm d}^{3}{\bf x}\,\rho_{1}\Phi_{1}=\frac{1}{4}\int r^{2}{\rm d}r\,{\rm d }^{2}\Omega(\rho_{\rm c}\Phi_{\rm c}+\rho_{\rm c}\Phi_{\rm c}^{*})+{\rm CC}\), where "CC" denotes the complex conjugate of the preceding terms in a sum. Using the orthonormality of the spherical harmonics (eq. C.44), we have

\[X=\frac{1}{16\pi G}\int_{0}^{\infty}{\rm d}r\,\frac{{\rm d}\Phi_{0}}{{\rm d}r }s_{lm}^{*}\left(\frac{{\rm d}}{{\rm d}r}r^{2}\frac{{\rm d}}{{\rm d}r}s_{lm} \frac{d\Phi_{0}}{{\rm d}r}-l(l+1)s_{lm}\frac{d\Phi_{0}}{{\rm d}r}\right)+{\rm CC}.\] (H.6)

We now carry out the derivatives in the first term in square brackets, and integrate the term involving \({\rm d}^{2}s_{lm}/{\rm d}r^{2}\) by parts. The boundary terms can be shown to vanish (\(\Phi_{1}\) decays at least as fast as \(r^{-2}\) as \(r\to\infty\) because mass conservation dictates that there cannot be any monopole component of \(\Phi_{1}\); thus \(s_{lm}\) is constant or decaying as \(r\to\infty\)). We simplify the result using Poisson's equation in the form

\[\frac{d^{2}\Phi_{0}}{{\rm d}r^{2}}+\frac{2}{r}\frac{d\Phi_{0}}{{\rm d}r}=4\pi G \rho_{0},\] (H.7)which can be differentiated to eliminate the term involving \(\mathrm{d}^{3}\Phi_{0}/\mathrm{d}r^{3}\). After some algebra we find

\[\begin{split} X=&\tfrac{1}{2}\int_{0}^{\infty} \mathrm{d}r\,r^{2}\frac{d\rho_{0}}{\mathrm{d}r}\frac{\mathrm{d}\Phi_{0}}{ \mathrm{d}r}|s_{lm}|^{2}\\ &-\frac{1}{8\pi G}\int_{0}^{\infty}\mathrm{d}r\left(\frac{d\Phi_{ 0}}{\mathrm{d}r}\right)^{2}\left(\left|r\frac{ds_{lm}}{\mathrm{d}r}\right|^{2} +(l^{2}+l-2)|s_{lm}|^{2}\right).\end{split}\] (H.8)

Similarly, we use equation (H.4) and the orthonormality of the spherical harmonics to evaluate \(Y\) in terms of \(s_{lm}(r)\),

\[Y=\tfrac{1}{2}\int_{0}^{\infty}\mathrm{d}r\,r^{2}\left|\frac{\mathrm{d}\rho}{ \mathrm{d}\Phi}\right|_{0}\left(\frac{\mathrm{d}\Phi_{0}}{\mathrm{d}r}\right) ^{2}|s_{lm}|^{2}=-\tfrac{1}{2}\int_{0}^{\infty}\mathrm{d}r\,r^{2}\frac{ \mathrm{d}\rho_{0}}{\mathrm{d}r}\frac{\mathrm{d}\Phi_{0}}{\mathrm{d}r}|s_{lm} |^{2},\] (H.9)

where we have assumed that \((\mathrm{d}\rho/\mathrm{d}\Phi)_{0}<0\) since \(\mathrm{d}p/\mathrm{d}\rho>0\) by assumption and \((\mathrm{d}p/\mathrm{d}\Phi)_{0}=-\rho_{0}<0\) by hydrostatic equilibrium (F.12). Combining equations (H.8) and (H.9), we have

\[X+Y=-\frac{1}{8\pi G}\int_{0}^{\infty}\mathrm{d}r\left(\frac{d\Phi_{0}}{ \mathrm{d}r}\right)^{2}\left(\left|r\frac{ds_{lm}}{\mathrm{d}r}\right|^{2}+(l^ {2}+l-2)|s_{lm}|^{2}\right).\] (H.10)

Non-radial modes have \(l\geq 1\), and for these \(l^{2}+l-2\geq 0\). Thus \(X+Y\leq 0\) for non-radial modes. Moreover \(Y\geq 0\) and \(X\leq 0\); the latter statement is evident from a comparison of equations (2.17) and (2.18). Thus equation (H.2) implies that \(W[\rho_{1}]\geq 0\) for non-radial modes.

