### _Appendix J:_ Angular-momentum transport in disks

We have shown in SS6.1.5 that the gravitational torques exerted by a trailing spiral pattern transport angular momentum outward in a disk galaxy. In this appendix we present a more complete description of angular-momentum transport in fluid and stellar disks.

### _J.1 Transport in fluid and stellar systems_

The angular momentum per unit volume in a fluid1 is \(\widetilde{\bf l}=\rho\,{\bf x}\times{\bf v}\). Its rate of change can be written in Cartesian coordinates as

Footnote 1: The tilde on \({\bf l}\) is a flag that in this appendix, the angular momentum and energy of a fluid element of mass \(m\) are defined as \(m{\bf x}\times{\bf v}\) and \(m(\frac{1}{2}v^{2}+\Phi)\), while in most other sections of this book, “angular momentum” and “energy” are shorthand for specific angular momentum (angular momentum per unit mass) and specific energy, \({\bf x}\times{\bf v}\) and \(\frac{1}{2}v^{2}+\Phi\).

\[\frac{\partial\widetilde{l}_{i}}{\partial t}=\epsilon_{ijk}x_{j}\frac{ \partial}{\partial t}(\rho v_{k}),\] (J.1)where the permutation tensor \(\epsilon_{ijk}\) is defined after equation (B.7) and we have used the summation convention defined just above that equation. Using equation (F.14) this can be rewritten as

\[\frac{\partial\widetilde{l}_{i}}{\partial t}=-\epsilon_{ijk}x_{j}\left(\frac{ \partial\Pi_{km}}{\partial x_{m}}+\rho\frac{\partial\Phi}{\partial x_{k}} \right)=-\frac{\partial\Lambda_{im}}{\partial x_{m}}-\rho({\bf x}\times\mathbf{\nabla}\Phi)_{i},\] (J.2)

where the momentum flux tensor \(\Pi_{km}=\rho v_{k}v_{m}+p\delta_{km}\) and

\[\Lambda_{im}=\epsilon_{ijk}x_{j}\Pi_{km}.\] (J.3)

The second term in equation (J.2) is simply the gravitational torque per unit volume exerted on the fluid. The first arises from the flow or current of angular momentum; specifically, the outward flow of the angular momentum component \(\widetilde{L}_{i}\) through a unit area with outward normal parallel to the unit vector \(\hat{\bf e}_{m}\) is \(\Lambda_{im}\), and thus \(\Lambda_{im}\) is known as the **angular-momentum flux tensor**. Note that \(\Lambda_{im}\neq\Lambda_{mi}\).

The analogous equation for a stellar system can be written down by inspection. The derivation of the momentum flux tensor in Appendix F relies on the equation of continuity and Euler's equation. The analogs to these equations for a stellar system are the Jeans equations (4.204) and (4.209); the only changes are that the velocity \(v_{i}\) is replaced by the mean velocity \(\overline{v}_{i}\), and the pressure is replaced by the product of the density and the velocity-dispersion tensor, \(\rho\sigma_{ij}^{2}\). Thus the momentum flux tensor for a stellar system must be

\[\Pi_{km}=\rho\overline{v}_{k}\overline{v}_{m}+\rho\sigma_{km}^{2}=\rho \overline{v_{k}v_{m}};\] (J.4)

the second expression follows from the first by equation (4.26). The angular-momentum flux tensor for a stellar system is then given by equation (J.3), if the momentum flux tensor is given by equation (J.4).

### J.2 Transport in a disk with stationary spiral structure

We now specialize to a razor-thin disk in the \(z=0\) plane. We assume that the disk has a stationary spiral pattern with pattern speed \(\Omega_{p}\); that is, the surface density \(\Sigma(R,\phi,t)\) and all other physical variables depend on the azimuthal angle \(\phi\) and time \(t\) only in the combination \(\phi-\Omega_{p}t\). We allow for sources and sinks of angular momentum, such as torques from an external potential, but assume that there are no sources or sinks of mass. We shall focus on the flow of angular momentum through the cylinder \(R=R_{0}=\mbox{\it constant}\).

The mass inside \(R_{0}\) is constant, since the surface density is constant in the frame rotating at the pattern speed. Thus the net flow of mass through \(R_{0}\) must be zero; that is, \(C_{\rm M}(R_{0})=0\) where

\[C_{\rm M}(R)=R\int_{0}^{2\pi}{\rm d}\phi\,\Sigma v_{R}\] (J.5)

is the **mass current**, and \(v_{R}\) is the radial velocity.

The rate of change of the angular momentum \(\widetilde{L}\) inside \(R_{0}\) is given by integrating equation (J.2) over this volume:

\[\frac{{\rm d}\widetilde{L}}{{\rm d}t}=\int_{R<R_{0}}{\rm d}^{3}{\bf x}\frac{ \partial\widetilde{l}_{z}}{\partial t}=-\int_{R<R_{0}}{\rm d}^{3}{\bf x}\left( \frac{\partial\Lambda_{zm}}{\partial x_{m}}+\epsilon_{zjk}\rho x_{j}\frac{ \partial\Phi}{\partial x_{k}}\right).\] (J.6)For an arbitrary vector field \({\bf F}\), the divergence theorem (B.43) implies that

\[\int_{R<R_{0}}{\rm d}^{3}{\bf x}\,\mathbf{\nabla}\cdot{\bf F}=\oint_{S}{ \rm d}^{2}{\bf S}\cdot{\bf F}=R_{0}\int_{0}^{2\pi}{\rm d}\phi\int_{-\infty}^{ \infty}{\rm d}z\,F_{R},\] (J.7)

where \(F_{R}={\bf F}\cdot\hat{\bf e}_{R}\) is the component of \({\bf F}\) in the radial direction, and \(S\) is the surface of the cylinder \(R=R_{0}\). We can apply this result to the tensor \(\Lambda_{zm}\) by treating the three components \(\Lambda_{zm}\), \(m=1,2,3\) as a vector; thus

\[\int_{R<R_{0}}{\rm d}^{3}{\bf x}\frac{\partial\Lambda_{zm}}{\partial x_{m}}=R_ {0}\int_{0}^{2\pi}{\rm d}\phi\int_{-\infty}^{\infty}{\rm d}z\,\Lambda_{zR}.\] (J.8)

We now write \(\rho(R,\phi,z,t)=\Sigma(R,z,t)\delta(z)\) and integrate over \(z\) to obtain

\[\frac{{\rm d}\widetilde{L}}{{\rm d}t}=-R_{0}^{2}\int_{0}^{2\pi}{\rm d}\phi\, \Sigma v_{\phi}v_{R}-\int_{0}^{R_{0}}{\rm d}R\,R\int_{0}^{2\pi}{\rm d}\phi\, \Sigma\frac{\partial\Phi}{\partial\phi}.\] (J.9)

This result is for a fluid; for a stellar system we simply replace \(v_{\phi}v_{R}\) by \(\overline{v_{\phi}v_{R}}\).

The first integral on the right side of this equation arises from advective or Reynolds transport of angular momentum due to the bulk motion of the fluid (see discussion following eq. F.70),2 and leads to a flow of angular momentum through radius \(R_{0}\) or **advective angular-momentum current**

Footnote 2: The term **lorry transport** was used by Lynden–Bell & Kalnajs (1972), who likened the fluid elements to a fleet of lorries or trucks carrying coal. The trucks travel outward full of coal and return empty, so there is an outward flow of coal but no net flow of trucks. Similarly the fluid elements carry angular momentum outward, deposit it near the apocenter of their orbits, and return to acquire more angular momentum near pericenter, leading to an outward flow of angular momentum with no outward flow of mass.

\[C_{\rm A}(R_{0})\equiv R_{0}^{2}\int_{0}^{2\pi}{\rm d}\phi\,\Sigma v_{\phi}v_ {R}.\] (J.10)

The second term on the right side of (J.9) represents the gravitational torque exerted on the fluid inside radius \(R_{0}\). The gravitational potential exerting this torque can be written \(\Phi\equiv\Phi_{\rm ext}+\Phi_{\rm in}+\Phi_{\rm out}\), where \(\Phi_{\rm ext}\) is the potential from sources external to the disk, \(\Phi_{\rm in}\) is the potential due to the disk mass inside \(R_{0}\), and \(\Phi_{\rm out}\) is due to the disk mass outside \(R_{0}\). The torque due to \(\Phi_{\rm in}\) must vanish, since the disk interior to \(R_{0}\) cannot exert any net torque on itself. The torque due to external sources is

\[\widetilde{N}(R_{0})\equiv-\int_{0}^{R_{0}}{\rm d}R\,R\int_{0}^{2\pi}{\rm d} \phi\,\Sigma\frac{\partial\Phi_{\rm ext}}{\partial\phi}.\] (J.11)

The torque due to the disk exterior to \(R_{0}\) adds angular momentum to the disk inside \(R_{0}\) and removes it from the disk outside \(R_{0}\). Hence this torque redistributes of the angular momentum in the disk; the corresponding **gravitational angular-momentum current** through \(R_{0}\) is

\[C_{\rm G}(R_{0})\equiv\int_{0}^{R_{0}}{\rm d}R\,R\int_{0}^{2\pi}{\rm d}\phi\, \Sigma\frac{\partial\Phi_{\rm out}}{\partial\phi}.\] (J.12)

This quantity was already defined and discussed in SS6.1.5.

The equation for angular-momentum conservation inside radius \(R_{0}\) is

\[\frac{{\rm d}\widetilde{L}}{{\rm d}t}=\widetilde{N}(R_{0})-C_{\rm A}(R_{0})-C_ {\rm G}(R_{0});\] (J.13)in words, the rate of change of the angular momentum of the disk interior to \(R_{0}\) equals the external torque on that region minus the advective and gravitational currents of angular momentum out of that region.

### J.3 Transport in perturbed axisymmetric disks

We next consider the case of small perturbations from axisymmetry, so

\[\eqalign{v_{R}=\epsilon v_{R1}+\epsilon^{2}v_{R2}+\cdots&;\quad v_{\phi}=v_{\phi 0 }+\epsilon v_{\phi 1}+\epsilon^{2}v_{\phi 2}+\cdots,\cr\Sigma=\Sigma_{0}+ \epsilon\Sigma_{1}+\epsilon^{2}\Sigma_{2}+\cdots&;\quad\Phi=\Phi_{0}+\epsilon \Phi_{1}+\epsilon^{2}\Phi_{2}+\cdots,\cr}\]

where \(\epsilon\ll 1\) and \(v_{\phi 0}(R)\), \(\Sigma_{0}(R)\) and \(\Phi_{0}(R)\) are independent of angle. Note that the zero-order terms in radial velocity \(v_{R}\) and external potential \(\Phi_{\rm ext}\) both vanish--the first since the unperturbed disk is in circular rotation, and the second since the external perturbations are assumed to be weak. To first order in the small quantity \(\epsilon\), equations (J.5), (J.10), (J.11), and (J.12) become

\[\eqalign{C_{\rm M1}(R_{0})&=\epsilon R_{0}\int_{0}^{2\pi}{\rm d}\phi\,\Sigma_ {0}v_{R1},\cr C_{\rm A1}(R_{0})&=\epsilon R_{0}^{2}\int_{0}^{2\pi}{\rm d}\phi \,\Sigma_{0}v_{\phi 0}v_{R1},\cr\widetilde{N}_{1}(R_{0})&=-\epsilon\int_{0}^{R_{0}}{ \rm d}R\,R\int_{0}^{2\pi}{\rm d}\phi\,\Sigma_{0}{\partial\Phi_{{\rm ext},1} \over\partial\phi},\cr C_{\rm G1}(R_{0})&=\epsilon\int_{0}^{R_{0}}{\rm d}R\,R \int_{0}^{2\pi}{\rm d}\phi\,\Sigma_{0}{\partial\Phi_{{\rm out},1}\over \partial\phi}.\cr}\]

The mass current \(C_{\rm M1}(R_{0})\) must vanish, since the mass current is zero to all orders in a stationary disk. Since \(C_{\rm A1}\) is just \(R_{0}v_{\phi 0}\) times \(C_{\rm M1}\), \(C_{\rm A1}\) must also vanish. Since \(\Sigma_{0}(R)\) is independent of the angle \(\phi\), the integrals for \(\widetilde{N}(R_{0})\) and \(C_{\rm G1}(R_{0})\) must also vanish. Thus _the angular-momentum transport in an axisymmetric disk due to external torques and non-axisymmetric density waves of strength \(\epsilon\) is of order \(\epsilon^{2}\)_. This result is closely related to the finding that the energy and momentum flux in a sound wave is second-order in the wave amplitude (Appendix F.3.1).

To quadratic order in \(\epsilon\) we have

\[\eqalign{C_{\rm M2}(R_{0})&=\epsilon^{2}R_{0}\int_{0}^{2\pi}{\rm d}\phi\,( \Sigma_{1}v_{R1}+\Sigma_{0}v_{R2}),\cr C_{\rm A2}(R_{0})&=\epsilon^{2}R_{0}^{2 }\int_{0}^{2\pi}{\rm d}\phi\,(\Sigma_{1}v_{\phi 0}v_{R1}+\Sigma_{0}v_{\phi 1}v_{R1}+ \Sigma_{0}v_{\phi 0}v_{R2}),\cr&=\epsilon^{2}R_{0}^{2}\int_{0}^{2\pi}{\rm d}\phi\, \Sigma_{0}v_{\phi 1}v_{R1}+R_{0}v_{\phi 0}C_{\rm M2}(R_{0})=\epsilon^{2}R_{0}^{2} \Sigma_{0}\int_{0}^{2\pi}{\rm d}\phi\,v_{\phi 1}v_{R1},\cr\widetilde{N}_{2}(R_{0})&=- \epsilon^{2}\int_{0}^{R_{0}}{\rm d}R\,R\int_{0}^{2\pi}{\rm d}\phi\,\Sigma_{1}{ \partial\Phi_{{\rm ext},1}\over\partial\phi},\cr C_{\rm G2}(R_{0})&=\epsilon^{2 }\int_{0}^{R_{0}}{\rm d}R\,R\int_{0}^{2\pi}{\rm d}\phi\,\Sigma_{1}{\partial \Phi_{{\rm out},1}\over\partial\phi}.\cr}\]

Here the last expression for \(C_{\rm A}(R_{0})\) follows because \(C_{\rm M}(R_{0})\) is zero to all orders. For stellar systems the advective current is obtained by replacing \(v_{\phi 1}v_{R1}\) by \(\overline{v_{\phi 1}v_{R1}}\)Following equation (6.42), we can write the perturbed quantities as a sum over azimuthal wavenumber \(m\geq 0\) of terms of the form

\[v_{R1}=\mathrm{Re}[v_{Ra}(R)\mathrm{e}^{\mathrm{i}m(\phi-\Omega_{\mathrm{p}}t)}]= \tfrac{1}{2}v_{Ra}(R)\mathrm{e}^{\mathrm{i}m(\phi-\Omega_{\mathrm{p}}t)}+\tfrac {1}{2}v_{Ra}^{*}(R)\mathrm{e}^{-\mathrm{i}m(\phi-\Omega_{\mathrm{p}}t)},\] (J.17)

etc. Axisymmetric perturbations (\(m=0\)) cannot transport angular momentum, so we may restrict the sum to terms with \(m>0\). Then equation (J.13) can be integrated over \(\phi\) to yield a sum over \(m\) of terms of the form

\[\begin{split}\frac{\mathrm{d}\widetilde{L}}{\mathrm{d}t}& =\widetilde{N}_{2}(R_{0})-C_{\mathrm{A}2}(R_{0})-C_{\mathrm{G}2}(R_ {0})\\ &=-\tfrac{1}{2}\pi\epsilon^{2}R_{0}^{2}\Sigma_{0}v_{\phi a}v_{Ra} ^{*}+\tfrac{1}{2}\pi m\mathrm{i}\epsilon^{2}\int_{0}^{R_{0}}\mathrm{d}R\,R \Sigma_{a}(\Phi_{\mathrm{ext},a}^{*}+\Phi_{\mathrm{out},a}^{*})+\mathrm{CC}, \end{split}\] (J.18)

where "CC" stands for the complex conjugate of the preceding terms.

Using equations (6.43), the advective current can be written, after some algebra, as

\[C_{\mathrm{A}2}(R_{0})=\frac{\pi\epsilon^{2}R_{0}\Sigma_{0}\mathrm{i}m}{2 \Delta}\left[\left(\Phi_{a}+h_{a}\right)^{*}\frac{\mathrm{d}}{\mathrm{d}R}( \Phi_{a}+h_{a})\right]+\mathrm{CC},\] (J.19)

where \(\Delta=\kappa^{2}-m^{2}(\Omega-\Omega_{\mathrm{p}})^{2}\).

### **J.4 Transport in the WKB approximation**

The expressions we have derived for the angular-momentum currents can be evaluated in the WKB approximation analyzed in SS6.2. In this approximation \(\mathrm{d}/\mathrm{d}R\) can be replaced by \(\mathrm{i}k\) in equation (J.19), where \(k\) is the radial wavenumber, so the advective current simplifies to

\[C_{\mathrm{A}2}(R_{0})=-\frac{\pi m\epsilon^{2}R_{0}\Sigma_{0}k}{\Delta}|\Phi_ {a}+h_{a}|^{2}.\] (J.20)

Using equations (6.30) and (6.45), we can express the perturbed enthalpy in terms of the perturbed potential,

\[h_{a}=v_{\mathrm{s}}^{2}\frac{\Sigma_{a}}{\Sigma_{0}}=-\frac{v_{\mathrm{s}}^{ 2}|k|}{2\pi G\Sigma_{0}}\Phi_{a},\] (J.21)

so the advective angular-momentum current is

\[C_{\mathrm{A}2}(R_{0})=-\frac{\pi m\epsilon^{2}R_{0}\Sigma_{0}k}{\Delta}\left( 1-\frac{v_{\mathrm{s}}^{2}|k|}{2\pi G\Sigma_{0}}\right)^{2}|\Phi_{a}|^{2}.\] (J.22)

The gravitational current has already been evaluated in equation (6.21); adjusting the notation to the present usage we have

\[C_{\mathrm{G}2}(R_{0})=\mathrm{sgn}\left(k\right)\frac{m\epsilon^{2}R_{0}| \Phi_{a}|^{2}}{4G};\] (J.23)

so the total angular-momentum current from a tightly wrapped wave in a fluid disk is (Goldreich & Tremaine 1979)

\[C_{\widetilde{L}}(R_{0})=C_{\mathrm{A}2}(R_{0})+C_{\mathrm{G}2}(R_{0})= \mathrm{sgn}\left(k\right)\frac{m\epsilon^{2}R_{0}|\Phi_{a}|^{2}}{4G}\left( \frac{v_{\mathrm{s}}^{2}|k|}{\pi G\Sigma_{0}}-1\right),\] (J.24)

where \(\Delta\) has been eliminated using the WKB dispersion relation (6.55). The WKB analysis of disk dynamics in SS6.2.2 shows that the total angular-momentum current is conserved, as it must be (eq. 6.56).

For a stellar disk the analogous formula is (Toomre 1969)

\[C_{\widetilde{\cal L}}(R_{0})=-{\rm sgn}\,(k)\frac{m\epsilon^{2}R_{0}|\Phi_{a}|^{ 2}}{4G}\left(1+2\frac{\partial\ln{\cal F}(s,\chi)}{\partial\ln\chi}\right),\] (J.25)

where \(s=m(\Omega_{\rm p}-\Omega)/\kappa\), \(\chi=\sigma_{R}^{2}k^{2}/\kappa^{2}\), and the reduction factor \({\cal F}(s,\chi)\) is defined in equation (6.63).

### _Appendix K:_ Derivation of the reduction factor

Our goal in this appendix is to derive the reduction factor \({\cal F}\), the factor by which the response of a stellar disk to an imposed potential is reduced below that of a cold disk (eq. 6.58). We assume that the potential is tightly wound, that the orbits in the disk can be described by the epicycle approximation, and that the disk is razor-thin and flat, with the df having the Schwarzschild form (6.62).

As usual we assume that the perturbations are small, so we can linearize the equations of motion. The linearized collisionless Boltzmann equation (5.15) reads

\[f_{1}({\bf x},{\bf v},t)=-\int_{-\infty}^{t}\,{\rm d}t^{\prime}[f_{0},\Phi_{1} ]_{{\bf x}^{\prime},{\bf v}^{\prime},t^{\prime}}=\int_{-\infty}^{t}\,{\rm d}t ^{\prime}\frac{\partial f_{0}}{\partial{\bf v}^{\prime}}({\bf x}^{\prime},{ \bf v}^{\prime})\cdot\mathbf{\nabla}^{\prime}\Phi_{1}({\bf x}^{ \prime},t^{\prime});\] (K.1)

here \(f_{0}({\bf x},{\bf v})\) and \(f_{1}({\bf x},{\bf v},t)\) are the equilibrium and perturbed dfs, \(\Phi_{1}({\bf x},t)\) is the perturbed gravitational potential, and \([\cdot,\cdot]\) is the Poisson bracket, evaluated along the unperturbed orbit \(({\bf x}^{\prime},{\bf v}^{\prime})\equiv({\bf x}[t^{\prime}],{\bf v}[t^{ \prime}])\) that arrives at \({\bf x},{\bf v}\) at time \(t\). In writing equation (K.1) we have assumed that \(f_{1}\to 0\) as \(t\to-\infty\), i.e., the perturbation was small in the distant past.

We write the potential perturbation in the disk plane in the form (cf. eqs. 6.42 and 6.51)

\[\Phi_{1}({\bf x},t)=\Phi_{a}(R){\rm e}^{{\rm i}(m\phi-\omega t)}=F(R){\rm e}^ {{\rm i}(\int^{R}k\,{\rm d}R+m\phi-\omega t)},\] (K.2)

where as usual only the real part is physical. The component of \(\mathbf{\nabla}\Phi_{1}\) in the disk plane is

\[\mathbf{\nabla}\Phi_{1}=\left[\hat{\bf e}_{R}\!\left(\frac{{\rm d}F }{{\rm d}R}+{\rm i}kF\right)+\hat{\bf e}_{\phi}\frac{{\rm i}mF}{R}\right]{\rm e }^{{\rm i}(\int k\,{\rm d}R+m\phi-\omega t)}.\] (K.3)

Since the potential is tightly wound, \(|kR|\gg 1\), we keep only the term proportional to \(k\):

\[\mathbf{\nabla}\Phi_{1}\simeq\hat{\bf e}_{R}\,{\rm i}kF{\rm e}^{{ \rm i}(\int k\,{\rm d}R+m\phi-\omega t)}.\] (K.4)

Substituting this result and the Schwarzschild df (6.62) into equation (K.1) yields

\[f_{1}(R,\phi,v_{R},v_{\phi},t)=-\frac{{\rm i}}{2\pi}\int_{-\infty}^{t}{\rm d}t ^{\prime}\left(\frac{\gamma kT\Sigma}{\sigma_{R}^{4}}\right)_{R^{\prime}}{\rm e }^{{\rm i}(\int^{R^{\prime}}k^{\prime\prime}\,{\rm d}R^{\prime\prime}+m\phi^{ \prime}-\omega t^{\prime})-w^{\prime}}v_{R}^{\prime},\] (K.5)

where the unperturbed trajectory at time \(t^{\prime}\) is \(({\bf x}^{\prime},{\bf v}^{\prime})=(R^{\prime},\phi^{\prime},v_{R}^{\prime}, v_{\phi}^{\prime})\),

\[w^{\prime}=\frac{{v_{R}^{\prime}}^{2}+\gamma^{2}(R^{\prime})[v_{\phi}^{\prime}- v_{\rm c}(R^{\prime})]^{2}}{2\sigma_{R}^{2}(R^{\prime})},\] (K.6)

and \(\gamma(R)=2\Omega(R)/\kappa(R)\).

The epicycle orbit equations (3.75), (3.91), and (3.97) yield the relations

\[\eqalign{R^{\prime}&=R_{\rm g}+X\cos(\kappa t^{\prime}+\psi),\cr v^{\prime}_{R}&=- \kappa X\sin(\kappa t^{\prime}+\psi),\cr v^{\prime}_{\phi}-v_{\rm c}(R^{\prime})& =-{\kappa\over\gamma}X\cos(\kappa t^{\prime}+\psi),\cr}\]

where the epicycle amplitude \(X\), guiding-center radius \(R_{\rm g}\), and phase \(\psi\) are determined by the boundary conditions that \(R^{\prime}=R\), \(v^{\prime}_{R}=v_{R}\), and \(v^{\prime}_{\phi}=v_{\phi}\) at \(t^{\prime}=t\). Straightforward manipulation of equations (K.7) yields

\[R^{\prime}=R-{\gamma\over\kappa}(v_{\phi}-v_{\rm c})(\cos\tau-1)+{v_{R}\over \kappa}\sin\tau\quad;\quad v^{\prime}_{R}=v_{R}\cos\tau+\gamma(v_{\phi}-v_{\rm c })\sin\tau,\]

where \(\tau=\kappa(t^{\prime}-t)\) and \(v_{\phi}\), \(v_{R}\) and \(v_{\rm c}(R)\) are all evaluated at \(t\).

We are assuming that the epicycle amplitude \(X\) is small, so \(X\) and \(|R^{\prime}-R|\) are always much less than \(R\). We are also assuming that the spiral wave is tightly wound, so \(|kR|\gg 1\). The epicycle amplitude can be either larger or smaller than the radial spacing between arms, so \(|kX|\) can be either large or small compared to unity. With this ordering, equation (K.5) can be simplified considerably:

(i) Since \(|R^{\prime}-R|\ll R\), to a good approximation \(k(R^{\prime})\), \(F(R^{\prime})\), \(\Sigma(R^{\prime})\), \(\sigma_{R}(R^{\prime})\), and \(\gamma(R^{\prime})\) can all be replaced by their values at \(R\) and taken outside the integral.

(ii) At the same level of approximation we can write

\[{\rm e}^{{\rm i}\int^{R^{\prime}}k^{\prime\prime}\,{\rm d}R^{\prime\prime}} \simeq{\rm e}^{{\rm i}[\int^{R}k^{\prime\prime}\,{\rm d}R^{\prime\prime}+k(R) (R^{\prime}-R)]}.\]

(iii) Since the azimuthal angle \(\phi^{\prime}\) enters only through the factor \(\exp({\rm i}m\phi^{\prime})\), and the epicycle amplitude is small, we may neglect the epicyclic motion in \(\phi\) and set \(\phi^{\prime}=\phi+\Omega(t^{\prime}-t)\). We cannot use the same argument to neglect the epicyclic motion in \(R\) since the small factor \((R^{\prime}-R)\) is multiplied by the large factor \(k\).

(iv) Equations (K.7) imply that \(w^{\prime}=\kappa^{2}X^{2}/(2\sigma_{R}^{2})\). Since \(X\) is an integral of the motion, \(w^{\prime}\) must therefore be conserved along the trajectory.1 Thus \(w^{\prime}=w\) and the factor \(\exp(-w)\) can be taken out of the integral.

Footnote 1: This result also follows from the Jeans theorem: since the unperturbed df depends on velocity only through \(w\), \(w\) must be an integral.

Inserting these approximations into equation (K.5), we find

\[\eqalign{f_{1}(R,\phi,v_{R},&v_{\phi},t)=-{{\rm i}\gamma kF\Sigma\over 2\pi \kappa\sigma_{R}^{3}}{\rm e}^{{\rm i}(\int^{R}k^{\prime\prime}\,{\rm d}R^{ \prime\prime}+m\phi-\omega t)}{\rm e}^{-(u^{2}+v^{2})/2}\cr&\times\int_{-\infty }^{0}{\rm d}\tau\,{\rm e}^{{\rm i}(k\sigma_{R}/\kappa)[u\sin\tau-v(\cos\tau- 1)]-{\rm i}s\tau}(u\cos\tau+v\sin\tau).\cr}\]

Here we have introduced the notations

\[s={\omega-m\Omega\over\kappa}\quad;\quad u={v_{R}\over\sigma_{R}}\quad;\quad v =\gamma{v_{\phi}-v_{\rm c}\over\sigma_{R}}.\]

The mean radial velocity at a given point is

\[\overline{v}_{R1}={\int{\rm d}^{2}{\bf v}\,(f_{0}+f_{1})v_{R}\over\int{\rm d}^ {2}{\bf v}\,(f_{0}+f_{1})}.\]Since \(\int{\rm d}^{2}{\bf v}\,f_{0}v_{R}=0\), to first order in the perturbation we have

\[\overline{v}_{R1}=\frac{\int{\rm d}^{2}{\bf v}\,f_{1}v_{R}}{\int{\rm d}^{2}{\bf v }\,f_{0}}=\frac{\sigma_{R}^{3}\int{\rm d}u{\rm d}v\,f_{1}u}{\gamma\Sigma}.\] (K.13)

Writing \(\overline{v}_{R1}=\overline{v}_{Ra}\exp[{\rm i}(m\phi-\omega t)]\) (eq. 6.42) and using equation (K.2) and the definition of the reduction factor \({\cal F}\) (eq. 6.58), we find

\[\begin{split}{\cal F}=&\ {\rm i}\frac{1-s^{2}}{2\pi s} \int_{-\infty}^{\infty}{\rm d}u\,u\int_{-\infty}^{\infty}{\rm d}v\,{\rm e}^{-( u^{2}+v^{2})/2}\\ &\times\int_{-\infty}^{0}{\rm d}\tau\,{\rm e}^{{\rm i}(k\sigma_{ R}/\kappa)[u\sin\tau-v(\cos\tau-1)]-{\rm i}s\tau}(u\cos\tau+v\sin\tau).\end{split}\] (K.14)

We now use the following integrals:

\[\begin{split}\int_{-\infty}^{\infty}{\rm d}x\,{\rm e}^{-x^{2}/2} {\rm e}^{{\rm i}\mu x}=\sqrt{2\pi}{\rm e}^{-\mu^{2}/2}\quad;\quad\int_{-\infty }^{\infty}{\rm d}x\,{\rm e}^{-x^{2}/2}x{\rm e}^{{\rm i}\mu x}={\rm i}\sqrt{2 \pi}\mu{\rm e}^{-\mu^{2}/2},\\ \int_{-\infty}^{\infty}{\rm d}x{\rm e}^{-x^{2}/2}x^{2}{\rm e}^{{ \rm i}\mu x}=\sqrt{2\pi}(1-\mu^{2}){\rm e}^{-\mu^{2}/2}.\end{split}\] (K.15)

Evaluating the \(u\) and \(v\) integrals and replacing \(\tau\) by \(-\tau\), we obtain

\[{\cal F}(s,\chi)={\rm i}\frac{1-s^{2}}{s}\int_{0}^{\infty}{\rm d}\tau\,{\rm e }^{{\rm i}s\tau-\chi(1-\cos\tau)}(\cos\tau-\chi\sin^{2}\tau),\] (K.16)

where \(\chi=(k\sigma_{R}/\kappa)^{2}\). Now write the integral as a sum of integrals from \(\tau=0\) to \(2\pi\), \(2\pi\) to \(4\pi\), etc.:

\[{\cal F}(s,\chi)={\rm i}\frac{1-s^{2}}{s}\sum_{n=0}^{\infty}{\rm e}^{2\pi{\rm i }ns}\int_{0}^{2\pi}{\rm d}\tau{\rm e}^{{\rm i}s\tau-\chi(1-\cos\tau)}(\cos\tau -\chi\sin^{2}\tau).\] (K.17)

The geometric series in (K.17) has the form \(\sum_{n=0}^{\infty}p^{n}\), where \(|p|=|\exp(2\pi{\rm i}s)|=\exp[-2\pi{\rm Im}(s)]<1\), since \({\rm Im}(s)>0\) (we have assumed that the perturbation vanishes as \(t\to-\infty\)). Hence the series is convergent, with sum \(1/(1-p)\). Thus

\[{\cal F}(s,\chi)={\rm i}\frac{1-s^{2}}{s}\frac{1}{1-{\rm e}^{2\pi{\rm i}s}} \int_{0}^{2\pi}{\rm d}\tau\,{\rm e}^{{\rm i}s\tau-\chi(1-\cos\tau)}(\cos\tau- \chi\sin^{2}\tau).\] (K.18)

Replacing the variable \(\tau\) by \(\tau+\pi\), we have

\[{\cal F}(s,\chi)={\rm i}\frac{1-s^{2}}{s}\frac{{\rm e}^{\pi{\rm i}s}}{1-{\rm e }^{2\pi{\rm i}s}}\int_{-\pi}^{\pi}{\rm d}\tau\,{\rm e}^{{\rm i}s\tau-\chi(1+ \cos\tau)}(-\cos\tau-\chi\sin^{2}\tau).\] (K.19)

We can write \((1-{\rm e}^{2\pi{\rm i}s})/{\rm e}^{\pi{\rm i}s}=-2{\rm i}\sin\pi s\). Also, if we write \({\rm e}^{{\rm i}s\tau}=\cos s\tau+{\rm i}\sin s\tau\), only the cosine term will contribute to the integral since the sine produces an integrand that is odd in \(\tau\). Thus

\[{\cal F}(s,\chi)=\frac{1-s^{2}}{s\sin\pi s}\int_{0}^{\pi}{\rm d}\tau\,{\rm e}^{- \chi(1+\cos\tau)}\cos s\tau(\cos\tau+\chi\sin^{2}\tau).\] (K.20)

The integral can be simplified by writing the second term of the integrand as \(\chi{\rm e}^{-\chi(1+\cos\tau)}\cos s\tau\sin^{2}\tau=\cos s\tau\sin\tau{\rm d}[ {\rm e}^{-\chi(1+\cos\tau)}]/{\rm d}\tau\) and integrating by parts:

\[{\cal F}(s,\chi)=\frac{1-s^{2}}{\sin\pi s}\int_{0}^{\pi}{\rm d}\tau\,{\rm e}^{ -\chi(1+\cos\tau)}\sin s\tau\sin\tau.\] (K.21)Yet another integration by parts yields a second form:

\[{\cal F}(s,\chi)=\frac{1-s^{2}}{\chi}\left[1-\frac{s}{\sin\pi s}\int_{0}^{\pi}{ \rm d}\tau\,{\rm e}^{-\chi(1+\cos\tau)}\cos s\tau\right].\] (K.22)

We obtain a third form, involving the modified Bessel function \(I_{n}\), by using the identities (C.64) and (C.67):

\[{\cal F}(s,\chi)=\frac{1-s^{2}}{\chi}\biggl{[}1-\frac{s}{\sin\pi s}{\rm e}^{- \chi}\sum_{n=-\infty}^{\infty}(-1)^{n}I_{n}(\chi)\int_{0}^{\pi}{\rm d}\tau\, \cos n\tau\cos s\tau\biggr{]}.\] (K.23)

It is simple to show that \(\int_{0}^{\pi}{\rm d}\tau\cos n\tau\cos s\tau=(-1)^{n+1}s\sin\pi s/(n^{2}-s^{2})\); hence

\[{\cal F}(s,\chi)=\frac{1-s^{2}}{\chi}\biggl{[}1+s^{2}{\rm e}^{-\chi}\sum_{n=- \infty}^{\infty}\frac{I_{n}(\chi)}{n^{2}-s^{2}}\biggr{]}.\] (K.24)

This equation can be simplified by rewriting the unit term in the square brackets as \(1={\rm e}^{-\chi}\sum_{n=-\infty}^{\infty}I_{n}(\chi)\), an identity that follows from equation (C.67) by setting \(\theta=0\). After some rearrangement, we obtain a final form,

\[{\cal F}(s,\chi)=\frac{2}{\chi}(1-s^{2}){\rm e}^{-\chi}\sum_{n=1}^{\infty} \frac{I_{n}(\chi)}{1-s^{2}/n^{2}}.\] (K.25)

### Appendix \(L\): The diffusion coefficients

We consider a subject star of mass \(m\) and velocity \({\bf v}\) moving through a homogeneous sea of field stars of mass \(m_{a}\) and df\(f_{a}({\bf v}_{a})\); the df is normalized so that \(\int{\rm d}^{3}{\bf v}_{a}\,f_{a}({\bf v}_{a})=n\), the number density of field stars. Due to encounters with the field stars, the subject star suffers a gradually accumulating velocity change \(\Delta{\bf v}\). Our goal is to calculate the diffusion coefficients \(D[\Delta v_{i}]\) and \(D[\Delta v_{i}\Delta v_{j}]\), which measure the average velocity changes per unit time,

\[D[\Delta v_{i}]=\frac{\langle\Delta v_{i}\rangle}{\Delta t}\quad;\quad D[ \Delta v_{i}\Delta v_{j}]=\frac{\langle\Delta v_{i}\Delta v_{j}\rangle}{ \Delta t}.\] (L.1)

The diffusion coefficients were first derived by Landau (1936) in the context of Coulomb interactions in plasmas, but the derivation here follows Rosenbluth, MacDonald, & Judd (1957).

Suppose that in a particular encounter the subject and field stars have velocities \({\bf v}\) and \({\bf v}_{a}\) respectively. The relative velocity is \({\bf V}={\bf v}-{\bf v}_{a}\). Its initial value is \({\bf V}_{0}\) and the change in velocity caused by the encounter is \(\Delta{\bf V}\). The dynamics of the encounter is described in SS3.1d and in particular the relation between \(\Delta{\bf V}\) and the change in velocity of the subject star \(\Delta{\bf v}\) is given by equation (3.45), which in the present notation reads

\[\Delta{\bf v}=\frac{m_{a}}{m+m_{a}}\Delta{\bf V}.\] (L.2)

We introduce a coordinate system \(\hat{\bf e}^{\prime}_{1},\hat{\bf e}^{\prime}_{2},\hat{\bf e}^{\prime}_{3}\), such that \(\hat{\bf e}^{\prime}_{1}\) is parallel to \({\bf V}_{0}\) (see Figure L.1). Thus

\[{\bf V}_{0}\cdot\hat{\bf e}^{\prime}_{1}=|{\bf V}_{0}|\equiv V_{0}\quad;\quad{ \bf V}_{0}\cdot\hat{\bf e}^{\prime}_{2}={\bf V}_{0}\cdot\hat{\bf e}^{\prime}_ {3}=0.\] (L.3)We denote the angle between the plane of the relative orbit and \(\hat{\bf e}_{2}^{\prime}\) by \(\phi\). The change in \({\bf v}\) during the encounter may be written

\[\Delta{\bf v}=-\Delta v_{\parallel}\hat{\bf e}_{1}^{\prime}+\Delta v_{\perp}(- \hat{\bf e}_{2}^{\prime}\cos\phi+\hat{\bf e}_{3}^{\prime}\sin\phi),\] (L.4)

where \(\Delta v_{\parallel}\) and \(\Delta v_{\perp}\) are the magnitudes of the components of \(\Delta{\bf v}\) that are parallel and perpendicular to \({\bf V}_{0}\). The signs are chosen to ensure that \(\Delta v_{\parallel}\) and \(\Delta v_{\perp}\) are positive if the interaction between the two particles is attractive.

Using the identity \(\Delta{\bf v}=\sum_{k=1}^{3}(\Delta{\bf v}\cdot\hat{\bf e}_{k}^{\prime})\hat{ \bf e}_{k}^{\prime}\) we have

\[\Delta v_{i} =\sum_{k=1}^{3}(\Delta{\bf v}\cdot\hat{\bf e}_{k}^{\prime})(\hat{ \bf e}_{i}\cdot\hat{\bf e}_{k}^{\prime}),\] (L.5) \[\Delta v_{i}\Delta v_{j} =\sum_{k,l=1}^{3}(\Delta{\bf v}\cdot\hat{\bf e}_{k}^{\prime})( \Delta{\bf v}\cdot\hat{\bf e}_{l}^{\prime})(\hat{\bf e}_{i}\cdot\hat{\bf e}_{k }^{\prime})(\hat{\bf e}_{j}\cdot\hat{\bf e}_{l}^{\prime}).\]

Since all angles \(0\leq\phi<2\pi\) are equally probable, and \(\Delta v_{\parallel}\) and \(\Delta v_{\perp}\) do not depend on \(\phi\), we can take averages of equations (L.4) and (L.5) over \(\phi\). Denoting these averages by \(\langle\cdot\rangle_{\phi}\), we have \(\langle\cos\phi\rangle_{\phi}=\langle\sin\phi\rangle_{\phi}=0\), \(\langle\cos^{2}\phi\rangle_{\phi}=\langle\sin^{2}\phi\rangle_{\phi}=\frac{1}{2}\). Thus equations (L.4) and (L.5) yield

\[\langle\Delta v_{i}\rangle_{\phi} =-\Delta v_{\parallel}(\hat{\bf e}_{i}\cdot\hat{\bf e}_{1}^{ \prime}),\] (L.6) \[\langle\Delta v_{i}\Delta v_{j}\rangle_{\phi} =(\Delta v_{\parallel})^{2}(\hat{\bf e}_{i}\cdot\hat{\bf e}_{1}^ {\prime})(\hat{\bf e}_{j}\cdot\hat{\bf e}_{1}^{\prime})\] \[\qquad+\tfrac{1}{2}(\Delta v_{\perp})^{2}[(\hat{\bf e}_{i}\cdot \hat{\bf e}_{2}^{\prime})(\hat{\bf e}_{j}\cdot\hat{\bf e}_{2}^{\prime})+(\hat {\bf e}_{i}\cdot\hat{\bf e}_{3}^{\prime})(\hat{\bf e}_{j}\cdot\hat{\bf e}_{3} ^{\prime})].\]

From equations (3.54) we have

\[\Delta v_{\perp}=\frac{2m_{a}V_{0}}{m+m_{a}}\frac{b/b_{90}}{1+b^{2}/b_{90}^{2} }\quad;\quad\Delta v_{\parallel}=\frac{2m_{a}V_{0}}{m+m_{a}}\frac{1}{1+b^{2}/ b_{90}^{2}},\] (L.7)where \(b\) is the impact parameter and

\[b_{90}\equiv\frac{G(m+m_{a})}{V_{0}^{2}}\] (L.8)

is the \(90^{\circ}\) deflection radius defined in equation (3.51).

We now sum the effects of all the encounters. The number density of field stars in the velocity-space volume \(\mathrm{d}^{3}\mathbf{v}_{a}\) is \(f_{a}(\mathbf{v}_{a})\mathrm{d}^{3}\mathbf{v}_{a}\). The number of encounters in a time \(\Delta t\) with impact parameters between \(b\) and \(b+\mathrm{d}b\) is just this density times the volume of an annulus with inner radius \(b\), outer radius \(b+\mathrm{d}b\), and length \(V_{0}\Delta t\), that is,

\[2\pi b\,\mathrm{d}b\,V_{0}\Delta tf_{a}(\mathbf{v}_{a})\mathrm{d}^{3}\mathbf{ v}_{a}.\] (L.9)

Thus

\[D[\Delta v_{i}]=\frac{\langle\Delta v_{i}\rangle}{\Delta t}=2\pi\int\mathrm{d} ^{3}\mathbf{v}_{a}\mathrm{d}b\,bf_{a}(\mathbf{v}_{a})V_{0}\langle\Delta v_{i} \rangle_{\phi},\] (L.10)

with a similar equation for \(D[\Delta v_{i}\Delta v_{j}]\). We can carry out the integration over the impact parameter \(b\) using equations (L.7). The range of integration is from \(0\) to \(b_{\mathrm{max}}\). The integrals involved are:

\[\int_{0}^{b_{\mathrm{max}}}\mathrm{d}b\,b\Delta v_{\parallel} =\frac{m_{a}V_{0}b_{90}^{2}}{m+m_{a}}\ln(1+\Lambda^{2}),\] (L.11) \[\int_{0}^{b_{\mathrm{max}}}\mathrm{d}b\,b(\Delta v_{\parallel})^{2} =2\left(\frac{m_{a}V_{0}b_{90}}{m+m_{a}}\right)^{2}\bigg{(}1-\frac {1}{1+\Lambda^{2}}\bigg{)},\] \[\int_{0}^{b_{\mathrm{max}}}\mathrm{d}b\,b(\Delta v_{\perp})^{2} =2\left(\frac{m_{a}V_{0}b_{90}}{m+m_{a}}\right)^{2}\left(\ln(1+ \Lambda^{2})+\frac{1}{1+\Lambda^{2}}-1\right),\]

where

\[\Lambda\equiv\frac{b_{\mathrm{max}}}{b_{90}}.\] (L.12)

The choice of the appropriate value for \(b_{\mathrm{max}}\) is discussed following equation (7.84), where it is argued that \(b_{\mathrm{max}}\) is given approximately by the radius of the subject star's orbit. In most applications, \(\Lambda\) is very large, and therefore without loss of accuracy we can discard terms involving \((1+\Lambda^{2})^{-1}\) and replace \(\ln(1+\Lambda^{2})\) by \(2\ln\Lambda\) in equation (L.11). Furthermore, although this is a less accurate approximation, we can discard terms of order unity compared to those of order \(\ln\Lambda\). In this manner we arrive at a simplified version of equations (L.11), keeping only terms of order \(\ln\Lambda\),

\[\int_{0}^{b_{\mathrm{max}}}\mathrm{d}b\,b\Delta v_{\parallel}=2\frac{m_{a}V_{ 0}b_{90}^{2}}{m+m_{a}}\ln\Lambda,\] (L.13)

At this point we investigate whether diffusion tensors of rank higher than two play an important role in gravitational scattering. By analogy with equation (L.6), a diffusion tensor of rank \(n\), \(D[\Delta v_{i1}\cdots\Delta v_{in}]\), will involve products of unit vectors with terms such as \((\Delta v_{\parallel})^{c}(\Delta v_{\perp})^{d}\) where \(c+d=n\). When \(b\gg b_{90}\), \(\Delta v_{\perp}\propto b^{-1}\) and \(\Delta v_{\parallel}\propto b^{-2}\), while for \(b\ll b_{90}\), \(\Delta v_{\perp}\propto b\) and \(\Delta v_{\parallel}\propto\mathit{constant}\). Therefore \((\Delta v_{\parallel})^{c}(\Delta v_{\perp})^{d}\propto b^{-2c-d}=b^{-n-c}\) for \(b\gg b_{90}\) and \(\propto b^{d}=b^{n-c}\) for \(b\ll b_{90}\). The integral over impact parameter in the analog of equation (L.10) will have the form \(\int\mathrm{d}b\,b^{1-n-c}\) for \(b\gg b_{90}\) and \(\int\mathrm{d}b\,b^{1+n-c}\) for \(b\ll b_{90}\). Since \(0\leq c\leq n\) these integralscannot give a divergent factor such as \(\ln\Lambda\) for \(n>2\). Thus diffusion tensors of rank three or higher are smaller than the tensors of ranks 1 and 2 by at least \({\rm O}(\ln\Lambda)^{-1}\), and hence can be dropped in the Fokker-Planck expansion (7.65) (Henon 1973b). An explicit calculation of the higher-rank tensors is given by Henon (1960a).

Using equations (L.6), (L.10), and (L.13) we can write the diffusion tensors as

\[\eqalign{D[\Delta v_{i}]&=-4\pi{m_{a}\over m+m_{a}}\int{\rm d}^{3}{\bf v}_{a} \,V_{0}^{2}b_{90}^{2}f_{a}({\bf v}_{a})\ln\Lambda(\hat{\bf e}_{i}\cdot\hat{\bf e }_{1}^{\prime}),\cr D[\Delta v_{i}\Delta v_{j}]&=4\pi\left({m_{a}\over m+m_{a} }\right)^{2}\int{\rm d}^{3}{\bf v}_{a}\,V_{0}^{3}b_{90}^{2}f_{a}({\bf v}_{a}) \ln\Lambda\cr&\qquad\qquad\times\big{[}(\hat{\bf e}_{i}\cdot\hat{\bf e}_{2}^{ \prime})(\hat{\bf e}_{j}\cdot\hat{\bf e}_{2}^{\prime})+(\hat{\bf e}_{i}\cdot \hat{\bf e}_{3}^{\prime})(\hat{\bf e}_{j}\cdot\hat{\bf e}_{3}^{\prime})\big{]}.}\]

Since we assume that \(\ln\Lambda\) is large, we do not make any significant additional error by replacing the factor \(V_{0}\) in \(\Lambda\) by some typical stellar speed \(v_{\rm typ}\). Thus

\[\Lambda={b_{\rm max}v_{\rm typ}^{2}\over G(m+m_{a})},\]

which is independent of \({\bf v}_{a}\) so \(\ln\Lambda\) may be taken outside the integral. Also, the expressions involving unit vectors can be simplified, since \(\sum_{p=1}^{3}(\hat{\bf e}_{i}\cdot\hat{\bf e}_{p}^{\prime})(\hat{\bf e}_{j} \cdot\hat{\bf e}_{p}^{\prime})=\hat{\bf e}_{i}\cdot\hat{\bf e}_{j}=\delta_{ij}\), and \((\hat{\bf e}_{i}\cdot\hat{\bf e}_{1}^{\prime})=V_{0i}/V_{0}\). After eliminating \(b_{90}\) using equation (L.8), we obtain

\[\eqalign{D[\Delta v_{i}]&=-4\pi G^{2}m_{a}(m+m_{a})\ln\Lambda\int{\rm d}^{3}{ \bf v}_{a}\,{f_{a}({\bf v}_{a})\over V_{0}^{3}}V_{0i},\cr D[\Delta v_{i}\Delta v _{j}]&=4\pi G^{2}m_{a}^{2}\ln\Lambda\int{\rm d}^{3}{\bf v}_{a}\,{f_{a}({\bf v} _{a})\over V_{0}}\left(\delta_{ij}-{V_{0i}V_{0j}\over V_{0}^{2}}\right).}\]

These equations can be simplified further by noting that \(V_{0}=[\sum_{i=1}^{3}(v_{i}-v_{ai})^{2}]^{1/2}\), so

\[{\partial\over\partial v_{i}}{1\over V_{0}}=-{V_{0i}\over V_{0}^{3}}\quad;\quad {\partial^{2}\over\partial v_{i}\partial v_{j}}V_{0}={\delta_{ij}\over V_{0}} -{V_{0i}V_{0j}\over V_{0}^{3}};\]

thus we can write

\[\eqalign{D[\Delta v_{i}]&=4\pi G^{2}m_{a}(m+m_{a})\ln\Lambda{\partial\over \partial v_{i}}h({\bf v}),\cr D[\Delta v_{i}\Delta v_{j}]&=4\pi G^{2}m_{a}^{2} \ln\Lambda{\partial^{2}\over\partial v_{i}\partial v_{j}}g({\bf v}),}\]

where the **Rosenbluth potentials** are

\[h({\bf v})\equiv\int{\rm d}^{3}{\bf v}_{a}\,{f_{a}({\bf v}_{a})\over|{\bf v}-{ \bf v}_{a}|}\quad;\quad g({\bf v})\equiv\int{\rm d}^{3}{\bf v}_{a}\,f_{a}({ \bf v}_{a})|{\bf v}-{\bf v}_{a}|.\]

To within a proportionality constant, \(h({\bf v})\) is simply the gravitational potential at \({\bf v}\) generated by a fictitious body in velocity space that has density \(f_{a}({\bf v}_{a})\) (cf. eq. 2.3).

When the field star df is isotropic, so \(f_{a}({\bf v}_{a})\) depends only on \(v_{a}=|{\bf v}_{a}|\), more explicit expressions can be obtained. In this case, symmetry dictates that the Rosenbluth potentials depend only on \(v=|{\bf v}|\). To evaluate \(g(v)\) and \(h(v)\), we use equation (C.35) to write

\[{1\over|{\bf v}-{\bf v}_{a}|}=\sum_{l=0}^{\infty}{v_{<}^{l}\over v_{>}^{l+1}}P_ {l}(\cos\gamma),\]where \(v_{<}\) and \(v_{>}\) are the smaller and larger of \(v\) and \(v_{a}\), \(P_{l}(x)\) is a Legendre polynomial, and \(\gamma\) is the angle between \({\bf v}\) and \({\bf v}_{a}\). Thus

\[h(v)=2\pi\sum_{l=0}^{\infty}\int_{0}^{\infty}{\rm d}v_{a}\,\frac{v_{a}^{2}v_{<} ^{l}}{v_{>}^{l+1}}f_{a}(v_{a})\int_{0}^{\pi}{\rm d}\gamma\,\sin\gamma\,P_{l}( \cos\gamma).\] (L.21)

Using the relation \(\int_{-1}^{1}{\rm d}x\,P_{l}(x)=2\delta_{l0}\) (eq. C.39 with \(m=n=0\)) we obtain

\[h(v)=4\pi\left[\frac{1}{v}\int_{0}^{v}{\rm d}v_{a}\,v_{a}^{2}f_{a}(v_{a})+\int _{v}^{\infty}{\rm d}v_{a}\,v_{a}f_{a}(v_{a})\right].\] (L.22)

To evaluate \(g(v)\) we write \(|{\bf v}-{\bf v}_{a}|=(v^{2}-2vv_{a}\cos\gamma+v_{a}^{2})/|{\bf v}-{\bf v}_{a}|\) and use equation (L.20) to expand \(1/|{\bf v}-{\bf v}_{a}|\). Using the identity \(\int_{-1}^{1}{\rm d}x\,xP_{l}(x)=\frac{2}{3}\delta_{l1}\) (eq. C.39 with \(m=0\), \(n=1\)) we find that

\[g(v)=\frac{4\pi}{3}\bigg{[}\int_{0}^{v}{\rm d}v_{a}\,\Big{(}3v_{a}^{2}v+\frac {v_{a}^{4}}{v}\Big{)}f_{a}(v_{a})+\int_{v}^{\infty}{\rm d}v_{a}\,\Big{(}3v_{a} ^{3}+v^{2}v_{a}\Big{)}f_{a}(v_{a})\bigg{]}.\] (L.23)

Since the Rosenbluth potentials depend only on \(v\), we can use the relation \(\partial v/\partial v_{i}=v_{i}/v\) to rewrite equations (L.18) in the form

\[\begin{split} D[\Delta v_{i}]&=\frac{v_{i}}{v}D[ \Delta v_{\parallel}],\\ D[\Delta v_{i}\Delta v_{j}]&=\frac{v_{i}v_{j}}{v^{ 2}}\big{(}D[(\Delta v_{\parallel})^{2}]-\frac{1}{2}D[(\Delta{\bf v}_{\perp})^ {2}]\big{)}+\frac{1}{2}\delta_{ij}D[(\Delta{\bf v}_{\perp})^{2}],\end{split}\] (L.24)

where

\[\begin{split} D[\Delta v_{\parallel}]=4\pi G^{2}m_{a}(m+m_{a}) \ln\Lambda\,h^{\prime}(v),\\ D[(\Delta v_{\parallel})^{2}]=4\pi G^{2}m_{a}^{2}\ln\Lambda\,g^{ \prime\prime}(v)\quad;\quad D[(\Delta{\bf v}_{\perp})^{2}]=\frac{8\pi G^{2}m_{a }^{2}\ln\Lambda}{v}g^{{}^{\prime}}(v).\end{split}\] (L.25)

The reason for this notation can be seen by considering the case in which \({\bf v}\) lies along one of the coordinate axes, say \(\hat{\bf e}_{1}\), so \(v_{1}=v\) and \(v_{2}=v_{3}=0\). Then \(D[(\Delta v_{\parallel})^{2}]=D[(\Delta v_{1})^{2}]\) is the total diffusion rate parallel to the velocity vector, and \(D[(\Delta{\bf v}_{\perp})^{2}]=2D[(\Delta v_{2})^{2}]=2D[(\Delta v_{3})^{2}]\) is the total diffusion rate in the two-dimensional plane perpendicular to the velocity vector. Note that here the subscripts "\(\parallel\)" and "\(\perp\)" refer to directions parallel and perpendicular to the subject star velocity \({\bf v}\), whereas in equations (L.4) to (L.13) they refer to directions parallel and perpendicular to the relative velocity \({\bf V}={\bf v}-{\bf v}_{a}\).

Substituting from equations (L.22) and (L.23) into (L.25), we have

\[\begin{split} D[\Delta v_{\parallel}]&=-\frac{16\pi^ {2}G^{2}m_{a}(m+m_{a})\ln\Lambda}{v^{2}}\int_{0}^{v}{\rm d}v_{a}\,v_{a}^{2}f_{ a}(v_{a}),\\ D[(\Delta v_{\parallel})^{2}]&=\frac{32\pi^{2}G^{2 }m_{a}^{2}\ln\Lambda}{3}\bigg{[}\int_{0}^{v}{\rm d}v_{a}\,\frac{v_{a}^{4}}{v^{ 3}}f_{a}(v_{a})+\int_{v}^{\infty}{\rm d}v_{a}\,v_{a}f_{a}(v_{a})\bigg{]},\\ D[(\Delta{\bf v}_{\perp})^{2}]&=\frac{32\pi^{2}G^{2 }m_{a}^{2}\ln\Lambda}{3}\\ &\qquad\times\bigg{[}\int_{0}^{v}{\rm d}v_{a}\,\Big{(}\frac{3v_{ a}^{2}}{v}-\frac{v_{a}^{4}}{v^{3}}\Big{)}f_{a}(v_{a})+2\int_{v}^{\infty}{\rm d}v_{a} \,v_{a}f_{a}(v_{a})\bigg{]}.\end{split}\] (L.26)

Notice that \(D[\Delta v_{\parallel}]\) depends only on the total number density of field stars traveling slower than the subject star. This is a simple corollary of Newton's first and second theorems from SS2.2.1: the Rosenbluth potential \(h({\bf v})\) (eq. L.19) is the velocity-spaceanalog of the definition (2.3) of the gravitational potential, with \({\bf v}\) replacing \({\bf x}\) and \(f_{a}({\bf v}_{a})\) replacing \(\rho({\bf x}^{\prime})\); the expression for \(D[\Delta v_{i}]\) in (L.18) involves the velocity-space gradient of \(h({\bf v})\), which is analogous to the force; so Newton's theorems tell us that this gradient depends only on the number of stars inside the velocity-space sphere of radius \(|{\bf v}|\).

_Appendix M:_ **The distribution of binary energies**

We have argued in SSSS4.10.1 and 7.3.2 that a stellar system has no maximum-entropy state and thus cannot be in thermal equilibrium. Nevertheless, for many purposes the distribution of soft binaries can be approximated by an equilibrium distribution, because soft binaries are so weakly bound that they approach equilibrium much faster than the stellar system as a whole.

**M.1 The evolution of the energy distribution of binaries**

As in SS7.5.7, we let \(B(\widetilde{E})\) denote the destruction rate for a binary of energy \(\widetilde{E}\), while \(C(\widetilde{E}^{\prime})\,{\rm d}\widetilde{E}^{\prime}\) and \(Q(\widetilde{E},\widetilde{E}^{\prime})\,{\rm d}\widetilde{E}^{\prime}\) are, respectively, the rate of creation of binaries with energy in the range \((\widetilde{E}^{\prime},\widetilde{E}^{\prime}+{\rm d}\widetilde{E}^{\prime})\) and the rate at which a binary of energy \(\widetilde{E}\) makes transitions to energies in this range.1 These functions are related by

Footnote 1: The tilde on \(\widetilde{E}\) is a flag that in this appendix “energy” denotes a quantity having units \({\rm mass}\times({\rm velocity})^{2}\), rather than the specific energy (energy per unit mass) with units \(({\rm velocity})^{2}\) that is common elsewhere in the book.

\[B(\widetilde{E})=\int_{0}^{\infty}{\rm d}\widetilde{E}^{\prime}Q(\widetilde{ E},\widetilde{E}^{\prime});\]

that is, the rate of destruction of binaries is the rate at which they make transitions to positive energy. The number of binaries \(n_{\rm b}(\widetilde{E},t)\) per unit volume and per unit energy must satisfy a master equation (cf. SS7.4.1 and Goodman & Hut 1993)

\[\eqalign{{\partial n_{\rm b}\over\partial t}&=C(\widetilde{E})-B(\widetilde{E })n_{\rm b}(\widetilde{E},t)\cr&\qquad+\int_{-\infty}^{0}{\rm d}\widetilde{E}^ {\prime}n_{\rm b}(\widetilde{E}^{\prime},t)Q(\widetilde{E}^{\prime},\widetilde {E})-n_{\rm b}(\widetilde{E},t)\int_{-\infty}^{0}{\rm d}\widetilde{E}^{ \prime}Q(\widetilde{E},\widetilde{E}^{\prime});}\]

the third and fourth terms on the right side denote respectively the rate at which binaries transition into a unit energy range centered on \(\widetilde{E}\), and the rate at which binaries already in this energy range are scattered to other bound energies. Equation (M.2) assumes that binaries interact only with single field stars, not with one another; this is reasonable if the fraction of stars in binaries is small.

Fitting functions for \(B(\widetilde{E})\), \(C(\widetilde{E})\), and \(Q(\widetilde{E},\widetilde{E}^{\prime})\) are given by Heggie & Hut (1993) and the steady-state solution for equation (M.2) is described by Goodman & Hut (1993).

### M.2 The two-body distribution function in thermal equilibrium

Consider a system of \(N\) identical stars of mass \(m\), contained in a volume \(V\). We denote the position and velocity of star \(n\) by \({\bf w}_{n}=({\bf x}_{n},{\bf v}_{n})\), and invoke the Jeans swindle to neglect large-scale gravitational fields within the system. As in SS7.2.3, we define the two-body df\(f^{(2)}\) such that the probability of finding two stars in the phase-space volume elements \({\rm d}^{6}{\bf w}_{1}\) and \({\rm d}^{6}{\bf w}_{2}\) is \(f^{(2)}({\bf w}_{1},{\bf w}_{2})\,{\rm d}^{6}{\bf w}_{1}{\rm d}^{6}{\bf w}_{2}\).

In thermal equilibrium we expect that

\[f^{(2)}({\bf w}_{1},{\bf w}_{2})=c{\rm e}^{-\beta\widetilde{H}},\] (M.3)

where \(c\) and \(\beta\) are positive constants and the Hamiltonian \(\widetilde{H}\) is given by

\[\widetilde{H}({\bf w}_{1},{\bf w}_{2})={{1\over 2}}m(v_{1}^{2}+v_{2}^{2})-{Gm^{2} \over|{\bf x}_{1}-{\bf x}_{2}|}.\] (M.4)

If the separation of stars 1 and 2 is large, their distributions should be independent, so \(f^{(2)}\) should be simply the product of one-body dfs. If these are Maxwellian, with dispersion \(\sigma\), then we have

\[f^{(2)}({\bf w}_{1},{\bf w}_{2})={1\over(2\pi\sigma^{2})^{3}V^{2}}{\rm e}^{-v_ {1}^{2}/2\sigma^{2}}{\rm e}^{-v_{2}^{2}/2\sigma^{2}}\quad{\rm as}\ |{\bf x}_{1}-{\bf x}_{2}|\to\infty.\] (M.5)

Comparison of equations (M.3) and (M.5) permits us to evaluate the constants \(c\) and \(\beta\). We find

\[f^{(2)}({\bf w}_{1},{\bf w}_{2})={1\over(2\pi\sigma^{2})^{3}V^{2}}\exp\left[- {1\over 2\sigma^{2}}\left(v_{1}^{2}+v_{2}^{2}-{2Gm\over|{\bf x}_{1}-{\bf x}_{2} |}\right)\right].\] (M.6)

### M.3 The distribution of binary energies in thermal equilibrium

In thermal equilibrium, the number density of binaries with internal energies in the range \((\widetilde{E},\widetilde{E}+{\rm d}\widetilde{E})\) is \(n_{\rm eq}(\widetilde{E}){\rm d}\widetilde{E}\), where

\[n_{\rm eq}(\widetilde{E})={N^{2}\over 2V}\!\int\!{\rm d}^{6}{\bf w}_{1}{\rm d }^{6}{\bf w}_{2}\,f^{(2)}({\bf w}_{1},{\bf w}_{2})\delta\big{[}\widetilde{E} ({\bf w}_{1},{\bf w}_{2})-\widetilde{E}\big{]}.\] (M.7)

In this expression we have used the identity (C.7), and we have divided by a factor of two because each binary pair is counted twice in the integration. The function \(\widetilde{E}({\bf w}_{1},{\bf w}_{2})\), the internal energy of the binary, is given by equation (7.162).

At this point it is convenient to convert the integration variables from \({\bf v}_{1}\) and \({\bf v}_{2}\) to the relative velocity \({\bf V}\) and the center of mass velocity \({\bf v}_{\rm cm}={1\over 2}({\bf v}_{1}+{\bf v}_{2})\). Following the arguments after equation (7.189), we have

\[\begin{array}{l} n_{\rm eq}(\widetilde{E})={n^{2}\over 16\pi^{3} \sigma^{6}V}\int{\rm d}^{3}{\bf x}_{1}{\rm d}^{3}{\bf x}_{2}{\rm d}^{3}{\bf v }_{\rm cm}{\rm d}^{3}{\bf V}\\ \times\exp\left[-{1\over\sigma^{2}}\left(v_{\rm cm}^{2}+{{1\over 4}}V^{2}-{Gm \over|{\bf x}_{1}-{\bf x}_{2}|}\right)\right]\delta\left({{1\over 4}}mV^{2}-{Gm^{2} \over|{\bf x}_{1}-{\bf x}_{2}|}-\widetilde{E}\right),\end{array}\] (M.8)

where \(N/V\) has been replaced by the number density \(n\). The integral over \({\bf v}_{\rm cm}\) is given in equation (7.192). Because of the \(\delta\) function, we can set \({1\over 4}V^{2}-Gm/|{\bf x}_{1}-{\bf x}_{2}|=\widetilde{E}/m\) in the exponential. We can replace the dummy variable \({\bf x}_{1}\) by \({\bf x}={\bf x}_{1}-{\bf x}_{2}\); moreover, since we are primarily interested in binaries whose separation \(|{\bf x}|\) is much less than the system size, we can just as well extend the integral overto cover all space (i.e., we neglect edge effects). In this approximation, the integral over \({\bf x}_{2}\) is independent of \({\bf x}\) and yields \(\int{\rm d}^{3}{\bf x}_{2}=V\). Thus

\[n_{\rm eq}(\widetilde{E})={n^{2}\over 16\pi^{3/2}\sigma^{3}}{\rm e}^{-\widetilde {E}/m\sigma^{2}}\int{\rm d}^{3}{\bf x}\,{\rm d}^{3}{\bf V}\,\delta\left({ {1\over 4}}mV^{2}-{Gm^{2}\over|{\bf x}|}-\widetilde{E}\right).\]

Since the integrand depends only on \(|{\bf x}|\) and \(|{\bf v}|\),

\[n_{\rm eq}(\widetilde{E})={\pi^{1/2}n^{2}\over\sigma^{3}}{\rm e}^{-\widetilde {E}/m\sigma^{2}}\int_{0}^{\infty}{\rm d}V\,V^{2}\int_{0}^{\infty}{\rm d}x\,x^{2 }\delta\left({{1\over 4}}mV^{2}-{Gm^{2}\over x}-\widetilde{E} \right).\]

Performing the integration over \(x\), replacing the number density \(n\) by the mass density \(\rho=mn\), and recalling that \(\widetilde{E}<0\), we obtain

\[n_{\rm eq}(\widetilde{E})={\pi^{1/2}G^{3}\rho^{2}m^{4}\over\sigma^{3}}{\rm e }^{|\widetilde{E}|/m\sigma^{2}}\int_{0}^{\infty}{{\rm d}V\,V^{2}\over({1\over 4 }mV^{2}+|\widetilde{E}|)^{4}}.\]

Using the result \(\int_{0}^{\infty}{\rm d}x\,x^{2}/(x^{2}+1)^{4}=\pi/32\), we find

\[n_{\rm eq}(\widetilde{E})={\pi^{3/2}G^{3}\rho^{2}m^{5/2}\over 4\sigma^{3}| \widetilde{E}|^{5/2}}{\rm e}^{|\widetilde{E}|/m\sigma^{2}}.\]

The integral \(\int{\rm d}\widetilde{E}\,n_{\rm eq}(\widetilde{E})\) diverges, both as \(|\widetilde{E}|\to 0\) and as \(|\widetilde{E}|\to\infty\). The divergence as \(|\widetilde{E}|\to\infty\) is not worrying because hard binaries are not in thermal equilibrium anyway; rather, as we have seen in SS7.5.7, there is a steady flux of hard binaries towards larger and larger values of \(|\widetilde{E}|\). The divergence as \(|\widetilde{E}|\to 0\) arises because of the large volume of phase space available to a loosely bound binary. However, in a realistic stellar cluster such binaries are very short-lived, because they are disrupted by tidal forces or encounters with field stars. In particular, tidal forces--which are not present in this analysis because of the Jeans swindle--impose an upper limit to the semi-major axis of a binary that is roughly the Jacobi radius \(r_{\rm J}\approx R/N^{1/3}\) (eq. 8.91), where \(M=Nm\) and \(R\) are the mass and radius of the host system. Hence the minimum \(|\widetilde{E}|\) for a binary is \(|\widetilde{E}|_{\rm min}\approx Gm^{2}/r_{\rm J}\approx GmM/(N^{2/3}R)\), and using the virial theorem in the form \(\sigma^{2}\approx GM/R\) we can write

\[|\widetilde{E}|_{\rm min}\approx{m\sigma^{2}\over N^{2/3}}.\]

Thus we expect that the relation (M.12) applies in the energy range \(|\widetilde{E}|_{\rm min}\lesssim|\widetilde{E}|\lesssim m\sigma^{2}\)--all soft binaries that can survive tidal disruption--so long as there has been time for thermal equilibrium to be achieved.

We may use these results to estimate the equilibrium total number of soft binaries in a cluster. We integrate equation (M.12) over the range just derived to obtain the number density of soft binaries,

\[n_{\rm soft}\simeq{\pi^{3/2}G^{3}\rho^{2}m^{5/2}\over 4\sigma^{3}}\int_{| \widetilde{E}|_{\rm min}}^{m\sigma^{2}}{\rm d}|\widetilde{E}|{{\rm e}^{| \widetilde{E}|/m\sigma^{2}}\over|\widetilde{E}|^{5/2}}\simeq{\pi^{3/2}G^{3} \rho^{2}m\over 4\sigma^{6}}\int_{N^{-2/3}}^{1}{\rm d}x{{\rm e}^{x}\over x^{5/2}}.\]

For \(N\gg 1\) the integral is dominated by the region \(x\ll 1\), so we can replace the exponential by unity to find

\[n_{\rm soft}\approx{\pi^{3/2}G^{3}\rho^{2}mN\over 6\sigma^{6}}.\]The total number of soft binaries is \(N_{\rm soft}\approx n_{\rm soft}R^{3}\), and if we set \(\rho\approx Nm/R^{3}\) and \(\sigma^{2}\approx GNm/R\), we find \(N_{\rm soft}\approx 1\), that is, there is only of order one soft binary in the whole system! Obviously, soft binaries play no role of any consequence in the evolution of stellar systems; even a large primordial population of soft binaries will rapidly be destroyed by encounters with field stars.

### **M.4 The principle of detailed balance**

Let us temporarily assume that a stellar system is in thermal equilibrium. The rate of creation of binaries per unit volume is \(C(\widetilde{E})\), and in thermal equilibrium this must equal the rate of destruction of binaries per unit volume, which is \(B(\widetilde{E})n_{\rm eq}(\widetilde{E})\). Similarly, the rate of transition of binaries from energies in the range \((\widetilde{E},\widetilde{E}+{\rm d}\widetilde{E})\) to energies in the range \((\widetilde{E}^{\prime},\widetilde{E}^{\prime}+{\rm d}\widetilde{E}^{\prime})\) is \(n_{\rm eq}(\widetilde{E})Q(\widetilde{E},\widetilde{E}^{\prime})\,{\rm d} \widetilde{E}\,{\rm d}\widetilde{E}^{\prime}\), and this must equal the rate of transition in the opposite direction, which is \(n_{\rm eq}(\widetilde{E}^{\prime})Q(\widetilde{E}^{\prime},\widetilde{E})\,{ \rm d}\widetilde{E}{\rm d}\widetilde{E}^{\prime}\). Thus

\[\eqalign{C(\widetilde{E})&=B(\widetilde{E})n_{\rm eq}(\widetilde{E})\cr n_{ \rm eq}(\widetilde{E})Q(\widetilde{E},\widetilde{E}^{\prime})&=n_{\rm eq}( \widetilde{E}^{\prime})Q(\widetilde{E}^{\prime},\widetilde{E}),}\]

where \(n_{\rm eq}(\widetilde{E})\) is given by equation (M.12). It is easy to verify that if these relations are satisfied then so is the master equation (M.2).

Equation (M.12) can be combined with the first of equations (M.16) to yield

\[C(\widetilde{E})={\pi^{3/2}G^{3}\rho^{2}m^{5/2}\over 4\sigma^{3}|\widetilde{E} |^{5/2}}{\rm e}^{|\widetilde{E}|/m\sigma^{2}}B(\widetilde{E}).\]

The rates \(B(\widetilde{E})\) and \(C(\widetilde{E})\) do not depend on the assumption that the binary distribution is in thermal equilibrium, so long as the field-star distribution is Maxwellian. Hence (M.17) must hold whether or not the binary distribution is in thermal equilibrium. This **principle of detailed balance** allows us to calculate both the formation and destruction rates if either one of them is known (Heggie 1975).

